/* Longer species accounts, in the style and length of a Lone Pine field guide.
   Keyed by species id, loaded separately from species.js, and shown in the optional
   "In depth" section on each species page. Paragraphs are separated by a blank line.
   This file grows over time; not every species has an account yet. */
window.SPECIES_NOTES = {
  "long-tailed-weasel": "The largest of the province's three weasels, this is a long, low-slung hunter you are most likely to glimpse crossing a fencerow or slipping along a brushy field edge. The coat is brown above and yellowish-white below, and the tail is long, more than 40 percent of the head and body, tipped with black. Size is the main clue that separates it from the smaller ermine and least weasel. In the north, animals moult to white for winter but hold onto that black tail tip.\n\nIt works brushy edges, open woodland, and farmland through all four seasons, usually not far from water. For its size it is bold. Rabbits and ground squirrels, both larger than the weasel itself, are regular prey, and it will follow small mammals right into their burrows. Southern and central Ontario is the range. Sightings tend to be brief, since a weasel on the move rarely pauses long enough for a second look.",
  "arctic-fox": "Ontario sits at the very southern edge of this fox's range. It turns up only along the Hudson Bay coast of the far north, and mostly in fall and winter. Smaller and stockier than a red fox, it has short rounded ears, a short muzzle, and a thick bushy tail. The winter coat is pure white; in summer it is brownish-grey. Furred feet and those small ears are the features that set it apart from a red fox at a distance.\n\nOn the tundra it makes part of its living by following others. Polar bears out on the coast leave seal remains behind, and the fox trails them to scavenge what is left. The heavy coat and compact build are what let it stay out through the coldest months. Anyone this far north should treat a sighting as a genuine find, since the animal is right at the limit of where it can survive in the province.",
  "least-weasel": "No carnivore in the world weighs less. A least weasel is smaller even than an ermine, brown above and white below, with a very short tail that never carries the black tip you see on the other two Ontario weasels. In winter the whole animal goes white. It works meadows, grasslands, farm fields, and marsh edges wherever voles and mice are thick.\n\nBeing tiny is the whole strategy. Light enough to slip into a mouse or vole burrow, it hunts its prey underground in the runways where they live and nest. That habit, along with its size, keeps it hard to see, and it is seldom recorded in the province. The range runs through central and northern Ontario. If you do find one, note it. Encounters are uncommon, and a clean look at the plain tail tip is what confirms it against the black-tipped ermine.",
  "deer-mouse": "Big ears and large dark eyes give this mouse an alert look. It is brown to grey above and clean white below, with a sharply two-toned, well-furred tail. Those same features fit the closely related white-footed mouse, and telling the two apart in the hand is genuinely difficult. It lives almost everywhere in the province, in forests, fields, rocky ground, and cabins, and it comes out at night.\n\nThis is the main North American carrier of hantavirus. In Ontario the real risk of infection is low, but the animal still calls for basic care. The virus can be present in droppings and nests, so avoid stirring up dust when cleaning a shed, cabin, or other closed space where mice have been. Wet the area down and let it air out rather than sweeping dry. As an abundant seed-eater it feeds much of what hunts small mammals here.",
  "house-mouse": "Wherever people store food, this small grey-brown mouse tends to turn up. It is dusty grey-brown more or less all over, tail included, and that tail is scaly and only sparsely haired rather than sharply two-toned. Up close, the upper incisors show a slight notch, and handled animals give off a musky smell. Barns, granaries, buildings, and disturbed ground near settlement are its habitat.\n\nIt is not native. Brought from the Old World with human trade, it has spread almost everywhere people live and keep grain. Active mainly at night, it stays close to food and shelter and can build up quickly indoors. The main practical concern is contamination, since droppings foul stored food, so grain and pantry goods are best kept in sealed containers. Out in open country it is largely replaced by native mice, and you are most likely to meet it in and around structures rather than in the bush.",
  "masked-shrew": "This is the shrew you are most likely to have around without knowing it. Ontario's most widespread, it is a tiny brownish-grey animal with a long pointed snout, pinprick eyes, velvety fur, and a fairly long tail for a shrew. At three to six grams it is easy to overlook, and it lives in moist forests, grasslands, and marsh edges across nearly the whole province.\n\nIts life is ruled by its metabolism. A body that small loses heat fast, so it burns energy at a furious rate and has to eat more than its own weight in food each day, mostly insects, worms, and other small invertebrates. That means it is active in short bursts around the clock rather than settling into a single daily rhythm, and it cannot go long without feeding. You are more likely to find a dead one dropped by a cat than to watch a live one, since it spends most of its time hidden in litter and runways.",
  "meadow-vole": "Part the grass in an old field and you may find the sign before the animal: narrow clipped runways worn through the thatch, kept clear by a small rodent that spends its life in them. The meadow vole is stocky, with a blunt nose, ears nearly buried in the fur, and a short tail about a third of the body length. Grassy fields, meadows, and marsh edges with dense cover are where it lives.\n\nFew Ontario mammals are as productive. It breeds heavily, and its numbers rise and fall in strong cycles, some years abundant and others scarce. Those swings ripple outward, because voles are a staple food for owls, foxes, hawks, weasels, and much else that hunts small mammals. In that sense a good vole year is a good year for a lot of predators. It is active off and on through day and night, and it stays out all winter, moving through runways beneath the snow.",
  "northern-short-tailed-shrew": "Larger and heavier than Ontario's other shrews, this one is a robust slate-grey animal with tiny eyes, a pointed snout, and a short tail under half its body length. It lives in forests, fields, and gardens wherever there is leaf litter and loose soil to tunnel through. Bulk and colour usually separate it from the smaller brown shrews.\n\nIt is one of the few venomous mammals. Its saliva carries a toxin that helps it subdue insects and even mice, prey it could not otherwise handle at its size, and it finds its way through dark tunnels partly by faint clicks. The venom is the reason not to pick one up. A bite can be genuinely painful, so leave the animal alone, and move it, if you must, with a container rather than a bare hand. It is common enough across southern and central Ontario, though its underground habits keep it mostly out of sight.",
  "norway-rat": "A big, coarse rodent of human places, the Norway rat is grey-brown with small ears, a blunt nose, and a thick scaly tail that is shorter than its head and body combined. That tail length is a useful check, since it separates this species from the black rat. It keeps to cities, farms, sewers, waterfronts, and dumps, and it is mainly active after dark.\n\nThe rat came a long way to get here, spreading from Asia through Europe and then around the world with shipping. It digs burrows and lives in colonies, often beneath buildings or along foundations, and it will damage stored grain where it can reach it. Sanitation and sealing entry points matter more than trapping alone. It can also carry diseases and parasites, so direct contact is worth avoiding, alive or dead. In Ontario it is a fixture of cities and farms across the south and of port towns, and much less a creature of open country.",
  "southern-red-backed-vole": "The rusty chestnut stripe running down the back is the field mark, standing out against greyer sides. Compared with a meadow vole, this one has larger ears and a longer tail, and it keeps to different ground: cool, damp coniferous and mixed forest with mossy logs and deep litter. It is a woodland vole rather than a field one.\n\nIts diet sets it apart. It eats a good deal of fungi, including the underground fruiting bodies of mycorrhizal fungi, the kind that grow in partnership with tree roots. By eating them and passing the spores, it helps spread the fungi that forest trees depend on to draw water and nutrients from the soil. That makes a small rodent part of how the forest keeps itself going. It is common in central and northern forests and turns up in the south as well, most often where the woods stay moist and shaded.",
  "white-footed-mouse": "Warm brown above, white on the feet and belly, this mouse is close to a twin of the deer mouse. The tail is a touch shorter and less crisply two-toned, but the difference is slight, and confirming which is which often comes down to measurements rather than a glance. It favours deciduous and mixed forest, brushy edges, and buildings in the southern part of the province, and it moves at night.\n\nIts importance goes beyond its looks. It is a key reservoir of the bacterium that causes Lyme disease and a major host for young blacklegged ticks, which feed on it and pick up the infection. That puts the mouse near the centre of how Lyme spreads through southern Ontario woods. The practical point is about the ticks, not the mouse itself: where it is common, check yourself for ticks after time in leafy edge habitat. The animal is otherwise a familiar seed- and insect-eater of the southern and central bush.",
  "arctic-shrew": "Most shrews are some even shade of brown or grey, which makes this one easier than usual to name. In fresh fur it is three-toned: a dark brown back, paler brown along the sides, and a grey-brown belly, with a two-toned tail to match. It is a stout shrew, and the banding is clearest in winter coat. Few other Ontario shrews show anything like it.\n\nIt keeps to cool, wet ground - marshes, bogs, sedge meadows, and damp forest edges - through central and northern Ontario. Like all shrews it lives fast, hunting insects and other small invertebrates in litter and runways and feeding often to keep up with its small body. The colour worn thin in summer fur can be harder to read, so a clean look in the colder months is the surer way to tell it. It is not a shrew of the far southern farmland, so location alone already narrows the possibilities.",
  "european-hare": "Larger than any native Ontario rabbit or hare, this animal was brought from Europe and turned loose near Brantford in the early 1900s. It has since spread across parts of the agricultural south. The coat is tan-brown, the ears very long and tipped with black, and the legs built for speed. On open ground it can reach more than 60 km per hour. The quickest way to separate it from a snowshoe hare is the winter coat: the European hare stays brown year-round, while the snowshoe turns white.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one at dawn or dusk in farmland, along field edges, and near hedgerows in the south. Through the day it rests in a shallow form pressed into the grass, then feeds in the low light. Being an introduced species, it is not native wildlife and carries no special protection here.",
  "hairy-tailed-mole": "A mole of well-drained sandy and loamy soils, it works forests, fields, and the edges between them across southeastern and central Ontario. The body is stout and black, the forefeet broad and turned outward for digging. The one detail that sets it apart from other moles is the tail, which is short and noticeably furry rather than nearly bare. Its naked nose has no fleshy star, so it will not be confused with the star-nosed mole.\n\nMost of the sign it leaves is underground work: raised ridges of soil where shallow tunnels run, and low molehills pushed up from deeper digging. It stays active all year. In winter it follows the soil down below the frost line, to where the ground does not freeze. You are far more likely to see its tunnels than the animal itself.",
  "least-chipmunk": "Ontario's smallest chipmunk trades the deciduous woods of the south for the conifers of the north. Find it in coniferous and mixed forest, on rocky outcrops, and along jack pine edges through central and northern Ontario, where it largely replaces the eastern chipmunk. It is slimmer and smaller than that familiar species, and the dark back stripes run all the way to the base of the tail rather than stopping short. A running least chipmunk usually carries its tail straight up.\n\nIt is active by day and stays above ground through the warm months, then goes below for winter. Like other chipmunks it caches seeds to carry it through the cold season. Beyond Ontario it is the most widespread chipmunk on the continent.",
  "meadow-jumping-mouse": "The very long tail is the giveaway, longer than the head and body together, trailing well behind a small yellowish-brown mouse with a dark band down the back and a white belly. The hind feet are large. When startled, the animal escapes in a series of long leaps rather than a straight run.\n\nIt lives in moist grassy meadows, along field edges, and around the borders of marshes, and it is widespread across the province. This is a true hibernator. It spends roughly half the year asleep, retreating underground in autumn and not emerging until late spring, so you will only meet it in the warmer months. It feeds at night on seeds, insects, and fungi, and puts on fat before the long sleep.",
  "smoky-shrew": "Cool, damp forest is where this shrew makes its living. It wants mature woods with deep leaf litter, moss, and fallen logs holding the moisture. It is a plain animal, uniformly dull grey-brown, larger than the common masked shrew, with a two-toned tail and pale feet that help separate it from its smaller relatives. Nests go under logs and into mossy banks along the humid forest floor.\n\nIn Ontario it belongs to the central and southern forests of the Canadian Shield. It stays active year-round, hunting insects, worms, and other small prey in the litter and the runways beneath it. Like all shrews it lives fast and burns through food quickly, so it forages around the clock rather than keeping to a fixed schedule.",
  "southern-bog-lemming": "Despite the name, this is a small vole-like rodent of bogs, wet meadows, and grassy or mossy openings, not a swarming animal of the tundra. It does not migrate or erupt the way arctic lemmings do. The body is stocky, the tail very short, the head broad and blunt.\n\nThe surest field mark is in the mouth: the upper incisors are grooved, a detail that separates it from the voles it otherwise resembles. Another clue is left in its runways, where the droppings are a bright green. It stays active through all seasons, tunnelling through grass and moss and cutting green stems for food. In Ontario it turns up in scattered spots across the south, the centre, and the north, never common but widely distributed.",
  "water-shrew": "Few shrews take to the water like this one. It is a large, dark shrew, blackish above and silvery below, with a long tail and big hind feet fringed with stiff hairs. Those hairs trap air, and the trapped air lets the animal skitter briefly across the surface of a pool before it dives. Underwater it hunts aquatic insects and other small prey.\n\nLook for it along cold, clear streams, ponds, and bogs where there is cover on the banks, mainly through central and northern Ontario. It stays active in every season, including winter, when it works open water and the spaces under ice and stream banks. It is seldom seen, being small, quick, and tied to running water in country that people do not often search closely.",
  "woodland-jumping-mouse": "Brighter and more colourful than its meadow cousin, this jumping mouse wears orange sides against a dark back, and its long tail ends in a clean white tip. That white tip is the quickest way to tell the two apart. Home is cool, moist coniferous and mixed forest with dense low plants, often close to a stream.\n\nWhen alarmed it can cover two to three metres in a single leap, bounding off through the undergrowth. It feeds at night and keeps to the warm months above ground. Like the meadow jumping mouse it is a deep hibernator, spending about six months underground through the Ontario winter. You will find it in the central and northern forests. Because it is nocturnal and quick to bolt, it is more often glimpsed than watched.",
  "franklins-ground-squirrel": "On the ground this one can pass for a grey squirrel: grey-brown, grizzled across the back without bold stripes, and carrying a fairly bushy tail. It is a ground squirrel, though, living in tall grass and shrubby edges around meadows, marshes, and woodland borders. In Ontario its range is very small. It is restricted to the Rainy River area in the far northwest, and nowhere else in the province.\n\nThe animal keeps to cover and is often heard before it is seen, giving a musical, bird-like trill from the grass. It is active by day through the warm months and hibernates through winter. It is not listed as at risk, but its narrow Ontario foothold and love of dense grass make it easy to overlook. A trill from a marshy edge is sometimes the only sign you get.",
  "pygmy-shrew": "Weighing about as much as a dime, this is one of the smallest mammals on Earth. It is a brownish shrew, even tinier than the masked shrew, with a relatively short tail, and it is genuinely hard to identify in the field. A sure call usually needs a look at the teeth.\n\nIt lives in forests, bogs, and moist openings with litter to hunt through, and it stays active in every season. Like other shrews it must eat almost constantly to fuel such a small body, working the ground cover for insects and other tiny prey around the clock. In Ontario it is scattered across the centre and north. It is rarely noticed, partly because of its size and partly because it is easily mistaken for the commoner shrews it lives among.",
  "rock-vole": "A warm wash of yellow to orange on the nose and rump marks this otherwise grey-brown vole, and it is the best clue to a species that is plain in every other respect. It lives close to mossy rock, in cool talus and boulder fields in forest near running water, and rarely strays far from it. Among the mossy boulders it often feeds on bunchberry.\n\nIts runways thread through the moss between stones rather than through open grass, which further separates it from the meadow voles of tidier ground. It is localized and seldom seen, active in all seasons but easy to miss in the broken, damp places it favours. In Ontario it belongs to the centre and northeast. Anyone hoping to find one looks for the right habitat first: moss-covered rock, cold air, and the sound of water nearby.",
  "thirteen-lined-ground-squirrel": "The stripes give it away. Thirteen alternating dark and light lines run the length of the back, and the dark ones are broken into neat rows of pale spots, a pattern no other Ontario mammal carries. It is a slender ground squirrel of short-grass ground: mowed roadsides, golf courses, airports, and dry fields with well-drained soil. When alert it stands bolt upright on its hind legs like a small sentinel, watching over the grass before dropping from sight.\n\nIt is a true hibernator, underground and asleep through the cold months, so it is a warm-season animal above ground. In Ontario it is found in the Rainy River area of the far west and at a few scattered sites in the southwest. It is active by day and keeps close to its burrow, diving in at the first sign of trouble.",
  "woodland-vole": "Of the province's voles, this is the one you are least likely to see. It spends most of its life underground, digging shallow tunnels through the loose soil and deep leaf litter of deciduous forest and old fields. The body is built for that work: a very short tail, reduced eyes and ears, and dense soft fur the colour of rusty earth.\n\nIn Ontario it holds on only in the Carolinian southwest, and it is listed as Special Concern, so records are thin and scattered. You are more apt to find its tunnels than the animal itself. It stays active through all four seasons, feeding on roots, tubers, and other plant matter it can reach without surfacing. Because it so rarely comes above ground, it goes unnoticed even in places where it lives.",
  "eastern-red-bat": "Rusty-red to orange fur sets this bat apart, and at rest it hangs alone in tree foliage, wrapped in its furred tail membrane so it reads as a dead leaf. Males run brighter than the frosted-looking females. The wings are rounded, and on summer nights it often works the air around streetlights for insects.\n\nThis is a tree bat and a long-distance migrant, present in southern and central Ontario through the warm months and gone south for winter. In 2023 COSEWIC assessed it as Endangered. The main problem is wind turbines, which kill migrating tree bats in large numbers. If you find one on the ground, leave it alone. Never handle a bat with bare hands, since a bite or scratch carries a rabies risk. Report a grounded or injured animal rather than picking it up.",
  "eastern-small-footed-bat": "One of the smallest bats in Ontario, and one of the rarest. A black facial mask across golden-brown fur, very small black feet, and dark wing membranes mark it out from the province's other small Myotis. It favours rocky ground: cliffs, talus slopes, and the forest close by, and it moves into caves and abandoned mines to hibernate. Look for it in eastern and central Ontario on that kind of broken terrain.\n\nIt is listed as Endangered here. White-nose syndrome, the fungal disease that has swept through hibernating bats, has hit it hard, and it was never common to begin with. Do not handle any bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk, and hibernating bats should not be disturbed at all, since rousing them burns the fat they need to last the winter.",
  "moose": "The moose is the largest member of the deer family and the unmistakable giant of Ontario's north. A big bull can stand over two metres at the shoulder and carry a rack of palmate antlers more than a metre and a half across, grown fresh each summer and shed in winter. For all that size, moose are strong swimmers and spend the warm months feeding in ponds and marshes, wading belly-deep to reach aquatic plants and to escape biting flies.\n\nTheir long legs and high shoulders let them move through deep snow and dense bush that would stop a deer. Autumn is the rut, when bulls grunt, thrash the brush, and spar for cows, and it is the season they are most often seen near roads. Give them room. A moose is calm until it is not, and a cow with a calf will not tolerate a close approach.",
  "white-tailed-deer": "The deer most people in Ontario already know. Reddish-brown in summer, greyer in winter, it turns up along farmland edges, in deciduous and mixed forest, and in suburban parks. When it bolts it lifts its tail to show a flash of white, which is where the name comes from. Bucks grow branching antlers and drop them each year.\n\nThis is the province's most abundant large mammal, most numerous in the south, and it is most active at dawn and dusk. Two cautions come with that abundance. It is a major host for blacklegged ticks, the ones that spread Lyme disease, so check yourself after walking in deer country. And it is a serious collision hazard on roads at first and last light, when animals are moving and drivers see least. Slow down through deer sign at those hours.",
  "american-black-bear": "Ontario's only bear across most of its range, usually all black with a brown muzzle and a straight facial profile. It lacks the shoulder hump of a grizzly, and grizzlies do not live here, so a large dark bear in Ontario is this one. It uses forest of every kind, from the boreal north to the Carolinian south, and often works berry patches in late summer. The provincial population is large and healthy.\n\nBears den for the winter and enter torpor rather than the deep hibernation people often picture, and they can rouse if disturbed. Treat it as the powerful animal it is. Never approach one, secure all food and garbage so animals do not learn to link people with meals, and give a sow with cubs a wide berth. A bear that finds food around people becomes a problem bear, and that rarely ends well for the bear.",
  "elk": "Bigger than a deer and smaller than a moose, with a tan body, a dark neck, and a pale rump patch. Bulls grow large sweeping antlers and bugle through the autumn rut, a high carrying call worth hearing once. Elk once lived in Ontario but were gone by the late 1800s. The animals here now trace to reintroductions that began in the late 1990s, with the best-known herd near Bancroft and others toward the Lake Huron north shore and Nipissing.\n\nSo the range is local, not continuous. Autumn and winter are the seasons you are most likely to encounter them, often in a mix of forest and open meadow. During the rut, bulls turn aggressive and unpredictable. Keep well back and do not crowd one for a photo.",
  "polar-bear": "The far northern coast is the only place in Ontario you will meet this animal. A massive cream-white bear with a long neck and small ears, it lives along the Hudson Bay and James Bay shores and out on the sea ice. Ontario's Hudson Bay coast holds the southernmost polar bears in the world. When the ice melts, they are stranded on land and fast through the summer, living off stored fat while they wait for freeze-up to hunt seals again.\n\nIt is listed as Threatened, and its future is tied to the ice, which now forms later and breaks up earlier. This is an apex predator and extremely dangerous. It will hunt people. Do not approach one under any circumstance, and travel with an experienced guide in bear country. There is no safe way to get close on your own.",
  "woodland-caribou": "A creature of the deep boreal, tied to old-growth forest, peatlands, and lichen-rich woodland. Both sexes may carry antlers, and you can pick it out by the pale neck mane and the broad concave hooves that spread its weight on snow and soft bog. Ground and tree lichens make up much of its winter diet, which is one reason it needs old, undisturbed forest.\n\nOntario's boreal population has pulled steadily northward as the southern edge of its range has been logged, roaded, and settled. It is listed as Threatened. Fall and winter are when it moves and gathers most. Many people know the animal only from the Canadian quarter, without ever seeing one in the bush. It is shy, thinly spread, and quick to leave disturbed ground, so a sighting in the wild is a genuine event.",
  "coyote": "Smaller than a wolf, with a narrow pointed muzzle and a bushy tail it carries low when it runs. In the east, many coyotes carry some wolf ancestry, which makes them larger than their western counterparts. It gets by almost anywhere: farmland, forest edges, ravines, and right into cities, Toronto included, where it hunts rodents and rabbits and scavenges what people leave out.\n\nYou will more often hear the yipping chorus at night than see the animal. It is not at risk, and its adaptability keeps it common. The main rule is simple. Do not feed it, and do not let it grow comfortable around people, because a fed coyote loses its wariness and becomes a problem. Keep small pets supervised, especially at dawn, dusk, and after dark. A healthy coyote wants nothing to do with you.",
  "raccoon": "The black mask and the bushy ringed tail need no introduction, and neither do the dexterous front paws. It lives in forests, wetlands, and farmland, and it thrives in cities, where Toronto's raccoons are close to a civic mascot. Those clever hands and a quick mind let it open latches, lids, and the 'raccoon-proof' bins built to stop it. Spring through fall is when it is most active, working after dark.\n\nIt is common and not at risk. The caution is real, though. Raccoons are a primary carrier of rabies in Ontario and also spread raccoon roundworm, whose eggs pass in their droppings and can infect people. Never handle one, keep pets' vaccinations current, and secure garbage so animals do not den or feed around your home. Clean up any latrine site carefully, or have it dealt with.",
  "red-fox": "A rusty-red coat, black stockings, and a long white-tipped brush make this one easy. Colour is not fixed, though, and cross and silver morphs turn up, so a darker fox with a white tail tip is still a red fox. It works fields, forest edges, ravines, and city neighbourhoods.\n\nIts winter hunting is worth watching. A fox listens for a mouse moving under the snow, then leaps and plunges through the crust to pin it, and it tends to line up those pounces toward the northeast, apparently using the Earth's magnetic field to judge the distance. Spring and winter are good times to catch one out in the open. It is common and not at risk. Mostly it hunts at dawn and dusk, trotting a regular route through its range.",
  "striped-skunk": "Cat-sized and black, with a white stripe down the head that splits into a V along the back. It moves slowly and does not spook easily, which is a clue to how it defends itself. When cornered it can spray a sulphurous musk with accuracy over several metres, and a stamp of the front feet is the final warning before it does. Back off when you see that.\n\nIt lives in farmland, woodland edges, and urban neighbourhoods, and it is out mostly at night from spring through fall. Common and not at risk. Beyond the smell, which is potent and lingers for days on skin, fur, and clothing, the skunk is a notable rabies carrier in the province. Do not approach one, keep dogs away, and if a skunk is calm and out in daylight, give it room and leave it be.",
  "ermine": "The smallest of Ontario's weasels, and one of the smallest carnivores in North America. In summer it is brown on the back and white underneath. Come late fall the coat turns fully white, leaving only a black tip at the end of the tail, and this winter fur is the \"ermine\" that was once cut into royal robes. The long, thin body lets it follow mice and voles right into their burrows. You might see one anywhere with cover - forest, field, a brushy fence line, the edge of a wetland - moving in quick low bounds and pausing to stand upright and look around. Activity is not tied to a single time of day.\n\nNames have shifted recently. What was long called Mustela erminea across the continent was split in 2021, and North America's mainland short-tailed weasel is now Mustela richardsonii. In the field the change means nothing. A tiny white weasel with a black-tipped tail in winter is an ermine, whatever the label on the specimen drawer says.",
  "american-marten": "About the size of a house cat but built long and low, with a sharp face and a patch of pale orange across the throat. The coat is a rich brown. Anyone who knows a fisher will notice the marten is clearly smaller and finer. This is an animal of older coniferous and mixed boreal forest, and it does not do well where those forests are cut hard. It hunts in the trees as readily as on the ground, and it dens in cavities. Voles and red squirrels make up much of the diet.\n\nLook for it in central and northern Ontario through the colder months, when tracks in fresh snow often give it away before you see the animal itself. It stays active in winter rather than sleeping the season out. Marten need standing dead trees and downed wood for denning and cover, so the best places to find one are stands that have been left to grow old.",
  "american-mink": "Along a marsh edge or a river bank at dusk you may catch a low dark shape working the shoreline. Glossy and dark brown, slender, with a small white patch on the chin, the mink is at home in the water without being anything like the size of an otter. It swims and dives well and hunts a wide menu: fish, frogs, crayfish, and muskrats among them. Most of its activity comes after dark.\n\nYou can find mink almost anywhere in the province as long as there is water with cover along the banks. Summer and fall are good times to look. Tracks near the waterline and the smell of a den can mark a stretch of shore that a mink is using. It is closely tied to shorelines and rarely strays far from them, so a quiet approach along a bank in low light gives the best chance of a look.",
  "fisher": "Bigger than a marten and darker, with a long bushy tail and a low-slung walk, the fisher is an agile climber that lives in mature mixed and coniferous forest with plenty of cover. Males run noticeably larger than females. Despite the name it does not fish. What sets it apart from other predators is its habit of killing porcupines, which it does by working at the face, the one part not protected by quills.\n\nIt is widespread through forested parts of Ontario and most active around dawn and dusk. Fall and winter, with snow to hold tracks, are the easiest times to know one has passed through. Fishers cover a lot of ground and are shy of people, so a direct sighting is largely a matter of luck. Where porcupines are common the fisher usually is too, and it helps keep their numbers in check.",
  "river-otter": "Few animals give away their ease in a place the way an otter does. Large and sleek, with dense brown fur, webbed feet, and a thick tail that tapers to a point, otters are often seen in family groups and leave slides worn into muddy or snowy banks. They are social, they mark shared latrine sites along the shore, and they can stay under for several minutes while chasing fish.\n\nGood populations need clean water with healthy fish stocks, so an otter on a river or lake is a fair sign the water is in decent shape. They range widely across the province wherever those conditions hold. Winter and spring are good seasons to look, and open water below rapids or at the edge of ice is a reliable spot. Activity is not fixed to one part of the day. Tracks, slides, and the droppings left at a latrine often tell you otters are around before you see them.",
  "american-badger": "This is Ontario's rarest carnivore, and most people who live here will never see one. Built flat and low to the ground, with a grizzled grey coat and a white stripe that runs from the nose back over the head, the badger is made for digging, and its claws are the tool. It works open country: sandy remnants of tallgrass prairie and farmland in the far southwestern corner of the province.\n\nThe population is in real trouble. The jacksoni subspecies found here is listed as Endangered, with likely fewer than 200 animals left. They are nocturnal and secretive, so the sign you are most likely to find is a burrow rather than the animal. If you come across one, leave it be and report it. Loss of open habitat and road deaths are the main pressures on a population this small, and every animal counts. Summer and fall are when they are most active above ground.",
  "bobcat": "A medium-sized cat, tawny and spotted, with a short tail that looks cut off. The tail is barred with black and white underneath, and the ears carry only modest tufts. Next to a lynx the bobcat has smaller feet and a less long-legged build, which matters where the two might overlap. It uses mixed forest, rocky ridges, and swampy thickets, and hunts mostly around dawn and dusk.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one in southern and central Ontario. Fall and winter are decent times to look, though bobcats are quiet and easy to miss. The species is more a southern animal than the lynx, and it has been slowly expanding its range in the province as winters grow milder. That trend is worth watching. Deep snow favours the lynx and its huge feet, so where snow stays shallow the bobcat holds the advantage.",
  "canada-lynx": "Everything about a lynx says deep snow. The paws are broad and furred like snowshoes, the legs are long, the ears carry long black tufts, and a flared ruff frames the face. The tail ends in a solid black tip, all the way round, which separates it from the black-and-white barred tail of a bobcat. This is a boreal cat, and its life is bound up with one prey animal above all, the snowshoe hare.\n\nThat link shows in the numbers. Lynx populations rise and fall on a cycle of about ten years, closely following the boom and crash of the hares they depend on. Look for them in the northern boreal forest, and winter is the season that suits them. They are nocturnal and thinly spread, so tracks in snow are the usual evidence. Where hares are scarce, lynx are scarce too, and they may wander far in a poor year.",
  "cougar": "Few wildlife questions in Ontario are argued over as much as the status of the cougar. The animal itself is unmistakable when clearly seen: a large cat of plain tawny colour, with a very long tail ending in a dark tip. That tail is the feature that rules out lynx and bobcat. The species was long presumed extirpated from eastern Canada. The occasional confirmed report may be a wild animal wandering in from the west, or a released captive, and telling one from the other is rarely easy.\n\nIt is listed as Special concern. Records are sparse and fall mostly in northern and central Ontario, many of them in fall and winter. If you are ever close to one, treat it with respect. Do not run. Make yourself look large, and back away slowly. Attacks on people are very rare, but a powerful predator deserves that caution.",
  "grey-fox": "Look up. The grey fox is the one North American canid that regularly climbs trees, and it will rest or even den on a branch well off the ground. The coat is a grizzled grey with rusty colour along the sides and neck, and the tail carries a black tip rather than the white one of a red fox. It favours deciduous and mixed woodland with dense cover, and here that means the far south.\n\nIn Ontario the grey fox reaches the northern edge of its range and is listed as Threatened, found only in the warm Carolinian corner. It is nocturnal and uncommon, so a sighting is unusual and worth reporting. Summer and fall are the seasons you are most likely to be out where one lives. Its foothold in the province is small and depends on the survival of the southern woodlands it needs, which are themselves under pressure.",
  "grey-wolf": "Bigger and longer in the leg than a coyote, with a broader muzzle and a deep, low howl, the wolf is an animal of wild forested country where deer, moose, and beaver are found. Ontario has two kinds. The grey wolf of the north is not at risk. The smaller eastern, or Algonquin, wolf, centred on Algonquin Park in central Ontario, is a distinct animal and is separately protected as Threatened.\n\nYou are most likely to hear a wolf before you see one, and fall and winter are the best times to listen. They range across central and northern Ontario and are mostly active at night. Wild wolves very rarely threaten people. That said, they should never be fed or approached, and feeding is exactly what turns a wary animal into a problem one. A wolf that loses its fear of people is a wolf headed for trouble, usually its own.",
  "wolverine": "Built more like a small bear than a weasel, though a weasel is what it is, and the largest of them on land. The coat is dark brown with pale stripes down each side, the head is broad, and the body is stocky and powerful. That power is real. A wolverine can drive wolves off a kill and will travel enormous distances across its range. It lives in the remote boreal forest and tundra of the far north.\n\nIn Ontario it is listed as Threatened and holds on in the northern boreal and the Hudson Bay lowlands. It is intensely wary of people and needs large, undisturbed country, which is exactly what makes it scarce and hard to see. Winter is when it is most often recorded, usually as tracks crossing open ground. Few people will ever meet one in the field. Its presence is a sign of wild land with little human traffic, and that kind of land is not getting more common.",
  "american-beaver": "The largest rodent in North America, and one built for water. A beaver is rich brown, thickset, and best known for the flat, scaly tail it slaps against the surface as an alarm before diving. That tail, along with webbed hind feet and a heavy body, marks it out from the muskrat, which has a thin, ropelike tail.\n\nYou meet beavers on streams, ponds, and lakes edged with shrubs and trees, most often at dawn or dusk from spring through fall. They cut wood to build dams and lodges, and those dams do more than shelter one family. By flooding low ground they create wetlands that hold water, slow spring runoff, and support a wide range of other plants and animals across a whole watershed. The beaver is Canada's national emblem, and the work it does on the land is a large part of why. Widespread across Ontario.",
  "red-squirrel": "Loud out of all proportion to its size. The red squirrel is a small, rusty-coloured tree squirrel with a white ring around the eye, and in summer a dark stripe along each side. If a squirrel is scolding you from a spruce with a long, rattling chatter, this is usually the one.\n\nIt holds a territory year-round and defends it hard, mostly against other red squirrels. Conifer seed is the main food. A red squirrel cuts green cones and stores them in large piles called middens, often at the base of a favourite tree, and works through the cache over winter. Look for it in coniferous and mixed forest, active by day, spring through fall, and widespread wherever those forests grow. The scattered cone scales and stripped cores left on a stump or log are a reliable sign that one is nearby even when the animal stays out of sight.",
  "eastern-chipmunk": "A striped ground squirrel of the forest floor, small enough to sit in a cupped hand. Five dark stripes run down the back and a single dark line crosses the eye. When the cheek pouches are packed with seed they bulge out past the face, which is often the first thing you notice.\n\nChipmunks favour deciduous woods, brushy edges, gardens, and old stone walls, where gaps give quick cover and burrow entrances. They are active by day from spring into fall, carrying seeds and nuts down to underground larders. Through the cold months a chipmunk enters torpor rather than a deep, unbroken hibernation, waking now and then to feed from the stores it laid up. Common across southern and central Ontario. A sharp, repeated chip from ground level, or a striped shape darting along a wall, is usually this animal.",
  "eastern-cottontail": "The common wild rabbit of southern Ontario. Grey-brown overall with a rusty patch on the nape and the small white underside of the tail that gives it its name. Next to a hare it looks compact, with shorter ears and feet, and unlike the snowshoe hare it stays brown right through winter.\n\nLook for cottontails along brushy fields, hedgerows, gardens, and suburban parks, feeding at dawn and dusk and lying low in dense cover by day. Unlike hares, whose young are furred, open-eyed, and able to move almost at once, cottontail kits are born naked, blind, and helpless in a shallow nest scraped into the ground and lined with grass and fur. The nest is easy to disturb by accident while mowing, and a mother visits only briefly to nurse, so a nest that looks abandoned usually is not. Found mainly in the settled south, where mild edges and gardens suit it well.",
  "eastern-grey-squirrel": "Bigger and heavier than the red squirrel, and the squirrel most people picture in a city park. Colour is misleading: it may be plain grey with a pale belly, or an all-black melanistic form, both common in the same population. The tail is broad and bushy and often held over the back.\n\nOntario is a stronghold for the black morph, which can outnumber grey animals in some towns. Grey squirrels live in mature deciduous forest and do just as well among big trees in parks and neighbourhoods. Active by day from spring through fall, a grey squirrel buries nuts and acorns one at a time across its range and relies on memory and scent to find them again. Many are never recovered, and those forgotten seeds grow into trees, so the animal quietly helps plant the forest it lives in. Widespread, and most common in the south.",
  "groundhog": "A marmot that lives at ground level rather than in the mountains. The groundhog, or woodchuck, is a chunky brown ground squirrel with short legs, a short bushy tail, and the habit of sitting bolt upright at its burrow mouth to watch for danger. Fields, meadows, roadsides, and garden edges all suit it, wherever the digging is good.\n\nIt is one of the region's true hibernators. Through winter a groundhog's body temperature falls to near freezing and its heart rate drops to a few beats a minute, which is why it vanishes entirely from late fall until spring. That deep sleep is behind Groundhog Day and Ontario's own Wiarton Willie, though a real groundhog emerging in February is reading the season, not forecasting it. Active by day in spring and summer, and most common in the settled south. Its burrow systems can be extensive, which endears it to few gardeners and farmers.",
  "muskrat": "A wetland rodent often mistaken for a small beaver, until you see the tail. The muskrat's tail is thin, flattened from side to side, and nearly bare, held out behind as it swims rather than trailing flat like a paddle. The body is brown and about the size of a large squirrel or small cat.\n\nMarshes, ponds, and slow rivers thick with cattails are the places to look, at dawn and dusk. Rather than dam a stream, a muskrat builds a domed lodge of cattail and mud, and cuts smaller feeding shelters called push-ups over holes in the ice. It swims well in both directions, forward and back, and spends much of its life in the water. Muskrats are important prey for mink and otter, and their feeding and lodge-building open up patches of marsh that other wetland species then use. Widespread in wetlands across Ontario.",
  "porcupine": "Slow, rounded, and covered in quills, the porcupine is hard to confuse with anything else. An adult may carry up to about thirty thousand quills over its back and tail, hidden among coarser guard hairs. It is a capable climber and is often found high in a tree, gnawing bark and buds, active mainly at night through fall and winter.\n\nThe quills are modified hairs tipped with tiny backward-facing barbs. A porcupine cannot throw them, despite the old story. What happens is simpler and worse: on contact the loosely rooted quills pull free and lodge in whatever touched them, and the barbs make them work inward and hard to pull out. A curious dog that gets a face full is a common and painful result, and quills near the eyes or mouth usually mean a trip to the vet. Give a porcupine room, keep dogs leashed near one, and it will amble off on its own. Widespread in mixed and coniferous forest.",
  "northern-flying-squirrel": "A nocturnal glider of northern forests, rarely seen unless you go looking after dark. Grey-brown above with a loose fold of furred skin between wrist and ankle, it stretches that membrane to glide from a high trunk down to a lower one, steering with its flattened tail. It does not truly fly.\n\nTwo flying squirrels live in Ontario and they are easy to confuse. This is the larger of the two, and its belly fur is grey at the base; on the southern flying squirrel the belly hairs are white all the way down. The northern species ranges farther into the boreal, in coniferous and mixed forest. Lichens and underground fungi like truffles make up much of the diet, and by eating those fungi and passing the spores the squirrel helps spread them through the forest, where many trees depend on them at the root. Active summer through winter across central and northern Ontario.",
  "snowshoe-hare": "Named for the oversized hind feet that carry it over deep snow. In summer the snowshoe hare is brown; as the days shorten it moults to white, keeping only black tips on the ears, which makes it hard to pick out against snow. The large feet separate it at a glance from the smaller cottontail.\n\nIt lives in boreal and mixed forest with thick understory and young conifers for cover, feeding in the open at dawn and dusk and slipping back into the tangle by day. Its numbers rise and crash on a cycle of roughly ten years, and that cycle drives one of the best-known predator patterns in the north: Canada lynx, which depend heavily on hares, boom and bust a step behind them. Most common in central and northern Ontario. In a mild, snowless winter a white hare against brown ground stands out badly to predators, a growing problem as winters warm.",
  "star-nosed-mole": "There is no mistaking this one. Around the tip of its snout the star-nosed mole carries a ring of twenty-two fleshy pink tentacles, splayed out like a small starburst, above broad forefeet built for digging. The body is blackish and thickly furred, roughly twelve centimetres long before the tail.\n\nIt lives in the wettest ground: marshes, swamps, wet meadows, and streambanks where the soil stays saturated. The star is not for smelling. It is a touch organ, the most sensitive known in any mammal, and the mole sweeps it over the ground and through the water to find prey, identifying and swallowing a small item in a fraction of a second. This mole swims and forages underwater as readily as it tunnels, and it stays active year-round. Found in wetlands across southern, central, and northeastern Ontario. It is secretive and mostly underground, so a sighting, often of a dead one, is a small piece of luck.",
  "virginia-opossum": "Canada's only marsupial, and a fairly recent arrival at that. About the size of a house cat, it has grizzled grey fur, a narrow white face, a pointed pink nose, and a long naked tail that can grip like a fifth limb. Bare ears and that ratlike tail give it an odd look no other Ontario mammal shares.\n\nWoodlands, farmland, and the edges of towns in the south all suit it, and it forages at night for more or less anything: fruit, insects, carrion, scraps. Cornered and unable to flee, an opossum may 'play dead', an involuntary collapse into a stiff, open-mouthed state that can last minutes and often puts a predator off. It handles cold poorly, and its bare ears and tail are prone to frostbite, which long kept it out of Ontario. Milder winters have let its range creep steadily north, and it is now established across the southern part of the province and still spreading.",
  "southern-flying-squirrel": "Most people who share a woodlot with this squirrel never know it is there. It comes out only after dark, and it is small enough to sit in a cupped hand. The fur is silky grey-brown, the eyes are large and dark for gathering what little light there is, and the belly hair is white right to the base. A loose fold of skin runs from wrist to ankle. When the animal launches from a high trunk, that membrane opens into a glider and the flattened tail works as a rudder. It does not flap. It steers a long controlled descent, then climbs and does it again.\n\nIn Ontario the range is limited to the Carolinian forest of the far south, where mature oak and hickory supply the nuts and cavities it depends on. Summer and fall are when you are most likely to find sign of it. It is listed as a species of special concern, which tracks the steady loss of old deciduous woods. Leaving dead and hollow trees standing is one of the more useful things you can do for it.",
  "big-brown-bat": "This is the bat you are most likely to see working a streetlight on a cool evening. It is noticeably bigger than a little brown bat, with a broad muzzle and a slow, steady flight that is easy to follow against a pale sky. On chilly nights it is often the last one still hunting after the others have quit.\n\nIt roosts in buildings, barns, and hollow trees, and it hunts over yards, gardens, and lit streets where insects gather. Unlike most Ontario bats, it holds up reasonably well against white-nose syndrome, and it will sometimes hibernate inside a heated building rather than a cave. That tolerance is part of why it stays common while several relatives have collapsed. It is not considered at risk. One rule stands regardless of the species. Never pick up a bat, even one that seems grounded or dead. A bite or scratch carries a rabies risk, and a bat found on the ground is more likely to be sick. If one gets into the house, confine it to a single room and call for advice.",
  "hoary-bat": "Ontario's largest bat carries a coat that looks brushed with frost. The grey fur is tipped white, which gives the hoary name, and there is a yellowish wash at the throat with a tail membrane furred right across. You are unlikely to find it in a barn or attic. It roosts alone in the foliage of trees, tucked among leaves where it passes for a dead cluster by day.\n\nRather than hibernating in a cave, it migrates long distances, and that habit is now the problem. In 2024 it was added to Ontario's Species at Risk List as endangered, mostly because so many are struck and killed at wind turbines during migration. You meet it here in summer and fall, foraging over forest and open ground after dark. Its range across the province is wide, but the numbers behind that range are thin. Handle no bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk and there is no good reason to touch one. If you find a grounded bat, leave it where it is and report it.",
  "little-brown-bat": "Within living memory this was the common bat of Ontario, the one over every dock and pond at dusk. It is small, with glossy brown fur and a plain face that carries no flaps or folds. A useful field mark is the long hairs on the toes that reach past the claws, though the small Myotis bats look much alike and identification takes care.\n\nThen came white-nose syndrome, an introduced fungal disease that rouses hibernating bats and burns through their winter fat. Populations have fallen by more than ninety per cent, and the species is now listed as endangered. Summer is when you are most likely to see one, hunting over water and forest edge before returning to a roost in a building, tree, or cave. A bite or scratch from any bat is a rabies risk, so do not handle one under any circumstances. If bats are living in your attic, wait until the young can fly, then exclude them and seal the gaps rather than killing them.",
  "silver-haired-bat": "Dark fur frosted with silver-white tips gives this bat both its name and a good field mark, best seen as it passes early through the last light of evening. The flight is slow and erratic, and it tends to appear over woodland ponds and clearings before most bats are out. It is a tree bat, roosting under loose bark and in cavities rather than in buildings.\n\nLike the hoary bat, it migrates long distances instead of hibernating in caves, and it was added to Ontario's Species at Risk List as endangered in 2024. The main cause is death at wind turbines during migration. Look for it in summer and fall across much of the province, though wide range does not mean large numbers. Bats should never be picked up. A bite or scratch can transmit rabies, and an animal you can catch by hand is usually one that is sick or hurt. Report grounded bats rather than moving them yourself.",
  "northern-long-eared-bat": "The long ears are the tell. On a small brown Myotis they stand out, and the tragus, the pointed flap inside the ear, is likewise long and narrow. This bat keeps to the interior of the forest rather than open edges, and it hunts in an unusual way, gleaning insects straight off leaves and bark instead of taking them in open air. It roosts under bark and in cavities through the summer and hibernates in caves and mines.\n\nThat reliance on cave hibernation left it badly exposed. White-nose syndrome has hit this species among the hardest of any Ontario bat, with population losses that can only be called catastrophic. It is listed as endangered, and it persists in small numbers where forests still hold the standing dead trees it needs. Do not handle it, or any bat. A bite or scratch is a rabies risk. If you manage forest land, leaving mature interior woods and dead standing trees intact is the most useful thing you can do for it.",
  "tri-colored-bat": "Ontario's smallest bat is a scrap of an animal with a weak, fluttering flight that has been likened to a large moth. Up close the back hairs show three bands, dark at the base, yellow-brown in the middle, and dark again at the tip, and the forearms are a distinctive pinkish red. In summer it works forest edges and waterways. In winter it hibernates in caves and abandoned mines.\n\nThose hibernation sites are where the trouble reached it. White-nose syndrome collapsed its colonies, and it is now one of the province's rarest bats, listed as endangered. Its Ontario range covers the south and central parts of the province, but encounters are few even where it still occurs. The safety point is the same as for every bat. Never touch one. A bite or scratch can carry rabies, and a bat on the ground is more likely to be sick than simply resting. Keep pets away from it and call a local authority for advice.",
  "broad-winged-hawk": "A compact forest buteo, smaller than the more familiar Red-tailed Hawk. Broad black-and-white bands cross the tail and the wingtips come to a point. A dark border frames the white underwings. It nests inside deciduous and mixed forest across central and southern Ontario, where it is easy to overlook through the summer. The call is a thin, high whistle that carries between the trees.\n\nSeptember changes that. Migrating birds gather into swirling flocks called kettles, riding thermals in slow spirals before streaming south. On a good day thousands pass over a southern Ontario hawk watch. Cool mornings after a cold front, with the wind out of the north, tend to move the most birds. Watch for a high circling flock that suddenly peels off into a long line. They winter in Central and South America, so by late fall they have gone.",
  "coopers-hawk": "Built for chasing birds through cover, this medium-sized accipiter has a long rounded tail tipped with a wide white band and a head that looks large for its body. Adults are barred rusty below with a blue-grey back; young birds are brown and streaked. Its size and big-headed look separate it from the smaller Sharp-shinned Hawk, though the two remain a genuine identification problem.\n\nOnce a bird of woodlots and forest edges, it has settled into southern Ontario cities and leafy suburbs. Feeders draw it in. A Cooper's Hawk will perch nearby, then make a fast low pass at the doves and pigeons crowded around the seed. This unsettles some feeder watchers, but it is ordinary hawk behaviour. It is present year-round in the south. In flight, look for stiff quick wingbeats between short glides, with that long tail trailing behind.",
  "sharp-shinned-hawk": "Ontario's smallest hawk is only jay-sized, a slight accipiter with a small head, thin legs, and a square-tipped tail. It reads like a scaled-down Cooper's Hawk, and separating the two takes practice. Breeding birds keep to coniferous and mixed forest, where they stay secretive and are easy to miss. On migration they turn up in woodlots and backyards, hunting small birds.\n\nAutumn is when the species becomes obvious. Large numbers follow the Great Lakes shorelines, reluctant to cross open water, so they concentrate along the shore and stream past. Thousands are counted each fall at Holiday Beach and Hawk Cliff. A north or northwest wind behind a cold front tends to bring the biggest flights. The flight itself is a quick flap-flap-glide on short rounded wings. Some birds linger through winter in the south, often near feeders where small birds gather.",
  "american-goshawk": "The largest and most powerful of Ontario's accipiters, a bird of big unbroken forest. Adults are blue-grey above and pale grey below, with a bold white eyebrow set over a red eye. It needs large tracts of mature boreal and mixed forest, which keeps it mostly in the central and northern parts of the province. It is uncommon, and often hard to find even where it lives.\n\nCaution matters near the nest. A goshawk defends its young fiercely and will dive at people who wander too close in spring and summer. If one starts calling loudly and swooping, you are near a nest and should leave rather than press on. This is not something to seek out. Away from the nest it is elusive, hunting grouse and hares in fast flights through the timber. Some move south in winter, more so in years when prey is scarce in the north.",
  "eastern-screech-owl": "Small enough to sit in the hand, this robin-sized owl carries short ear tufts and comes in two colour forms, one rusty-red and one grey. Both can occur in the same area. It lives across southern Ontario in woodlots, ravines, orchards, and older neighbourhoods with big trees, wherever cavities exist to roost and nest in. Nest boxes suit it well.\n\nThe name misleads. Its voice is not a screech but a soft descending whinny and a steady even trill, given after dark from inside a tree cavity. Learning those two sounds is the surest way to know it is present, since the bird itself is easy to miss by day. When it does roost in the open, it often sits at a cavity entrance with its eyes closed, blending into the bark. It stays year-round and does not migrate. A hole in an old maple is a good place to check.",
  "merlin": "Stockier than a kestrel, the Merlin is a small dark falcon that hunts hard and fast. The wings are pointed and the wingbeats quick and powerful. The tail is boldly banded. It lacks the strong facial stripes of the American Kestrel, and the whole impression is of a compact bird driving straight through the air after smaller birds.\n\nOnce mainly a bird of the north, it has moved into Ontario towns and cities over recent decades. It often takes over an old crow nest in a spruce, and a noisy pair around a churchyard or a residential street has become a familiar sight in many communities. Listen for a rapid loud chatter, which usually means a bird is perched close or defending a nest. Some stay through winter in the south. Look on high bare perches, spruce tops, and utility poles, anywhere a bird can sit and watch open ground.",
  "northern-harrier": "Slim and long-winged, this hawk hunts low and slow over open ground, tilting from side to side with its wings held in a shallow V. A white patch at the base of the tail is the clearest mark and shows at a distance. Males are pale grey, females and young birds brown. It works marshes, wet meadows, grasslands, and farm fields across the province.\n\nUnusual among hawks, it has an owl-like facial disc that funnels sound, so it finds voles by ear as much as by eye. That lets it hunt effectively over tall grass where prey stays out of sight. It nests on the ground, which leaves it exposed where fields are mowed early or drained. Watch open country in any season, though birds are most widespread in migration and summer. A low coursing flight with that flash of white rump is usually enough to name it, even in poor light.",
  "northern-saw-whet-owl": "Tiny and tuftless, with a big round head, yellow eyes, and a streaked pale facial disc, this is one of the smallest owls in the province. It roosts by day in dense conifers and thickets, tucked against a trunk, and birds found this way are often very tame. That tameness is not a reason to crowd or handle one. A roosting owl needs its rest.\n\nIn spring the male sings a long series of evenly spaced toots, a monotonous sound that carries well on still nights. The bird is far more common than it seems. Banding stations show that large numbers move through Ontario each fall, passing unseen in the dark. It lives in wooded areas across the province and stays through the year in much of its range. Finding one usually means checking dense conifers with care, or listening after dark in early spring. Whitewash and pellets under a tree can point the way.",
  "red-shouldered-hawk": "A forest buteo of mature deciduous woods and swampy bottomland. Rusty shoulders and reddish barred underparts mark the adult, and in flight the tail shows narrow white bands while the wings show pale crescent windows near the tips. It is vocal, with a loud repeated scream that Blue Jays like to copy. It favours wet woodland with big trees and standing water, which limits where it turns up.\n\nThere is a recovery story here. The bird was once on Ontario's at-risk species list, but roadside surveys showed the population had rebounded, and it was removed from that list in 2007. Today it is found across central and eastern Ontario, mostly in spring, summer, and fall before it withdraws south for winter. Keeping large blocks of mature swamp forest intact is what holds it in place. Listen for the ringing call in April, when pairs are noisy over the nesting woods.",
  "rough-legged-hawk": "Named for its feathered legs, an adaptation to Arctic cold, this large buteo is a bird of the Ontario winter. It hunts over open fields, marshes, and shorelines that recall the tundra where it breeds, and it often hovers on long wings, a habit few large hawks share. Look for a dark belly, dark patches at the wrist of pale underwings, and a white tail base ending in a dark band.\n\nThis is a bird you meet in the colder months. It nests on the Arctic tundra and moves south to spend the winter across Ontario's open country, hunting voles in the same fields that harriers and Snowy Owls use. Numbers vary from year to year, rising when the northern vole supply crashes and pushes more birds south. Scan fence posts, hay bales, and lone trees at the edges of large fields. A hovering hawk over winter farmland is very often this one.",
  "short-eared-owl": "An owl of open ground, buff and brown, with a pale facial disc, dark eye patches, and ear tufts so short they seldom show. It flies low over fields on long wings with a floppy, moth-like beat unlike any hawk. It hunts voles over marshes, grasslands, and farm fields, mostly at dawn and dusk, which makes it one of the few owls a person has a real chance of seeing by daylight.\n\nIt is a species of special concern in Ontario. The open grasslands and large wetlands it needs have been drained, developed, or turned to intensive farming, and the bird has declined with them. Nesting on the ground leaves it exposed to early mowing and disturbance. Numbers are local and shift between years and sites. Look in late fall and winter over big open tracts and along Great Lakes shorelines, watching at last light as birds begin to course over the fields.",
  "golden-eagle": "Dark overall, with a golden wash across the head and neck, this is a large eagle of wild country. Young birds show white patches in the wing and a white base to the tail. Fully feathered legs, right down to the toes, separate it from the Bald Eagle, which has bare lower legs. It breeds on remote cliffs and in forest in the far north, and hunts over open ridges and shorelines when it is on the move.\n\nIn Ontario it is listed as Endangered. Fewer than about 250 breeding adults hold on in the far north, and most sightings farther south are migrants passing through in spring and fall, with a few lingering into winter. Ridges and Great Lakes shorelines concentrate these travellers. An injured raptor of this size is dangerous to approach. The talons and beak are powerful and can do serious harm, so a grounded or injured bird should be left alone and reported to a licensed wildlife rehabilitator rather than handled.",
  "great-gray-owl": "Ontario's tallest owl looks bigger than it truly is. It is dusky grey overall, with a huge round facial disc, small yellow eyes set close together, and a white mark below the chin like a bow tie. Under all that fluff it weighs less than a Great Horned Owl. It lives in boreal forest with nearby bogs and clearings, and it hunts by sound, plunging through deep snow after voles it never sees.\n\nMost years it stays north. Some winters, when the vole supply fails, numbers push south and birds turn up along field edges and roadsides where southern watchers rarely see them otherwise. That is when it draws crowds. An owl that has to feed itself through a hard winter does not need people pressing in or tossing bait to stage a photo. Keep your distance and let it work. Watched from the road, it will often go on hunting as if you were not there.",
  "long-eared-owl": "Slim and upright, this owl presses itself thin against a trunk and all but vanishes. Look for long, close-set ear tufts, an orange facial disc, and rusty streaking that runs vertically down the breast. By day it roosts in dense conifers and shelterbelts set beside open ground where it hunts after dark.\n\nSecretive is an understatement. Even where it is present it goes unnoticed for weeks, and finding one usually means checking thick evergreens and watching for whitewash and pellets on the ground below. In winter several may share a single dense conifer, a communal roost that can hold a handful of birds in one tree. It is widespread across Ontario but local and genuinely hard to find, more often turned up by a patient searcher than met by chance. A soft, low hoot given in spring is one of the better clues to a nesting pair.",
  "american-black-duck": "At a glance this is a dark duck the shape of a Mallard, and that is the right instinct. The body is sooty brown, the head and neck paler grey by contrast, and the wing carries a violet-blue patch with no white borders. In flight the silver-white underwings flash bright against the dark body. It uses marshes, beaver ponds, wooded swamps, rivers and sheltered bays, and stays through all four seasons where water remains open.\n\nThe complication is the Mallard. The two hybridize freely, and as Mallards have spread the Black Duck has lost ground and declined across the province. Many birds now carry mixed traits, so a clean Black Duck, with no green sheen and no curled tail feathers, is worth a careful second look. It remains most common in the east and north, and the pale head against the dark body is the mark to trust.",
  "american-coot": "It swims like a duck and floats among them, but the coot is a rail. The body is dark slate grey, the bill white and blunt like a chicken's, and the feet end in lobed toes rather than webs. Those lobes do double duty, paddling in open water and spreading the bird's weight so it can walk over soft mud. Watch one cross a pond and it pumps its head back and forth with each stroke.\n\nIt gathers on marshes, ponds and open lakes with vegetation along the edge, sometimes in large loose rafts on migration. In Ontario it is a spring-through-fall bird of marshy water, widespread and easy to find once you learn to pick the grey body and white bill out of a mixed flock. It dives for plants and also grazes at the surface and along the shore.",
  "american-wigeon": "A grazer as much as a swimmer, the wigeon often feeds on lawns, fields and grassy shorelines well away from deep water. Males carry a white crown, source of the old name baldpate, and a broad green stripe running back from the eye. Both sexes flash a large white patch on the forewing in flight, the best mark at a distance.\n\nIt turns up on marshes, lakes and shorelines, mostly as a migrant through Ontario with some birds staying to breed. One habit is worth watching for. It hangs at the surface near diving ducks and coots and snatches the plants they haul up from below, letting others do the work of diving. Spring and fall bring the largest numbers, usually mixed into flocks of other dabbling ducks, so scan a raft of grazing birds for that clean white cap.",
  "blue-winged-teal": "Small and quick off the water, this teal is easy to miss until it flushes and shows a powder-blue patch on the forewing. Breeding males add a bold white crescent in front of the eye. It favours grassy marshes, wet meadows and shallow ponds, and it is found across the south and north through the warmer months.\n\nIt does not linger. Among the first ducks to leave, most are gone from Ontario by early autumn, and some travel a very long way, wintering as far south as South America. Because it arrives late in spring and departs early, its window in the province is shorter than that of most dabblers. Pairs and small groups feed by tipping in the shallows for seeds and aquatic invertebrates rather than diving. A late-summer marsh is a good place to catch one before it goes.",
  "bufflehead": "One of Ontario's smallest ducks, the Bufflehead is a plump, fast little diver that rides open water in tight groups. Males are crisp black and white, with a large white wedge wrapping around the back of a dark, puffy head that can glint green or purple in good light. It works lakes, rivers, sheltered bays and open water, mostly as a migrant and winter visitor.\n\nIts size shapes how it breeds. It nests almost entirely in old Northern Flicker holes, a cavity just big enough for a bird this small and too tight for larger ducks. That tie to flickers links its fortunes to a woodpecker's. Look for it on cold open water in spring, fall and winter, diving with a quick roll forward and popping back up a short time later. Small flocks often keep to themselves rather than mixing with other ducks.",
  "common-goldeneye": "A bright gold eye gives this diving duck its name, and on the male a round white spot sits just in front of it against a dark, green-glossed head. The rest of the drake is mostly white. Females are greyer with a brown head. It winters on open water in southern Ontario and breeds to the north, nesting in tree cavities in the forest.\n\nThe wings beat fast enough to whistle in flight, which is why hunters have long called it the whistler. On a still winter day you can sometimes hear a flock before you see it. It favours rivers, lakes and other open water, and it dives for aquatic invertebrates and small fish. Cold weather concentrates birds where current or depth keeps the water from freezing, and those open stretches are the places to look through the coldest months.",
  "common-merganser": "Long and low in the water, this is a fish-eating duck with a thin red bill hooked at the tip. Drakes are clean white with a dark green head. Females are grey with a rusty head and a sharp white chin, often the easier bird to name. It wants clear water and holds to lakes, large rivers and reservoirs across much of Ontario year-round.\n\nFamily life is worth watching. Ducklings often ride on the female's back in their first days, and broods from several hens sometimes merge into a large creche trailing one or two adults. It dives and chases fish underwater, and the serrations along its bill help it grip slippery prey. Where rivers stay open through winter, mergansers gather in the fast, cold water, sometimes in strung-out groups working a single stretch.",
  "double-crested-cormorant": "All dark, long-necked, with a hooked bill and a patch of orange skin on the throat, the cormorant sits low and swims lower, often with just the head and neck showing above water. It nests in colonies on islands and in dead standing trees, and it is abundant on the Great Lakes and large inland lakes from spring through fall.\n\nThe wing-drying pose is the giveaway. Its feathers are not fully waterproof, so after a dive it perches with wings spread to dry, a posture no duck adopts. That partial wetting is thought to reduce buoyancy and help it work underwater after fish. Colonies can be large and noisy, and over time the birds' droppings kill the trees they nest in. In flight it travels in lines and loose Vs, neck slightly kinked, an easy shape to learn once you have seen it a few times.",
  "gadwall": "Plain is the honest word for the male Gadwall, grey overall with a black rear end and a dark bill, a duck that rewards a second look more than a first. In flight both sexes show a small white patch on the inner wing, usually the quickest way to pick one out of a flock. It keeps to marshes, reservoirs and quiet ponds with plenty of vegetation, dabbling for plants in the shallows.\n\nThe trend here runs opposite to many ducks. Its numbers have grown steadily, and it now breeds across southern Ontario where it was once scarce. Spring through fall is the season to find it, generally in ones and twos among larger flocks of more colourful dabblers. Up close the fine grey vermiculation on the flanks is more detailed than the drab first impression suggests, and the black stern stands out against the pale body.",
  "green-heron": "Small, stocky and dark, this heron crouches at the water's edge and can look like a lump on a log until it moves. The neck is chestnut, the back green-black, the legs short and yellow-orange, and the whole bird is far smaller than the herons most people picture. It hunts wooded ponds, streams, marshes and shrubby shorelines, standing still or creeping low before it strikes.\n\nIt ranks among the few birds known to use tools. It will drop bait onto the surface, an insect or a small twig, and take the fish that come up to investigate. In Ontario it is a warm-season bird of southern and central wetlands, present from spring through fall and gone south for the winter. When flushed it flies off with a sharp call and often raises a shaggy crest, so a startled squawk from a pond edge is worth a look.",
  "green-winged-teal": "The smallest dabbling duck in North America, about a third the weight of a Mallard. That size shows in the field. Flocks are compact and quick, twisting low over shallow water in tight bunches. A drake at close range has a chestnut head marked with a green patch behind the eye, and a vertical white bar running down the side of the grey body. Both sexes flash a green wing patch in flight.\n\nLook for teal on shallow marshes, mudflats and flooded fields, where they tip forward to feed on seeds and small invertebrates in shallow water. In Ontario they pass through in numbers during spring and fall migration, and some breed in the north. They arrive early in spring and linger late into fall, often among the last dabblers on the water before freeze-up. A hunted species across much of its range, so numbers on a given marsh can shift with the season and the weather.",
  "herring-gull": "A big, pink-legged gull of the Great Lakes, large inland lakes, rivers and landfills. Adults show a pale grey back, a white head and body, and a yellow bill with a red spot near the tip. Getting there takes time. A Herring Gull needs four years to reach full adult plumage, passing through a run of mottled brown immature stages that trip up many observers.\n\nThe red spot has a purpose. Chicks peck at it, and the pecking prompts the adult to bring up food for them. You can find this gull in every season across the province, on open water and shorelines, and around anywhere people leave food. It eats almost anything, from fish and shellfish to scraps and the eggs of other birds. Common and adaptable, it is one of the default large gulls to learn before sorting through the rarer species that turn up in winter flocks.",
  "hooded-merganser": "Small and thin-billed, this is the diving duck of quiet wooded water. Beaver ponds, swamps, shaded ponds and slow rivers all suit it. A drake can raise a fan-shaped white crest bordered in black, which he flares in display and folds flat when at ease. Females are greyer, with a loose cinnamon crest of their own.\n\nUnlike the other mergansers, this one breeds only in North America. It nests in tree cavities and readily takes to nest boxes, and the ducklings leave the hole within a day of hatching, dropping to the water below. The thin, serrated bill is built for gripping fish, though it also takes crayfish and aquatic insects. In Ontario it breeds widely on wooded wetlands and moves through in spring and fall, usually in pairs or small groups rather than large rafts. Look on sheltered water with trees close by, and watch for the crest going up as one bird passes another.",
  "lesser-scaup": "One of two very similar scaup, and the one more likely inland. It dives for its food on lakes, large ponds and sheltered bays, often in tight rafts that ride well out from shore. A drake has a dark head glossed with purple, a pale grey back and a peaked crown that gives the head a tall, cornered look. Females are brown with a clean white patch at the base of the bill.\n\nSeparating it from Greater Scaup takes care. The peaked head shape is the first clue, and in flight the white wing stripe is shorter, stopping partway along the wing rather than running well out toward the tip. In Ontario it is mainly a migrant, passing through in good numbers on open water in spring and fall. It feeds by diving to the bottom for molluscs, seeds and aquatic insects. On a distant raft, structure and head shape matter more than colour, since the light rarely cooperates.",
  "northern-pintail": "Long-necked and trim on the water, a drake is easy once seen well. A chocolate-brown head, a thin white stripe running up the side of the neck, and long, needle-like black central tail feathers that give the bird its name. Those tail feathers grow only on breeding males. Females are plain grey-brown but still slim and long-necked, with a more pointed tail than most dabblers.\n\nLook on shallow marshes, flooded fields and sewage lagoons during migration, where flocks tip up to feed in shallow water. In Ontario it is mostly a migrant, moving through in spring and fall, with scattered breeding in the province. It is an early spring migrant, often on the move while ice still lingers. Wary and quick to flush, pintails tend to sit in open water where they can see trouble coming. A dabbler rather than a diver, so it feeds by upending rather than going under.",
  "northern-shoveler": "Nothing else on the marsh carries a bill like this. The oversized, spatula-shaped bill settles the identification at any distance. A breeding drake adds a green head, white chest and rusty sides, and often sits low with the heavy bill angled down toward the water. Females are mottled brown but wear the same great bill, so the shape carries the identification even when the colour does not.\n\nThe bill does real work. Comb-like structures called lamellae line its edges and strain tiny plants and animals from the water as the bird sweeps its head from side to side. Small groups sometimes swim in slow circles, stirring food up toward the surface. Find it on shallow marshes, sewage lagoons and muddy ponds, in food-rich water. In Ontario it is a widespread migrant in spring and fall and a local breeder in the south. It feeds low and rarely upends fully, letting the bill do the sorting.",
  "pied-billed-grebe": "A small brown diver that keeps to marshes, ponds and quiet bays with plenty of emergent plants. The stubby, chicken-like bill sets it apart from the ducks it shares the water with. In summer that pale bill carries a black ring across it. More often heard than seen, it gives a loud, whinnying series of calls from cover.\n\nIt can control its buoyancy, squeezing air from its feathers to sink slowly straight down like a submarine and vanish without a ripple. That trick lets it drop out of sight when disturbed rather than fly. It dives for fish, crayfish and aquatic insects, and its lobed toes drive it well underwater. In Ontario it is widespread on marshy waters through spring, summer and fall, and it breeds on vegetated wetlands, building a floating nest anchored to plants. Shy and easily overlooked, it often works the edge of the reeds and slips back into cover at the first sign of attention.",
  "red-breasted-merganser": "A fish-eating diver of big water, most familiar on the Great Lakes in migration and winter. Both sexes wear a shaggy double crest that gives the head a wind-blown look, and the bill is thin, red and serrated for holding slippery prey. A drake adds a green head, a white collar and a speckled rusty breast.\n\nIt favours open, cold water, and in migration flocks sometimes work together, herding and chasing fish through the shallows. In Ontario it moves through on the Great Lakes and large lakes in spring and fall and lingers into winter where water stays open. It breeds in the far north rather than in the settled south. Look for it well offshore, riding the swell in loose groups and diving in sequence as it hunts. The similar Common Merganser is cleaner-headed and heavier-billed, so the ragged crest and slim red bill are worth checking on any distant merganser flock.",
  "ring-billed-gull": "The gull most people picture without knowing its name. Parking lots, beaches, farm fields, rivers and city waterfronts across southern Ontario all draw it. A medium gull with a pale grey back and yellow legs, and a yellow bill crossed by a neat black ring that names the bird. That ring separates the adult from the larger Herring Gull, which has pink legs and a red spot rather than a ring.\n\nIt nests in dense colonies on Great Lakes islands, sometimes thousands of pairs together, then spreads out to feed wherever food is easy. It eats almost anything, from insects and fish to human scraps, which is part of why it does so well around people. Present in every season, it is abundant in the south and one of the first gulls to learn. Numbers swell in migration and around productive feeding sites. Immatures are mottled brown and take a few years to reach the clean grey-and-white adult look.",
  "ring-necked-duck": "A diving duck of wooded water, often on bogs, beaver ponds and marshy lakes where dabblers thin out. The head is tall and peaked, and a drake is boldly patterned in black and grey with a white ring around the bill. Females are brown with a pale eye-ring and a fainter bill ring.\n\nThe name misleads. The cinnamon neck ring the bird is named for is almost never visible in the field, so the bill ring is the mark to trust. In Ontario it breeds across the north and passes through the south in good numbers as a migrant in spring and fall. It dives for seeds, tubers and aquatic invertebrates, often on smaller, sheltered waters than the scaup it resembles. A peaked head, the white bill ring, and a grey rather than pale back help pull it out of a mixed diving-duck flock. Small groups are more usual than the large rafts scaup form.",
  "caspian-tern": "The largest tern there is, roughly the size of a gull and built heavier than any of its relatives. The thick, blood-red bill is visible at a distance and settles the identification. A black cap in the breeding season, a pale grey-and-white body and a shallowly forked tail complete the look.\n\nIt patrols Great Lakes shorelines, large lakes and river mouths, flying with slow, deliberate wingbeats and dropping headfirst after fish. Great Lakes islands hold a large share of its North American breeding population, which puts Ontario waters near the centre of its range. In the province it is present spring through fall and nests in colonies on open, sparsely vegetated islands. Listen for the harsh, low call, deeper than any smaller tern. It leaves before hard weather and winters well to the south. On a beach full of gulls, the size, the red bill and the neat black cap pick it out quickly.",
  "great-egret": "A tall white heron that stands out on any marsh. Long-legged and slim, it wades the shallows on black legs, holding a heavy yellow bill ready and striking at fish, frogs and other small prey. Size and shape separate it from smaller white waterbirds, along with the yellow bill and black feet.\n\nPlume hunters once nearly wiped it out for the long breeding feathers it grows in spring, and the effort to protect it became a rallying point for early bird conservation. In Ontario it haunts marshes, lake edges and slow rivers with shallow water, mainly in the south, though it has been slowly expanding north. Look for it spring through fall, often standing motionless for long stretches before a sudden stab. After the breeding season, egrets sometimes wander well beyond their nesting range, turning up on wetlands where they did not breed.",
  "american-redstart": "This warbler is easy to pick out once you learn its habit of fanning its tail and wings. Males wear black with orange patches on the wings, tail and sides. Females and young males show the same pattern in grey-olive and yellow. It nests in second-growth deciduous woods, forest edges and shrubby regrowth, and you can expect it across much of Ontario from spring through fall.\n\nBy fanning its bright patches, the redstart startles resting insects into flight, then chases them down through the lower branches. It moves almost constantly while feeding, dropping and turning as it goes. Active by day, it gives a thin, variable song that shifts through the breeding season.",
  "american-tree-sparrow": "The name is a poor fit, since this sparrow has little to do with trees. In Ontario it belongs to the cold months. Look for a rusty cap and eyeline, a plain grey breast marked with a single dark central spot, and a bill that is dark above and yellow below. It breeds far to the north and reaches southern and central Ontario only when most other sparrows have already gone south for the winter.\n\nThrough fall, winter and into spring you will find it in weedy fields, along marsh edges, in hedgerows and at feeders, often in loose flocks working seeds from standing weeds and scattered grain. It feeds by day, hopping on the ground and reaching up to bend seed heads down. Its arrival is a fair marker of the turning season. When it leaves in spring, the Arctic breeders are heading home.",
  "bank-swallow": "The smallest of Ontario's swallows, brown above and white below with a clean brown band across the chest. Its wings are shorter than those of other swallows, and it flies with quick, fluttery beats. You meet it near water, along sand and gravel banks on rivers and lakes and in pits, from spring through fall. It digs nesting burrows straight into vertical sandy banks and nests in colonies, sometimes many pairs in one exposed face.\n\nIt is listed as Threatened in Ontario. Numbers have dropped, and the loss and disturbance of nesting banks is part of the problem. Active pits, shoreline changes and bank collapse all affect where it can breed. If you find a colony, keep well back from the face during the nesting season. Digging, driving or building near an occupied bank can destroy burrows and the young inside.",
  "black-and-white-warbler": "You often notice this one on a tree trunk before you place it as a warbler. Streaked black and white from head to tail, it creeps head-first along trunks and large branches in a way that recalls a nuthatch more than a warbler. An unusually long hind claw gives it the grip to work bark this way, prying insects and their eggs from crevices most warblers cannot reach.\n\nIt nests in deciduous and mixed forests with large trees and is widespread through Ontario woodlands from spring into fall. The song is a thin, high, squeaky series, easy to miss over the rest of the dawn chorus. It arrives early for a warbler, sometimes before the leaves are fully out, which makes the bark-creeping habit easier to watch in open branches.",
  "black-throated-green-warbler": "The song gives it away before you see it, a buzzy zoo-zee-zoo-zoo-zee that carries through the forest and is one of the easier warbler songs to learn. The bird itself has a bright yellow-green face, a black throat and upper breast, and two white wing bars. Look for it in coniferous and mixed forests, especially where hemlock and spruce grow.\n\nIn Ontario it breeds through central and northern forests and passes through the south as a migrant in spring and fall. It gleans insects from foliage, often fairly high in the canopy, moving steadily along the branches. The face pattern stays clean and recognizable through the seasons, so even a quiet bird in fall migration is not hard to name once you get a good look.",
  "blackburnian-warbler": "The male's throat and face burn orange, framed by a black triangular cheek patch, a colour no other warbler in Ontario matches. Seeing it means a look into the treetops, since the bird often feeds high in mature spruce and hemlock, and a sore neck is part of the deal.\n\nIt breeds in mature coniferous and mixed forest through central and northern Ontario and moves through the south on migration. The song is very high and thin, rising at the end past the range some ears can follow. Females and fall birds trade the flame for a more muted yellow-orange but keep the same face pattern. Work the tops of tall conifers in spring and you have a fair chance of finding one.",
  "brown-creeper": "A small streaked brown bird that behaves like a wind-up toy on bark. It spirals up a trunk searching for insects, then drops to the base of the next tree and starts again. The streaked brown back blends into bark almost perfectly, so the movement usually catches your eye before the bird does. A thin curved bill and a white belly help confirm it.\n\nIt lives in mature forests with large trees and is present across Ontario through all four seasons, though it is easy to overlook. Its call is a very high, thin note, so high that some people cannot hear it at all. In winter it turns up in mixed woods and sometimes at the edges of yards with big trees. Patience helps. Watch one tree base and wait for the small brown shape to land low and begin its climb.",
  "brown-thrasher": "Bigger than it first seems, this is a long, rusty-brown bird with a long tail, a curved bill, staring yellow eyes and heavy dark streaks down the breast. It keeps to thickets, hedgerows and shrubby field edges, where it feeds on the ground and can be hard to see. When a male sings, though, he often climbs to an open perch and delivers his phrases in pairs, a habit that helps separate his song from the mockingbird and catbird.\n\nThe repertoire is enormous, among the largest of any North American bird. You find it in southern and central Ontario from spring through fall. It rakes through leaf litter with its bill for insects and other food, and it will scold sharply if you press too close to a nest in a low shrub.",
  "brown-headed-cowbird": "This blackbird builds no nest and raises no young of its own. Instead it lays its eggs in the nests of other birds and leaves them to do the work, which puts it at odds with many songbirds whose chicks lose out to the larger cowbird nestling. The male is glossy black with a brown head and a short, thick bill, and gives a liquid, gurgling call. The female is a plain grey-brown and easy to pass over.\n\nIt follows open ground, turning up in fields, along forest edges and at feeders across Ontario from spring through fall. The name comes from an old habit of following grazing animals to catch the insects they stir up. It is native and widespread, not an introduced pest, though its spread into cleared and fragmented land has made life harder for some of the smaller birds it parasitizes.",
  "chestnut-sided-warbler": "Look for the line of chestnut down the sides that gives this warbler its name, set against clean white underparts and a yellow-green crown. The song is a bright, whistled pleased-pleased-to-meetcha, one of the more quotable warbler songs and a good way to find the bird in dense cover. It favours shrubby second-growth and regenerating clearings, the tangled young woods that come up after cutting or clearing.\n\nThat preference has served it well. As forests were cleared and then grew back into shrubland, it became more common than it likely was before. In Ontario it is widespread in this kind of second-growth from spring through fall, feeding actively and often holding its tail cocked as it gleans insects from low branches. Fall birds look different, with a lime-green cap and a white eye-ring, and lose most of the chestnut.",
  "chipping-sparrow": "That long, dry, one-pitched trill running across a summer lawn usually belongs to this small sparrow. In breeding plumage it wears a rusty cap, a white eyebrow and a black line through the eye, over a clean grey breast. It is one of the tamer sparrows, at home in open woods, along conifer edges and on lawns, in parks and gardens.\n\nYou will find it widely across Ontario from spring through fall. It feeds mostly on the ground, taking seeds and insects, and often nests low in an ornamental conifer close to houses. The trill can be confused with a junco's or a pine warbler's, so a look at the bird helps. In fall the cap fades and fine streaking appears, and the crisp summer bird becomes a plainer, browner version of itself.",
  "cliff-swallow": "Where you find one mud nest tucked under a bridge or a barn eave, you usually find many. This swallow nests in colonies, building gourd-shaped nests of mud pellets against vertical surfaces, often near water. In flight look for a square tail, a buffy rump, a dark chestnut throat and a pale forehead, with steady wingbeats broken by long glides.\n\nIt ranges widely across Ontario from spring through fall, over farmland and around cliffs, bridges and buildings. Both members of a pair gather mud, beakful by beakful, to shape the nest, and a single site can hold dozens of them side by side. It feeds on insects taken on the wing, often high up in good weather and lower before rain. The pale forehead and square tail separate it from the similar Barn Swallow, which has a long forked tail.",
  "common-grackle": "A blackbird that looks stretched out, long in the body, with a long keel-shaped tail and a pale yellow eye set in a dark head. In good light the plumage shows a purple gloss on the head running into bronze across the back. Grackles are bigger than red-winged blackbirds or cowbirds, and the tail is the giveaway when they walk across a lawn or feed along the edge of a field. The voice is not much of a song. Most calls sound like a rusty hinge being forced open.\n\nYou will meet them almost anywhere in settled parts of Ontario from spring through fall, in farmland, towns, marsh edges and open woods. They eat about anything, which is part of why they do well around people. In fall they gather into large flocks, sometimes numbering in the thousands, and move and roost together. They are not at risk and are one of the more familiar birds of the season.",
  "common-yellowthroat": "Small and secretive, this warbler keeps to low cover in wet places. The male is easy once you see the face: a broad black mask bordered above with white, and a bright lemon-yellow throat. Females lack the mask and are plainer olive and yellow, which makes them harder to name. Both stay down in dense vegetation and rarely sit in the open for long.\n\nListen for the song before you look for the bird. The rolling witchety-witchety-witchety carries a surprising distance across a cattail marsh and runs right through the summer. Look for yellowthroats in marshes, wet thickets, roadside ditches and brushy field edges across the province, wherever there is thick low growth near water. They arrive in spring, breed through the warm months, and drift south again in fall. Not at risk, and one of the more reliable marsh birds to find by ear.",
  "dark-eyed-junco": "For many people in southern Ontario the junco means winter. It turns up at feeders and along brushy edges once the weather cools, which earned it the old name snowbird. The look is clean and quick to learn: a slate-grey hood and back, a sharp cutoff to a white belly, a small pink bill, and white outer tail feathers that flash when it flushes.\n\nJuncos breed in coniferous and mixed forest across central and northern Ontario, so summer birders in the south usually have to go and find them in the right woods. Come fall they move down and spread out, and by winter they are among the most common birds under feeders in the south, often feeding on the ground in loose flocks. They scratch for fallen seed rather than perching at the feeder itself. Not at risk. A dependable cold-season bird.",
  "eastern-kingbird": "Upright on a wire or fence post over an open field, blackish above and clean white below, this flycatcher is built to watch and wait. The best mark is the white band across the tip of the tail, seen as it sallies out after insects and returns to its perch. The voice is a set of sharp, buzzy notes rather than a musical song.\n\nThe name fits its temperament. A kingbird will launch at hawks and crows that cross over its nest, chasing much larger birds well beyond the tree it is defending. Look for them in open country with scattered perches: field edges, farmland and shorelines through southern and central Ontario. They come north in spring, nest through summer, and head back to South America in fall, where they switch to a fruit diet in the tropics. Not at risk, and hard to miss once it takes up a summer perch in the open.",
  "eastern-phoebe": "A plain flycatcher that gives itself away by habit more than by colour. Grey-brown above with a darker head, pale below, and lacking both wing bars and an eye ring, it is easy to overlook until it pumps its tail down and up while perched. That steady tail wag, repeated over and over, is the surest field mark. The song is a raspy two-part fee-bee, roughly what the name suggests.\n\nPhoebes are among the first migrants back each spring, often arriving in late March when much of the province is still bare and cold. They favour woodland edges, streams and farmyards, and they take readily to human structures, tucking a mossy nest onto a ledge under a bridge or the eaves of a building. The same sites are often used year after year. Look for them across southern and central Ontario from that early return through fall. Not at risk.",
  "eastern-wood-pewee": "Heard far more often than seen, this woodland flycatcher sings a slow, clear pee-a-wee that drifts down from the mid-level and canopy on summer afternoons. The bird itself is understated, grey overall with two pale wing bars and no eye ring, which helps separate it from the small Empidonax flycatchers that do show an eye ring. It perches upright on an exposed branch, sits still between sallies, and returns to the same spot after catching an insect.\n\nLook and listen for pewees in deciduous and mixed forest across southern and central Ontario through the breeding season. Numbers have fallen enough that the species is now listed as Special Concern in Ontario. The reasons are not fully settled, but loss and change of forest habitat and declines in flying insects are both thought to play a part. It is not endangered, but the trend is worth watching.",
  "european-starling": "Stocky and short-tailed with a long, pointed bill, the starling stands apart from most other dark birds around town. Its plumage shifts through the year, heavily spotted with white in winter, then wearing down to a glossy green-and-purple sheen with a yellow bill by spring. On the ground it waddles rather than hops, probing the turf as it goes. It is a strong mimic and works other birds' sounds into a rambling, wheezy jumble.\n\nEvery starling in North America traces back to a small number released in New York in the 1890s. From that start the species spread across the continent and is now found in cities, farms and towns throughout Ontario in every season. Being introduced, it competes with native cavity nesters such as bluebirds and some woodpeckers for nest holes, which is one reason it is not welcomed the way its abundance might suggest. It is common and firmly established.",
  "golden-crowned-kinglet": "One of the smallest birds in the province, barely heavier than a couple of coins, this kinglet is a restless mover through conifer branches. It is olive above and pale below, with a bright crown stripe bordered in black, yellow in the female and orange-centred in the male. It rarely holds still, flicking its wings as it hops among the outer needles. The calls are very high and thin, easy to miss if your hearing has dropped off at the top end.\n\nWhat sets it apart is toughness. Golden-crowned Kinglets stay through Ontario winters, gleaning tiny insects, spiders and eggs from conifer twigs when almost nothing else is feeding up there. They breed in coniferous forest across the north and spread into mixed woods farther south in the colder months. Look for them in loose company with chickadees and creepers in winter. Not at risk. It stays active even on very cold days.",
  "gray-catbird": "Named for its voice, this slate-grey skulker gives a drawn-out mew that sounds convincingly like a cat. Get a clear look and it is plain grey all over with a neat black cap and a rusty patch under the tail that is easy to overlook. It keeps to dense cover and often stays hidden while it calls, working through the middle of a shrub rather than sitting up in the open.\n\nThe catbird is a mimic, stringing together squeaks, whistles and borrowed phrases from other birds, and dropping that catlike mew in among them. The song rambles on without much repetition, which helps separate it from thrashers and mockingbirds. Look for it in thickets, hedgerows and shrubby edges across southern and central Ontario from spring through fall. It likes fruit and often turns up where berry-bearing shrubs are thick. Not at risk, and easier to find by ear than by eye.",
  "great-crested-flycatcher": "A large flycatcher of the treetops, more often heard than seen as it calls from high in the canopy. The pattern is worth learning: a grey throat and chest, a lemon-yellow belly, and a rusty wash in the wings and tail, with a slight bushy crest that gives the head a peaked look. The call is a loud, rising wheep that carries through the forest.\n\nUnusual among Ontario flycatchers, this one nests in tree cavities, using old woodpecker holes and sometimes nest boxes. It has an odd habit of working a shed snake skin, or these days a strip of plastic, into the nest lining. Look and listen for it in deciduous and mixed forest with tall trees across southern and central Ontario through the breeding season. It comes north in spring, nests through summer, and heads for the tropics in fall. Not at risk. The wheep call is the quickest way to place one overhead.",
  "hermit-thrush": "Brown-backed and spotted below, this thrush is best told from its relatives by its tail. The tail is warmer and redder than the back, and the bird raises it slowly and lets it drop, a movement the others do not share. Dark spots mark a white breast, and the whole bird has a quiet, watchful manner as it forages on the open floor of a shaded forest.\n\nThe song is one of the finer sounds in the northern woods, a clear rising flute phrase repeated at different pitches with a pause between. Hermit Thrushes breed in coniferous and mixed forest through central and northern Ontario and pass through the south on migration. This is the hardiest of the province's spotted thrushes, the last to leave in fall and among the first back in spring, and a few may linger later than expected. Not at risk. Learn the tail motion and the song and it becomes an easy bird to name.",
  "house-finch": "A small streaky finch of town and suburb, usually found close to people. The male shows red on the forehead, throat and upper breast, with plain brown streaking down the flanks, though the amount and shade of red vary from bird to bird. The female is plain brown and streaked with no red at all, which is where the confusion usually starts. Both have a stout seed-eating bill and a cheerful, warbling song that runs downhill and often ends on a buzzy note.\n\nThe eastern population began with birds released or escaped in the New York area in the 1940s. From there it spread north and reached Ontario, where it is now a common bird of cities and towns in the south, at feeders in every season. It can be mistaken for the Purple Finch, which is stockier and more raspberry-toned; the streaked brown flanks of the House Finch are a good place to look. Not at risk.",
  "house-wren": "Small and plain, the House Wren makes up for a drab look with a big voice. It turns up in gardens, thickets and shrubby forest edges across southern and central Ontario from spring through fall. Look for a faint pale eyebrow, a thin bill and the tail often cocked up over the back. You are more likely to hear it than see it well. The song is a long bubbling chatter delivered from cover or a low perch.\n\nIt nests readily in birdhouses and will use almost any cavity it can find. Old cans, boots or other odds left outside sometimes hold a nest. Males may fill several potential sites with sticks before a female settles on one. Around the yard it works through low vegetation for insects and spiders. Give it a nest box near shrubs and it will often move in.",
  "magnolia-warbler": "Bright and boldly marked, this is one of the easier warblers to name. Yellow underparts carry heavy black streaks that form a necklace, and there is a white patch on the wing. The tail is banded black and white. A reliable mark shows from below: the tail has a white base and a broad black tip, easy to catch as the bird moves through foliage.\n\nIn Ontario it breeds in young conifer stands and spruce-fir forest across the central and northern parts of the province. Farther south you meet it as a migrant in spring and fall, when it works through mixed woods and even suburban trees. It feeds low to mid-level, gleaning insects from branch tips and often fanning that patterned tail. The name comes from a magnolia tree where an early specimen was collected, not from any strong tie to the plant.",
  "marsh-wren": "Where cattails and bulrushes grow thick, listen for a rattling, gurgling song coming from deep in the stems. That is usually a Marsh Wren, a small brown bird with a white eyebrow and black-and-white streaks down the back. It stays low and hidden, and clear views take patience. When it does climb a stalk it often straddles two stems with its feet.\n\nIt lives in Ontario's southern and central wetlands from spring into fall. Males are busy builders. A single male may put up several dummy nests within his territory, only one of which is used for eggs. The extra nests may confuse predators or serve in courtship. Feeding happens down among the reeds, where the bird takes insects and other small prey off the stems and water surface. Drain or fill the marsh and the wren goes with it, so it depends closely on healthy cattail stands.",
  "ovenbird": "This is a warbler that behaves like a small thrush. Olive-brown above and white below with bold dark spots, it walks rather than hops across the open, leaf-littered floor of mature deciduous and mixed forest. Look for the black-bordered orange crown stripe and the bold white eye-ring. More often you hear it first: a loud, ringing song usually written as teacher-teacher-teacher, rising as it repeats.\n\nThe name comes from the nest. On the forest floor the female builds a domed structure with a side entrance, shaped a little like an old outdoor oven and well hidden in the leaf litter. Ovenbirds are widespread in Ontario forests from spring through fall and favour large, unbroken tracts. Because they nest on the ground in forest interior, they do poorly where woodlots are small and broken up. Walking a mature forest in June, the steady teacher song overhead and around you is often this bird.",
  "red-breasted-nuthatch": "A compact bird of conifer and mixed forest, the Red-breasted Nuthatch climbs trunks and branches in any direction, often working head first down the bark. It has a black cap, a white eyebrow, a black line through the eye and rusty underparts. The call is a nasal, repeated yank, thin and tin-horned, one of the easier winter woodland sounds to learn.\n\nIt is present year round in much of Ontario and comes to feeders in winter, especially for sunflower seeds and suet. In some years numbers move south in large flights when the northern cone crop fails. When nesting it excavates or uses a cavity and smears sticky conifer resin around the entrance hole, which may deter insects or predators. The bird avoids the mess by diving straight in. It often travels with chickadees and kinglets in mixed feeding flocks through the colder months.",
  "red-eyed-vireo": "Few forest birds sing as persistently. Through the heat of a summer day, when most others fall quiet, the Red-eyed Vireo keeps repeating short whistled phrases from the canopy, over and over. Seeing it takes more effort than hearing it. The bird is olive above and white below, with a grey cap and a dark line through the eye. The red eye that gives it its name shows only at close range and looks dark from a distance.\n\nIt lives high in deciduous and mixed forest across Ontario's wooded areas from spring through fall. This is one of the most abundant breeding birds in the province's forests, though its habit of staying in the leaves keeps it out of sight. It gleans insects and, later in the season, eats small fruit. Learn the endless daytime phrases and you will notice it in almost any mature woodland.",
  "ruby-crowned-kinglet": "One of the smallest birds you will meet in Ontario, this kinglet is a restless scrap of olive that rarely holds still. A bold white eye ring and two wing bars are the marks to check. The red crown patch that gives it its name belongs to males and shows only when the bird is agitated; most of the time it stays hidden. Watch for the constant nervous wing-flicking, a useful clue on its own.\n\nIt breeds in spruce and fir forests of the north and passes through the south in numbers on migration, when it turns up in mixed woods, thickets and gardens. Spring and fall are the times most southern birders see it. The song is a surprise: loud, rich and tumbling, far bigger than the bird producing it. It feeds actively on tiny insects, hovering at branch tips and hanging from twigs to reach them.",
  "savannah-sparrow": "Open country is where you find this streaky brown sparrow: hayfields, pastures, grasslands, dunes and shorelines. It is heavily streaked, with a short notched tail, and many birds show a yellow patch in front of the eye that helps separate it from similar sparrows. It often perches on a fence wire or weed stalk to sing a thin, buzzy, insect-like song.\n\nThe name is misleading. It comes from Savannah, Georgia, where the species was first described, not from any grassy savanna. In Ontario it is widespread across open habitats from spring through fall and can be common in farmland. It feeds on the ground, taking insects and seeds, and tends to run through the grass rather than fly when disturbed. In good habitat several males may sing within earshot of each other, spread across a field.",
  "song-sparrow": "A familiar bird of brushy places, the Song Sparrow lives in fields, marsh edges, hedgerows, shorelines and gardens across much of Ontario. It is heavily streaked brown, and the streaks on the breast usually gather into a dark central spot, a handy field mark. In flight it pumps its tail. The bird is widespread and often sings from an open perch, which makes it a good one to learn early.\n\nThe song usually opens with three clear notes before breaking into a jumble of trills and buzzes. Males may learn several versions and switch among them. Most are present from spring through fall, and some linger into winter in the south where cover and food hold out. It feeds low and on the ground, taking insects in the warm months and seeds later on. Brushy edges near water are a good place to expect it.",
  "swamp-sparrow": "Wet ground suits this sparrow. It keeps to marshes, bogs, fens and wet meadows with cattails and sedges, where it skulks low in the vegetation and can be hard to see well. Look for a rusty cap and rusty wings, a grey face and breast and a clean white throat. The song is a slow, even trill, richer and slower than a Chipping Sparrow's.\n\nIt is widespread in Ontario wetlands from spring through fall. Unlike most sparrows, it will wade into shallow water to feed and sometimes dips its head under the surface to grab aquatic insects and other small prey. That habit, and its longer legs, fit its marshy life. Patience helps: sit quietly at a marsh edge and a Swamp Sparrow will often work up a cattail stalk into view before dropping back into cover.",
  "tree-swallow": "Over water and open fields, this swallow cuts wide circles on long pointed wings, catching insects in the air. Adults are steely blue-green above and clean white below, a simple pattern that reads well even at a distance. It is widespread across Ontario in the warmer months and often gathers in numbers around marshes and ponds.\n\nIt nests in tree cavities and readily takes to nest boxes, which makes it a common tenant on bluebird trails and around farms. The nest is usually lined with feathers, often white ones the birds chase down and carry in. Tree Swallows return early in spring, sometimes while nights are still cold, and can fall back on berries when flying insects are scarce. By late summer they form large flocks before heading south. A box on a post near open water is a good way to host a pair.",
  "veery": "Named for its song, this is a plain-faced thrush of moist deciduous forest with dense undergrowth. It is warm cinnamon-brown above, with only faint, blurry spots on the breast, less marked than the other brown thrushes it shares the woods with. It stays low and can be shy. The song falls downward in an echoing, spiralling veer-veer, one of the more distinctive sounds of an Ontario summer evening.\n\nThat ringing quality comes from the syrinx, a two-sided voice box that lets the bird sound two notes at once and harmonize with itself. Veeries breed in southern and central Ontario from spring through fall, feeding on the ground and in low cover for insects and, later, small fruit. Dusk is the best time to hear one. Walk a damp, shrubby wood at last light in June and the downward song carrying through the trees is likely this bird.",
  "warbling-vireo": "A plain bird that gives itself away by sound. The Warbling Vireo has almost no field marks to work with - grey above, pale below, a faint line over the eye, no wing bars and no bold pattern. What settles the identification is the song, a long rolling warble that comes from high in the leaves. You will hear it far more often than you see it.\n\nIn Ontario it holds to the southern and central parts of the province from spring into fall. Look in deciduous trees near water, and do not overlook parks and yards, where it nests in shade trees along rivers and town streets. It gleans insects slowly through the canopy, moving with less urgency than a warbler. The lack of markings frustrates beginners, but once the song is learned this becomes one of the easier summer birds to place.",
  "white-crowned-sparrow": "Crisp is the word for the head. Bold black and white stripes run back over the crown, set off by a plain grey face and breast and a bill that ranges from pale pink to orange. That clean look separates it at a glance from the streakier sparrows feeding beside it.\n\nFor most people in Ontario this is a bird of passage. It moves through in spring and again in fall, pausing in weedy fields, brushy edges and hedgerows to feed on the ground and along low cover. Only in the far north, in coastal shrub, does it stay to nest. Watch mixed sparrow flocks on migration and check the crowns; young birds wear tan and brown stripes instead of black and white, which can slow you down until you notice the same clean face and pale bill. It scratches for seeds in the open more readily than many sparrows, so an unhurried look is usually possible.",
  "white-throated-sparrow": "Few sparrow songs are as easy to learn. The thin whistled phrase, often written as Oh sweet Canada Canada Canada, carries through northern woods all summer and is worth memorizing. The bird itself shows a clean white throat patch, a small yellow spot between the eye and the bill, and a striped crown that comes in two forms, one black and white, the other tan and brown.\n\nIt breeds across central and northern Ontario along coniferous and mixed forest edges and in brushy clearings, and it passes through the rest of the province on migration. In spring and fall it is one of the more reliable sparrows at feeders, scratching under the platform for spilled seed. The two crown colours are not male and female; both occur in both sexes, and birds tend to pair with the opposite type. Listen at dawn and dusk, when the whistled song runs most persistently.",
  "winter-wren": "Small and dark and hard to see, this is a bird you track by ear and by movement. It is one of the tiniest birds in the province, deep brown with a stubby tail cocked straight up, and it works low through fallen logs and root tangles like a mouse. A flick of motion at the base of a stump is often all you get.\n\nThe song makes up for the poor views. For such a small bird it delivers an extremely long series of high tinkling notes, ranked among the longest and most complex songs of any Ontario bird, and it can run on for several seconds without a break. Look and listen in damp coniferous forest, especially where blowdown and moss give it cover. It breeds through central and northern Ontario and moves south on migration, when a few turn up in shaded ravines and cedar swamps well outside the breeding range. Cold weather pushes most of them out of the province.",
  "wood-thrush": "The song is the reason many people learn this bird before they ever see it. A clear, flute-like ee-oh-lay rings out of the forest interior at dawn and dusk, one of the more musical sounds in an eastern summer woodland. The singer is a large thrush, warm reddish-brown above and boldly marked below with round black spots on a white breast, rustier and heavier than the other spotted thrushes it can be confused with.\n\nIt nests in the interior of mature deciduous forest with a well-developed shrub layer, mainly in southern Ontario. That habitat is shrinking and breaking into smaller pieces, and the Wood Thrush is listed as Special Concern in the province. Fragmentation exposes more nests to cowbirds and predators, and numbers have fallen across its range. Where it still breeds, listen along shaded trails through large blocks of hardwood. It forages on the ground, turning leaf litter for insects, and slips back into cover quickly, so patience helps.",
  "yellow-warbler": "About as yellow as an Ontario bird gets. It is bright yellow all over, with a plain open face, a dark eye that stands out on the blank yellow, and, on males, fine chestnut streaks running down the breast. The overall glow and the absence of any strong pattern set it apart from the other yellow warblers.\n\nLook for it low in willow and shrub thickets, along wet edges and streamside tangles, and in leafy gardens, where its bright sweet-sweet-sweet song is easy to pick up in early summer. It is widespread across the province. When a cowbird lays an egg in the nest, this warbler will sometimes build a fresh floor over the intruder's egg and start the clutch again, occasionally stacking several storeys when a cowbird returns. That habit is a small window into an arms race most people never see. It arrives in spring and is gone by early fall, one of the earlier warblers to move through.",
  "yellow-rumped-warbler": "The bright yellow rump does most of the work here. Grey and black overall, with yellow patches on the sides and a yellow crown spot, this warbler flashes a clean yellow rump patch whenever it flies, which is usually the quickest way to name it. It is one of the more numerous warblers on migration and turns up in almost any habitat then, from lakeshores to backyards.\n\nIt breeds in coniferous and mixed woods across central and northern Ontario and passes through the whole province in spring and fall. Unlike most warblers, which leave once insects thin out, this one can digest the waxy fruit of bayberry and juniper. That lets it linger later into fall than its relatives, and a few sometimes hang on into early winter where fruit holds. In spring the males are sharp in grey, black and yellow; by fall many are drabber brown, but the yellow rump still shows and settles the identification.",
  "common-redpoll": "A winter finch from the far north, and not a bird you can count on in any given year. Small and streaky brown, it wears a red patch on the forehead and a small black chin, and males add a pink wash across the breast. Flocks are restless and are often first noticed as a twittering mass lifting off a weedy field.\n\nIts appearances follow the seed crop. In winters when birch and alder seeds run short in the north, redpolls push south and can flood weedy fields, birch and alder stands, and feeders across Ontario; in other years they stay away almost entirely. When they do arrive they favour nyjer and small seeds and feed alongside siskins and goldfinches. They handle hard cold well, roosting in dense cover and sometimes tunnelling into snow. Check any flock of small streaked finches on the seedheads and look for the red cap. A good flight year can bring hundreds to a single stand.",
  "evening-grosbeak": "Heavy-bodied and big-headed, with a pale bill thick enough to crack large seeds, this is a finch built for the feeder tray. Males are golden-yellow with a bright yellow eyebrow and big white wing patches, and the whole bird has a blunt, front-heavy look. A flock dropping onto a sunflower feeder is hard to miss.\n\nWithin living memory these were a common winter sight across much of Ontario. Numbers have fallen sharply since, and COSEWIC assessed the species as special concern in 2016. The causes are not fully settled, though changes in forest and food supply are part of the picture. It breeds in coniferous and mixed forest across central and northern Ontario and moves south in some winters, in the irregular pattern winter finches are known for. There is no guarantee it will show in a given year. When a flock does find a feeder, keep it well stocked with sunflower seed; the birds go through a surprising amount.",
  "fox-sparrow": "Bigger and redder than the sparrows most people know. It is a large, richly rusty bird with heavy triangular spots gathered on the breast and a grey wash across the face. Even in poor light the warm reddish tone stands out among the browner sparrows it travels with.\n\nOften you hear it before you see it. It feeds by scratching hard in leaf litter, kicking backward with both feet in a quick double hop, and the racket it makes under a thicket is what draws the eye. Look in brushy thickets, forest edges and hedgerows during migration, mainly in spring and again in fall, when it moves through the province between more northern breeding grounds and southern wintering areas. A cold snap can push one under a feeder, where it works the ground beneath the seed. It rarely stays long, so a passing Fox Sparrow is worth stopping for.",
  "pine-siskin": "Easy to pass off as a streaky sparrow until it flashes yellow. This is a small, heavily streaked brown finch with a sharp, pointed bill and yellow edging in the wings and tail that shows best in flight or when it stretches. Flocks are tight and noisy, giving a rising buzzy call that is worth learning.\n\nNumbers swing hard from year to year. When conifer seed crops fail in the north, siskins pour south and can appear in large flocks at feeders stocked with nyjer and small seeds; in other winters there are almost none to be found. They breed in coniferous and mixed forest, mostly in the north, and can turn up in Ontario in any season, though winter brings the big flights. They mix readily with goldfinches and redpolls, so scan mixed flocks and pick out the streaking and the fine bill. In an irruption year a single feeder can host dozens at once.",
  "purple-finch": "The name undersells the colour; the males are raspberry, not purple. A male looks as though it has been dipped head first in raspberry juice, the wash spreading over the head, back and breast rather than settling into a cap. That even spread is the key mark separating it from the House Finch, whose red is brighter and mostly confined to the head and chest. Females are brown and streaky, with a bold whitish eyebrow that gives the face a patterned look.\n\nIt favours coniferous and mixed woods and comes to feeders, most reliably in winter. Birds breed across central and northern Ontario and move into the south in the colder months, though how many appear varies year to year. At a feeder, compare any reddish finch carefully, since House Finches share the same trays; look at where the colour sits and check the female's face pattern. The two are among the more commonly confused feeder birds.",
  "yellow-bellied-sapsucker": "The neat horizontal rows of small holes you sometimes find drilled into a live trunk are the work of this woodpecker. It taps shallow wells into the bark, then makes regular rounds to drink the sap that seeps out and pick off insects the sap has trapped. Other animals use the wells too, from hummingbirds to squirrels.\n\nLook for a black-and-white bird with a red forehead, a long white stripe down the folded wing, and a faint yellow wash on the belly. Males add a red throat. In Ontario it turns up in deciduous and mixed woods, aspen stands, and old orchards from spring through fall, then withdraws south for winter. Its drumming is uneven, a short roll followed by scattered taps, which helps separate it from steadier drummers. Nest holes are chiselled into live or dying trees, often the same tree used in earlier years. It is the migratory woodpecker of the group, so a sapsucker seen in the cold months is worth a second look.",
  "black-backed-woodpecker": "Burned forest can look empty, but it draws this woodpecker. After a fire, wood-boring beetles lay eggs in the dead trunks, and their larvae become an abundant food supply. The bird works the standing snags, flaking bark off in flat chips rather than drilling deep, listening and probing for the grubs beneath.\n\nRecognize it by the solid glossy black back, barred flanks, and, on males, a small yellow crown patch. It has three toes instead of the usual four. It stays in the boreal spruce forest year round and does not migrate, so winter is as good a time to find one as any. Because it depends on recent burns and beetle-killed stands, its numbers rise and fall with where fire has passed. A silent, dark bird against a black trunk is easy to walk past. Watch for falling bark chips and listen for slow, deliberate tapping, and check any patch of forest that has burned in the last few years.",
  "red-bellied-woodpecker": "The name misleads people, because the red belly is only a faint wash that is hard to see in the field. The head is what catches the eye: a band of red running from the bill back over the crown to the nape on males, limited to the nape on females. The back is closely barred black and white, and the face and underparts are plain pale grey.\n\nThis is a southern bird that has been moving north. Over recent decades it has spread into southern Ontario, and it now shows up at suburban feeders, especially in the Carolinian zone. It stays through the winter and takes suet and seed readily. Its rolling call carries through open deciduous and mixed woods. If you live in the south and hear a woodpecker you cannot place, this is an increasingly likely candidate. Farther north it remains scarce, though the edge of its range keeps shifting.",
  "american-three-toed-woodpecker": "Quiet and easy to miss, this is a bird of dead and dying spruce in the northern forest. It works slowly, flaking bark from failing trees to reach bark beetle larvae, and it rarely draws attention to itself. It closely resembles the Black-backed Woodpecker, but the centre of its back carries white barring rather than solid black. Males show a yellow crown patch, and, as the name says, the foot has three toes instead of the usual four.\n\nLook for it in boreal conifer forest, often around burns, bogs, or stands of beetle-killed spruce. It stays north all year and does not migrate. Numbers concentrate wherever recent disturbance has left standing dead wood full of insects, so a patch that looks dead to us can be good habitat. Finding one usually means standing still and watching for the small movements of a bird that is in no hurry. Falling bark flakes are often the first clue.",
  "red-headed-woodpecker": "Few Ontario birds are as clean-cut as an adult of this species: an entirely crimson head, a black back, and large white patches on the wings that flash in flight. Juveniles are duller, with a brown head. The behaviour stands out as much as the plumage. It hawks insects in mid-air like a flycatcher and caches acorns and other food in bark cracks and crevices to eat later.\n\nLook for it in open oak woods, savanna, forest edges, and places with standing dead trees, which it needs for nesting. It is listed as endangered in Ontario and has declined sharply. Loss of open woodland and of the dead trees it depends on is part of the reason, and sightings are now local and scattered across the southern and eastern parts of the province. It arrives in spring and leaves before winter. A stand of old savanna or dead trees near open ground is the kind of place still worth checking, and any sighting is worth reporting.",
  "wilsons-snipe": "Most encounters begin with a bird bursting up almost underfoot from wet ground, calling harshly and jinking away low in a zigzag. That is this snipe, a stocky shorebird built for concealment. The plumage is a fine pattern of browns and buff, with bold stripes along the head and back, and the bill is very long and straight, used to probe soft mud for worms and larvae.\n\nIt lives in marshes, wet meadows, bogs, and flooded ditches, and is most active around dawn and dusk. The spring display is the memorable part. A displaying male climbs high and then dives, and air rushing through his stiff outer tail feathers produces a hollow, pulsing winnowing sound that seems to come from nowhere. People often hear it over wet fields at dusk without ever seeing the bird. On the ground it stays still and trusts its camouflage, so you will usually flush one before you spot it.",
  "gray-partridge": "Farmland is the place to look for this compact, grey-brown partridge. It keeps to weedy field edges, grassy ditches, and stubble, where its colours blend into bare winter ground. A rusty face and a dark horseshoe-shaped patch on the belly are the marks to check, though you often see the bird only as it flushes, wings whirring and a short orange tail flaring as the covey scatters.\n\nIt was brought to North America from Europe for hunting and now lives wild in scattered parts of eastern and southern Ontario. Winters here are hard on it, but coveys stay together and huddle in the snow, sharing heat through the cold nights. Numbers are patchy and tied to farming practices, and it is easy to miss where hedgerows and weedy margins have been cleared. Listen for a hoarse, grating call from open fields, and scan bare ground at field edges in the colder months when cover is thin.",
  "sharp-tailed-grouse": "Open country in the north holds this pale, spotted grouse. It favours grassland, muskeg edges, brushy fields, and clearings in the boreal forest, ground where scattered cover meets open space. The short, pointed tail gives the bird its name and shows white edges as it flies away.\n\nSpring is when it is easiest to find. Males gather at traditional open sites called leks and dance to compete for females. They stamp their feet in a fast blur and inflate purple sacs on the neck. Yellow combs rise over the eyes. The same grounds are used year after year. Away from the lek the birds are quieter and harder to see, feeding on buds, leaves, and insects, and they stay in the north through the winter. The range covers northern and northwestern Ontario and does not reach the settled south. A visit to a lek at first light in spring is the best chance to watch the display, from a distance that does not disturb the birds.",
  "spruce-grouse": "A dark, chunky grouse of the northern conifer forest, this bird is best known for how little it fears people. Walkers sometimes pass within a few steps of one that simply watches from a low branch, a habit that earned it the old nickname of fool hen. Males are grey with a black throat and a red comb above the eye, females barred brown and easy to overlook.\n\nIt lives in boreal spruce, pine, and tamarack, and stays put all year. Winter diet is the interesting part. When other food is buried, it feeds almost entirely on conifer needles, a poor food that most birds cannot live on, and it manages because its digestive tract is long enough to break down the tough material. In summer it adds berries, buds, and insects. Look for it along quiet forest roads and trails in the north, often on the ground or perched low. Its tameness makes it easy to watch once found.",
  "northern-bobwhite": "This small, rounded quail takes its name from the male's clear whistled call, which sounds like the two words bob-white. Males wear a bold white throat and eyebrow stripe outlined in black; females show the same pattern in buff. It keeps to brushy fields, hedgerows, and grassland with scattered low cover, where a covey can feed and hide together.\n\nIn Ontario it is endangered. The species was once widespread across the southwest, but the only remaining wild native population now survives mainly on Walpole Island in the extreme southwest of the province. Loss of the weedy, brushy farmland it needs, along with hard winters, drove the decline. Birds seen elsewhere are usually released or escaped stock rather than part of the native population. It does not migrate, so the coveys face the full length of an Ontario winter. Anyone hoping to find the wild birds is looking at one small corner of the map, and the population there is fragile.",
  "willow-ptarmigan": "Winter turns this plump grouse almost entirely white, leaving only a black tail, which lets it disappear against snow. In summer the male is reddish-brown with white wings, a different bird to the eye though the same one underneath. The change is a seasonal moult, timed so the plumage matches the ground through the year.\n\nIn Ontario it lives only in the far north, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands, where open tundra, willow thickets, and bog edges give it food and cover. As the name suggests, willow buds and twigs are a staple, especially in winter. It stays north all year and is not something a traveller meets in the settled parts of the province. Feathered feet act like snowshoes and help it move and stay warm on frozen ground. For most people in Ontario it remains a bird of the map's top edge, seen only by those who make the trip to the coast.",
  "rock-pigeon": "The common pigeon of city squares and farmyards is this bird, so familiar that people stop seeing it. The classic form is blue-grey with two black bars across the wing and a white rump, but centuries of domestication have left the population in every colour from pure white to rusty brown and chequered patterns.\n\nIt came to North America from Europe long ago and descends from a wild pigeon that nested on sea cliffs and rock ledges. That history explains its habits. Building ledges, bridge girders, and overpasses stand in for the cliffs its ancestors used, which is why it does so well in cities and around barns and grain. It stays put all year and will breed in almost any month when food allows. Flocks feed on the ground on seeds and scraps and roost on ledges above. Look closely at an ordinary flock sometime and notice how much variation it holds.",
  "spotted-sandpiper": "Along almost any Ontario shoreline with open water, a small sandpiper works the edge with a constant teeter, bobbing its rear end up and down as it walks. That motion is the quickest way to name it. In breeding plumage the white underparts carry bold dark spots and the bill turns orange. Outside the breeding season the spots fade and the bird looks plainer, but the teetering habit stays.\n\nIt turns up on lakes, rivers, ponds, and streams from spring through fall, usually alone rather than in flocks. When flushed it flies low over the water on stiff, shallow wingbeats. Its breeding life runs against the usual pattern. A female often mates with several males in a season and leaves each one to incubate the eggs and raise the young on his own. She holds the territory and moves on to the next mate. Look for it wherever land meets water; it does not need much cover, just a bit of shoreline to patrol.",
  "black-billed-cuckoo": "This slim, long-tailed cuckoo is easy to miss. It keeps to shrubby thickets, forest edges, and overgrown fields across southern and central Ontario. It looks much like the Yellow-billed Cuckoo but shows an all-dark bill, a thin red ring around the eye, and only small pale spots on the underside of the tail. In flight it lacks the rufous wing flash of its relative.\n\nIt is heard more often than seen. The birds move quietly through dense cover, and a person can stand a few metres away without noticing one. Like the Yellow-billed, it eats large numbers of tent and other hairy caterpillars that most birds leave alone, spines and all. Numbers can rise in years when caterpillars are abundant. Look and listen for it from spring into fall in brushy, overgrown ground, especially near water. Patience helps more than anything; the bird rarely comes to you.",
  "common-nighthawk": "At dusk over towns, clearings, and rock barrens, a slim grey-brown bird cuts erratic loops through the air, catching insects on the wing. A white bar crosses each long, pointed wing and shows well against the sky. Despite the name it is not a hawk, and it is active mainly at dawn and dusk rather than deep night.\n\nListed as a Special Concern species in Ontario, it has declined along with the flying insects it depends on. It nests on open ground, including gravel and flat gravel rooftops, which puts eggs and young at risk as roofing changes. The male performs a display dive. He climbs, then dives steeply and pulls up sharply, and air rushing through the wing feathers makes a deep booming sound. You meet nighthawks from spring through fall, often in loose groups on warm evenings and sometimes in real numbers during fall migration. Watch open sky in the hour after sunset.",
  "eastern-whip-poor-will": "You will almost always hear this bird before you have any chance of seeing it. Its loud, repeated whip-poor-will carries through the dark from open dry forests, pine and oak barrens, and rocky clearings, mostly in central Ontario. By day it sits still on the ground or along a branch, and its mottled grey-brown plumage makes it nearly impossible to pick out.\n\nIt is a threatened species in the province. The birds nest on the bare forest floor with no built nest, so ground disturbance and the loss of open, barren habitat both hurt them. Feeding happens at dusk and on moonlit nights, when it hunts moths and other large insects on the wing with a wide gape. Bright moonlight seems to matter to its breeding cycle. Listen for it on warm nights from spring into fall near rock and pine barrens. Seeing one usually means catching eye-shine in a light, or nearly stepping on a bird that flushes at your feet.",
  "upland-sandpiper": "This is a sandpiper that avoids the shore. It lives in open grassland - native prairie remnants, hayfields, pastures, and the wide grassy margins of airports - across southern and eastern Ontario. The shape is distinctive: a small head, a large dark eye, a long thin neck, and a long tail. It often perches on a fence post, briefly raising its wings over the back before folding them.\n\nIn flight over the fields it gives a long, eerie whistle that carries a fair distance, a sound easier to place than the bird itself. It arrives to breed in spring and is mostly gone by late summer, so the window to find it is short. Because it depends on large open grasslands, it does well where hayfields and pastures stay open and poorly where they are lost or cut early in the season. Scan fence lines and posts along quiet rural roads, and watch the tops of tall grass for the small head and long neck.",
  "yellow-billed-cuckoo": "Dense deciduous thickets, willow and scrub near water, and shady woodland edges in southern Ontario hold this slim, long-tailed bird, though it takes some looking to find. It is brown above and white below, with a yellow lower bill, a flash of rufous in the wings when it flies, and large white spots on the underside of the tail. Those tail spots and the wing colour separate it from the Black-billed Cuckoo.\n\nLike its relative, it feeds heavily on hairy caterpillars, tent caterpillars among them, that most birds refuse. In years when caterpillars break out, cuckoo numbers can climb in response. It moves slowly and quietly through cover and is often detected by its low, knocking call rather than by sight. Look for it from spring through fall in brushy, damp ground and along wooded edges. This is a southern species in Ontario, so the odds are better in the far south than farther north. A still, watchful approach beats chasing it.",
  "osprey": "A fish-eating hawk of open water, the Osprey is white below with a dark stripe through the eye and long wings that bend at the wrist, giving a gull-like look in the air. It hunts by hovering over lakes, rivers, and wetlands, then plunging feet-first, sometimes going right under the surface. Nests are big stick platforms built on poles, snags, and artificial structures near water, and pairs often return to the same site year after year.\n\nIt is built for the job. Reversible outer toes and rough, barbed pads on the feet let it grip a slippery fish and carry it head-first, which cuts drag in flight. You see Ospreys near water from spring through fall, then they leave for the south in autumn. They took a hard hit from DDT decades ago and have recovered well, helped in part by nesting platforms. A bird carrying a fish toward a bulky roadside nest is a common sight along many Ontario lakes and rivers.",
  "red-tailed-hawk": "The large hawk perched on a highway pole or fence post through much of Ontario is most often this one. It is bulky and heavy-chested, pale across the breast with a band of dark streaks on the belly, and adults show the brick-red upper tail that gives the bird its name. Young birds lack the red tail, which can make them harder to name.\n\nIt hunts open country, farmland, and roadside woodlots, watching from a perch or soaring in slow circles before dropping on rodents and other small prey. It is present year-round, and numbers along roadsides can rise in winter. One odd bit of fame: its rasping, descending scream is the sound films and television dub over almost any bird of prey, so the eagle on screen is usually a Red-tailed Hawk you are hearing. In life the call goes with a big, patient hawk that does much of its living within sight of a road.",
  "turkey-vulture": "Few birds are as easy to name in the air. The Turkey Vulture soars on wings held in a shallow V, rocking and tilting as it rides updrafts, its flight feathers showing silvery from below against darker wing linings. Up close the small head is bare and red, an adaptation for feeding on carcasses without fouling feathers.\n\nIt is one of the few birds with a keen sense of smell, and it finds carrion by scent from high in the air, sometimes locating a dead animal hidden under the forest canopy. It feeds on carcasses and does not kill for itself. Vultures gather to roost communally on snags, towers, and cliffs, and drift over open and semi-open country in loose groups on warm days. In Ontario they are common in the south and centre and have been expanding northward. Present from spring through fall, they leave before winter. A wobbling, high dark shape on raised wings overhead is almost always this bird.",
  "american-kestrel": "North America's smallest falcon is about the size of a jay and often the most colourful raptor on a country road. Males and females both show a rusty back and tail and two bold black stripes down the face. Perched on a wire or post, the bird frequently pumps its tail. It favours open farmland, grasslands, and roadsides where scattered perches and old cavities for nesting are both available.\n\nWatch it hunt and the identification gets easier still. It will hang in one spot, hovering into the wind with quick wingbeats, then drop on grasshoppers, mice, and small birds below. It nests in tree cavities and will use nest boxes, which has helped in places where natural cavities are scarce. You find kestrels in open country from spring through fall. Numbers have slipped in parts of the range, so a bird bobbing its tail on a roadside wire is a good one to stop and watch.",
  "bald-eagle": "An adult is hard to mistake: a dark body with a clean white head and tail and a heavy yellow bill. It takes several years to reach that plumage, and younger birds are mottled brown with dark bills, which leads to plenty of confusion with other large raptors. Look for it near large lakes, rivers, and coastlines with tall shoreline trees for nesting and perching.\n\nIts recent history in Ontario is a recovery story. Once nearly gone from the south because of DDT thinning eggshells, the population rebuilt to roughly 1,400 breeding pairs, and the bird was removed from the provincial species-at-risk list in 2023. It fishes, scavenges, and takes food from other birds, and can be seen year-round, with birds concentrating near open water in winter. If you find an injured eagle or any large raptor, keep well back and call a licensed wildlife rehabilitator. The talons and beak are powerful and a frightened bird can cause serious injury, so do not try to handle it.",
  "barred-owl": "Mature mixed and swampy forests with big old cavity trees are the home of this large, round-headed owl. It has no ear tufts, dark eyes rather than yellow, horizontal barring across the chest, and vertical streaks down the belly. That dark eye alone separates it from most other Ontario owls.\n\nIt is best known by voice. The rhythmic hooting is often written as \"Who cooks for you, who cooks for you-all?\" and carries well through the woods at night. Pairs will call back and forth, sometimes breaking into a longer, raucous exchange. Active mainly after dark, it hunts from a perch, taking mice, other small mammals, amphibians, and the odd bird. It stays in Ontario year-round and does not migrate, so a territory held one season is often held the next. It is widespread in suitable mature forest. Listening on a still night, especially in spring, is the surest way to know one is nearby, since the bird itself sits quietly and is easy to overlook.",
  "great-horned-owl": "Few night sounds carry as far as the deep, even hooting of this owl, usually heard at dusk from a wooded edge. It is a large bird with prominent ear tufts, a rusty facial disc and a white throat patch that shows when it calls. You can find it in almost any wooded setting, from northern forest to city ravines and suburban parks, in every season. It hunts after dark, taking mammals and birds up to surprising size. It is one of the few predators that regularly kills and eats skunks, which says something about both its appetite and its poor sense of smell.\n\nNesting starts early, often in late winter, and the pair will take over an old hawk or crow nest rather than build their own. A nesting bird defends its territory hard and can strike an intruder with its talons. Give any active nest a wide berth. Most of the year the owl goes unseen, betrayed only by that voice.",
  "peregrine-falcon": "A crow-sized falcon with a dark hood that reads like a helmet, pointed wings and fast, driving wingbeats. In level flight it can look unhurried, but in a hunting dive, called a stoop, it exceeds 300 km/h and is the fastest animal on Earth. It strikes other birds in mid-air. Look for it on Great Lakes cliffs, along shorelines, and on the ledges of tall city buildings, which stand in for the cliffs it would otherwise use. Bridges and downtown towers now host nesting pairs in several Ontario cities.\n\nIt is listed as a species of special concern. The population crashed in the mid-1900s from DDT, which thinned eggshells, and the bird was gone from much of its range for decades. Banning that pesticide and a long reintroduction effort brought it back, though it is still watched closely. Spring through fall is the season to look; many birds move south for winter. A perched falcon is easy to miss until it drops.",
  "snowy-owl": "White plumage and a round, tuftless head make this a hard owl to confuse with any other. Adult males can be nearly pure white; females and young birds carry heavy dark barring. It belongs to the Arctic tundra and comes south only in winter, when it settles on open ground that resembles home - farm fields, airport verges, lakeshores and frozen marshes. Numbers vary a great deal from year to year. In seasons when Arctic lemmings are abundant, more young survive, and the following winter can bring an irruption that pushes many birds far south.\n\nUnlike most owls it will hunt in daylight, sitting low on a post, fence or snowbank and watching for voles. Wintering owls are often tired and hungry, and they tire further when people crowd them. Do not approach for a closer photo, and never bait one with bought mice to draw it in. Watch from a distance and let it hunt.",
  "canada-goose": "Almost everyone knows this goose: a brown body, a black head and neck, and a white strap across the chin. In flight the long lines and V-formations are loud enough to hear before you see them. It uses lakes, ponds, rivers, farm fields, parks and golf courses, and it grazes on grass as readily as on water plants. The old picture of the goose as a long-distance migrant no longer fits everywhere. Many birds in southern Ontario now stay through the year, using open lawns and unfrozen water in cities where food is easy and hunting pressure is low.\n\nThat has made the goose common to the point of nuisance in some parks. Nesting pairs are not to be crowded. A goose guarding a nest or a brood of goslings will hiss, spread its wings and charge, and a large bird moving fast is worth stepping back from. Give them room in spring and early summer and the display usually ends there.",
  "common-loon": "Nothing else on a northern lake sits quite like a loon: low in the water, heavy-bodied, with a straight dagger of a bill. A breeding adult has a black head, a checkered black-and-white back and a striped collar around the neck. The voice carries across the water at night, a wailing yodel that stands in for wild country and that put the bird on the one-dollar coin. It needs clear, deep lakes where it can see and chase fish underwater, which is why cottage country and the north suit it.\n\nIt dives well and rides so low that it can sink out of sight without a splash. Pairs return in spring as the ice goes and raise one or two chicks, sometimes carried on a parent's back when small. Loons are sensitive to boat wakes near nests and to lead fishing tackle, which poisons birds that swallow a lost sinker. Use non-lead tackle and keep your distance from a bird on a nest.",
  "great-blue-heron": "A tall, blue-grey wader with a long neck, a heavy dagger bill and a black plume trailing back from the eye. In flight it folds the neck into a tight S and trails its legs, which tells it apart from a crane. You meet it standing at the edge of a marsh, lake or river, or in a flooded field, often so still it looks like part of the shoreline. That stillness is the hunting method. It waits, then drives the bill down in a fast strike to take fish, frogs and the occasional small mammal.\n\nIt nests in colonies, building bulky stick nests high in trees, and a rookery can hold many pairs in one stand. The bird is present spring through fall, with many leaving before hard freeze-up. If you find one injured, keep your hands away from it. The bill is long and sharp and a frightened heron aims for the face. Leave a rescue to people equipped for it.",
  "mallard": "The green-headed drake is the duck most people picture first. Males show a glossy green head, a yellow bill and a thin white ring at the base of the neck; females are mottled brown. In both sexes a blue patch on the wing, the speculum, is bordered front and back with white, and that mark is useful when the birds are dull or in eclipse plumage. It turns up on nearly any water, from wild marshes to ditches and city ponds, and stays through winter wherever water stays open.\n\nFew ducks are as adaptable. It is the ancestor of most domestic ducks, and it interbreeds freely with them and with other wild ducks, so odd-looking hybrids are common around parks where tame birds mix in. Feeding bread does these birds no good; it fouls the water and gives poor nutrition. If you want to help, leave them to forage. The tail-up dabbling in the shallows is the natural way they take seeds and plants.",
  "mute-swan": "An all-white swan with an orange bill and a black knob at its base, usually holding its neck in a curved S rather than straight up. It is easy to name, but it does not belong here. Brought from Europe as an ornamental bird for parks and estates, it escaped and established wild-breeding populations, and it is now treated as invasive. On the lower Great Lakes and on sheltered bays and ponds it can push native waterfowl off nesting and feeding areas, and it tears up large amounts of aquatic plants as it feeds.\n\nA pair defends a big territory. Near the nest the birds are aggressive, and a swan will drive at a person or a boat with wings raised and neck lowered. It is a strong animal and the charge is not a bluff, so keep clear in the nesting season. It is present year-round wherever water stays open. Telling it from the native trumpeter and tundra swans comes down to the orange bill and the knob.",
  "sandhill-crane": "Standing about a metre tall, grey overall, with a bare red crown and a shaggy bustle of feathers over the tail. In flight it holds the neck straight out, which separates it at a glance from a great blue heron, and it often circles high on set wings. The call is the giveaway even when the bird is out of sight - a loud, rolling bugle that carries for kilometres. In spring, pairs bounce and leap in a courtship dance, wings half open, and both members bugle together.\n\nLook for it in open marshes, bogs and wet meadows, and out in adjacent farm fields where it feeds on waste grain and roots. For years it was mainly a bird of central and northern Ontario, but the breeding range has been spreading south, and small flocks now show up in farmland where they were once rare. It nests on the ground in wet cover and raises one or two long-legged young, called colts, that follow the adults through the season.",
  "trumpeter-swan": "The largest waterfowl in North America, an all-white swan with an all-black bill. The black of the bill runs up to meet the eye with no break, and many birds show a thin red line, like lipstick, along the lower edge of the bill. Those marks separate it from the orange-billed mute swan. This species was wiped out of Ontario long ago, shot out and lost to habitat change, and it was gone for generations.\n\nA long reintroduction effort using released birds has rebuilt the provincial population to more than 3,000, and marked birds carry wing tags that let biologists track them. Look on marshes, open wetlands and lakes, and in winter on stretches of water that stay ice-free. It is a heavy, powerful bird and defends its nest hard, so keep well back in the breeding season. Numbers are still managed and watched.",
  "wood-duck": "The drake is among the most ornate ducks on the continent, iridescent green and chestnut with a swept-back crest and bold white lines mapped across the face. The female is quieter, grey-brown with a white teardrop-shaped ring around the eye. Both perch in trees, which few ducks do, and the species nests in cavities - old woodpecker holes, natural hollows, or the nest boxes people put up for it. Look on wooded swamps, beaver ponds and slow, tree-lined rivers, mostly in southern and central Ontario, through spring and fall.\n\nThe nesting habit leads to one of the odder sights in the woods. A day after hatching, the ducklings climb to the cavity entrance and jump, sometimes from high up, bouncing on the ground unhurt, then follow the calling female to water. Nest boxes placed near water have helped the species recover from low numbers a century ago. It is shy and flushes fast, often with a rising squeal, so a quiet approach helps.",
  "american-goldfinch": "In summer the male is hard to miss: bright lemon-yellow with a black cap set forward on the forehead and black wings marked with white. Females are duller, and in winter both sexes fade to a soft olive-brown that throws people who only know the summer bird. It feeds almost entirely on seeds, working weedy fields, meadows, roadsides and gardens, and it clings to thistle and other seed heads to pull them apart. The flight is deeply looping, up and down, often with a light call given on each rise.\n\nIt nests later than most songbirds, waiting until mid to late summer. That timing lines up with thistle, which provides both seed to eat and down to line the nest with. It stays through the year in much of the province, so a feeder stocked with nyjer or sunflower seed will hold it into winter, when the drab plumage can make it easy to overlook. Small flocks are usual outside the breeding season.",
  "american-robin": "Most people know this bird before they know its name. The robin turns up on lawns across Ontario, hopping a few steps, then standing still with its head cocked to one side as if listening for worms. It is really watching for them. Look for grey-brown upperparts, a warm brick-red breast, a dark head and a broken white ring around the eye. It is a medium-sized thrush.\n\nRobins live almost anywhere there is open ground and a little cover: gardens, parks, fields, forest edges. They arrive early and the song is one of the first heard at dawn in spring. Many stay through fall, and some linger into winter where fruit holds out, shifting from worms and insects to berries once the ground freezes. Their return is treated as a sign of spring, though a few are usually around before the season properly turns.",
  "baltimore-oriole": "A flame-orange male in a leafy treetop is hard to miss. Males are orange and black with white wing bars. Females and young birds are yellow-orange and plainer, which can make them easy to overlook. Both keep to the canopy of open deciduous woods, riverside trees, orchards and older suburbs with tall shade trees.\n\nOrioles arrive in spring and are gone again by late summer, so the window to see one is short. They weave a deep, hanging nest that dangles like a sock from the tip of a branch, often high and out over open space. The diet runs to insects, nectar and fruit. An orange cut in half and set out, or a small dish of grape jelly, will sometimes draw one to a yard that has the right trees nearby. Listen for a clear, whistled song from overhead before you spot the bird itself.",
  "barn-swallow": "Few birds are as tied to human buildings as this swallow. It nests in barns, under bridges and on sheltered ledges, and hunts low over open fields and water, twisting and turning after flying insects. In flight the deeply forked tail is the giveaway. No other swallow in Ontario has a tail cut that deeply. It is steely-blue above, with a rusty throat and buff underparts.\n\nNumbers have fallen. Aerial insect-eaters have declined across the region, and this species is now listed as special concern in Ontario. The reasons are not fully understood, but loss of nesting sites and fewer flying insects both appear to matter. If a pair nests on a building you own, leaving the nest in place through the summer is a real help. Barn Swallows arrive in spring, raise their young through the warm months, and are gone by fall, wintering far to the south. You most often meet them out over farmland.",
  "black-capped-chickadee": "Small and tame, this is often the first bird to find a new feeder. The black cap and bib, white cheeks and buffy sides make it simple to name, and the buzzy chick-a-dee-dee-dee call it is named for carries well through the woods. It stays in Ontario all year, working through mixed woods, edges, thickets and backyards in loose flocks, often alongside nuthatches and other small birds.\n\nWinter is the hard test, and the chickadee is built for it. It caches food through the fall, tucking seeds and insects into bark and moss, and its brain grows extra cells to help it remember where. In cold weather it can lower its body temperature at night to save energy. At a feeder it takes one seed at a time, carries it to a perch, and hammers it open. Sunflower seeds will bring them close, sometimes to the hand if you are patient and stand still.",
  "blue-jay": "Loud and bold, the jay announces itself before you see it. The crest, the blue upperparts with black-and-white barring on the wings and tail, the white underparts and the thin black necklace add up to a bird few people confuse with anything else. It lives year-round in deciduous and mixed woods, parks and suburbs, and comes readily to feeders.\n\nJays belong to the crow family and it shows. They are quick learners and good mimics, and will sometimes copy the scream of a hawk, which can send smaller birds scattering from a feeder. In fall they gather acorns and other nuts and bury them, thousands over a season, far more than they ever dig up again. The forgotten ones sprout, so jays help spread oaks across the landscape. Not every Ontario jay stays put. Some drift south in loose daytime flocks in fall, while others hold their ground through winter.",
  "cedar-waxwing": "Smooth is the word that fits this bird. The plumage looks pressed and even, fawn-brown shading to grey, with a crest, a black mask, a yellow band at the tip of the tail and small red droplets on the wing that look like sealing wax. Those waxy tips give the group its name. It moves in tight, sociable flocks and calls in thin, high whistles that are easy to miss.\n\nFruit runs its life. Waxwings follow the crop, showing up wherever berries are ripe along woodland edges, in orchards and in planted suburbs, so their movements are hard to predict from one year to the next. They will pass berries down a line of birds perched along a branch. On overripe fruit that has begun to ferment, a flock can take in enough alcohol to become visibly drunk, and birds sometimes hurt themselves flying into windows afterward. They turn up in any season, as likely in a parking lot with fruit trees as in wild country.",
  "northern-cardinal": "A red bird at a winter feeder against snow is one of the plainest pleasures of a southern Ontario yard. Males are deep red with a black face and a pointed crest. Females are warm buff-brown with red in the wings, crest and tail, and a coral bill of their own. Both keep to shrubby edges, hedgerows and gardens, and rarely stray far from cover.\n\nCardinals do not migrate. A pair often holds the same patch through the year, which is why you see them in every season. The range has crept steadily northward over the past century, and winter bird feeding is thought to have helped by giving the birds a reliable food supply through hard months. Both sexes sing, which is less common among songbirds, with clear whistled phrases often delivered from a high perch. In spring a male may attack his own reflection in a window or a car mirror, mistaking it for a rival, and can keep at it for days.",
  "red-winged-blackbird": "Come spring, every cattail marsh seems to have one. Males sit up on the reeds, glossy black with red-and-yellow shoulder patches, and throw back their heads to deliver a scratchy conk-la-ree. Females look completely different: heavily streaked brown, sparrow-like, and easy to misname. This is one of the most abundant birds on the continent, and it fills wet ditches, marshes, hayfields and shrubby field edges across Ontario.\n\nA male defends his territory hard. He will chase off much larger birds, and at the height of nesting season he may dive at people who walk too close to a marsh, sometimes clipping a hat. It is bluff more than real danger, but it can startle you. Keeping to the path and moving on settles it. Most arrive early in spring, well ahead of the leaves, and numbers build into large flocks by fall before they move south. The red shoulders are often hidden, showing in full only when a male is displaying or alarmed.",
  "ruby-throated-hummingbird": "Nothing else in Ontario moves quite like it. This is the only hummingbird that breeds in eastern Canada, a tiny, iridescent-green bird that hovers at flowers and feeders and can fly backwards. Males show a ruby throat that flares in good light and looks flat black when the light is wrong. Females and young birds lack the red.\n\nYou meet them from spring into fall around gardens, clearings and forest edges with tubular flowers, which they favour for nectar. They also take small insects and spiders for protein. Sugar water will bring them in, one part white sugar to four parts water, no dye, kept clean and changed often in the heat. For such a small bird, the migration is long. Many cross the Gulf of Mexico in a single nonstop flight, hundreds of kilometres over open water, on a body that weighs about as much as a few paperclips. They leave Ontario by early fall for the trip south.",
  "white-breasted-nuthatch": "Watch a tree trunk long enough in a mature wood and you may see a small blue-grey bird walking down it head-first, which few other birds do. That habit, along with the white face and breast and the neat black cap, names it at once. It gives a nasal, honking call that carries through bare winter woods.\n\nThe name comes from feeding. A nuthatch wedges a seed or nut into a crack in the bark and hammers, or hatches, it open with its bill. It caches food for later too, tucking seeds into crevices. This is a year-round resident of southern and central woodlands, and it comes to feeders for sunflower seeds and suet, often travelling with chickadees and other small birds in winter. Going down the trunk headfirst lets it spot food that birds climbing upward tend to miss. A pair usually holds a patch of mature deciduous or mixed forest through the seasons rather than wandering.",
  "bobolink": "On a June morning in a hayfield, the male sings in bubbling, mechanical notes while flying low over the grass, and the sound is unlike anything else in Ontario. In breeding plumage he is oddly patterned: black below, with a creamy patch on the back of the head and white across the back, as if the suit were on backwards. Females and fall birds are buffy and streaked, easy to pass off as large sparrows.\n\nThe bird depends on grassland, and grassland has grown scarce. It nests in hayfields and meadows in southern and eastern Ontario and is listed as threatened in the province. Early or repeated haying can destroy nests before the young can fly, so delaying the cut where possible is one of the clearest ways to help. Bobolinks are here only in the warm months. The migration is long, a round trip that can reach around 20,000 kilometres to South America and back, among the longest of any songbird on the continent.",
  "eastern-bluebird": "Set against a bare fence post in open country, the male is an easy bird to fall for: brilliant blue above with a rusty-orange throat and breast. Females wear the same pattern in softer tones, greyer, with blue washing the wings and tail. It hunts from a low perch, dropping to the ground for insects, and favours orchards, pastures and forest edges with open spaces and scattered perches.\n\nThe bluebird nests in cavities, and that need nearly undid it. As old trees and wooden posts grew scarce and introduced house sparrows and starlings took the holes that remained, numbers fell. Volunteers turned it around by putting up and tending nest boxes along country roads and field edges, and the bird has recovered well. You can find it from spring through fall across southern and central Ontario, and some linger later where berries and shelter allow. A well-placed, properly sized nest box, kept clear of competitors, still makes a difference.",
  "eastern-meadowlark": "Despite the name, this is not a lark but a member of the blackbird family. On the ground in a hayfield it can be hard to pick out, but a bird facing you shows a bright yellow breast crossed by a bold black V. In flight the white outer tail feathers flash open. You meet it from spring through fall in the grasslands, pastures and hayfields of southern and eastern Ontario, usually first by ear: a clear, slurred whistle delivered from a fence post or a tall stem.\n\nIt nests on the ground in tall grass, which is the heart of its trouble. Hayfields are now cut early and often, and mowing during the nesting weeks destroys eggs and young. The species is listed as Threatened in Ontario, and its song is heard in fewer fields each decade. Delaying a first cut, or leaving uncut margins, is one of the few things that helps.",
  "downy-woodpecker": "The smallest woodpecker in Ontario, and the one most likely to turn up at a backyard feeder. It is patterned in black and white, with a short, stubby bill; males carry a small red patch on the back of the head. Present all year, it works woodlands, parks, orchards and weedy fields, and moves readily into towns.\n\nThe short bill is the key to how it makes a living. It can forage on goldenrod galls and slender weed stems that are too thin to bear the weight, or the hammering, of larger woodpeckers. In winter it often joins loose flocks of chickadees and nuthatches moving through the trees. It is easy to confuse with the Hairy Woodpecker, which looks like the same bird enlarged; on the Downy the bill is noticeably short relative to the head. A quiet, high 'pik' note and a descending whinny are worth learning.",
  "northern-flicker": "Most woodpeckers stay on tree trunks. This one spends much of its time on the ground, and ants are its favourite food. It is a large brown woodpecker barred with black, marked by a black crescent across the chest; in flight it shows a white rump and, on our eastern birds, yellow under the wings and tail. Males add a black mark back from the bill, sometimes called a moustache.\n\nLook for it in open woods, forest edges, fields and suburbs from spring into fall, often hopping across a lawn probing for ants. Many leave the province for winter, though a few linger in the south. The loud, ringing 'wick-a-wick-a' call and a long, even series of notes carry well in spring. Because it feeds so much on the ground, it is one of the woodpeckers most often seen in the open rather than glimpsed against bark.",
  "hairy-woodpecker": "Set a Hairy and a Downy side by side and the Hairy looks like the larger of two versions of the same bird: black and white, with the male's red nape. The reliable difference is the bill, which on the Hairy is long and thick, close to the width of its head.\n\nIt favours mature forests, woodlots and wooded suburbs, and stays through all four seasons. It often follows Pileated Woodpeckers and other heavy diggers, gleaning insects from wood that has just been opened up. Its call is a sharp 'peek', louder and flatter than the Downy's, followed at times by a rattling run of notes that holds one pitch rather than falling away. Alone, size is a poor guide, since distance is hard to judge in the field. The bill length is what to check.",
  "pileated-woodpecker": "About the size of a crow, this is the largest woodpecker in Ontario and hard to mistake. It is black with bold white neck stripes and a pointed red crest. When it hunts carpenter ants it chisels large rectangular holes into trunks and dead wood, deep enough to reach the galleries inside. These excavations are often the first sign one is in the area, along with piles of coarse chips at the base of a tree.\n\nIt needs large tracts of mature deciduous and mixed forest with big dead trees, and it stays year-round. The call is a loud, ringing series that rises and falls, sometimes mistaken for a flicker but heavier. Its old holes matter beyond the bird itself. Once abandoned, the cavities shelter owls, ducks, squirrels and other animals that cannot dig their own.",
  "ruffed-grouse": "A chunky, well-camouflaged bird of the forest floor, mottled brown or grey, with a small crest, dark ruffs at the sides of the neck and a banded tail it can spread into a fan. It lives in young to mixed-age forests with a dense understory and a good component of aspen or birch, and it is present year-round across most of the province.\n\nIt is more often heard than seen. In spring the male stands on a log and beats his wings to make a deep, thumping sound that accelerates like a distant motor starting up; the effect carries far through the woods, though the source is hard to locate. Flushed, the grouse explodes from cover in a startling burst of wings, which is often your only look. In winter it grows comb-like fringes on its toes that work as snowshoes and roosts in soft snow to stay warm.",
  "wild-turkey": "A large, dark, ground-dwelling bird with a bare head that shows blue and red skin. Males are the showier sex, fanning a broad tail, puffing out the body feathers and trailing a coarse beard from the chest. Turkeys disappeared from Ontario by 1909, wiped out by hunting and land clearing. They were reintroduced in the 1980s, the effort took hold, and they are now common across southern and central Ontario in hardwood and mixed forest with clearings, field edges and farmland.\n\nYou may find them in any season, often in flocks scratching through leaf litter, and they roost in trees at night. One caution applies in the spring breeding season: some males become bold and will confront people, especially where they have been fed. Do not feed them, and give a displaying male room rather than standing your ground.",
  "american-woodcock": "Built like a shorebird but living in the uplands, this is a plump, cryptically patterned brown bird that is far easier to hear than to see. The bill is very long, the eyes are large and set high on the head, and it walks with a curious rocking, back-and-forth step. Its plumage matches dead leaves so closely that it often sits tight until nearly stepped on.\n\nLook for it in young forests, moist thickets and shrubby field edges with rich soil, where it probes soft ground for earthworms. It is active mainly at dawn and dusk. The reason to be out at those hours in spring is the male's display: from a clearing he gives a buzzy 'peent', then spirals high into the darkening sky on twittering wings before tumbling back down. It arrives early in the season and can be one of the first migrants to return.",
  "ring-necked-pheasant": "An introduction from Asia, brought to North America as a game bird, and now hanging on locally in the farm country of far southern Ontario. The male is hard to miss: coppery and iridescent, with a green head, red skin around the face and a white ring at the base of the neck, trailing a long pointed tail. The female is plain mottled brown and easy to overlook.\n\nLook along hedgerows, brushy field edges, grassy ditches and the margins of farmland. It is present year-round but does not spread far, and hard winters take a toll. The male gives a loud, harsh double note, often followed by a quick burr of wingbeats. Flushed birds rise noisily and glide off low over the fields. Because it is not native, numbers in many areas depend on continued stocking rather than a self-sustaining wild population.",
  "american-crow": "All black from bill to tail, familiar almost everywhere, and announced by its harsh 'caw'. Against the larger Common Raven it is slimmer-billed, with a fan-shaped rather than wedge-shaped tail, and it flaps steadily rather than soaring. It turns up in farmland, towns, woodlots and along shorelines throughout the province, and stays year-round in the south.\n\nCrows are unusually sharp. They recognize individual human faces and remember anyone who has threatened them. Other crows learn that wariness from them. Outside the breeding season they gather in large communal roosts, sometimes numbering in the thousands, streaming in from all directions at dusk. They eat almost anything, from waste grain and carrion to insects and the eggs of other birds. A crow making a racket while mobbing something in a treetop is often the quickest way to find a roosting owl or a hawk.",
  "belted-kingfisher": "A stocky blue-grey bird with an oversized head, a shaggy crest and a heavy dagger of a bill. It wears a blue-grey band across a white chest; the female adds a second, rusty band, one of the cases where the female is the brighter bird. Watch for it along rivers, lakes, ponds and streams with clear water, perched on a bare branch over the surface or hovering before it drops head-first after a small fish.\n\nIt gives itself away with a loud, dry rattle as it flies the length of a shoreline. For nesting it needs a steep earthen bank, into which the pair digs a tunnel that can run up to two metres back to the nest chamber. Present spring through fall, most move south when the water freezes, though a few stay near open, fast water in winter. One bird will often patrol and defend a stretch of shoreline as its own.",
  "common-raven": "Bigger than a crow, with a heavy bill, a shaggy throat and a tail that comes to a wedge in flight. It croaks rather than caws, a deep, rolling note, and it soars, rolls and tumbles through the air in a way crows rarely do. Long a bird of forests, cliffs and northern wilderness, it has been spreading south and is now regular around towns and highways, present year-round.\n\nRavens rank among the most intelligent of all birds. They use and manipulate objects to solve problems, and they respond to individual people. They also appear to play, sliding down snowbanks for no obvious purpose. They are wide-ranging feeders and rely heavily on carrion, which is part of why highways and their road-killed animals suit them. Pairs mate for life and hold large territories. To tell one from a crow at distance, the wedge tail and the croak are the surest marks.",
  "killdeer": "It is a shorebird that mostly avoids the shore. Killdeer nest on gravel lots, ballfields, driveways and bare fields, often nowhere near water. The bird itself is a slender brown-and-white plover, and the two black bands across the white breast are the mark to look for. In flight a rusty rump shows. Most of the time you hear one before you see it, a loud ringing 'kill-deer' carrying across open ground.\n\nKilldeer arrive in spring and stay through fall, active by day. They feed on insects and other small invertebrates, running a few steps and stopping in the way plovers do. The nest is a bare scrape on open ground with no cover, which leaves eggs and young exposed. When a predator or a person comes too close, the adult drops a wing and drags it along the ground while calling loudly, pulling the threat away before flying off once the distraction has done its job. The eggs are well camouflaged and easy to step on. Watch your footing on gravel in nesting season.",
  "mourning-dove": "Soft grey-brown and slim, with a long tapered tail edged in white, this is one of the most familiar birds across southern and central Ontario. Small black spots mark the wing. It turns up in open woods, farmland, along roadsides, in towns and at backyard feeders, usually feeding on the ground below.\n\nThe name comes from the call, a low drawn-out cooing that sounds mournful and often gets mistaken for an owl. On takeoff the wings make a sharp whistling sound, a good way to know a dove has flushed even when you missed it leaving. Unlike many songbirds it stays through winter here, so you can meet it in any season. Doves feed heavily on seeds and grain, which is why feeders draw them in numbers. They nest on flimsy platforms of twigs and can raise several broods over a long season. Nothing about the bird is cautionary.",
  "canada-jay": "Go far enough north into spruce and fir country and a grey bird may glide in without a sound to look you over. The Canada jay, long known as the whiskyjack, is fluffy and round-headed with a white face, dark nape and no crest. It is tame and curious around people, and boreal campgrounds are a reliable place to meet one. It is not shy about food.\n\nThis is a year-round northern resident, active by day in every season, deep winter included. It gets through those winters by caching food during the warmer months, gluing bits together with sticky saliva and tucking them under bark and lichen, then living off that scattered larder when little else is available. It is an omnivore and will take almost anything, part of why it approaches campers so readily. Feeding wildlife is generally a poor idea, and that holds here too, even for a bird that makes it this easy.",
  "chimney-swift": "Look up over a town on a summer evening and you may catch a dark, cigar-shaped bird twisting across the sky on stiff, curved wings. This one flies almost without pause, feeding on insects with rapid flickering wingbeats, and its outline in the air has earned it the nickname 'flying cigar.' You rarely see it settled, because it cannot perch on a level surface at all.\n\nInstead it clings upright with tiny feet inside chimneys and other vertical shafts, which is where it roosts and nests. That habit is also its trouble. It is listed as threatened in Ontario, one of several aerial insectivores in decline, and the loss of old open chimneys has cut into the sites it needs. Look for it from spring through fall over southern and central Ontario towns and cities; it leaves before winter. If you have a chimney that swifts use, leaving it uncapped through the breeding season helps. Capping one while birds are inside can trap them.",
  "midland-painted-turtle": "Painted turtles are the ones you see lined up on a log on a warm afternoon, sometimes several to a row. The shell is smooth and dark, 10 to 18 cm long, with red bars along the outer scutes and yellow stripes running back from the head. They live in shallow, slow water with soft bottoms and plenty of logs to climb onto: marshes and quiet bays. You will find them across southern and central Ontario from spring through fall, active by day. They warm up by basking, then drop into the water at any sign of trouble.\n\nOne detail sets them apart. Hatchlings can survive freezing in the nest, tolerating ice in their own tissues through their first winter before digging out in spring. This is Ontario's most familiar turtle and it is not at risk provincially. It is still a specially protected reptile, so leave it where you find it and do not take one home.",
  "snapping-turtle": "A snapping turtle is built like something older than it is. The head is massive, the tail long and saw-toothed, and the plastron underneath is small and cross-shaped, leaving the legs exposed. Shells run 20 to 36 cm and large animals can reach 16 kg. They live in slow lakes, ponds, rivers, and marshes with soft mud and thick plants, spending most of their time on the bottom. In the water they are calm and will move away from you. The danger is on land. Females cross roads to nest in June, and a cornered snapper strikes fast with real force.\n\nThese turtles can live past 100 years and do not breed until 15 to 20, so every adult killed on a road is a loss the population feels for a long time. Snapping Turtle is listed as special concern in Ontario. If you help one across, never lift it by the tail, which can injure its spine. Grasp the rear of the shell, or slide it onto a car mat and drag it the way it was already headed.",
  "blandings-turtle": "The yellow throat is the giveaway. Blanding's Turtle has a bright yellow chin and throat and a high-domed dark shell flecked with yellow, and the set of its jaw gives it a permanent faint smile. Shells run 15 to 27 cm. It lives in shallow weedy wetlands, marshes, and ponds, but it does not stay put. It travels widely overland between wetlands, which is when many are lost on roads. Look for it in southern and central Ontario within the Great Lakes basin, active by day through the warmer months.\n\nThis is a slow-lived animal. Individuals often pass 75 years, and females may not nest for the first time until their late teens, so populations recover slowly from losses. Blanding's Turtle is listed as threatened in Ontario. If you find one crossing a road, move it across in the direction it was going and report the sighting to a provincial reptile atlas. Do not relocate it elsewhere. These turtles know their home wetlands and will try to return.",
  "common-musk-turtle": "Small and secretive, the stinkpot spends its life walking the bottom of shallow, slow water rather than swimming in the open. The shell is high-domed and only 5 to 14 cm long. Two pale stripes run along the face and short barbels hang from the chin. It rarely climbs out to bask the way painted turtles do, so it is easy to overlook. It lives in muddy-bottomed lakes and quiet rivers across southern and central Ontario in the Great Lakes basin, most active at dawn and dusk through the warmer months.\n\nThe name is earned. Handled, it releases a foul musky odour from glands near the shell, enough to make most predators, and most people, put it down. This is a special concern species in Ontario. If you meet one, a quick look is fine, but set it back gently where you found it and expect the smell.",
  "northern-map-turtle": "The shell is where the name comes from. Fine yellow lines wander across the olive carapace like contour lines on a map, and there is a low keel down the middle and a yellow spot behind each eye. Females are much larger than males, reaching 18 to 27 cm against the males' 9 to 16. Map turtles keep to large rivers and lakes with slow current, soft bottoms, and good basking sites. In Ontario that means the Great Lakes and big waterways like the St. Lawrence, Ottawa, and Rideau.\n\nThey are among the wariest turtles you will try to watch. A whole basking group pours off a log into the water at the first hint of movement, so binoculars work better than a close approach. Large females have powerful jaws for crushing snails and clams, which make up much of their diet. Northern Map Turtle is listed as special concern in Ontario. Watch from a distance and let basking animals settle.",
  "red-eared-slider": "This is the turtle from the pet store, now living wild in parts of Ontario. A red stripe behind each eye is the clearest mark, and adults are often larger and paler than the native painted turtles they sit beside. Shells reach 13 to 28 cm. Sliders turn up in warm weedy ponds, urban stormwater ponds, and quiet lake edges, mostly in and around southern Ontario cities, wherever released pets have survived and sometimes bred.\n\nThey are native to the central United States, not here. Every Ontario animal traces back to the pet trade. Where they establish, they can compete with native turtles for the limited basking sites those species depend on. Never release a pet turtle into the wild. It is illegal, and it rarely ends well for the animal. A pet turtle you can no longer keep should go to a rescue or a vet, not a pond.",
  "spiny-softshell": "Nothing else in Ontario looks like a spiny softshell. The shell is flat, round, and leathery, more like a pancake than the hard scutes of other turtles, and the snout draws out into a long snorkel that lets the animal breathe while its body stays hidden in sand. Small spines line the front edge of the carapace. Females are much larger than males and can reach 43 cm. They need large rivers and lakes with soft sand or mud and few obstructions, and in Ontario that comes down to a short list: Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and the Thames and Sydenham rivers.\n\nSoftshells can take up oxygen through the skin and the lining of the throat, which lets them stay under for a very long time. This is an endangered species in Ontario, so a sighting is worth reporting. The long neck reaches farther than you expect and the jaws are sharp. If you must move one off a road, keep clear of the head and handle it as little as possible.",
  "spotted-turtle": "Black shell, yellow polka-dots. Spotted Turtle is small, 8 to 13 cm, its smooth dark shell sprinkled with bright yellow spots that carry onto the head and legs. It lives in shallow, clean wetlands: fens, bogs, marshy meadows, and wet woodlands. This is an early riser. It is one of the first turtles active in spring, sometimes moving over ice, then it goes quiet and estivates through the heat of summer, so spring is realistically your only window to see one. In Ontario it is scattered across the south and east and around Georgian Bay.\n\nSpotted Turtle is endangered here, and illegal collection for the pet trade is one of the main reasons. That fact shapes how you should treat a sighting. Enjoy it, do not handle or move the animal, and do not share the exact location publicly. Reporting to a provincial reptile atlas is the right channel. Taking one is illegal and does real harm to a species already thin on the ground.",
  "wood-turtle": "Look at the shell and it seems carved. Each scute rises in a low pyramid of concentric ridges, giving a worked-wood texture, and there is orange on the neck and legs. Shells run 16 to 25 cm. This is the most terrestrial of Ontario's turtles. It keeps to clean, meandering rivers and streams with sandy bottoms but ranges well into the neighbouring forests and meadows to forage in summer, so you may meet one a fair distance from water. It is active by day through the warmer months.\n\nWood turtles are unusually clever for reptiles and have been shown to work out simple foraging problems. In Ontario they occur along the Great Lakes-St. Lawrence system and in scattered central and eastern rivers. Wood Turtle is endangered here. Clean flowing water and undisturbed nesting banks are what it needs, and adults lost to roads or collection are hard to replace. If you find one, leave it be and report the sighting rather than moving it.",
  "dekays-brownsnake": "One of the few snakes that does well in a city. DeKay's Brownsnake is small, 23 to 33 cm, plain brown with two rows of small dark spots down the back and a pale belly. It lives in the overlooked places, vacant lots, gardens, parks, and woodland edges, often tucked under a board, a rock, or a piece of debris, and several may share one cover object. Its food is earthworms and slugs, which is part of why it manages so well among people. In Ontario it is a southern snake, most common in the urban Carolinian zone, and active from spring through fall.\n\nThis snake is completely harmless. It has no venom and rarely attempts to bite. If you lift a board and find one, have your look and set the cover gently back the way it was so the animal keeps its shelter. It is not at risk in the province.",
  "eastern-gartersnake": "If you have seen a snake in Ontario, it was probably this one. The eastern gartersnake wears three yellow stripes down a dark body, though the background colour varies a great deal from one animal to the next. Most run 45 to 66 cm, with the odd individual reaching much longer. It turns up almost anywhere there is cover and prey: meadows, gardens, forest edges, wetlands, roadsides. This is the most common and widespread snake in the province, out by day from spring through fall.\n\nFemales give birth to live young in late summer rather than laying eggs, and in autumn the snakes gather at communal dens, sometimes in numbers, to spend the winter below the frost. It is harmless. Picked up, it may thrash and smear a musky discharge on your hands, which washes off. There is no venom and no real danger. A gartersnake in the garden is worth leaving alone. It eats slugs, worms, and other small prey.",
  "northern-watersnake": "Seen swimming at a lake edge, this snake gets mistaken for a venomous water moccasin more than any other in Ontario. There is no water moccasin here. The northern watersnake is a harmless native, stout-bodied and 60 to 135 cm, patterned with dark bands and blotches that are clearest toward the head. It lives along the edges of lakes, rivers, ponds, and marshes and basks on rocks, logs, and docks across southern and central Ontario. Given room, it will move off.\n\nCornered, it defends itself in earnest. It flattens its body to look bigger, releases musk, and bites. The saliva carries a mild anticoagulant, so a bite may bleed more than the wound warrants, but there is no venom involved. Do not grab it, and do not try to move it off a dock by hand. Left alone, it will leave on its own. This snake is not at risk in the province.",
  "eastern-milksnake": "A tan-grey snake marked with reddish, black-edged blotches, with a Y or V shape on the neck where it meets the head. You meet it around farmland, fields, rocky outcrops, forest edges, and old buildings, anywhere mice and other small mammals gather. It hunts them by constriction, which makes it worth having near a barn. Active mainly at dawn and dusk through spring, summer, and fall.\n\nThe name traces to an old belief that it drinks milk from cows. It does not. What it does do when alarmed is vibrate its tail in dry leaf litter, and that buzz, together with the blotched pattern, gets it mistaken for a rattlesnake. The comparison does not hold. This is a harmless species, common across southern and central Ontario, and one that is killed far too often out of plain fear.",
  "eastern-ribbonsnake": "Slender even by garter standards, with three bright yellow stripes and a tail that runs to nearly a third of its length. A clean white spot sits just ahead of each eye, and the whole animal looks like a stretched, narrowed gartersnake. Look for it at the edges of marshes, bogs, ponds, and slow streams, in dense shoreline plants, rarely far from water.\n\nIt hunts frogs and small fish and can move across the surface of the water without sinking. Active by day through the warm months. It is listed as a species of special concern in Ontario, and its fortunes are tied to the health of the wetlands it depends on, so shoreline development and drainage work against it. If you find one, watch it and leave it be. It ranges through southern and central Ontario and the Great Lakes basin.",
  "red-bellied-snake": "One of the smallest snakes in Ontario, running from about the length of a pencil to a hand-span. The back is plain brown to grey, but turn one over and the belly is brick red or orange, usually with three pale spots just behind the head. It lives in forests, bogs, and clearings with good ground cover, and spends most of its time out of sight under logs and bark.\n\nSlugs and earthworms are the main food. When picked up it sometimes curls its upper lip to show tiny teeth, a bluff that does no harm at all. It is completely harmless to people. Widespread from southern and central Ontario up to the boreal edge, and active spring through fall, by day or night depending on conditions. Easy to overlook, and easy to set gently aside if you turn one up under a board.",
  "ring-necked-snake": "Secretive and rarely out in the open, this small snake keeps to moist forests and rocky hillsides with flat rocks and logs to hide under. The back is slate grey, broken by a clean yellow-orange ring across the neck, and the belly matches that colour. When alarmed it may coil and lift its tail to flash the bright underside as a warning.\n\nSalamanders, worms, and other small snakes make up the diet. It is most active at night through spring, summer, and fall, which is part of why it goes unnoticed even where it is common. Found across southern and central Ontario along the edge of the Canadian Shield. Harmless. Most people who live near them never see one, and the usual way to turn one up is to lift a rock and then set it back carefully where it was.",
  "smooth-greensnake": "Uniformly bright green above with a white to pale-yellow belly, this slender snake all but vanishes in grass. Moist meadows, grasslands, marsh edges, and open fields with thick low cover are where it lives. It is one of the few snakes that eats insects rather than vertebrates, taking mostly spiders and caterpillars, which ties it closely to healthy, unsprayed grassland.\n\nActive by day through the warm months. It is harmless and rarely bites. One odd note for anyone who finds a dead one: the green pigment breaks down after death and the body fades to blue. Because it depends on insect-rich meadows, pesticide use and heavy mowing hurt it. It ranges across southern and central Ontario.",
  "blue-racer": "Large and fast, with a slender build and smooth grey-blue to blue-green sides, a pale belly, big eyes, and a dark mask through the eye. It is among the longest and quickest snakes in the province. In Canada it now survives in a single place: Pelee Island in Lake Erie. The last reliable mainland record was in 1983.\n\nThere it lives in tallgrass prairie, savanna, alvar, and old fields with scattered rocky cover. It is non-venomous. Its instinct is to flee at speed, and it will bite only if cornered and handled. Endangered, with a tiny range and few animals left. It is protected, and its habitat matters. If you are on Pelee and see one pour off through the grass, that is a piece of the entire Canadian population moving away from you. Give it distance. Active by day, spring through fall.",
  "butlers-gartersnake": "A small gartersnake with three yellow-orange stripes and a head that looks slightly undersized for the body. The side stripes sit higher up the flank than on the common eastern gartersnake, which is one way to tell them apart, though the two are genuinely hard to separate in the field. It is harmless.\n\nAn earthworm specialist, it keeps to tallgrass prairie, wet meadows, and old fields near water, including scrappy urban and industrial edges. In Canada it is restricted to a few pockets of southwestern Ontario, with the main populations near Windsor and Sarnia, at Parkhill, and at Luther Marsh. That narrow range is why it is listed as endangered. Active by day through spring, summer, and fall. The remnant prairie patches it depends on are themselves rare, so protecting the snake means protecting those fields.",
  "eastern-foxsnake": "One of the largest snakes in Ontario, yellowish-brown with dark blotches down the back and a reddish, coppery head. When disturbed it vibrates its tail in dry leaves, a rattlesnake impression that gets it killed by people who take the bluff at face value. It is entirely harmless and a strong swimmer, at home along Great Lakes shoreline marshes, prairies, rocky shores, and nearby farmland, where it earns its keep on rodents.\n\nTwo isolated populations remain in the province: the Carolinian population along the Lake Erie and St. Clair shores, which is endangered, and the eastern Georgian Bay population, which is threatened. Road mortality and habitat loss are the main pressures. Active by day, spring through fall. If you see a large blotched snake buzzing its tail near the water, the correct response is to leave it alone. It cannot hurt you.",
  "eastern-hog-nosed-snake": "Stout-bodied, with an upturned pig-like snout and colour that varies so much from one animal to the next that pattern alone is unreliable. The snout is the giveaway. It lives in sandy or well-drained soils in open woodland, dunes, and beaches where toads, its main prey, are common.\n\nThe display is what people remember. When threatened it flattens its neck like a cobra, hisses, and strikes with a closed mouth. If that fails to move you, it rolls belly-up and plays dead, sometimes with the tongue lolling out. It is harmless to people and not dangerously venomous. Listed as threatened, it turns up in Great Lakes sand areas, around Georgian Bay, in Norfolk, and in eastern Ontario, with roads and habitat loss the chief threats. Active by day through the warm months. The whole performance is a bluff. Watch it, then move off and let it recover.",
  "gray-ratsnake": "The longest snake in Ontario. Adults are glossy black with a pale chin; juveniles are strongly blotched and look like a different animal entirely. It is an excellent climber, and much of its hunting happens off the ground, going up trees to reach bird nests and into burrows for rodents, which it kills by constriction. Deciduous and mixed forests, forest edges, rock ridges, and old barns all suit it.\n\nHarmless to people. Two populations survive in the province: the Frontenac Axis population in eastern Ontario, listed as threatened, and the Carolinian population in the southwest, listed as endangered. Both are hurt by habitat loss and road deaths, and both are slow to recover, because the snakes are long-lived and reproduce slowly. Active by day, spring through fall. A big black snake in a barn is doing you a favour on the rodent front.",
  "lake-erie-watersnake": "A pale relative of the northern watersnake, often nearly uniform grey with its banding faded or gone. It basks on the rocky and cobble shorelines, limestone ledges, and shallows of the Lake Erie islands, and it lives nowhere else. Non-venomous, though like any watersnake it will bite and release musk if grabbed, so there is no reason to grab one.\n\nThis is a genuine recovery story. Once endangered, it rebounded and was downlisted to special concern in Ontario in 2017. Part of that turnaround came from an unexpected food source, the invasive round goby, which now makes up much of its diet. Active by day through the warm months, on Pelee, Middle, East Sister, and neighbouring islands. It is still tied to that small island range, so it remains worth watching, but the trend has been in the right direction.",
  "massasauga": "Ontario's only venomous snake, and a shy one. The body is thick, marked with dark saddle blotches, and the head is triangular with vertical pupils. At the tail is a small segmented rattle that buzzes at a higher pitch than the rattle of a large western rattlesnake. It lives in wetlands, rock barrens, prairies, and shoreline forests, most reliably around eastern Georgian Bay and the Bruce Peninsula. It is nearly gone from the southwest.\n\nIts nature is to rattle and retreat, not to attack. Bites are very rare and almost always happen when someone tries to handle or kill one. Do not do either. Give it room and walk around it. If a bite does happen, stay calm, keep the limb still, and get to medical care immediately; antivenom is available and deaths are extremely rare with prompt treatment. Listed as threatened, it is protected by law. Active spring through fall, by day or night depending on the weather.",
  "queensnake": "The queensnake lives along clean, rocky streams and rivers where riffles run over stone and crayfish are common. To find this snake, find good water first. Look for a slender olive-brown snake, often draped on a branch above the current, with a yellow belly crossed by four dark stripes and a pale stripe low along each side. It rarely strays far from flowing water.\n\nIt depends on crayfish, and on a narrow window in particular. The queensnake feeds on soft-shelled crayfish in the hours just after they moult, before the new shell hardens. That specialization ties it to water quality. Where crayfish decline, so does the snake. In Ontario it holds on in only a few southwestern watersheds: the Grand, Thames, Sydenham, and Ausable. It is listed as Endangered. A warm day from spring through fall is your best chance to see one, basking or working the shallows. It is non-venomous and harmless to people, and should be left alone.",
  "common-five-lined-skink": "Ontario has just one native lizard, and this is it. The common five-lined skink is a smooth, shiny reptile with five pale stripes running the length of a darker body. Young ones carry a bright blue tail, and in spring the breeding males show orange along the jaws. It keeps to rock outcrops, sandy open woodland, and ground littered with logs and flat rocks, where it can warm in the sun and vanish under cover in an instant.\n\nHandle one carelessly and you may be left holding only a tail. The skink can drop its still-wriggling tail to distract a predator, then regrow it slowly. Look for it by day from spring into fall. Two separate populations occur in the province: a Carolinian group in the far southwest, listed as Endangered, and a Southern Shield group around Georgian Bay, listed as Special concern. Both are protected under provincial law. If you lift a rock to check for one, set it back exactly as it lay. That cover is part of the habitat, and the animal depends on it.",
  "american-bullfrog": "This is the biggest frog in Ontario. It grows to around 20 cm and sits heavy in the water, usually with just its eyes and the top of its head showing. The clearest mark is what it lacks: the dorsolateral ridges, the folds of skin running down the back, that most of our frogs have. Instead a short ridge curls around the very large eardrum behind each eye. The call carries a long way, a deep resonant \"jug-o-rum\" from warm, permanent ponds, marshes, and slow rivers thick with vegetation. You meet it in summer, mostly in southern Ontario and around the lower Great Lakes.\n\nBullfrogs are ambush hunters and will take almost anything they can swallow, including other frogs. Development is slow in the north. Tadpoles can spend two or three years in the water before they finish changing into frogs, so a single pond may hold several year-classes at once. Not at risk.",
  "american-toad": "Warty and brown, this common toad turns up in gardens, forests, and fields across most of the province. The best field mark sits behind the eyes: kidney-shaped parotoid glands that stay separate from the crests on the head and never quite touch them. That small detail separates it from Fowler's toad. The spring breeding call is a long, musical trill that can run up to 30 seconds from shallow ponds and ditches.\n\nToads are patient, mostly nocturnal hunters. A single adult can eat thousands of insects, slugs, and worms over a season, which makes one worth having around a garden. They are long-lived for a small animal, sometimes past 10 years. Handle one and you will find out why most predators leave it alone. The parotoid glands release a secretion that irritates eyes and mouth. It will not hurt you through intact skin, but wash your hands after holding a toad and keep your fingers away from your face. Not at risk.",
  "gray-treefrog": "Not colour but climbing gear marks this frog. Large sticky toe pads let it work high into trees and shrubs, where it spends most of its time out of sight. The skin is warty and shifts between grey and green with temperature, activity, and background, so colour alone will not identify it. The giveaway is a flash of bright yellow-orange on the hidden inner thighs when it jumps. The voice is a loud, short, flute-like trill from wooded areas near water.\n\nYou hear far more of these than you see, mostly on spring and summer nights in southern and central Ontario. Cold does not stop it the way it stops most frogs. It floods its tissues with glycerol and tolerates partial freezing, which lets it overwinter under leaf litter and bark. Not at risk.",
  "green-frog": "The banjo player of the marsh. Its call is a single loud \"gunk\" with a plucked, twangy quality, often repeated a few times. Green to bronze on top, it is most easily told from the bullfrog by its dorsolateral ridges, the folds of skin that run down the back but stop short of the groin. The bullfrog has none at all. Size overlaps between the two, so the ridges are the reliable mark.\n\nYou find it in permanent ponds, streams, and the vegetated shallows of lakes through southern and central Ontario, spring into summer. Males hold calling territories and will square off with rivals. Startle one at the water's edge and it gives a sharp squawk as it leaps, a good clue to what just jumped before you got a proper look. Not at risk.",
  "northern-leopard-frog": "Green or brown and covered in rounded dark spots, each ringed with a paler halo. Two prominent light dorsolateral ridges run down the back. This is a frog of open, wet ground, at home in wet meadows, marshes, and the grassy edges of ponds and slow streams, where it hunts by day and often sits well out in the grass away from water. The call is a low snore followed by a few chuckling grunts, easy to miss in a busy spring chorus.\n\nYou can meet it across much of the province from spring through fall. It was once one of the most abundant frogs on the continent, collected in huge numbers for the frog-leg trade and for classroom dissection. Numbers have risen and fallen since, but in Ontario it remains widespread and is not considered at risk.",
  "spring-peeper": "The voice is out of all proportion to the animal. A tiny tan-to-brown frog barely longer than a thumbnail, it is one of the first to call each year, often while nights are still cold. A single peep is a high, clear, rising note. A full chorus of hundreds can top 90 decibels and carry a long way from woodland ponds, marshes, and wet meadows near forest.\n\nSeeing one is harder than hearing it. Look for the dark X-shaped mark on the back that gives the species its name, crucifer, and the small toe pads that let it climb low vegetation. It calls on spring and summer nights and is widespread across Ontario. Once breeding is done it moves into the woods and mostly falls silent, spending the rest of the year in leaf litter hunting small insects. Not at risk.",
  "wood-frog": "Among the first frogs to appear each spring, sometimes over snow, this one heads for temporary woodland pools while they are still cold. It is tan to reddish-brown with a dark \"robber's mask\" running back from each eye, the clearest field mark. The breeding chorus is soft and clucking, more like a group of ducks than a typical frog.\n\nIts cold tolerance is hard to beat. It can freeze solid over winter, heart stopped, then thaw out alive in spring, using glucose in its tissues as an antifreeze. That trick lets it live farther north than any other amphibian in North America, and it is found across Ontario including the far north. Breeding is fast, tied to the brief life of the meltwater pools it uses. After the pools dry, adults spend the rest of the year in moist deciduous and mixed woodland, away from open water. Not at risk.",
  "boreal-chorus-frog": "In the northwest corner of Ontario this small grey-brown frog replaces the very similar western chorus frog. Three broken dark stripes run down the back. By sight the two are nearly impossible to tell apart, so range does most of the work: this is the chorus frog you meet around the Rainy River and Kenora districts. The call is a slow, rising \"crreek,\" like a fingernail dragged over the teeth of a comb.\n\nIt lives in marshes, wet meadows, roadside ditches, and pond edges, and it breeds early. It ranges about as far north as any frog on the continent and may start calling while ice still rings the breeding pond. You hear it through spring and into summer. Not at risk.",
  "mink-frog": "A frog of cool boreal water, easy to mistake for a green frog until you look at the legs or pick one up. The hind legs carry irregular dark blotches rather than the clean crossbands of the green frog. Handle one and the reason for the name is obvious: the skin gives off a musky smell often compared to mink. The call is a rapid, hollow \"cut-cut-cut.\"\n\nIt stays close to lakes, bogs, and slow streams where lily pads are thick, often sitting on the pads themselves. This is a northern species, at home in boreal Ontario and scarce or absent in the south. You meet it mainly in summer. It spends much of its life in or right at the water and does not wander far from it. Not at risk.",
  "pickerel-frog": "Two neat parallel rows of squarish dark spots run down the back, set between the ridges. That squared-off, ranked pattern separates it from the leopard frog, whose spots are rounded and scattered. A flash of bright yellow-orange on the hidden inner thighs shows when it jumps. The call is a low, steady snore, often given underwater and easy to overlook. It favours cool clear streams, springs, and bog margins in southern and central Ontario, and sometimes turns up around cave entrances.\n\nThis one is worth handling with care, mostly for the sake of other animals. Its skin secretions are irritating and can be toxic to other amphibians confined with it, so never bag it alongside other frogs. Those same secretions probably help it deter predators. For people the risk is minor, but wash your hands after handling and keep them away from your eyes. Not at risk.",
  "western-chorus-frog": "A small grey-brown frog with three dark stripes down the back and a pale line along the upper lip. You are far more likely to hear it than see it. The call sounds like a finger dragged slowly over the teeth of a comb, rising as it goes, from grassy marshes, wet meadows, and seasonally flooded fields. It calls early, mostly in spring, in eastern and southern Ontario.\n\nThe Great Lakes / St. Lawrence Lowlands population has dropped sharply, largely through the loss and drainage of the shallow, temporary wetlands it breeds in. It is federally listed as Threatened. The most useful thing anyone can do is leave those wet meadows and flooded fields intact and undrained rather than disturb the frogs. By sight it cannot be reliably separated from the boreal chorus frog; in this part of the province, range points to this species.",
  "fowlers-toad": "In Canada this toad hangs on only along the north shore of Lake Erie, at places like Long Point and Rondeau. It looks much like an American toad, and telling them apart takes a close look at two features. The parotoid glands behind the eyes touch the crests on the head, and each dark blotch on the back holds three or more warts. The call is a short, nasal, unmusical \"waaah,\" nothing like the American toad's long trill. It is active on spring and summer nights and spends the day burrowed into loose sand.\n\nIt lives on sandy beaches, dunes, and open shoreline, habitat that is limited and heavily used by people. The species is Endangered and legally protected. Do not disturb, handle, or collect it, and give calling and breeding areas a wide berth. Its skin secretions are irritating like other toads', but the real reason to keep your hands off is its protected status and how few are left.",
  "eastern-newt": "This species wears two very different looks across its life. As an aquatic adult it is olive-green with a keeled tail; the juvenile stage, the red eft, is bright orange with a row of red spots ringed in black and dry, faintly rough skin. Efts leave the water after the larval stage and wander the moist forest floor for two to seven years before returning to water to mature. You can meet them by day across central and southern Ontario on the Canadian Shield, often out in the open after rain when other salamanders stay hidden. The bold colour is a warning. Skin toxins make the eft distasteful to predators, and those toxins are harmful if swallowed, so wash your hands after any handling and keep them clear of your mouth and eyes.",
  "eastern-red-backed-salamander": "Lift a damp log in deciduous or mixed forest and this is the salamander you are most likely to find under it. It is slender and lungless, breathing through its skin and mouth lining, so it stays where the ground holds moisture. Look for a straight red-orange stripe down the back, though a plain grey 'lead-backed' form is common too, with a mottled salt-and-pepper belly on both. There is no aquatic stage. Eggs are laid on land, often inside a rotting log, and the female curls around them until they hatch as tiny versions of the adult. It is among the most abundant forest vertebrates in eastern North America. It is small and nocturnal, so it is easy to overlook. If you turn a log to look, set it back gently.",
  "blue-spotted-salamander": "Slim and nearly black, this salamander is sprinkled with pale blue flecks along the sides, limbs, and tail. It breeds in spring in vernal pools set in deciduous and mixed forest, and it ranges widely across central and southern Ontario. Most of the year it stays underground or under cover and is rarely seen. The best chance to find one is on the first warm rainy nights of spring, when adults move to water.\n\nThe genetics are unusual. This is one member of a unisexual complex, where all-female polyploid lineages produce eggs but borrow sperm from related Ambystoma species to start development. The result is a tangle of pure and hybrid animals that can be hard to tell apart in the field. For a walker the practical point is simpler. A small dark salamander with blue speckling near a woodland pool in early spring is very likely this one.",
  "mudpuppy": "Large and fully aquatic, this salamander keeps its larval form for life. It never loses its bushy red external gills, which flare when the water is low in oxygen, and it has four toes on each foot. It lives year-round in lakes, rivers, and streams across the Great Lakes and their connecting waters, sheltering under rocks and debris and hunting at night.\n\nWinter anglers sometimes hook one through the ice and are alarmed by it. There is no reason to be. Despite old folklore, it is not venomous and it is completely harmless. If you catch one, unhook it gently and return it to the water unharmed. Mudpuppies are sensitive to pollution, so their presence indicates good water quality.",
  "northern-two-lined-salamander": "Cool running water is where you meet this one. It is slender and yellowish, with two dark lines running back from the eyes toward the tail, and it lives along rocky streams, seeps, and springs in forest across eastern and central Ontario. It seldom strays far from moving water, hiding by day under stones at the edge and emerging at night.\n\nLike other lungless salamanders it has a defence worth knowing about. When a predator grabs its tail, the tail can break off and keep twitching while the salamander slips away, then regrow over time. If you turn stones at a streambank to look for one, set them back as you found them so the small animals sheltering underneath keep their cover.",
  "spotted-salamander": "Two irregular rows of round yellow spots, often orange toward the head, mark this large, glossy-black salamander. It is stout and can grow longer than a hand. For most of the year it lives underground in mature forest and is almost never seen. The exception is a few nights in early spring, when adults travel to fishless vernal pools to breed, sometimes in numbers on the first warm rains.\n\nThe egg masses hold a small surprise. A green alga lives inside the developing embryos in a genuine symbiosis, the alga gaining nutrients and the embryos gaining oxygen from its photosynthesis. It is one of the few known cases of an alga living within a vertebrate's cells. You will find this salamander in southern and central Ontario, most reliably at a breeding pool on a wet night in early spring.",
  "allegheny-mountain-dusky-salamander": "In Canada this salamander lives in one place only. Its entire national range is a single cascading stream in the Niagara Gorge, a Carolinian population that likely holds fewer than 100 adults. That makes it Endangered and legally protected. Do not disturb the habitat or handle the animals.\n\nIt is a slender lungless salamander of spray zones, seeps, and mossy rock faces beside cool forested streams. Look for a straight-edged dorsal band, often with a wavy border, and a pale line from the eye to the jaw. The tail is rounded in cross-section, which helps separate it from the Northern Dusky Salamander that shares the gorge and carries a keeled, flattened tail. Given how few remain and how small the range is, the right thing is to watch from the trail and leave everything as you found it.",
  "four-toed-salamander": "The belly gives this small salamander away. It is enamel-white, boldly marked with black spots, unlike anything else you are likely to turn up in an Ontario bog. Two other marks help: four toes on each hind foot, and a distinct constriction at the base of the tail, where the tail breaks off cleanly if a predator seizes it. It lives in sphagnum bogs and swampy woods with mossy pool margins, scattered across southern and central Ontario.\n\nNesting is often communal. Females lay their eggs together in moss overhanging water, and one clutch may be tended by several females. When the larvae hatch they wriggle free and drop into the pool below to finish growing. It is nocturnal and easy to miss, so most people find it by carefully checking moss at a pool edge and then putting everything back.",
  "jefferson-salamander": "Among the earliest amphibians to move each spring, this salamander migrates to its breeding pools on the first warm rainy nights, sometimes while ice still lingers. That timing puts it on roads before most people are thinking about salamanders, and road crossings are one of the threats it faces. It is Endangered in Ontario and legally protected, so it should not be disturbed or handled.\n\nIt is slender and dark grey-brown, with long toes and faint bluish flecks low on the sides when young. It breeds in fishless vernal pools in upland deciduous forest and spends the rest of the year underground. In Canada it is limited to the Carolinian far south, around Niagara, Hamilton, and Halton. It also overlaps with the unisexual Ambystoma complex, which can make individual animals hard to identify with confidence. If you find one near a pool, keep your distance and let it carry on.",
  "northern-dusky-salamander": "Like its close relative in the same gorge, this salamander survives in Canada at a single stream in the Niagara Gorge, where the Carolinian population numbers only a handful of animals. It is Endangered and protected. The habitat is fragile and the numbers are tiny, so do not turn rocks or handle anything.\n\nIt is a stout lungless salamander of cool rocky seepage streams and springs in shaded ravines. The marks to check are a pale diagonal line from the eye to the angle of the jaw and a keeled, laterally flattened tail. Its hind legs are noticeably stouter than the front, and a disturbed animal can jump well to escape. That keeled tail sets it apart from the Allegheny Mountain Dusky Salamander, which has a rounded tail and shares the same gorge. For a visitor there is nothing to do but look from the path and leave the stream undisturbed.",
  "small-mouthed-salamander": "In Canada you will find this salamander in exactly one place: Pelee Island. It is Endangered and protected, and the whole national range is that single island, so there is no room for disturbance. It lives in moist woodland and fields near the seasonal ponds where it breeds in spring.\n\nThe name fits. The head is notably small and the snout short and blunt, set on a dark body flecked with greyish, lichen-like markings. Those proportions are the easiest way to tell it from other mole salamanders. As with several relatives, it takes part in the Ambystoma unisexual complex here, where all-female lineages use sperm from related species, so some animals are hybrids rather than pure Small-mouthed Salamanders. If you are on Pelee Island near a breeding pond on a wet spring night, watch your step and leave any salamander where it is.",
  "white-perch": "This is a fish that does not belong here. It arrived in the Great Lakes through the canal system and has settled into the warm open water, harbours, and sheltered bays of the lower lakes. Look for a deep, silvery to greenish body with no clear horizontal stripes. That plain flank separates it from the White Bass, which carries thin dark lines along its sides. The two dorsal fins are joined at the base rather than standing apart. You will meet it from spring through fall around Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, and connecting waters like the Bay of Quinte.\n\nThe trouble it causes is real. It eats the eggs of walleye and other fish and competes with native panfish for food and space. Anglers catch plenty of them, but the fish itself is a marker of a changed system, not a welcome addition. Handle it with care. The dorsal spines are sharp and will jab a careless hand.",
  "white-bass": "Roving schools near the surface of warm lakes give this one away. It moves in large groups through open water and big bays, and in spring it runs up rivers to spawn. The body is deep and silvery, marked with several thin dark stripes running lengthwise, one of them carrying all the way to the tail. The lower jaw juts forward, and the two dorsal fins stand clearly apart. Those stripes and separated fins tell it from the White Perch, which is plain-sided with joined dorsals.\n\nIn Ontario it is most at home in the warm lower Great Lakes, Lake St. Clair, Lake Nipissing, and the Bay of Quinte. Spring is the season most anglers know it, when the spawning run concentrates fish in the rivers, but it stays catchable through summer and fall. Watch your hands. The dorsal fin and the gill covers both carry sharp spines.",
  "black-bullhead": "Few fish tolerate the water this one lives in. It holds out in warm, turbid ponds, backwaters, and sluggish streams over soft mud, in places where low oxygen would drive most fish out. The body is dark and chunky, a small catfish with dark chin barbels and a nearly square tail. A pale crescent at the base of the tail is the mark to look for, along with a grey base to the anal fin. That pale bar where the tail meets the body is the quickest field check.\n\nYou will find it across southern and western Ontario, in the Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and Lake of the Woods drainages, from spring into fall. It is a bottom feeder that works muddy water by touch and smell. Handle it carefully when you unhook one. The pectoral and dorsal spines are sharp and can drive into a hand that grips too quickly.",
  "green-sunfish": "An oversized mouth sets this sunfish apart from its relatives. The body is stout, blue-green on the sides and flecked with pale spots, with yellow-orange edges to the fins and a black ear flap rimmed in pale. That large mouth lets it take bigger prey than most sunfish, and it shrugs off warm, low-oxygen water that keeps other fish out. It lives in weedy ponds, ditches, and slow creeks.\n\nIts range in southern Ontario is growing, mainly through the Lake Erie and Lake St. Clair drainages. It moves into water other sunfish cannot use, and it can crowd small waters and out-compete native panfish. You will catch it from spring through fall. The gill covers and fins carry sharp spines, so mind your grip when you handle one.",
  "white-crappie": "Murky water is where this crappie has the edge. It handles more turbid conditions than the Black Crappie and holds in warm, sheltered bays, creek mouths, and slow rivers over sand or mud. The body is silvery, marked with faint vertical bars rather than the scattered speckles of its relative. The surest check is the dorsal spines. This one has six, against the seven or eight of the Black Crappie, so a careful count settles which fish is in hand.\n\nIn Ontario it keeps to the warm lower Great Lakes: western Lake Ontario, Lake Erie, Lake St. Clair, and southern Lake Huron. It is a schooling panfish, most active around cover, and you will find it from spring through fall. Nothing about it needs special caution beyond the usual care with a spiny-finned fish.",
  "yellow-bullhead": "The chin gives this catfish away. Its barbels are pale, white or nearly so, while the Brown and Black bullheads both carry dark ones. The body is yellow-brown with a rounded tail. Where the ranges overlap, checking barbel colour is the fastest way to be sure which bullhead you have caught.\n\nIt keeps to warm, weedy, slow water - ponds, marshes, and sluggish creeks with soft bottoms - in southern Ontario, through the Lake Erie and Lake Ontario drainages. Like other bullheads it feeds along the bottom and is active from spring into fall. There is a bit of parenting behind the plain look. After the eggs hatch, the adults stay with the young, guarding the tight school of dark fry until they grow. Handle it with care when unhooking. The pectoral and dorsal spines are sharp and will jab.",
  "northern-sunfish": "This is a fish to admire and leave alone. It is small and colourful, with wavy blue lines across the cheek and a long black ear flap tipped in white or orange. It lives in clear, weedy bays and slow streams with clean substrate, and in Ontario it holds on in only a few waters of the southwest, in parts of the Lake St. Clair, Lake Erie, and Lake Huron drainages.\n\nIt carries the status of special concern under both SARO and COSEWIC, which reflects its narrow range and its dependence on clean, clear water. Anything that clouds or degrades those streams works against it. For years it was treated as the northern form of the Longear Sunfish before being recognized on its own. If you encounter one, note it and let it be. A fish this restricted does not have room to spare.",
  "bloater": "Down in the cold and dark is where this cisco lives. It is a small, silvery deepwater chub with large eyes and a lower jaw that projects past the upper. It holds far below the surface in the deep offshore water of Lake Superior and Lake Nipigon, deeper than the Cisco, or Lake Herring, that it resembles. Because it stays in cold water year round, it is present through all four seasons, though rarely seen by anyone but commercial and research nets.\n\nIt was lost from Lake Ontario, and a reintroduction effort is now under way to bring it back, part of an attempt to rebuild the offshore food web that native trout and salmon depend on. As a forage fish it feeds on plankton and small invertebrates in the depths. It is not one you will catch on a line, but it matters to the systems that hold the fish you do.",
  "pink-salmon": "An accident put this salmon in the Great Lakes. A small release near Thunder Bay in 1956 seeded a population that now reproduces entirely on its own, with no stocking behind it. It is the smallest of the Pacific salmon in these waters, silvery, with large oval spots on the back and across both lobes of the tail. Spawning males develop a pronounced humped back, which is why the species is also called humpback salmon.\n\nMost run on a strict two-year cycle, returning to the tributary streams to spawn in fall after growing in the cool open water of the lakes. It is most common in Lake Superior and Lake Huron and their spawning rivers, and you are most likely to encounter it in summer and fall. It is a naturalized introduction, established and self-sustaining, but not part of the original Great Lakes fauna.",
  "round-whitefish": "Shape is the thing to check with this whitefish. The body is slender and nearly cylindrical, rounder in cross-section than the deep-bodied Lake Whitefish, with a small mouth set beneath a pointed snout. One more mark settles it: a single flap of skin between the nostrils, where the Lake Whitefish has two. It lives in cold, deep water of large lakes and clear rivers.\n\nIn Ontario it turns up in northern lakes and in all the Great Lakes except Lake Erie, whose warmer water does not suit it. It feeds along the bottom on insects, small invertebrates, and fish eggs. Spawning comes in late fall over gravel in shallow water, which is part of why it shows up in spring, fall, and winter records more than in the heat of summer. It is not a fish most anglers target, but it is a clean indicator of the cold, well-oxygenated water it needs.",
  "splake": "This trout came out of a hatchery, not a wild spawning bed. It is a cross of a male Brook Trout and a female Lake Trout, and it looks the part, carrying the wormlike markings of a Brook Trout on its back but a moderately forked tail closer to a Lake Trout's. The blend sits between its two parents in most features.\n\nOntario stocks it into cold, deep inland lakes because it grows quickly and rarely reproduces in the wild, which lets managers put a catchable fish into a lake without it taking over. You will find it in stocked lakes across central and northern Ontario and parts of Georgian Bay, and it stays active through all four seasons, including under the ice. It behaves much like its parent species, holding in cold water and taking baitfish and insects. Treat it as a managed fishery. Its numbers depend on continued stocking.",
  "aurora-trout": "Few fish have come this close to being lost. Native to only two lakes, Whirligig and Whitepine, north of Sudbury, it was wiped out in the wild by acid rain by the late 1960s. It survives today through a captive breeding and reintroduction program, and it is listed as endangered under both SARO and COSEWIC.\n\nIt is a colour form of Brook Trout, but a distinctive one. It lacks the yellow spots and wormlike back markings of a typical brook trout, showing plain sides with red spots reduced or absent. Breeding males take on a deep orange-red belly. It lives in a handful of cold, acid-sensitive lakes on the highlands, water that careful management works to keep habitable. You may find it now in the Lady Evelyn-Smoothwater area and a few designated stocked lakes. Its recovery is real but not secure, tied to lakes that stay vulnerable to acidification.",
  "shortjaw-cisco": "The deepwater ciscoes are a difficult group, and this one takes a close look to name. Its lower jaw sits about even with the upper, or a little shorter, where most ciscoes have a jaw that projects. Counting gill rakers helps confirm it and separates it from the similar chubs it lives among. This is a fish of deep, cold water in large northern lakes. In Ontario it now holds on in Lake Superior and Lake Nipigon, and it is gone from Lakes Huron and Erie where it once occurred. Overfishing and the arrival of invasive species pushed it out of much of its former range, and it is listed as Threatened by both SARO and COSEWIC.\n\nBecause the deepwater ciscoes look so alike and have shifted so much under fishing pressure, even biologists have argued over how to define this one. You are unlikely to meet it without a net set in deep water.",
  "alewife": "A silvery herring of open water, the alewife travels the Great Lakes in mid-water schools. It is deep-bodied and flattened side to side, with a large eye, a belly edged like a saw, and a deeply forked tail. A single dark spot sits just behind the gill cover.\n\nIt came from the Atlantic and spread through the upper Great Lakes, where it has done real damage. Alewife eat the eggs and larvae of native fish, and their arrival reshaped the food web. In some years huge numbers have died off in late spring and washed up along beaches in windrows. It is not a fish anyone sets out to catch. It matters mostly as forage for stocked salmon and trout, and as a problem, and it turns up dead on shorelines often enough that most lakeshore residents know it by sight.",
  "blacknose-dace": "In small cold creeks across southern and central Ontario, this is often the minnow you catch first and most. It is a little brown fish with a dark band that runs from the snout, through the eye, and back to the base of the tail. The snout is pointed and overhangs a small mouth, and there is a tiny barbel at the corner. In spring the males take on orange tints along the lower body and fins.\n\nIt holds in the faster, gravel-bottomed water of headwaters and riffles, where it feeds on drifting insects and bottom life. Common and hardy, it is a familiar catch in a dip net or minnow trap. Its abundance makes it an important food for larger stream fish.",
  "brook-stickleback": "The brook stickleback comes armed. Along its back, ahead of the dorsal fin, stands a row of about five short spines set separately from one another. The body is scaleless and olive to nearly black, and the tail base is very slender. It lives in weedy, cool water: ponds, ditches, marshes, and slow streams.\n\nThe nesting is unusual for such a small fish. In spring the male builds a small barrel-shaped structure out of plant fragments, binding them with a sticky secretion produced by his kidneys. He then guards the eggs and the newly hatched young against anything that comes near. It is too small to interest anglers except as occasional bait, but it is easy to find and easy to recognize once you learn the spines. Handle it gently if you catch one, since the spines lock upright.",
  "central-mudminnow": "Few small fish tolerate bad water as well as the central mudminnow. It is a stubby, dark brown fish with a rounded tail and a dark vertical bar just before the tail. The single soft dorsal fin sits well back on the body. Look for it over mucky bottoms in weedy, oxygen-poor ponds, ditches, marshes, and slow streams, places where most fish cannot live.\n\nIt gets by there with two tricks. It can gulp air at the surface and hold it in its swim bladder, and it burrows down into soft mud when conditions turn against it. That lets it ride out stagnant, low-oxygen water. It is widespread across southern and central Ontario. You will most likely turn it up in a dip net through weeds rather than on a line.",
  "common-shiner": "A deep-bodied silvery minnow of clear streams, the common shiner shows large scales on its sides that stand out plainly and look taller than they are wide. That scale pattern is the quickest way to name it. It lives in gravel-bottomed streams and small rivers, and along the edges of the lakes those streams feed.\n\nIn late spring the males come into colour, with rosy fins and small hard bumps, called tubercles, scattered over the head. They spawn on gravel, sometimes over nests that other stream minnows build. It is one of the more common and easily caught minnows in southern and central Ontario, often taken in bait nets. At a good size it makes a lively baitfish. Out of the water the pink of a breeding male fades quickly, so the colour is best seen in a clear jar or in the shallows.",
  "creek-chub": "Fish almost any Ontario creek and the creek chub will be part of the catch. It is a stout minnow with a large mouth and a dark spot at the front base of the dorsal fin. A small flap-like barbel hides in the groove above the upper lip, which takes a close look to see. Young fish carry a dark stripe down the side and a spot at the base of the tail.\n\nIt holds in creeks and small streams with a mix of pools and gravel riffles. The spawning behaviour stands out. The male builds a low ridge of gravel by picking up stones in his mouth and carrying them one at a time, then guards the nest. Big ones take a hook readily and are sometimes kept for the pan or used as bait. In small water it is often the largest minnow around.",
  "emerald-shiner": "Out over open water in the Great Lakes and large rivers, big schools of this slender minnow feed near the surface. It is translucent and silvery with a faint green sheen along the back, which gives it its name. The eye is large and the mouth opens at the front of a pointed snout. A fairly long anal fin and that slim shape set it apart from the stubbier minnows of the same waters.\n\nIt matters far out of proportion to its size. Emerald shiner are a main forage fish in the Great Lakes, and their surface schools feed walleye, perch, and other predators. Anglers know them as bait and as the thing gulls and larger fish are working when the surface boils. Individually plain and easy to overlook, they are one of the fish that keep the open-water food web running.",
  "fathead-minnow": "If you have bought minnows in Ontario, some were probably fatheads. It is a small, chunky, dull olive fish with a short rounded snout and a dark blotch at the front of the dorsal fin. The lateral line does not run the full length of the body. Breeding males change a good deal. They darken, grow a spongy grey pad across the nape, and roughen the snout with rows of small tubercles.\n\nIt lives in ponds, ditches, and small turbid streams, and puts up with warm, low-oxygen water that would kill most fish. That toughness is why it is one of the standard live baits, sold as the 'tuffy' and, in an orange domestic form, the 'rosy red'. It is raised on fish farms for the trade. In the wild it is widespread and abundant, often one of the last fish holding on in a marginal pond.",
  "gizzard-shad": "Larger than most fish in this group, the gizzard shad is a deep silvery herring of warm, often murky lakes and slow rivers, where it gathers in big schools. The belly is edged like a saw, and a dark shoulder spot sits behind the head. The snout is blunt and overhangs a small mouth set low, and the last ray of the dorsal fin draws out into a long trailing thread. It feeds by filtering plankton and detritus, which it grinds in a muscular, gizzard-like stomach that gives it its name.\n\nIn the lower Great Lakes, Erie, Ontario, and the St. Clair system, it is abundant. Cold is its weakness. Hard winters kill it in large numbers, and dead shad wash up along shorelines in late winter and spring. It is little use to anglers directly, but it is important forage for walleye and other predators.",
  "golden-shiner": "A deep, slab-sided minnow with a golden-bronze cast, the golden shiner is a fish of quiet, weedy water: lakes, ponds, and slow streams. Two features name it. The lateral line curves strongly downward along the lower side rather than running straight, and a scaleless fleshy keel runs along the belly behind the pelvic fins, like the bottom edge of a knife. It has no barbels.\n\nColour ranges from silvery in young fish to a clear brassy gold in adults from tea-stained water. Most run 8 to 18 cm, though some grow larger. It is among the most widely used baitfish in the province and is raised in bait ponds for the trade, which is part of why it turns up in so many waters. In the wild it schools among weeds and feeds on plankton and small invertebrates, and it is common prey for bass, pike, and other predators.",
  "johnny-darter": "On sand and gravel in the shallows, the johnny darter spends its life on the bottom, propped up on its spread pelvic fins between short darting moves. It is small and slender, pale tan, and marked along the side with a scatter of dark spots shaped like W's and X's. It has two separate dorsal fins. Unlike some of its relatives, it wears no bright colour, even in the breeding season.\n\nWatch a stream margin or a clear lake shallow and you may see it hold still, then dart a body length to snap up a small insect, then hold again. When it spawns, the male tends the eggs on the underside of a stone and guards them until they hatch. It is common across southern and central Ontario and easy to overlook. A patient look into clear, shallow water is the usual way to meet it.",
  "logperch": "Largest of Ontario's darters, this is a slender, cigar-shaped fish marked with many narrow dark bars down a yellow-green body. The snout is conical and blunt, almost pig-like, and there are two dorsal fins set close together. It works sand and gravel bottoms in lakes and larger streams, and it earns part of its living by flipping small stones with that snout to reach the insects and other prey sheltering underneath. Look for it over clean bottoms across southern and central Ontario, spring through fall.\n\nLike other darters it lives on the bottom rather than in open water, so you are more likely to see one while wading or snorkelling a clear stream than while fishing for it. It is not at risk. Nothing about it bites or stings, and at this size it is best watched and left where you find it.",
  "longnose-dace": "Fast water suits this small minnow. Streamlined and bottom-hugging, it holds position in riffles and rocky runs where the current is too strong for most fish to stay put. The long fleshy snout overhangs an underslung mouth, and there is a small barbel at the corner. Adults are fairly plain and lack the bold dark side stripe that marks the similar blacknose dace, which is one useful way to tell the two apart.\n\nBesides stream riffles, it turns up along wave-washed rocky lake shores, another place with steady water movement over rock. It ranges widely across Ontario in cold, clean water. You meet it spring through fall, usually by turning stones in a riffle rather than by casting to it. Not at risk, and harmless to handle, though like any small fish it is easily stressed and best returned quickly.",
  "longnose-sucker": "A cold-water sucker of central and northern Ontario, this one ranges farther north than the more familiar white sucker. The body is long and round, with a fleshy snout that overhangs a downward-facing sucker mouth, lips covered in small bumps, and fine scales that crowd together toward the head. It tends to run darker than a white sucker. In spring, spawning fish develop a rosy stripe along the side, which is the easiest time to notice one.\n\nIt lives in cold, clear lakes and streams and feeds along the bottom, drawing in small invertebrates and other material through those thick lips. Look for it spring through fall in the colder waters of the north. Not at risk. Suckers are edible but bony, and telling exactly which sucker you have is a fussy business best done with a good key rather than a glance.",
  "mottled-sculpin": "Broad-headed and tadpole-shaped, this little bottom fish presses itself against the rocks of cold, clear streams and rocky lake shallows. It has no scales, large fan-like pectoral fins that help it hold to the bottom, and two dorsal fins. The colour is a mottled brown with dark saddles across the back, good camouflage on a gravel bottom.\n\nWhere you find sculpins, the water is usually clean and well oxygenated, which is why they are taken as a good sign in trout streams. They share that cold water with trout and belong to the same community. You will not catch one on a line; look instead among stones in a clear riffle, spring through fall. It is not at risk. Handle it gently if at all, since there is a small sharp spine on the gill cover that can prick a careless hand.",
  "rainbow-smelt": "Slender and silvery, with an iridescent wash of purple, green and silver, this small open-water fish carries a trout-like adipose fin and a surprisingly large, toothy mouth for its size. A fresh one smells distinctly of cucumber, which is a reliable field mark. It lives in the cold open water of the Great Lakes and many inland lakes, coming inshore and running up streams to spawn in early spring.\n\nThat spring run is when most people meet it, dipping nets by lantern light for a fish that gathers in large numbers. It was introduced to the upper Great Lakes basin in the early 1900s and spread widely from there. Fishing for it is spring and winter work, through the ice or at the spawning streams. It is not at risk, being an introduced species that is now well established. Smelt are a traditional pan fish, small enough to eat whole after cleaning.",
  "shorthead-redhorse": "Redhorse suckers are hard to tell apart, and the tail is what settles this one. A silvery, large-scaled sucker with a notably small head, the shorthead carries a bright red or orange tail fin that stands out against the rest of the body. Up close, the lower lip has fine grooves and a rear edge that runs nearly straight across, another feature that separates it from its relatives.\n\nIt lives in clean lakes and in moderate to fast rivers, feeding along gravel and sand bottoms. It is one of the more common redhorse in Ontario rivers, found across the southern and central parts of the province from spring through fall. Not at risk. Like other suckers it is edible but full of fine bones, and telling redhorse species apart takes a careful look at lips and fins rather than a first impression.",
  "trout-perch": "The name records an oddity. This small fish carries a soft, trout-like adipose fin on its back and, at the same time, weak spines in its fins of the sort you expect from a perch, a combination that once puzzled the people who named it. The body is semi-translucent with rows of dark spots along the back and side, a large blunt head, and a tapering rear.\n\nIt lives over sand and gravel bottoms in lakes and larger rivers, keeping to deep water by day and moving into the shallows to feed after dark. That night habit means it usually goes unseen. It ranges across Ontario and is active spring through fall. Not at risk. It is small and harmless, of little interest to anglers except as forage for the larger fish that eat it.",
  "goldfish": "The goldfish in the wild is rarely gold. Released from aquariums and garden ponds, feral fish revert to a plain olive-bronze or grey, and they look much like a small carp. The body is carp-like but lacks the barbels a carp has, and the long dorsal fin is stiffened by a serrated spine. Feral populations have taken hold in warm, weedy, often stagnant water, including sheltered harbours such as Hamilton Harbour, where they can also hybridize with carp.\n\nThis is an introduced and invasive species, present spring through fall in southern Ontario ponds and Great Lakes harbours. A clear rule comes with it. Never release pet goldfish or any live fish into the wild. It is illegal in Ontario, and released fish can establish themselves and damage native waters. If you no longer want an aquarium fish, rehome it rather than dumping it in a pond.",
  "quillback": "At a glance this looks like a carp, deep-bodied and high-backed and silvery, but it is a sucker and the differences show on a closer look. There are no barbels around the mouth, which is small and sucker-like, and the long dorsal fin has its front rays drawn out into a tall quill that gives the fish its name and a sail-like profile.\n\nIt keeps to slow pools of large rivers and lakes, over soft bottoms where it feeds. In Ontario it belongs to the lower Great Lakes and the larger southern rivers, and you meet it spring through fall. Not at risk. The carp resemblance means it is often misidentified, so check for barbels: a carp has them, a quillback does not. Like the other suckers it is bony eating.",
  "rainbow-darter": "Few Ontario fish carry as much colour as a breeding male of this small darter. In spring he turns out in alternating bars of blue-green and orange across the body and fins, while females and out-of-season fish stay a modest mottled brown. Like other darters it has two dorsal fins and lives on the bottom, holding among gravel and cobble in the riffles of clear, fast streams.\n\nIn Ontario its range is narrow, centred on the southwestern streams of the Grand and Thames watersheds. Being a bottom fish of clean, moving water, it is found by turning stones or watching a riffle rather than by fishing. It is best seen in spring, when the males are at their brightest. Not at risk in Ontario, though a fish tied to clear streams depends on those streams staying clean. It is tiny and harmless, worth a careful look and nothing more.",
  "silver-redhorse": "Where the shorthead redhorse shows a red tail, this one shows grey. Heavy-bodied and large-scaled, the silver redhorse is the thickest-lipped of Ontario's common redhorse, with full, deeply grooved lips ending in a rounded rear lobe. The tail runs grey to slate rather than red, and that colour is the quickest way to separate it from the red-tailed shorthead.\n\nIt favours the deeper pools of medium to large rivers and the lakes connected to them, feeding along the bottom through those heavy lips. Found in the larger rivers of southern and central Ontario, spring through fall. Not at risk. Redhorse are notoriously alike, and getting to species means looking closely at the lips and tail rather than trusting a first impression. Edible but bony, like the rest of the suckers.",
  "largemouth-bass": "A greenish fish with a bold dark stripe running the length of its side, the largemouth is named for a jaw that extends back past the eye. Its two dorsal fins are nearly separated, which helps tell it from the smallmouth bass. It holds in warm, weedy water, in lakes and slow backwaters with plenty of cover to ambush from. That oversized mouth is not just for show, since it can take prey up to about a third of its own length.\n\nThis is a warm-water gamefish and a popular one in southern and central Ontario, active by day and most catchable through the warmer months. Not at risk. Anglers should check the current fishing regulations before keeping any, since seasons and limits apply and vary by zone. It fights hard for its size, which is much of the reason people fish for it.",
  "northern-pike": "Long and torpedo-shaped, the pike hangs motionless in the weeds and lets prey come to it. When something passes within reach it bursts out and seizes it, sometimes taking fish nearly half its own length. The body is dark green with rows of light, bean-shaped spots. To tell it from a muskie, check the cheeks and gill covers: on a pike the cheeks are fully scaled but the gill covers only partway. You will find pike in weedy bays, along shallow shorelines, and in slow rivers across the whole province, and they stay active through the ice.\n\nA caught pike is worth handling with respect. The mouth holds rows of needle-sharp teeth, so grip firmly behind the head and use pliers to free a hook.",
  "smallmouth-bass": "Bronze-sided and built for current, the smallmouth holds in clear lakes and cool rivers where gravel and boulders break the flow. The body carries faint vertical bars and the eye is often tinged red. A quick way to separate it from a largemouth: the jaw ends at or before the eye, not behind it. For its size it fights harder than almost anything in fresh water, running deep and throwing itself clear of the surface.\n\nLook for it around rocky points, mid-lake shoals, and river runs from spring through fall. It favours cooler, cleaner water than the largemouth and shares much of southern and central Ontario with it. Crayfish and small fish make up much of what it eats.",
  "walleye": "The glassy, reflective eye is the giveaway, and it is also the clue to how this fish lives. A light-gathering layer at the back of the eye, the tapetum lucidum, lets the walleye see and feed in murky water and in the low light of dusk and dawn. That is when it moves up onto reefs, drop-offs, and shoreline flats to hunt. During bright midday it usually sits deeper or in stained water. Look for the white tip on the lower lobe of the tail, and note the plain spiny dorsal fin, which separates it from the closely related sauger.\n\nWalleye are found across the province and stay catchable through the ice. Handling one calls for some care: the jaws hold sharp teeth, the first dorsal fin is spiny, and the edges of the gill covers are sharp enough to cut.",
  "yellow-perch": "Few fish are as easy to recognize as the yellow perch. The body is golden-yellow, crossed by six to eight dark vertical bars, with lower fins washed in orange. Perch travel in schools, sometimes large ones, moving through weed beds and open water alike. Where you find one you usually find many, which is part of why they are among the most popular fish on the Ontario table.\n\nThey feed by day on insects, small fish, and invertebrates, and they keep biting through winter, making them a mainstay of the ice fishery. You will meet them in lakes and slow rivers across the province. No special handling worries here, though the spiny first dorsal fin is worth a moment's attention when you unhook one.",
  "muskellunge": "Ontario's largest member of the pike family is hard to catch. Anglers call it the fish of 10,000 casts, a nod to how rarely one commits to a lure. The pattern is the reverse of a pike: dark bars or spots on a lighter background. To confirm an identification, check that only the lower half of the cheek and gill cover is scaled, and count six or more sensory pores under each side of the jaw. Muskie hold near weedlines, rocky bars, and deeper structure in large lakes and rivers, and they are most active through summer and into fall.\n\nThe best water for them is the bigger systems: the Kawarthas, the Great Lakes, and northern rivers. These are powerful fish with a mouthful of teeth. Use a heavy leader, and cradle a large one in the water to release it rather than lifting it clear.",
  "sauger": "At a glance the sauger looks like a small walleye, and the two are close relatives. Look closer and the differences show: rows of distinct dark spots run through the spiny dorsal fin, the tail has no white tip, and dark saddle-shaped blotches mark the sides. Sauger handle turbid, current-swept water better than walleye and are more at home in big, murky rivers and the lakes connected to them. Where the two overlap they sometimes cross, producing a hybrid known as the saugeye.\n\nLike the walleye, it feeds most in low light, at dawn and dusk. You will find it in larger rivers and lakes, including parts of the Great Lakes and northern watersheds, mostly through spring, fall, and winter. The same handling cautions apply: sharp teeth, a spiny first dorsal fin, and sharp-edged gill covers.",
  "black-crappie": "Silvery and flecked with irregular black speckling, the black crappie earns its old nickname 'calico bass' from that mottled pattern. It is a schooling fish, roaming in loose groups around weed beds, sunken wood, and other cover in warm lakes and slow rivers. To separate it from the white crappie, count the dorsal spines: seven or eight here, five or six on the white.\n\nCrappie feed most actively in the low light of morning and evening, and they gather in numbers, so finding one often means finding a school. In Ontario they are largely a southern and central fish. They stay active under the ice, which makes them a common winter catch. The flesh is well regarded on the table, and there is nothing about handling them that calls for particular care.",
  "bluegill": "Round and deep-bodied, the bluegill is one of the small sunfish that crowd weedy, warm water. Two marks pin it down: a solid dark ear-flap with no coloured margin, and a dark blotch at the rear base of the soft dorsal fin. It lives among vegetation in lakes and ponds, rarely straying far from cover.\n\nIn late spring and early summer the males clear saucer-shaped nests on the bottom, often packed side by side in colonies, and guard them hard against anything that comes near. That is when they are easiest to catch and most fun on light tackle. Bluegill are a southern and central Ontario fish, active by day through the warm months. They take small baits readily and are a common first catch for children learning to fish.",
  "brown-bullhead": "A small catfish of soft, weedy bottoms, the brown bullhead feels its way through the dark rather than relying on sight. The whisker-like barbels around its mouth are lined with taste buds, and it uses them to find food in murky water and at night, when it is most active. Recognize it by the squarish tail, the dark chin barbels, and the saw-toothed rear edge on the pectoral spine. It lives in warm lakes and slow rivers across the province, usually over mud or among plants.\n\nOne warning worth taking seriously: the stiff spines at the front of the pectoral and dorsal fins are sharp and can drive a painful wound into a careless hand. Grip a bullhead carefully, keeping fingers clear of those spines, when you unhook one.",
  "pumpkinseed": "Among the most colourful of the small sunfish, the pumpkinseed is marked by an ear-flap tipped with a bright red-orange spot and wavy blue lines running across the cheek. It keeps to weedy shallows in lakes, ponds, and quiet river margins, seldom far from cover. Snails are a staple of its diet, and it is well equipped for them: a set of crushing pharyngeal jaws deep in the throat lets it break open hard shells that other fish leave alone.\n\nPumpkinseed are widespread through southern and central Ontario and active by day in the warm months. They are small, willing biters, easy to catch from a dock, and often the first fish a young angler brings in. Handling them poses no particular problem.",
  "rock-bass": "The red eye gives this one its common name, 'redeye,' and it is the fastest way to know it. Chunky and rather plain next to the brighter sunfish, the rock bass wears rows of dark spots along a brassy body. Count the anal spines to be sure: five or six here, against three in the true sunfish like the bluegill and pumpkinseed. As the name suggests, it holds close to rock - boulders, ledges, and sunken logs along the margins of lakes and rivers.\n\nIt is widespread through southern and central Ontario and active by day from spring into fall. Rock bass take a bait quickly and often aggressively, which makes them easy company on the water. There is nothing about handling them to watch out for beyond the usual spiny fins.",
  "brook-trout": "Despite the name, this is not a true trout but a char, and it belongs to the coldest, cleanest water Ontario has. The back carries wormlike markings, called vermiculations, the sides are dotted with red spots each ringed by a pale blue halo, and the lower fins show a clean white leading edge. Brook trout need cold, well-oxygenated water and do not tolerate warming or pollution, so their presence is a fair sign of a healthy stream.\n\nYou will find them in headwaters, spring-fed creeks, and clear northern lakes, where lake-dwelling fish grow larger than the stream fish. They are most reachable in spring and fall, when the water is cool. Because they depend so completely on cold, clean habitat, brook trout populations are a useful measure of a watershed's condition, and many local waters are managed with that in mind.",
  "chinook-salmon": "The biggest of the Pacific salmon, Chinook were stocked into the Great Lakes to hold alewife numbers in check and now anchor the region's salmon fishery. They spend most of the year in open water, feeding and growing fast, before running up rivers in fall to spawn. Most fall between 60 and 100 cm, though some push past 120.\n\nTo tell a Chinook apart, look inside the mouth: the gums and mouth interior are black, and small black spots mark both lobes of the tail. Summer trolling brings them within reach of anglers, and fall crowds them into tributary mouths. Like other Pacific salmon, they die after spawning, so a run is a one-way trip. These are introduced fish, not native to the lakes, and their presence is maintained through stocking. That makes local numbers depend heavily on how many young fish are released each year and on the state of the forage base they feed on.",
  "cisco": "Also called lake herring, the cisco is a slender, silvery fish of open, cool water, moving in schools through the pelagic zone. The deeply forked tail and the small fleshy adipose fin mark it as a member of the salmon family. A useful field mark is the lower jaw, which projects slightly beyond the upper. That is the reverse of the lake whitefish, whose snout overhangs its mouth.\n\nCisco matter to the food web out of proportion to their size. Lake trout and walleye rely on them as forage, and where cisco are healthy the predators tend to be too. They are also a fine fish on the table and take well to smoking. Look for them in fall and winter, when they gather to spawn and come within reach of ice anglers on many cold inland lakes as well as the Great Lakes. Numbers have slipped in some waters, so local regulations are worth checking before you keep a catch.",
  "lake-trout": "Lake trout are a char, not a true trout, and they live where few other large fish can: the deep, cold, oxygen-rich basins of northern lakes. A dark body scattered with pale cream spots and a deeply forked tail set them apart, and unlike some of their relatives they carry no pink or red spots.\n\nThey grow slowly and live a long time, sometimes more than 40 years, which makes a population slow to recover once it is knocked back. Ontario holds a large share of the world's lake trout lakes, most of them on the Canadian Shield, along with populations in the Great Lakes. You can find them through spring and summer and again through the ice in winter. Because they hold in deep water for much of the year, catching them often means fishing well below the surface. A big fish is an old fish, and removing many of them thins a slow-growing stock.",
  "lake-whitefish": "A bottom feeder of deep, cold water, the lake whitefish makes its living on invertebrates it picks from the lake floor. The small mouth set under an overhanging snout is the giveaway, paired with a silvery body and a deeply forked tail. That overhanging snout separates it from the cisco, which has a slightly projecting lower jaw. Both belong to the whitefish group within the salmon family.\n\nThis is one of the more valued fish in Ontario waters, both commercially and on the plate, with firm pale flesh that many rank among the best of the cold-water fishes. Whitefish spend the open-water season in deep parts of the Great Lakes and large inland lakes, then move shallower to spawn over gravel in late fall. That fall movement, and the winter that follows, is when most anglers meet them, often through the ice. They feed quietly near bottom, so light rigs and small baits tend to work best.",
  "rainbow-trout": "Lake-run rainbow trout go by a second name, steelhead, and the two are the same fish living different lives. In the lakes they turn chrome-bright and hard to separate from other silvery fish, but a pink-to-red band along the side and small black spots scattered across the body and tail give them away.\n\nThey spend much of the year in cold Great Lakes water and ascend tributaries to spawn, mostly in spring, with fish staging in rivers through fall and winter as well. On their spawning runs, steelhead will leap small falls to reach the water they are headed for, which makes river mouths and lower rapids good places to watch for them. Rainbows were brought here from western North America and have naturalized, breeding on their own in many streams rather than depending entirely on stocking. Unlike the Pacific salmon, they do not necessarily die after spawning and may run more than once.",
  "brown-trout": "Brought over from Europe, the brown trout has settled into cool streams and the nearshore zones of the Great Lakes. Its spots tell the story: black and red-orange, each ringed with a pale halo, over a squarish tail that carries few spots or none. That pattern separates it from the more heavily spotted rainbow and from the char, which show light spots on a dark ground.\n\nBrowns have a reputation for being hard to catch, and it is earned. Large individuals become mostly nocturnal and grow wary, holding to cover and feeding in the low light of dawn and dusk. Look for them in spring and fall, in streams and along shorelines where the water stays cool. They will take a range of baits and lures, but the bigger fish reward patience and a quiet approach. As an introduced species they compete with native trout in some waters, so local rules on size and season are worth knowing.",
  "coho-salmon": "Coho are the smaller, scrappier of the two Pacific salmon stocked into the Great Lakes, known among anglers for aggressive, hard-charging strikes. The mouth sorts them from the similar Chinook: a coho has a white or pale gumline with a dark tongue, and black spots on the upper lobe of the tail only, not both.\n\nThey spend the growing season in open water and run up tributaries to spawn, mostly in fall. Summer and fall are the seasons to look for them, first out in the lake and later staging near river mouths. Like the other Pacific salmon here, coho die after spawning, so a run is the end of the line for those fish. They were introduced and are not native, so their numbers lean on stocking and on the health of the baitfish they feed on. Where the forage base is strong, the fish come back heavier the following season.",
  "atlantic-salmon": "Unlike the Pacific salmon in the Great Lakes, the Atlantic salmon is a native fish here, or was. The Lake Ontario stock was gone by the late 1800s, lost as its rivers were dammed, logged, and overfished, and the fish you might encounter today are the work of an ongoing effort to bring it back. Look for black spots shaped like Xs or crosses above the lateral line, a slim caudal peduncle where the body narrows to the tail, and a tail that carries no spots.\n\nIt uses the cold lakes and rivers of the Lake Ontario system, running into tributaries to spawn in fall, with fish also present in spring. This is a species at risk in the plainest sense: its original population was wiped out. The reintroduction is still finding its footing and depends on years of stocking and habitat repair. If you catch one, handle it gently and know the local rules, which may require release.",
  "common-carp": "A heavy-bodied import from Eurasia, the common carp thrives in warm, shallow, often murky water over soft, muddy bottoms. Two barbels hang at each corner of the mouth, the scales are large, and a long dorsal fin runs down the back with a hardened, serrated spine at its front edge. Adults commonly reach 40 to 80 cm, and some pass a metre.\n\nCarp feed by rooting through bottom sediment, and that habit is the trouble with them. Stirring up mud clouds the water and uproots plants, degrading habitat that native fish and waterfowl depend on. That is why the species is treated as invasive across southern Ontario. They are most active through spring and summer, often in the low light of dawn and dusk, and are easiest to find in shallow bays and slow river stretches. Strong and long-lived, they are caught by anglers who fish for them on purpose, and their stubborn fight makes them a target in their own right.",
  "freshwater-drum": "Freshwater drum spend their time on the bottoms of large lakes and rivers, over sand and gravel, where they crush snails and other hard-shelled prey. The body has a high, arched back and a rounded tail, and the lateral line runs unusually far, continuing out onto the tail fin itself.\n\nThe name comes from a sound the males make. By vibrating muscles against the swim bladder they produce a low grunting or drumming that carries through the water and, at times, up to a boat. Look for them in summer and fall, most active in the low light around dawn and dusk. They are common in the Great Lakes and the large rivers of southern Ontario, and they are frequently caught by anglers fishing bottom rigs for other species. Drum are edible, though not widely sought as table fish. The heavy throat teeth that let them handle snails also make them one of the few fish here that feed heavily on molluscs.",
  "round-goby": "Small, bottom-hugging, and out of place, the round goby is an invader from the Black and Caspian seas that reached the Great Lakes in the ballast water of ships. It settles on rocky and gravel bottoms, where it competes with and displaces native bottom fish and eats their eggs. Two field marks stand out: the pelvic fins are fused into a single round suction disc on the belly, and there is a distinct black spot at the rear of the first dorsal fin. Most are only 7 to 15 cm long.\n\nYou are most likely to meet one in summer, often when it grabs a bait meant for something else. This is a fish to know and to handle correctly. It is illegal in Ontario to use or transport round goby as bait, and moving it to new water spreads the problem. If you catch one, do not put it back and do not carry it away alive. Its spread has already reshaped parts of the Great Lakes food web.",
  "white-sucker": "The white sucker feeds with its mouth turned down toward the bottom, working over the floor of streams and lakes across most of the province. That ventral mouth, with its thick fleshy lips, is the clearest mark, set on a rounded body whose scales grow noticeably smaller toward the head. It is one of the most widespread fish in Ontario and turns up in water of many kinds, from small creeks to large lakes.\n\nSpring is when suckers announce themselves, gathering into large runs up streams to spawn, sometimes in numbers that fill a riffle. They matter as food and as bait. Young suckers feed many predators, and the species is a common baitfish. On the table they are bony but perfectly edible, and were long a staple of spring fisheries. If you use them as bait, use them only where the law allows, and never move live fish between waters.",
  "bowfin": "The bowfin is one of Ontario's older fish lineages, the sole survivor of a group that mostly died out long ago. You find it in warm, weedy backwaters and marshy lake bays where the water sits still and the oxygen runs low. It gets by there by gulping air at the surface, using a modified swim bladder as a crude lung. That habit lets it hang on in water that would kill most other fish.\n\nLook for a long, low dorsal fin with no spines running most of the back, and a rounded tail. Males carry a dark eyespot near the base of the tail. The body is thick and almost cylindrical, olive to brown. Anglers meet it in spring and summer, most often around dawn and dusk. It takes bait readily and fights hard.\n\nHandle it with care. The jaws are strong and the teeth are sharp, so keep your fingers clear when unhooking. It is native and common through southern and central Ontario, and not at risk.",
  "burbot": "Ontario's only freshwater member of the cod family lives deep and cold. Burbot hold in the depths of large lakes and big rivers, moving shallower mainly in winter. The body is long and mottled brown, almost eel-like toward the tail, with a single barbel under the chin and long soft dorsal and anal fins that run back to meet the rounded tail.\n\nIt goes by several names, ling and eelpout among them. Unlike most fish here, it spawns under the ice in midwinter, sometimes gathering in writhing balls of intertwined fish over sand or gravel. It feeds at night, taking smaller fish and prey off the bottom.\n\nBecause it lives deep, most people who catch one are ice fishing or working a bait near bottom in cold water. The flesh is firm and good to eat. Burbot are widespread in the Great Lakes and cold inland lakes across the province, and carry no special protection.",
  "channel-catfish": "A deeply forked tail sets the channel catfish apart from Ontario's other catfish, which have square or rounded tails. The anal fin is rounded, and young fish carry scattered dark spots that often fade with age. Colour runs from slate to olive over a pale belly, and four pairs of barbels frame the mouth.\n\nIt favours large warm rivers and lakes with some current and deep holes to rest in. The skin is covered in taste buds, so the fish reads its surroundings by taste over nearly its whole body, an asset for finding food in murky water after dark. Spring and summer bring it within reach of anglers, and it feeds mostly at night.\n\nTake care when handling one. The stiff spines at the front of the pectoral and dorsal fins can drive a painful puncture, so grip the fish behind them. In Ontario it turns up in southern rivers and the lower Great Lakes, including Lake St. Clair and the Grand River. It is good eating and not at risk.",
  "lake-sturgeon": "Few Ontario fish live as long or grow as large as the lake sturgeon. It can pass a century and top 100 kilograms, though fish that size are old and now rare. Rows of bony plates called scutes run along the body, the tail is shark-like with an upper lobe longer than the lower, and four barbels hang ahead of a toothless, tube-like sucker mouth that extends to vacuum prey off the bottom.\n\nIt lives in the deep pools of large rivers and lakes, feeding on the bottom, mostly at night. Slow to mature and slow to reproduce, it was heavily overfished, and its populations have not recovered evenly. Status ranges from threatened to endangered depending on the population.\n\nIf you catch one, the law protects it. Release it unharmed, and mind the scutes, which are sharp. Seasons are closed in many waters, so check the regulations before fishing where sturgeon live. You are most likely to meet it in the Great Lakes and large northern rivers.",
  "longnose-gar": "Nothing else in Ontario looks like a longnose gar. The jaw is drawn out into a long, narrow beak lined with teeth, more than twice the length of the head, and the body is armoured in diamond-shaped ganoid scales that interlock like plate. It is an ancient design that has changed little over millions of years.\n\nThe fish hangs near the surface of warm, weedy bays and slow rivers, often basking almost motionless, then striking sideways to seize small fish in that toothy jaw. Unlike most of the fish it lives among, it is active by day. Spring and summer are when you are most likely to see one.\n\nTwo cautions matter. The teeth are sharp, so handle the head carefully. More important, the roe is toxic to people. Never eat gar eggs. The flesh is eaten by some, but the eggs are dangerous, so be certain of exactly what you are keeping. Longnose gar live in southern Ontario, including the Great Lakes and the Kawarthas, and are not at risk.",
  "sea-lamprey": "The sea lamprey is not a fish in the usual sense. It is jawless and eel-like, with no paired fins, and it feeds by fastening its round, tooth-filled sucker mouth to the side of a larger fish and rasping through the skin to take blood and body fluids. Seven small gill pores run along each side behind the eye.\n\nNative to the Atlantic, it reached the upper Great Lakes through shipping canals and did heavy damage to lake trout and other stocks. Adults ascend tributary streams in spring to spawn, then die. Control programs, mainly targeted lampricides applied to spawning streams, now hold numbers down, though the work never stops.\n\nThis is an invasive parasite. Where control programs apply, do not move live lampreys between waters or return them alive. If you find one attached to a fish, it is doing exactly what it does for a living. The species is present throughout the Great Lakes and their tributaries.",
  "american-eel": "Every American eel in Ontario began life in the Atlantic. They hatch in the Sargasso Sea and drift thousands of kilometres as tiny larvae before entering fresh water, where they may live for years or decades before returning to the sea to spawn once and die. It is the reverse of the salmon's journey.\n\nThe body is snake-like, with a single long dorsal fin that runs into the tail, smooth and slippery skin over tiny embedded scales, and a lower jaw that projects past the upper. It moves and feeds at night. In Ontario it belongs to the Lake Ontario, St. Lawrence, and Ottawa River systems.\n\nThe species is endangered and protected here. It is illegal to fish for, keep, or harm American eel. Numbers fell sharply after dams and other barriers cut off its migration routes. If you catch one by accident, release it unharmed.",
  "eastern-red-cedar": "The name misleads. This is a juniper, not a true cedar. Look for a narrow, dense evergreen carrying two kinds of leaf at once: sharp, awl-like needles on young or vigorous growth, and tight scale-like leaves pressed flat against older twigs. Female trees are hung with pale blue cones that look like berries and wear a waxy bloom. The bark is reddish-brown and peels off in thin, shreddy strips.\n\nYou meet it on dry limestone ground in southern and eastern Ontario, on alvars, in old fields, along fencerows, and on rocky shores. It holds its foliage year round. Cedar waxwings and other birds eat the berry-like cones and carry the seeds off, which is much of how it moves into open land. It tolerates thin, poor soil that many trees cannot, so it often marks bare rock and abandoned pasture where little else has taken hold.",
  "scots-pine": "An orange-barked pine on sandy ground is usually this one. The colour shows best on the upper trunk and larger limbs, where the bark flakes off in thin plates and can be picked out from a distance. Needles come two to a bundle, short, blue-green, and often twisted.\n\nIt is not native. Brought from Eurasia and planted heavily for reforestation and as a Christmas tree crop, it has since seeded itself out of plantations into old fields, roadsides, and open sandy sites across southern and central Ontario. You find it standing year round. Much of the planted stock was poorly formed, so escaped trees are often crooked or forked rather than straight. It counts as naturalized here, established well enough to reproduce on its own, though it does not spread with the force of some other introductions.",
  "canada-yew": "Unlike most conifers, this one stays low and sprawling, rarely more than knee to chest height, its branches leaning and straggling through the shade. The flat needles resemble hemlock but run longer, sit in two ranks along the twig, stay green on both sides, and end in a fine point. Females carry seeds set singly in a soft, red, cup-shaped aril.\n\nIt grows in cool, moist forest, in ravines, and on shaded rocky slopes, mostly through central and northern Ontario with scattered stands in the south. Deer feed on it hard through winter, and heavy browsing has thinned or erased it from much of the settled south.\n\nTreat it as poisonous. The foliage and seeds contain taxine alkaloids and can be dangerous or fatal to people and livestock if eaten. The red flesh of the aril is not the toxic part, but the seed inside it is, and there is no safe reason to test the difference.",
  "red-spruce": "Spruces are told apart by small details, and this one rewards a close look. The needles are four-sided, yellow-green, shiny, and sharp at the tip. Rolled between the fingers they feel square. The twigs are finely hairy, and the reddish-brown cones fall not long after they release their seed, which helps separate it from black spruce, a tree that hangs onto its small cones for years.\n\nOntario sits at the western edge of its range. It grows in the east and centre of the province, in cool, moist upland forest and mixed woods, often on shallow soil over rock. Where its range meets that of black spruce the two can hybridize, and the in-between trees can be genuinely hard to name. It is native and secure here, if never as common as the spruces of the boreal north.",
  "pitch-pine": "Count the needles. Three to a bundle sets this pine apart from the two-needle pines that are far more common in Ontario. They are stiff and often a little twisted. The cones are egg-shaped and armed with a small prickle on each scale, and the trunk frequently carries odd tufts of needles sprouting straight from the bark.\n\nIt keeps to hard ground: dry sandy flats and rocky ridges on poor, acidic soil. In the province it is local and uncommon, found in the east on the Frontenac Axis and in the Ottawa Valley, near the northern edge of its range. Fire shapes its life. Where many pines are killed outright, this one can push new shoots from its trunk and root collar after a burn, one of the few eastern pines able to do so. Fire suppression, by closing in the open ground it depends on, works against it over time.",
  "alternate-leaf-dogwood": "Every other dogwood in Ontario holds its leaves in opposite pairs. This one sets them singly, alternately along the twig, though they bunch near the branch tips closely enough to look whorled at a glance. The surer sign is the shape of the whole plant. Branches spread out in flat, tiered layers, one above the next, which is why it is also called pagoda dogwood.\n\nLook for it along forest edges, in the understory, and in clearings in moist woods through southern and central Ontario, growing as a large shrub or small tree. In early summer it carries flat-topped clusters of small white flowers. These give way to blue-black berries held on bright red stalks, a combination that birds find quickly. It is native and common enough within its range. The layered branching and alternate leaves make it easy to name once you have seen them.",
  "american-mountain-ash": "Compound leaves with eleven to seventeen toothed leaflets give this small tree a look that recalls a true ash, though the two are not related. Flat clusters of white flowers open in spring and set into tight bunches of bright orange-red berries by late summer. The bark stays smooth and grey.\n\nIt favours cool, moist ground, growing through northern and central Ontario in damp woods, along rocky shores, and at the edges of bogs. The berries are its most useful field mark in the cold months, since the clusters cling on well into winter. Grosbeaks, waxwings, and ruffed grouse strip them over the season. The fruit is sour and firm and not something to eat raw off the tree, though the birds that depend on it are reason enough to leave it be. A close relative, showy mountain-ash, overlaps with it and looks much alike.",
  "bebb-willow": "Willows are a hard group, and this is one of the more common ones you will run into almost anywhere in Ontario, from southern fields to the boreal north. It grows as a tall shrub or small tree in moist open woods, clearings, fens, and along streams. The dull green leaves are widest above the middle, a little wrinkled on top, with veins that stand out clearly.\n\nIts best-known feature is not really the plant's own doing. A fungus attacks the stems and the wood responds by forming deep, diamond-shaped grooves down the trunk. Carvers value this figured wood, and diamond willow walking sticks and crafts are cut from stems marked this way. Even without the fungus it turns up often enough to be worth learning. Naming willows to species usually takes patience and more than one feature, so read the leaf shape and the setting together rather than trusting any single mark.",
  "bitternut-hickory": "Check the buds in winter and this hickory names itself. They are bright sulphur-yellow, unlike the buds of any other tree in its range, and they hold that colour through the cold months. In leaf it carries compound leaves with seven to nine leaflets. The nut is round, wrapped in a thin husk, and as the name warns, bitter.\n\nIt grows in moist ground, in lowland and slope forests through southern and south-central Ontario, where it is the commonest hickory and the one that ranges furthest north. The nuts are not worth gathering to eat; the kernel is bitter enough that even squirrels tend to take them late, after better food is gone. The wood is hard and strong like that of other hickories. For identification, though, nothing beats those yellow buds, which settle the question at a glance long after the leaves and nuts have dropped.",
  "black-locust": "A pair of short, sharp spines at the base of each twig is the quickest way to place this tree. The leaves are compound, with seven to nineteen rounded leaflets that carry no teeth. The bark is thick and deeply furrowed into a network of interlacing ridges. In June it hangs out clusters of fragrant white flowers shaped like pea blossoms, followed by flat brown pods that rattle on the tree into winter.\n\nIt is not native to Ontario. Brought north from the central Appalachians, it has naturalized across the south and spreads aggressively by seed and by root suckers, often forming thickets on old fields, roadsides, and sandy disturbed ground. The wood is hard and strongly rot-resistant, long valued for fence posts that outlast most others. Treat the plant itself with care. The bark, leaves, and seeds are toxic if eaten, so it is not a tree to sample or to plant where livestock graze.",
  "black-willow": "Most willows in Ontario are shrubs. This is the one that grows into a proper tree, and the largest native willow in Canada. It often leans out over the water on several trunks at once, with dark, deeply furrowed bark and a broad, untidy crown. The leaves are narrow, finely toothed, and green on both surfaces rather than pale beneath.\n\nWater is where you find it, along riverbanks, lakeshores, and other wet ground through southern Ontario. Its roots knit into the soil and hold banks against the current, so it does real work stabilizing shorelines where it grows. The wood is soft and light and not much used for lumber. Like other willows the twigs are brittle and snap off cleanly, and broken pieces washed downstream can root and start new trees, which is part of how it spreads along a river system over time.",
  "downy-serviceberry": "Among the first trees to flower in spring, often while the woods are still bare. The blossoms come in drooping clusters, each with five narrow white petals, opening before the leaves have fully unfolded. The young leaves are wrapped in soft white down that wears off as they mature. The bark is smooth and grey with darker vertical streaks.\n\nLook for it in dry woods, on forest edges, in clearings, and on rocky slopes through southern and central Ontario, growing as a large shrub or small tree. The berries ripen in early summer, which is where the name juneberry comes from. They are sweet and edible, among the more reliable wild fruits in the province, taken readily by people and by birds. As with any wild food, be sure of the plant before eating, though serviceberries are distinctive in flower and fruit. The related smooth serviceberry overlaps and is likewise edible.",
  "manitoba-maple": "Among Ontario's maples this is the odd one out. Its leaves are compound, split into three to seven toothed leaflets, so a quick glance can suggest ash rather than maple. The paired samaras settle the question, hanging in dense clusters through the season. Twigs are green and often coated with a waxy bloom. You meet it along southern and central rivers and floodplains, and just as often on disturbed ground and road edges, where its speed and cold tolerance let it get ahead of slower trees. It is the fastest-growing and most cold-hardy of the native maples, which is also why it turns up where nobody planted it.\n\nThe seeds contain hypoglycin A, a compound linked to seasonal pasture myopathy, a muscle disease that is often fatal in horses. Keep horses off pastures where the seeds have fallen, especially in autumn and after storms have brought down a heavy drop.",
  "mountain-maple": "A small, often shrubby maple of the shaded understory. It rarely gains much height, growing as a multi-stemmed thicket in moist ravines and along streambanks beneath taller trees. The leaves are modest, three-lobed and coarsely toothed. The feature that identifies it in the field is the flower and seed cluster. Where nearly every other maple lets its samaras hang, this one holds its spikes upright. That detail separates it from every other maple in the province.\n\nIt grows as understory across most of Ontario, seldom noticed until you learn to read the standing seed spikes. In autumn the leaves can turn red and orange, and because the tree stays low, that colour sits close to eye level rather than overhead. Look for it in cool, shaded woods where the soil holds moisture through the summer.",
  "pin-cherry": "Look for shiny reddish-brown bark marked with pale horizontal lines, the lenticels that run across it. The leaves are narrow and pointed, and the fruit is a small, sour red cherry carried on a long stalk in loose clusters. It is one of the first trees to seed in after a fire or a clearcut, which earns it the name fire cherry. That habit makes it a short-lived pioneer, quick to hold open ground while slower forest trees establish. It grows across the whole province.\n\nThe cherries are very sour and are used mostly in jelly rather than eaten off the tree. Be careful with the rest of the plant. The leaves, twigs and pits contain cyanide compounds and are toxic to livestock, and wilted leaves are especially dangerous to grazing animals. The thin flesh of the ripe cherry is the only part people use.",
  "pussy-willow": "In late winter the twigs produce silky grey catkins that push out before the leaves, and for most people that is the whole reason to know this willow. Those soft catkins are among the earliest signs of spring in Ontario wetlands, and they give bees an early source of pollen when little else is flowering.\n\nIt grows as a large shrub or small tree in wet meadows, at marsh edges, along streams and in damp thickets. The leaves come later, elliptic, dark green above and pale beneath, with wavy or shallowly toothed margins. Twigs are reddish-brown. You will find it through southern and central Ontario wherever the ground stays wet. Cut branches are often brought indoors in early spring, and the catkins hold their shape for a long time once they dry.",
  "striped-maple": "The bark gives this one away. It is smooth and green, streaked with vertical white lines, unlike any other tree in an Ontario hardwood forest. The leaves are large and three-lobed, broad enough that the shape is often compared to a goose's foot. It stays small, living out its life in the shade of taller hardwoods rather than reaching the canopy.\n\nIt is also called moosewood, because moose and deer browse its twigs and bark through winter when other food is scarce. Look for it in cool, shaded hardwood and mixed forest in central and eastern Ontario. In autumn the leaves turn a clear yellow. Because it grows in deep shade and seldom gets large, it is easy to walk past until you notice the striped bark at close range.",
  "american-sycamore": "Few trees are easier to name from a distance. The bark flakes away in patches and leaves the upper trunk and limbs smooth and pale, mottled cream and white, and that shows plainly across a winter floodplain. The leaves are very large and maple-like, and the seeds ride in round balls that hang singly on long stalks and stay on through winter.\n\nIt has the largest trunk diameter of any hardwood in eastern North America, and old riverside specimens can be enormous. In Ontario it keeps to the Carolinian southwest, lining rivers, floodplains and moist bottomlands. Winter is the season to look, when the leaves are down and the white upper bark stands out against bare woods. It needs consistent moisture, so it follows the water and does not stray far up onto dry ground.",
  "black-maple": "This is a near-twin of sugar maple, and telling the two apart takes attention. The leaf edges tend to droop, the lobes usually number three rather than five, and the undersides feel fuzzy to the fingertip. The bark runs darker. It is a close relative of sugar maple and is tapped for syrup in exactly the same way, with sap of similar quality. In Ontario it is a tree of rich, moist floodplain and bottomland soils, limited to the southwest.\n\nWhere the two grow together they hybridize, which blurs the line further, so an intermediate-looking tree may be exactly that. Autumn colour runs to yellow and orange. If you are trying to confirm it, check the drooping leaf margins and the fuzzy undersides together rather than leaning on any single feature.",
  "black-oak": "A tree of dry, sandy ground, this oak defines the black oak savannas at places like Ojibway Prairie and the Rice Lake plains. The leaves are glossy with bristle-tipped lobes, and their shape varies a good deal even on one tree, which can make identification difficult. Two features help. The inner bark is orange-yellow, and the winter buds are grey and noticeably hairy.\n\nIt grows on the southern Ontario sand plains, in oak savanna and open woods on dry sandy soils. That orange inner bark was once the source of a yellow dye called quercitron. Like other members of the red oak group it carries bristle tips on its lobes, so look to the buds and inner bark to separate it from its relatives. In autumn the leaves colour and the open savanna is easy to read.",
  "grey-birch": "Poor, dry ground is where this birch makes its living, seeding into old fields, burns and rocky clearings that few trees will take. The bark is chalky white to grey, but unlike white birch it does not peel away in papery sheets, and a dark triangular chevron sits below each branch where it meets the trunk. The leaves are long-pointed triangles that tremble in the wind like aspen.\n\nIt is a short-lived pioneer, quick to colonize disturbed and infertile ground and then give way as richer growth catches up. In Ontario it grows mainly in the east. The non-peeling bark and the dark chevrons are the quickest way to separate it from white birch at a glance.",
  "pin-oak": "The branch pattern is worth learning. Lower branches droop toward the ground, middle branches stand out horizontal, and upper branches angle up. The leaves are deeply cut with bristle tips and wide U-shaped sinuses, and the acorns are small. Dead lower branches tend to stay attached rather than dropping, studding the trunk with short spurs, and that pin-covered look is what names the tree.\n\nIt grows on poorly drained clay flats and floodplain edges. In Ontario it is native only to the wet clay plains near Lake Erie in the far south. Autumn brings deep red to russet colour. It is widely planted in towns well outside its native range, so a pin oak on a city street tells you nothing about local wild distribution.",
  "rock-elm": "This elm carries wood that is hard, heavy and shock resistant. That toughness once made it a wood of choice for hockey sticks, wagon wheel hubs and shipbuilding. In the field it is a tall tree with a narrow, cylindrical crown. Its leaves are smaller and smoother than those of other elms but keep the uneven, lopsided base typical of the family. A good clue is the twigs, which often develop irregular corky ridges or wings.\n\nIt grows in dry to moist upland woods, on ridges and limestone slopes through southern and central Ontario. Like other elms it is vulnerable to Dutch elm disease, which has thinned its numbers over the past century, so a large mature rock elm is less common now than it once was.",
  "showy-mountain-ash": "Two mountain-ashes grow in the north, and they are easy to confuse. This one has fewer and broader leaflets that end in a shorter point, and its berries are larger and a deeper red than those of the American mountain-ash. The winter buds are dark and sticky, another useful check. The two often share the same ground, so compare leaflet shape and fruit size when both are present.\n\nLook for it on rocky shores, cliffs and in cool moist woods, often near water, through northern Ontario and the upper Great Lakes region. The fruit clusters hang into winter and feed birds well after the leaves have dropped. The berries are bitter and astringent when raw, and are used cooked, in jelly, rather than eaten straight off the tree.",
  "slippery-elm": "The inner bark tells you what this is. Peel a strip and it comes away reddish and slimy, and that slime is what named the tree. People once chewed it or brewed it as a remedy for a sore throat. Above ground the leaves are the other giveaway: large, lopsided at the base, and rough enough on top to feel like fine sandpaper. The buds carry rusty hairs through the winter.\n\nLook for it in moist lowland woods, on slopes and floodplains across southern Ontario, where it mixes into the hardwood forest. It is native and not considered at risk, but it lives under the same shadow as white elm. Dutch elm disease reaches it too, and large old trees are uncommon for that reason. Most you meet are young or mid-sized, tucked in the understorey or along a damp edge. Spring, summer and fall all give you something to check, though the rough leaves and slimy bark make it easiest to place in the leaf-on months.",
  "swamp-white-oak": "Flip a leaf and the identification is half done. Dark green above, the underside is pale to nearly white and slightly fuzzy, which is where the name bicolor comes from. The acorns hang on long stalks, longer than most oaks bother with, and the young branches often peel and curl in a ragged way that catches the eye once the leaves are gone.\n\nThis is an oak of wet ground. It grows in bottomlands, along swamp edges and on floodplains, tolerating flooded soil that would kill most of its relatives. In Ontario it keeps to the wet woods of the far south. It is native and secure. Summer and fall are the seasons to know it, the two-toned leaves through the warm months and the stalked acorns dropping later. If you are standing in a soggy southern woodlot and find an oak that seems comfortable with wet feet, this is the likely candidate.",
  "american-chestnut": "Once a common forest tree, it is now something you almost have to hunt for. An introduced fungal blight swept through in the twentieth century and killed nearly every mature tree. What survives in Ontario is a few hundred, scattered through the Carolinian southwest, and it is listed as Endangered. Often all you find is a cluster of sprouts rising from an old blight-killed stump, growing for a while before the blight finds them again.\n\nThe leaves are long and narrow with large curved teeth, each tooth ending in a small bristle. The nuts sit inside spiny green burs. The spines on those burs are sharp and will jab bare hands, so handle fallen ones with care. Summer and fall are when the tree shows itself, in leaf and then in fruit. Report any you find to a conservation authority, given how few are left.",
  "blue-ash": "Among Ontario's ashes this one stands apart, and the twigs prove it. They are four-angled and squarish in cross-section, often with corky ridges running along the corners, a feature no other native ash shares. The compound leaves carry seven to eleven sharply toothed leaflets. The name comes from a blue dye: crush and soak the inner bark and it colours the water blue.\n\nIt grows on dry limestone soils, alvars, rocky slopes and open woods, mostly in the extreme south around Pelee Island and the Lake Erie shore. It is listed as Special Concern. The emerald ash borer has gutted ash populations across the province, and while blue ash shows more resistance than the ashes it grows beside, it is not immune, and its small range keeps it vulnerable. Check the square twigs in spring or after leaf-fall. Once you have felt the four hard angles, the tree is hard to mistake for anything else.",
  "chinquapin-oak": "The leaves look more like a chestnut than an oak, narrow and edged with many pointed teeth, but lacking the bristle tips a chestnut carries. The acorns are among the sweeter ones in the oak family. The bark is grey and flaky.\n\nThis is one of Ontario's rarest native oaks. It grows on thin soils over limestone, on dry slopes, alvars and rocky bluffs, including Manitoulin Island and the Niagara region. It is native but provincially rare, so a wild tree is a genuine find rather than something you stumble on. Fall is the season it announces itself, when the acorns drop. The acorns are edible and less bitter than most, but the tree's scarcity is reason enough to leave them for the wildlife that depend on them. If you are botanizing on limestone in the right corner of the province, chestnut-like leaves on an oak are the thing to watch for.",
  "common-hoptree": "More shrub than tree, it stays small and often grows low and many-stemmed near the water. The leaves split into three glossy leaflets that smell strongly when you crush them. Small greenish-white flower clusters give way to flat, round, papery winged seeds that look a good deal like elm fruit. Those winged fruits were once used in place of hops in brewing, which is how the tree got its name.\n\nIts home is the sandy and gravelly shorelines, dunes and open woods of the lower Great Lakes, along Lake Erie and Lake St. Clair, including Point Pelee and Pelee Island. It is listed as Threatened in Ontario, tied to a narrow band of shoreline habitat that is easily lost to development and erosion. Summer and fall are when to look, first for the scented three-part leaves, then for the clusters of round paper wings that hang on into autumn.",
  "cucumber-tree": "This is Canada's only native magnolia, and in Ontario it hangs on in just a few small wild populations in the southwest, around the Norfolk and Niagara areas. It is endangered. The large oval leaves run from about 10 to 25 cm long. The flowers are greenish-yellow and tulip-shaped, sitting high in the canopy in late spring where they are easy to walk under and never notice. The name comes from the unripe fruit, green and knobby and shaped a little like a small cucumber.\n\nLook for it in rich, moist deciduous forest in the Carolinian zone, the same warm pocket of the province that holds most of Ontario's rarities. Spring and summer give you the best chance, first the high flowers, then the odd green fruit. Given how few wild trees remain, leave any you find undisturbed.",
  "flowering-dogwood": "The spring display is a bit of a trick of the eye. What look like four broad white petals are actually bracts, modified leaves, arranged around a small cluster of the true flowers, which are tiny and greenish in the centre. The leaves are oval with veins that curve to follow the leaf edge, a reliable dogwood trait, and they turn deep red in fall alongside clusters of red berries. The bark breaks into small blocky plates.\n\nIt is a small understorey tree of rich deciduous forests and shaded slopes in the Carolinian zone of extreme southwestern Ontario, and it is endangered here. Dogwood anthracnose, a fungal disease, has killed many trees and is a large part of why. Spring and fall are the seasons it stands out, the white bracts early and the red leaves and berries late. In between it fades into the green understorey and is easy to pass by.",
  "kentucky-coffee-tree": "The leaves are among the largest you will see on a native tree here, twice-compound and sometimes close to a metre long, divided into many small leaflets. For much of the year, though, the tree stands bare. Its stout blunt twigs leaf out late and drop early, giving it a gaunt, dead-looking profile in the off-season. Female trees carry thick, flat, reddish-brown pods.\n\nSettlers roasted the seeds as a coffee substitute, and that is where the name comes from. This matters for safety. The raw pods and seeds contain a toxic alkaloid and should not be eaten. Roasting is what made them usable, and the raw material is not food. In Ontario only a few natural stands survive, in the floodplain and bottomland woods of the southwest, and it is listed as Threatened. Summer and fall are the seasons to find it, by the huge leaves and, on female trees, the hanging pods.",
  "pignut-hickory": "A hickory of dry ground, it holds to upland woods and ridges in the Carolinian southwest, where it reaches the northern edge of its range. The compound leaves usually carry five leaflets. The bark stays tight, with shallow interlacing ridges rather than the shaggy, peeling strips of some relatives, so it will not be confused with shagbark. The nut is pear-shaped inside a thin husk.\n\nThe husk is the detail worth knowing. On this tree it splits only partway down, unlike the husks of other hickories that open fully and free the nut. Fall is the season to sort it out, when the nuts are on the ground and the husk behaviour is plain to see. It is native and not at risk, though in Ontario you will only meet it in the far southwest. Anywhere north of the Carolinian zone, you are almost certainly looking at a different hickory.",
  "balsam-fir": "The needles settle the identification. They are flat and blunt, and if you turn one over it shows two pale white bands down the underside. The bark is smooth and grey and studded with resin blisters that pop stickily if you press them. The cones stand upright on the branch rather than hanging, and they break apart in place instead of dropping whole, so you rarely find an intact fir cone on the ground.\n\nThis is the fragrant Christmas tree many people picture. The resin from those bark blisters, known as Canada balsam, was once used as an optical cement for mounting lenses and microscope slides. It grows in cool moist woods and boreal mixedwoods through central and northern Ontario. Summer and winter both suit a visit, since it holds its needles year-round. The scent alone, released when you crush a needle between your fingers, is often enough to name it.",
  "black-spruce": "A tree of cold wet ground, it thrives in bogs and muskeg where the soil is waterlogged and poor, conditions most trees refuse. The crown is narrow and spire-like, often with a distinct tuft at the top, and the needles are short and stiff. The cones are small and sit in clusters near the top of the tree.\n\nIt has a useful trick for spreading in soft ground. Lower branches that sag down and touch the wet moss can take root and send up new shoots, a process called layering, so a single tree can slowly form a small ring of clones around itself. This helps it hold ground in the boreal peatlands of northern Ontario, where it is one of the defining trees. Summer and winter are both fair times to know it, since like other spruces it stays evergreen. On a northern bog, the skinny black spire with the tufted top is usually this.",
  "eastern-hemlock": "Cool ravines and shaded north slopes are where this tree does best. The needles are short and flat, set unevenly along the twig, with two pale lines on the underside. The topmost shoot, the leader, bends over in a soft droop that helps separate it from spruce and fir at a distance. It grows slowly and tolerates deep shade better than almost any other Ontario conifer, so seedlings can wait years in the understorey before a gap in the canopy opens. Individual trees may live for centuries.\n\nYou find it through central and southern Ontario, usually in moist forest where the soil stays cool. It holds its needles year-round and reads much the same in summer and winter. One thing to watch: the hemlock woolly adelgid, a small sap-feeding insect from elsewhere, has reached southern Ontario and threatens stands there. Infested trees show white woolly flecks at the base of the needles.",
  "eastern-white-cedar": "Foliage is flat and scale-like, arranged in fan-shaped sprays rather than as separate needles. The bark is reddish and shreddy, peeling in long vertical strips. It grows in swamps and moist ground over limestone, and also clings to dry cliff faces where little else takes hold. Some of the oldest trees in Ontario are stunted cedars rooted in cracks along the Niagara Escarpment, gnarled and slow-growing, far older than their small size suggests.\n\nAn open-grown cedar keeps a dense, conical shape and stays green through winter. It tolerates calcareous soils that many trees avoid. Deer browse it heavily where they are common, which can strip the lower foliage and thin regeneration in southern stands. The light, rot-resistant wood has long been used for fence posts, shingles and canoe ribs. You meet it across central and southern Ontario, most often near water or on rock.",
  "eastern-white-pine": "The provincial tree of Ontario, and for a long time the centre of its timber trade. Soft blue-green needles come in bundles of five, a reliable mark since no other native pine here carries five to a bundle. The cones are long and slender, often curved and sticky with resin. Older trees take on a tiered, wind-swept crown that rises above the surrounding canopy and can be picked out from a distance.\n\nIt grows across much of Ontario on sandy soils, rocky shores and in mixed forest, reaching great height and age on good sites. Logging through the 1800s removed most of the original old-growth pine, so the largest trees today are uncommon. It keeps its needles year-round. Widespread and easy to find, it remains one of the more familiar trees in the central and Great Lakes-St. Lawrence forests.",
  "jack-pine": "Poor ground suits this pine. It takes hold on sandy flats and bare rock in the boreal forest where richer soils are absent, often forming even-aged stands after fire. The needles are short, twisted and paired, and the small cones curve and stay clamped tight to the branch, sometimes for years.\n\nThose cones are serotinous, sealed with resin that only the heat of a fire melts, so the seed is released onto freshly burned, open ground. This ties the tree closely to the fire cycle of the north. It is smaller and scrubbier than most Ontario pines, seldom straight or tall on the poorest sites. You find it through boreal and northern Ontario, green year-round. Where fire has been suppressed, jack pine can struggle to regenerate, since without heat many cones simply stay shut.",
  "red-pine": "Two stiff needles to a bundle, and a clean snap when you bend one, set this apart from white pine at a glance. The bark breaks into broad reddish plates, which is where the name comes from. It favours dry sandy and rocky sites and often grows in pure stands.\n\nMuch of what you see in Ontario was planted. Red pine was used heavily in reforestation through the last century, and rows of it still mark old plantation blocks on sandy ground. Left to grow it forms a tall, straight trunk with a fairly open crown, and it holds its needles through winter. It occurs across central and northern Ontario, and the wood goes to poles, posts and lumber. On natural sites it, like jack pine, is adapted to fire and regenerates well on exposed mineral soil.",
  "tamarack": "Unusual among conifers, this one drops all its needles every autumn. The soft needles grow in tufted clusters along the twig, green through summer, then turn clear gold in fall before dropping, leaving the tree bare and grey through winter. That gold is a good way to pick tamarack out of a boreal wetland in October.\n\nIt grows in cold bogs, fens and wet lowlands where the ground stays saturated and acidic, conditions few trees tolerate. The tough roots were once used to sew birchbark canoes and the wood for boat knees. You find it across boreal and northern Ontario wetlands, less often to the south. Because it needs open, wet, sunny ground, it is easily shaded out on drier sites. In spring the new needles come in a soft pale green.",
  "white-spruce": "Roll a needle between your fingers and it turns easily, since spruce needles are four-sided rather than flat like those of fir or hemlock. Crushed foliage gives off a sharp, skunky smell that some find unpleasant, one way to separate it from black spruce. It grows on well-drained boreal soils, along riverbanks and through mixed northern forest.\n\nThis is a major timber tree in Ontario, cut for pulpwood and lumber across the north. The crown is dense and conical, green year-round, and the slim cones hang down before dropping whole. It handles cold and a range of soils, part of why it is so widespread in the boreal. You will meet it throughout boreal and northern Ontario, often mixed with poplar, birch and balsam fir.",
  "american-basswood": "Leaves are the quick tell: large, heart-shaped and noticeably lopsided at the base, with toothed edges. In early summer it hangs clusters of small, fragrant yellow flowers from a narrow leafy bract, and the scent draws bees in numbers. It is one of the better nectar trees in the southern hardwood forest, and basswood honey is well known.\n\nIt grows in rich, moist woods and on lower slopes through southern and central Ontario. The wood is soft, pale and even-grained, which makes it a favourite for hand carving. Basswood often sprouts from the base, so you find several trunks rising in a ring from one old stump. It loses its leaves in fall. A useful mid-canopy tree to learn, since the crooked leaf shape is hard to confuse once you have seen it.",
  "american-beech": "Smooth blue-grey bark sets this tree apart in a hardwood forest, staying tight and unbroken even on old trunks. The leaves are oval and toothed, the winter buds long, pointed and shaped like thin cigars. Its nuts are small and three-sided, held in a bristly husk, and feed bears, deer and many birds. It grows in rich, moist forest, often alongside sugar maple, through central and southern Ontario. Beech holds some of its pale dead leaves through winter, which makes young trees easy to spot in a bare wood.\n\nThere is a real concern here. Beech bark disease, caused by an introduced insect together with a fungus, is killing beech across much of the province. Affected trunks develop pocked, cankered bark, and many mature trees are declining or dead. Where the disease has passed through, you often see dense thickets of root sprouts in place of the old canopy trees.",
  "balsam-poplar": "In spring the buds give it away. They are sticky with fragrant resin that scents the air on a warm day, a smell once gathered as \"balm of Gilead.\" The leaves are oval and pointed, dark green above and whitish beneath, on furrowed grey bark. It grows fast in moist bottomlands, along rivers, on floodplains and in boreal clearings, and it is one of the northernmost hardwoods on the continent.\n\nBees collect the bud resin and work it into propolis for their hives. You find it across Ontario but especially in the north, often in wet ground where poplar and willow mix. Like other poplars it is short-lived and quick to colonize open, disturbed sites, and the leaves turn yellow before dropping in fall. The resinous buds and their scent are the most reliable way to name it, since the leaves alone can be mistaken for other poplars.",
  "black-cherry": "Mature bark looks like dark, curled cornflakes, broken into small scaly plates, and that is the surest field mark on an older trunk. The leaves are narrow, glossy and finely toothed, and in summer the tree hangs long drooping clusters of small dark cherries that birds strip quickly. It grows along woodland edges, in old fields and mixed forest through southern and central Ontario, often seeded in by birds. The reddish-brown wood is valued for furniture and cabinetmaking and is among the more sought-after hardwoods in the province.\n\nA caution worth taking seriously: the wilted leaves, twigs and seeds contain cyanogenic compounds and are toxic if eaten. This matters most for livestock, which can be poisoned by browsing branches or eating fallen, wilting leaves. Wildlife eat the fruit pulp, but the stones and foliage should not be treated as safe to chew or swallow.",
  "eastern-cottonwood": "Big triangular leaves with toothed edges, set on flattened stalks that let them flutter in the least wind, mark this streamside poplar. The bark is grey and deeply furrowed on old trunks, and the tree grows quickly to a large size. It keeps to floodplains, sandy riverbanks and lakeshores in southern Ontario, where its roots reach reliable water.\n\nIn early summer the female trees release quantities of cottony, wind-borne seed that drifts and piles along shorelines and roadsides, which is where the name comes from. It is one of the fastest-growing native trees here and among the largest hardwoods of the south. The soft wood is weak and the branches shed readily, so old cottonwoods drop limbs. The leaves turn yellow before falling. On a warm windy day the steady rustle of the leaves is a good clue even before you reach the tree.",
  "green-ash": "Green Ash grows where the ground stays wet: floodplains, riverbanks and low forests that flood in spring. The leaves are compound and set opposite each other on the twig, green on both surfaces, which separates it from white ash with its pale undersides. Its keys are single-winged and narrower than a white ash key. For decades it was planted along streets because it grew fast and took hard conditions.\n\nThe emerald ash borer has undone that. The beetle, introduced from Asia, tunnels under the bark and kills nearly every ash it reaches, and green ash has been hit hard across the southern Ontario lowlands. Standing dead ash are now common along many rivers. Fall is the season to notice it, when the leaves turn yellow. Native. If you have one on your property, expect it to need attention; untreated ash in infested areas rarely survive long.",
  "ironwood": "Ironwood is a small understory tree of dry upland woods, growing in the shade beneath oaks and maples. The bark is its best mark: grey-brown and shredding into narrow vertical strips that lift at the ends. Leaves look much like a birch, oval and doubly toothed. In late summer it hangs clusters of papery sacs that resemble hops, each holding a seed, which is where the other name, hop-hornbeam, comes from.\n\nThe wood is true to the name. It is among the hardest and heaviest of any Ontario native tree, dense enough that it was long used for tool handles and fence posts. The tree itself is easy to overlook, rarely growing tall or straight, but it is common through southern and central Ontario. Look for it in summer and fall. Native.",
  "largetooth-aspen": "The coarse teeth along the leaf edge give this aspen its name. Where trembling aspen has fine teeth, largetooth aspen carries large, rounded ones, only a handful to a side. In spring the new leaves open silvery and downy, a soft grey flush that fades to green as they mature. It leafs out noticeably later than trembling aspen, which is a reliable way to separate the two in early spring before the leaves reach full size.\n\nIt favours dry to moist upland soils, often sandy ground, old clearings and forest edges through central and southern Ontario. Like other aspens it grows quickly, does not live especially long, and is cut for pulpwood. The soft buds and bark feed grouse, deer and beaver. In fall the leaves turn yellow, sometimes all at once across a stand. Native.",
  "norway-maple": "This is a European maple planted across southern Ontario cities for its dense shade and easy growth, and it has since walked out of yards into nearby woods. The five-lobed leaves are broad, wider than they are long, and close enough to sugar maple to fool people. The quickest check is the leaf stalk: snap it, and Norway maple leaks a drop of milky white sap where sugar maple runs clear. The keys hang in wide pairs set almost in a straight line, nearly horizontal.\n\nWhere it invades native forest the trouble is the shade. It casts such heavy shade that native tree seedlings and spring wildflowers cannot get the light they need beneath it, and over time it replaces them. It is not recommended for planting, and pulling young ones before they seed is the usual advice. Introduced and invasive. Easiest to notice in spring flower and fall colour.",
  "red-maple": "Few trees are as willing to grow anywhere as red maple. It turns up in wet swamps and on dry ridges alike, which makes habitat a poor way to identify it. The name points to the better marks. The twigs, buds, flowers and fall leaves all run red, and in early spring it is one of the first trees to bloom, tinting whole swamps a dull red before other trees leaf out. The leaves have three to five lobes with toothed, jagged edges, shallower than a sugar maple's and rougher along the margin. Fall colour ranges from yellow to deep scarlet, often on the same tree. It is common through central and southern Ontario and easy to find. Native.",
  "red-oak": "Red oak is a tall, straight tree of well-drained uplands and rocky ridges through central and southern Ontario. Its leaves have pointed lobes, each tipped with a fine bristle, which separates the red oak group from the white oaks with their rounded lobes. On older trunks the grey bark carries smooth, shiny ridges that run down the tree like ski trails, a mark worth learning.\n\nThe acorns take two full years to ripen, so a tree carries two sizes at once. When they drop they are a heavy food for deer, wild turkeys, blue jays and squirrels, and a good acorn year feeds a lot of animals. It grows fairly fast for an oak and makes strong, useful wood. Fall is when the leaves turn, often a deep russet red. Native and common.",
  "silver-maple": "Silver maple belongs to wet ground: floodplains, riverbanks and bottomlands that flood in spring. Its leaves are deeply cut into five narrow lobes and pale silvery-white underneath, so when the wind turns them the whole crown flashes silver. It grows fast, faster than most native maples, and pays for it with weak wood. The branches are brittle and shed readily in ice and windstorms, which is worth keeping in mind with an old one leaning over a house or driveway.\n\nThe seeds are large for a maple and ripen in late spring, unusually early among our trees. Look for it through southern Ontario along rivers and in low, wet woods, often growing with green ash and elm. Fall colour is an ordinary yellow rather than anything bright. Native.",
  "sugar-maple": "This is the maple most people picture, the one on the flag and the source of Ontario syrup. It grows in rich, well-drained upland forests and can reach a good size, forming much of the hardwood canopy across central and southern Ontario. The leaf has five lobes with smooth-edged sinuses between them and no teeth along the margin, which is the cleanest way to separate it from red and silver maple.\n\nIn fall it turns a strong orange-red, and a hillside of sugar maple is much of what draws people out to look at autumn colour. The paired winged keys spin down in fall. Sap is drawn in late winter, when cold nights and mild days get it running, then boiled down to syrup. It is common and long-lived. Native.",
  "trembling-aspen": "The trick is in the leaf stalk. Trembling aspen hangs round leaves on flattened stems, so the least breeze sets them shaking and the whole tree seems to shiver. The bark is smooth and greenish-white, sometimes mistaken for birch, but it does not peel in papery sheets. It is a pioneer, first onto burns, clearings, roadsides and any bare disturbed ground, growing fast in full sun.\n\nMuch of its spread is underground. It sends up suckers from the roots, so a single tree can become a whole grove of genetically identical stems that leaf out and turn gold together in fall. It is one of the most widely distributed trees in North America and grows across all of Ontario. Individual trunks are short-lived, but the clone can persist a long time. Native and common.",
  "white-elm": "White elm, also called American elm, once lined streets across Ontario in tall arching rows before Dutch elm disease took most of them. The classic shape is a vase: a single trunk spreading into arching limbs that meet overhead. Leaves are oval and doubly toothed, with a base that is clearly lopsided, one side meeting the stalk lower than the other. It grows in moist bottomlands, floodplains and old fields through southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe disease, a fungus spread by bark beetles, still kills most elms before they reach full size, so large old ones are now uncommon and worth noting when you find them. Younger elms remain common in hedgerows and second growth, since the trees seed and grow before the disease reaches them. Fall colour is yellow. Native.",
  "paper-birch": "The white bark is the giveaway. Paper birch peels in thin horizontal papery sheets, chalky white on the outside and orange-tan beneath, and it holds that colour through winter, which is one of the good seasons to pick it out in a bare wood. The leaves are small, oval and doubly toothed. It is a pioneer of the boreal and mixed forest, quick onto burns and clearings, and grows across all of Ontario.\n\nThe waterproof bark was traditionally used for canoes, containers and roofing, and it still lights a fire when damp. One caution: stripping bark from a living tree wounds it and can let in rot, so take bark from fallen trees rather than standing ones. Fall colour is yellow. Native.",
  "white-ash": "White ash is a tree of rich, well-drained upland forest, the drier counterpart to green ash. The leaves are compound and opposite, made of several leaflets, and pale, almost whitish, on the underside, which gives the tree its name. On older trunks the grey bark is ridged in a tight, even diamond pattern that is easy to learn. The keys are single-winged and paddle-shaped.\n\nAcross Ontario its future is grim. The emerald ash borer, an introduced beetle, tunnels beneath the bark and kills nearly every ash it infests, and white ash populations are collapsing along with the other ashes. Standing dead trees are now a common sight where the borer has passed through. Fall colour can be a deep purple, unusual among our trees. Native. An untreated ash in an infested area is unlikely to survive.",
  "yellow-birch": "Scratch a twig and you get a faint smell of wintergreen. That, together with the shiny bronze-yellow bark peeling in thin papery curls, is the quickest way to name this birch. It grows tall for the group, reaching 18 to 25 metres, and stands as the largest native birch in Ontario. Look for it in cool, moist mixed forests and along stream edges, mostly through the central and northern parts of the province.\n\nSeedlings often start life on a rotting log or mossy stump, and mature trees sometimes end up perched on stilt-like roots where the nurse log has since rotted away. The wood is hard and even-grained, which is why it turns up in furniture and flooring. Fall and winter are good times to pick it out: the leaves turn a clear yellow, and once they drop the peeling bark stays legible against the snow.",
  "american-hornbeam": "Run a hand over the trunk and it feels like muscle under skin. The smooth blue-grey bark ripples in sinewy, fluted ridges, which is where the names musclewood and blue-beech come from. This is a small understory tree, usually 5 to 10 metres, living in the shade of rich hardwood forests and along streambanks in southern and central Ontario. The leaves are doubly toothed and birch-like, and by late summer the seeds hang in loose leafy clusters, each nutlet set against a small three-pointed wing.\n\nDespite its modest size, the wood is among the hardest of any native tree here, dense enough to dull tools and rarely worth cutting for lumber. It seldom dominates a stand. Expect it as scattered individuals below the canopy, holding on in deep shade where taller trees crowd out most competitors. Summer and fall show it best.",
  "black-ash": "A tree of wet ground, black ash grows in swamps, fens, wet lowland forests and along slow streams, mainly through central and northern Ontario. You can separate it from other ashes by the leaflets, which sit directly on the stalk with no small stems of their own, and by the soft, corky grey bark that rubs and flakes under a thumb. The buds are nearly black.\n\nThe wood splits cleanly along its growth rings into thin, flexible strips, which is why it has long been central to Indigenous basket-making. It is now listed as Endangered in Ontario and protected under provincial law, so it should not be cut or damaged. The cause of the decline is the emerald ash borer, an invasive beetle that has killed ash across the province and left black ash populations falling steeply. Fall is the main season to notice it, though standing dead trees increasingly mark where it used to thrive.",
  "black-walnut": "Big compound leaves and round green nuts the size of a golf ball mark this southern Ontario native. It favours rich, moist bottomlands and valley soils, where it can reach 20 to 30 metres with a dark, deeply furrowed trunk. The wood is dark and richly grained, and it ranks among the most valuable hardwoods in North America, which has made large trees a target for theft in some areas.\n\nThe nuts are edible once you get past the thick husk, though the husk stains hands and clothes a lasting brown. That staining is harmless to touch. Less harmless to a garden is juglone, a compound the roots and leaves release into the soil. It suppresses tomatoes and many other garden plants growing within reach of the root zone, so what will grow under a walnut is limited. Late summer into fall, when the nuts drop, is the easiest time to find it.",
  "bur-oak": "Few trees shrug off as wide a range of conditions. Bur oak grows on floodplains, in old savannas and along prairie edges, tolerating both drought and periodic flooding, across southern and central Ontario. The acorns are the giveaway: large, half-buried in a deep cap fringed with a ragged, mossy edge unlike any other oak here. The leaves are big and fiddle-shaped, pinched in near the middle, and the bark forms thick corky ridges.\n\nThat bark is the key to how the tree lives. It is thick enough to insulate the trunk from grass fires that kill thinner-barked competitors, which let bur oak hold open savanna country before fire suppression changed those landscapes. Old trees grow broad and heavy-limbed in the open, narrower where forest closes in around them. Fall, when the acorns ripen and drop, is the easiest time to be sure of it.",
  "butternut": "Close kin to black walnut, butternut carries similar large compound leaves but sets a different nut: elongated and egg-shaped, sheathed in a sticky, hairy husk rather than a round one. The bark is grey and ridged, paler than its relative. It grows in rich, moist woods, on slopes and along streambanks through southern and central Ontario, usually as a scattered tree rather than a stand.\n\nIt is listed as Endangered in Ontario and protected under provincial law, so it should not be cut or damaged. The cause is butternut canker, a fungal disease that girdles branches and trunks and has killed a large share of the population, with no easy remedy. Healthy-looking trees are worth reporting, since disease-resistant individuals matter to recovery work. Summer and fall, when the nuts form and ripen, are the seasons you are most likely to notice one still standing.",
  "hackberry": "The bark tells you first. Grey and covered in corky, warty ridges and bumps, it is unlike anything else in southern Ontario woods. Hackberry grows on floodplains, along shorelines and on dry limestone soils, reaching 12 to 20 metres. The leaves are pointed and noticeably lopsided at the base, one side meeting the stalk lower than the other.\n\nSmall berry-like fruits ripen dark in fall, each about the size of a pea, with a thin sweetish flesh over a hard stone. They cling to the twigs well into winter after the leaves are gone, which makes the tree a reliable food source for waxwings, robins and other birds through the cold months. It is not a common tree here and tends to turn up singly along rivers and lakeshores. Summer and fall are when it stands out, though the warty bark identifies it year-round.",
  "sassafras": "One tree, three leaf shapes. On a single sassafras you will find plain ovals, two-lobed mittens and three-lobed leaves, all aromatic when crushed. The twigs are green and smell spicy when scratched. This is a southern, Carolinian species at the northern edge of its range in southwestern Ontario, where it grows in dry sandy woods and old fields, often spreading into thickets from root suckers. Reaching 10 to 20 metres, it turns clear orange and red in fall.\n\nThe plant has a long history of use for tea and flavouring, but caution is warranted here. The root bark and its oil contain safrole, a compound suspected of causing cancer and now restricted in food, so do not brew or eat any part of it. The tree itself is harmless to touch and pleasant to smell. Late summer and fall, with the mixed leaf shapes and strong colour, are the best times to know it.",
  "shagbark-hickory": "Mature bark peels away in long, curling vertical strips that hang loose from the trunk, and nothing else in southern Ontario looks quite like it. That shaggy plating gives the tree its name and its clearest field mark. Shagbark hickory grows in dry to moist upland forests, reaching 20 to 30 metres, with compound leaves usually carrying five leaflets.\n\nThe nuts are sweet and edible, held in a thick husk that splits into four sections when ripe, though squirrels and other animals usually reach them first. The loose bark matters to wildlife as well: bats roost in the gaps behind the peeling strips through the warmer months. Fall is when the tree comes into its own, dropping nuts and turning a warm yellow, but the bark reads clearly at any time of year. Young trees are smooth-barked and harder to place until they mature.",
  "tulip-tree": "The leaves settle identification on their own: four lobes with a broad, squared-off notch at the tip, as if the end had been clipped flat. In late spring the tree opens large tulip-shaped flowers, greenish with a band of orange at the base, though they sit high in the crown and are easy to miss from the ground.\n\nThis is one of the tallest hardwoods in eastern North America, reaching 25 to 35 metres, and in Canada it grows only in the rich, moist Carolinian woods of southern Ontario. It grows fast and straight, with a tall clean trunk, and it belongs to the magnolia family rather than the poplars that its other name, yellow-poplar, suggests. Spring for the flowers and fall for the clear yellow leaves are the times it stands out most. Look for it in the deep soils of the far southwest.",
  "white-oak": "Rounded leaf lobes, with no bristle tips, place this in the white oak group, and the pale, light-grey bark flaking in loose scales confirms it. White oak grows in dry to moist upland woods across southern Ontario, reaching 20 to 30 metres and living a long time. It grows slowly, and old open-grown trees develop broad, spreading crowns.\n\nThe wood is dense and, unlike red oak, has closed cells that hold liquid, which is why it was prized for barrels, ships and other work that had to stay watertight. Acorns ripen in a single season and carry less bitter tannin than red oak acorns, making them a favoured food for deer, turkeys and jays. Fall is the season to look, when the leaves turn a dull red-brown and often hang on the tree into winter.",
  "blue-vervain": "Look for a candelabra of thin, upright spikes in wet, sunny ground and you have probably found blue vervain. Each spike is ringed with small blue-purple flowers that open a few at a time from the bottom up, so at any moment only a narrow band is in bloom. That slow-climbing ring is the easiest way to know it. The stems are square in cross-section and rough to the touch, a trait it shares with the mints.\n\nIt grows in wet meadows, marsh edges, ditches, and along stream banks across southern and central Ontario, flowering through summer and into fall. The flowers draw bees and other insects, and the seed heads stand well after the colour is gone. It is native and common wherever the ground stays damp. Nothing much up close, but a stand of it catches the eye from a distance.",
  "blue-eyed-grass": "The name misleads. This is not a grass but a member of the iris family, and the grass-like look comes only from its flat, narrow leaves. The flowers give it away: small blue-violet stars with a yellow centre, each segment tipped with a tiny point. They open only in bright sun and close on cloudy days or by afternoon, so a patch is easy to walk past.\n\nFind it in moist meadows, open fields, and along shorelines through southern and central Ontario, blooming in late spring and early summer. The plants are small, often under 45 cm, and grow in loose tufts rather than showy clumps. Look low and in good light. Once you learn the flower you start noticing it in places you had overlooked.",
  "boneset": "The joined leaves settle the identification. Each pair meets around the stem so the stem appears to grow straight through a single long leaf, an odd trait shared by few other plants. Above sit fuzzy, flat-topped clusters of dull white flowers. It stands in wet ground - marshes, ditches, wet meadows, and shorelines - across southern and central Ontario, flowering from mid-summer into fall.\n\nThe name comes from old medicine, not from bones knitting. It was once a common folk remedy for fevers and aches. That history is worth knowing but not worth acting on: the plant contains compounds that can be toxic in quantity, and it is not something to eat or brew at home. Left alone in a wet meadow it does useful work, feeding late-season bees and flies when many other flowers have finished.",
  "bunchberry": "A dogwood that never leaves the ground. Bunchberry spreads in low mats on the forest floor, each stem carrying a whorl of oval, strongly veined leaves and, in the centre, what looks like a single white flower. The four white petals are actually bracts; the real flowers are the tiny cluster they surround. In late summer a tight bunch of red berries replaces them, which is where the name comes from.\n\nThose tiny flowers are built to move. They snap open explosively to fling their pollen, among the fastest movements known in any plant, though you are unlikely to catch it by eye. Look for bunchberry in cool, moist conifer and mixed woods and along bog edges, most common in central and northern Ontario but present in the south as well. It flowers in late spring and early summer. The red berries are edible but bland, more a curiosity than a food.",
  "butter-and-eggs": "Miniature snapdragons packed on a short spike, pale yellow with a deeper orange throat, are the mark of this plant. The two colours give it the old name butter-and-eggs. Narrow, blue-green leaves crowd the stem below. Each flower is closed at the mouth and holds its nectar behind a long spur, so only strong insects like bumblebees can force their way in.\n\nIt is a European introduction, now common along roadsides, railway lines, field edges, and other dry, open ground across southern and central Ontario. It flowers through summer and into fall and spreads by both seed and creeping roots, which lets it hold ground once established. Not a native plant, but not an aggressive problem in most settings either. Easy to recognize and easy to find once the roadside verges warm up.",
  "canada-mayflower": "Few woodland plants carpet the ground as thoroughly as this one. Across large patches of conifer and mixed woods it forms a dense green cover, most plants showing just a single shiny, heart-shaped leaf. The flowering stems carry two or three leaves and a short spike of small white star-shaped flowers. The flower parts come in fours, which is unusual for a member of the lily family.\n\nBy late summer the flowers give way to speckled pale berries that ripen to red. It is widespread across Ontario and flowers in late spring and early summer. The berries are best left alone: eaten in quantity they can cause stomach upset. For most people this is a plant to notice underfoot rather than to gather. Once you know the leaf you will see it almost everywhere the canopy is right.",
  "chicory": "Sky-blue flowers on a stiff, nearly leafless stem give chicory away at highway speed. Each head has square-tipped petals with a fringed edge, set close against the branching stems. The colour is unusual among roadside flowers and hard to mistake once you know it. Look on dry, gravelly waste ground, old fields, and roadsides.\n\nThe flowers keep short hours. They open in the morning and usually close by midday, so a plant that looked full of bloom at breakfast can seem bare by afternoon. Chicory is a European import, long grown for its root, which is roasted and ground as a coffee substitute or additive. It flowers through summer and into fall across southern and central Ontario and turns up wherever the soil is poor and disturbed. The plant is tough and common. It is one of the easier wildflowers for a beginner to name.",
  "coltsfoot": "One of the first flowers of the Ontario spring, often up while the ground is still cold. The yellow, dandelion-like heads appear on short, scaly stalks with no leaves in sight. That bare stalk is the tell: a true dandelion has a smooth hollow stem, while coltsfoot's is covered in small overlapping scales. The large, hoof-shaped leaves that give the plant its name come up only after the flowers have faded.\n\nIt is introduced from Europe and now spreads along roadsides, ditches, streambanks, and other disturbed ground across southern and central Ontario. It has a long history in folk medicine, particularly for coughs, but that use carries real risk. The plant contains pyrrolizidine alkaloids, which can harm the liver, so it is not safe to eat or brew. Worth watching for as an early sign of the season, not for gathering.",
  "common-blue-violet": "A familiar flower of spring lawns and moist woods, low and easy to overlook until it blooms. Both the heart-shaped leaves and the five-petalled flowers rise on their own stalks straight from the base, with no leafy stem between them. The flowers run from purple to blue, and the lower petals carry white throats marked with dark veins that guide insects in.\n\nThere is more to it than the spring show. Later in the season it produces a second kind of flower near the ground that never opens and simply pollinates itself, a reliable way to set seed even in a poor year. It grows in moist woods, meadows, streambanks, and ordinary lawns across southern and central Ontario. Common and native, it spreads easily and is one of the plants many people first learn to name as children.",
  "common-evening-primrose": "This one keeps to the evening. Its four-petalled yellow flowers open toward dusk and are worked by night-flying moths, then fade by the following afternoon, so a plant seen only in daylight can look half spent. The flowers are lightly scented and sit near the top of a tall, leafy stem that can reach two metres on good ground.\n\nIt is native to Ontario and favours sandy or gravelly open ground, old fields, and roadsides. Flowering runs through summer into fall, with new blooms opening night after night up the stem. After frost the dried stalks and their seed capsules stand through winter and feed birds such as goldfinches. To see it at its best, walk past a patch in the last light of a summer evening rather than at noon.",
  "common-mullein": "Hard to miss in a dry field: a single stout spike of yellow flowers rising a metre or more above everything around it, out of a ground-hugging rosette of large, soft, grey-green leaves. The whole plant feels like felt, and that woolly coat is the surest way to know it. In its first year mullein is only the rosette; the tall flowering spike comes up in the second year, after which the plant dies.\n\nIt is a European introduction found on dry, disturbed ground - fields, gravel pits, and roadsides - throughout southern and central Ontario, flowering in summer. A single spike can shed hundreds of thousands of seeds that stay alive in the soil for decades, which is why it turns up so readily on bare ground. The fine leaf hairs can irritate skin, eyes, and throat, so handle it lightly and keep it away from your face.",
  "common-yarrow": "Feathery leaves and a flat-topped head of tiny flowers make yarrow easy to learn. The foliage is soft and so finely divided it looks almost fern-like, and crushing it releases a sharp, spicy smell. The flowers are usually white, sometimes pale pink, packed into flat clusters on stiff stems. It grows in dry open ground - fields, lawns, roadsides - throughout Ontario and flowers from summer into fall.\n\nThe plant has a long medicinal history. The leaves were used to slow bleeding from wounds, recorded in old names like soldier's woundwort, and the scientific name honours the Greek hero Achilles. That is folklore worth knowing rather than instruction: handling yarrow can bring on a skin rash in some people, more so in sunlight. Native and common, it persists even in mown lawns, coming back again from the cut stems.",
  "dutchmans-breeches": "Look for a row of little white flowers shaped like upside-down pants hanging along an arching stalk. That shape gives the plant its name. The leaves are finely divided and fern-like, and the whole plant rises from a cluster of small pink bulblets. This is a spring plant of rich moist deciduous forests and rocky wooded slopes across southern and central Ontario. It flowers early, before the tree canopy closes, then dies back for the year.\n\nThe nectar sits deep in two long spurs, so most of the pollinating is done by long-tongued bumblebees that are out early in the season. Shorter-tongued insects often cannot reach the reward. The foliage contains toxic alkaloids and can irritate the skin, so handle it lightly and do not eat any part of it.",
  "false-solomons-seal": "An arching, unbranched stem with alternate oval leaves, ending in a branched plume of many small creamy-white flowers. That plume at the tip is the quickest way to tell it from true Solomon's seal, which hangs its flowers in pairs along the underside of the stem. By late summer the flowers give way to green berries speckled with copper, and these ripen to ruby red.\n\nYou meet it in moist woods and shaded forest edges through southern and central Ontario. It flowers in spring and holds its fruit into late summer. Birds take the ripe berries, but for people they can act as a laxative if eaten in quantity, so leave them be. The stem stays green and arching well after the flowers are gone, which makes the plant easy to find even out of bloom.",
  "fireweed": "After a fire or on freshly cleared ground, this is often the first plant to move in and bloom, which is where the name comes from. A tall spike of pink to magenta flowers opens from the bottom up, each with four petals, set against narrow willow-like leaves that run up the stem. Stands can be large, sometimes colouring whole hillsides.\n\nIt grows on burned ground, in clearings, and along roadsides across Ontario, and is common in the north. Flowering runs through summer, and as the season moves along you can read its progress by how far up the spike the blooms have reached. Once the flowers finish, the seed pods split to release seeds carried on fine white hairs, which is how it reaches new openings so quickly.",
  "foamflower": "A low cluster of maple-like lobed leaves sits at the base, and above them rises a slender spike of tiny white flowers whose long stamens push out past the petals. Those stamens are what give the flower head its soft, frothy look and the plant its name. It spreads by runners, so where you find one plant you usually find a patch.\n\nFoamflower belongs to moist rich woods, streambanks, and shaded slopes in southern and central Ontario, and it flowers in spring. It stays low to the ground and often carpets the forest floor where the soil is damp and shaded. The lobed leaves persist well after the flowers fade, so the colonies stay easy to pick out through the growing season. It asks for shade and steady moisture, which is why you rarely see it in the open.",
  "harebell": "Thin, wiry stems carry nodding blue-violet bells that sway in the lightest breeze. The stem leaves are narrow, and the round basal leaves that give the species its Latin name usually wither before flowering, so you may not see them. Each bell is about two centimetres long.\n\nHarebell holds on in tough places. It grows on rocky shores and cliffs, on dry banks, and in thin soil over rock, and turns up along the Great Lakes and across the Canadian Shield. It flowers through summer and into fall, later than most of the spring woodland plants. Despite the delicate look, it is a hardy thing that roots into cracks and shallow soil where little else will grow. It is native and not at risk.",
  "large-flowered-bellwort": "Everything about this plant droops, even when it is perfectly healthy. Bell-shaped yellow flowers with twisted petals hang from the tip of an arching stem, and the stem appears to pass straight through the base of each leaf. That leaf arrangement is called perfoliate, and it is a reliable field mark. The slightly wilted look is normal and not a sign of trouble.\n\nIt grows in rich deciduous forests and on wooded slopes with moist soil through southern and central Ontario, flowering in spring before the canopy fills in. After flowering the stem straightens and the leaves broaden as the plant puts on size for the year. Look for it on the same rich forest floors that hold trilliums and other spring woodland flowers.",
  "mayapple": "Colonies of umbrella-like leaves, each deeply lobed, spread across the forest floor in large patches. The plants come in two forms. A single-leaf plant does not flower, while a plant with two leaves carries one nodding white flower in the fork between them. The flower is easy to miss because the leaves hide it from above.\n\nMayapple grows in moist deciduous woods and shaded clearings in southern Ontario, flowering in spring and setting fruit through summer. Only the fully ripe yellow fruit is edible, and even then only in moderation. The leaves, roots, and unripe fruit contain podophyllotoxin and are poisonous if eaten. Because the ripe and unripe fruit look different and the timing matters, this is not a plant to sample casually. If you are not certain the fruit is fully ripe, leave it alone.",
  "ox-eye-daisy": "The plain white daisy of open ground, one flower head per stem, with white ray petals set around a flat yellow disc. The dark green leaves are spoon-shaped and toothed, shrinking as they climb the mostly unbranched stem. It wants full sun.\n\nBrought over from Europe, it has become one of the most common wildflowers along Ontario roadsides, and it fills fields, meadows, and pastures as well. A single plant can set more than a thousand seeds in a season, which is part of why it spreads so freely. Flowering runs through summer. It is introduced rather than native, but well established and not considered a problem in most settings. Farmers sometimes treat it as a weed in pasture, since livestock tend to leave it alone.",
  "queen-annes-lace": "A flat, lacy umbrella of tiny white flowers sits on top of a hairy stem, often with a single dark purple floret at the very centre. As the seeds ripen the whole cluster folds inward into a cup shape, sometimes called a bird's nest. Crush the root and it smells of carrot, which fits - this is the wild ancestor of the garden carrot.\n\nIt grows in dry fields, meadows, and roadsides in open sun across southern and central Ontario, flowering from summer into fall. Do not eat it. It closely resembles poison hemlock and water hemlock, two plants that are deadly, and telling them apart takes real care and experience. A mistake can kill. The sap can also cause a skin rash when the skin is later exposed to sunlight. Enjoy the flower where it stands and keep it out of your mouth.",
  "solomons-seal": "An arching, unbranched stem hung with alternate oval leaves, and beneath it small greenish bell-shaped flowers dangling in pairs along the underside. That paired arrangement under the stem is the difference from false Solomon's seal, which carries its flowers in a plume at the tip. Blue-black berries form later in the season.\n\nThe name comes from round scars on the underground rootstock, left where old stems broke away year after year, which someone thought resembled a wax seal. It grows in rich moist woods and on shaded slopes in southern and central Ontario, flowering in spring and holding fruit into summer. The blue-black berries are toxic if eaten, so admire them but do not taste them.",
  "spotted-jewelweed": "Orange trumpet-shaped flowers dotted with reddish spots hang from thin stalks over wet, shady ground. The stems are translucent and watery and snap easily between your fingers. Touch a ripe seed pod and it bursts open, flinging seeds a surprising distance, which is why the plant is also called touch-me-not.\n\nIt grows along streams, ditches, and springs throughout Ontario, in the damp shade it prefers, and flowers from summer into fall. The watery sap is a well-known folk remedy for poison ivy rash, and jewelweed often shares the same wet ground as poison ivy, so the two are worth learning together. It is native and not at risk. Hummingbirds and long-tongued bees work the flowers for nectar.",
  "spring-beauty": "Small and low to the ground, with five white to pink petals lined by darker pink veins that guide insects to the centre. A single pair of broad, lance-shaped leaves sits partway up the stem. On cloudy days the flowers stay closed, so it is a plant you see best in sun.\n\nIt carpets rich deciduous woods, forest floors, and shaded slopes in southern and central Ontario, flowering in early spring before the trees leaf out. The small underground corm is edible and was gathered as food by Indigenous peoples. As with any wild food, correct identification matters, and these are tiny plants easily confused with others when not in flower. Like other spring ephemerals it fades back quickly once the canopy closes overhead.",
  "starflower": "Look for a single whorl of pointed leaves near the top of a thin stem, with one or two white flowers held on hair-fine stalks above it. The petals usually number seven, an uncommon count among Ontario wildflowers, and once you notice it the plant is easy to name. It stands only 10 to 20 cm tall and is easy to miss on the forest floor.\n\nStarflower grows in moist conifer and mixed woods and along bog edges, mostly through central and northern Ontario but also in the south. Flowers appear in spring and into summer. The plant tends to spread into loose colonies rather than stand alone, so where you find one you usually find several. Nothing about it is showy, but the seven-pointed white stars are worth stopping for when you come across them.",
  "swamp-milkweed": "Where wet ground meets full sun, you may find tall stems topped with domed clusters of small pink to rose flowers. The leaves are narrow and lance-shaped, and a broken stem oozes the milky sap that gives milkweeds their name. It reaches 1 to 1.5 m and favours marsh edges, wet meadows, ditches, and shorelines across southern and central Ontario. The flowers open in summer.\n\nLike other milkweeds, it is a host plant for monarch caterpillars, which feed on the leaves, and the blooms draw a range of nectar insects while in flower. The milky sap is toxic if eaten and can irritate the skin and eyes, so wash your hands after handling a broken stem and keep the sap away from your face.",
  "turtlehead": "The flowers give this one away. Each white bloom, sometimes tinged pink, is shaped like a turtle's head with the mouth held shut, and several cluster at the top of the stem. Leaves are opposite, narrow, and sharply toothed. Plants stand 40 to 90 cm tall in wet ground.\n\nYou meet turtlehead along stream banks, in wet meadows and marshes, and at swamp edges across southern and central Ontario. It blooms in summer and into fall, later than many wetland flowers. The closed, mouth-like shape means only strong insects such as bumblebees can push inside to reach the nectar. It is also the main host plant in Ontario for the Baltimore checkerspot butterfly, whose caterpillars feed on the leaves, so a patch of turtlehead can be worth watching for the butterfly as much as the bloom.",
  "wild-ginger": "Paired heart-shaped leaves, softly fuzzy, lie low over the ground in rich moist forest, often on a slope. Between them, at ground level in the fork of the two leaf stalks, sits a single brownish-red cup-shaped flower. You usually have to lift the leaves to see it. Held down low, the flower is visited by ground-dwelling insects rather than flying pollinators. The root smells strongly of ginger.\n\nThat smell is misleading. Despite the name and scent, this is not related to culinary ginger and is not a safe substitute for it. The plant contains aristolochic acid, which can damage the kidneys, so it should not be eaten or brewed as a tea. Wild ginger grows in southern Ontario and flowers in spring. Enjoy the scent if you find it, but leave it at that.",
  "wild-sarsaparilla": "A single long-stalked leaf rises from the ground and divides into three groups of oval leaflets, spreading into a low leafy carpet across the forest floor. Below the leaf, on its own shorter stalk, sit rounded clusters of small greenish-white flowers that are easy to overlook. Because the flowers hide beneath the foliage, many people know the plant only by its leaf.\n\nIt grows in dry to moist woods and forest edges and is widespread across Ontario, flowering in spring and summer. The aromatic roots were once used as a flavouring and as a substitute for true sarsaparilla in root beer. That is history rather than a recommendation. The plant is common and unremarkable in flower, but its even green carpets are a familiar sight in Ontario woods, and the divided leaf is easy to learn.",
  "wild-strawberry": "Three toothed leaflets and white five-petalled flowers with a yellow centre mark this low plant of open ground. It grows in fields, meadows, roadsides, clearings, and open woods, only 5 to 15 cm tall, and is widespread across Ontario. Flowers come in spring, followed by small red berries in early summer.\n\nThe berries are edible and are often sweeter and more strongly scented than anything from a store. The seeds sit in tiny pits on the surface, which helps separate the real thing from the mock strawberry, a harmless lookalike with a yellow flower and a dry, tasteless fruit. There is no dangerous poisonous double here, but as with any wild food, be sure of what you are picking and take only from clean ground away from roadside spray.",
  "wintergreen": "A low evergreen of acidic conifer and mixed woods, often under pines, with shiny oval leaves clustered near the top of a short stem. Small white nodding urn-shaped flowers appear in summer, and bright red berries follow and persist right through winter. Crush a leaf and it smells strongly of mint.\n\nThat smell is oil of wintergreen, held in the leaves and berries. The berries have long been nibbled in small amounts and the leaves brewed for their flavour, but the concentrated oil is methyl salicylate and is toxic in large amounts, so this is a plant to sample lightly if at all, not to consume in quantity. It grows through central and northern Ontario. The evergreen leaves and persistent red berries make it easy to find in the cold months when little else stands out on the forest floor.",
  "wood-anemone": "A single white flower on a slender stalk, set above a whorl of deeply cut, toothed leaves, is the whole plant. What look like petals are actually five to nine petal-like sepals. The flower closes at night and in cool cloudy weather and reopens when the sun returns, so a patch can look bare on a grey morning and be open by afternoon.\n\nWood anemone grows in moist woods, thickets, and clearings across southern and central Ontario, flowering in spring before the canopy fills in. It often spreads into loose colonies. The sap contains protoanemonin, which can irritate the skin and is toxic if eaten, so it is a plant to look at rather than pick or taste. Small and easy to pass by, but worth a second look when the flowers are open to the sun.",
  "butterfly-milkweed": "Flat-topped clusters of bright orange flowers sit atop hairy stems, unmistakable when in bloom. Unlike its relatives, a broken stem gives clear sap rather than the usual milky white, which is a useful check. It favours dry sandy fields, prairies, and open banks and stands 30 to 90 cm tall.\n\nIn Ontario it is mostly limited to sandy areas in the south, chiefly the Carolinian zone, and it flowers in summer. The orange blooms draw butterflies heavily, and like other milkweeds it is a host plant for monarch caterpillars. All parts are toxic if eaten in quantity, so it is one to plant for pollinators and watch, not to sample. It grows well on dry sandy soil and is often used in native pollinator plantings.",
  "great-blue-lobelia": "Picture the red cardinal flower, then swap the red for blue. This is its blue counterpart, a dense spike of bright blue tubular flowers, each with a white-striped lower lip, on a stem 30 cm to 1 m tall. The two sometimes grow side by side in the same wet ground. Individual flowers are about 2.5 cm long.\n\nIt grows in wet meadows, on stream banks, and at marsh edges across southern and central Ontario, flowering in summer and into fall. The tubular flowers are pollinated mainly by bumblebees, which are heavy enough to work their way in. All parts are toxic if eaten, so keep it to looking. It suits a wet, sunny corner of a native planting, where the blue reads well against later-season growth.",
  "heath-aster": "By fall, this aster forms a bushy plant crowded with many tiny white flower heads, each with a small yellow centre and barely a centimetre across. The short, arching side branches are lined with stiff little leaves, and the whole plant can take on a frosted look when in full bloom. It stands anywhere from 30 cm to 1 m tall.\n\nHeath aster is a plant of dry open ground - fields, prairies, and alvars - in southern Ontario, and it flowers late, when much else has already finished. That timing makes the mass of small blooms a useful nectar source for insects heading into the cold. It handles poor, dry soil well, which is part of why it turns up on alvars and other thin-soiled open sites where less hardy plants struggle.",
  "nodding-trillium": "At first glance this can look like a plant with three broad leaves and no flower. Lift the leaves and the flower is there, a white bloom hanging on a bent stalk below the whorl, hidden from anyone looking straight down. The petals curve back to show dark pink or purple anthers at the centre.\n\nNodding trillium grows in moist rich woods, swamps, and along streambanks, and it ranges through southern, central, and northern Ontario. It flowers in spring. Standing 20 to 50 cm tall, it is easy to walk past because the flower stays out of sight beneath the leaves. As with other trilliums, the plant is slow to recover if picked, since the leaves feed the roots for the following year, so it is best left in place and simply looked under.",
  "painted-trillium": "Ontario has several white trilliums, and this one sorts itself out by a single mark. Each of the three white petals carries a bright pink or red V at the base, pointing inward, and that inverted V is the reliable field mark. The petal edges are wavy rather than smooth, and the pointed leaves sit on short stalks rather than clasping the stem. It stands 20 to 40 cm tall.\n\nLook for it in spring in cool conifer and mixed woods, and along bog edges, always on acidic soil. In practice that means central Ontario, including Georgian Bay and Muskoka. It is not at risk, but like all trilliums it is slow to recover from picking. The flower and leaves grow from a single shoot above the root, so removing them starves the plant. Leave it where it stands and it may bloom in the same spot for years.",
  "pink-ladys-slipper": "This is a wild orchid, and it announces itself with a single pink pouch-shaped flower split by a slit down the front. The bloom rises alone on a leafless stalk above two broad ribbed leaves at the base. It reaches 20 to 40 cm tall.\n\nYou meet it in spring and into summer in acidic pine and mixed woods and along bog edges, mostly through central and northern Ontario. It is slow to establish. The seed needs a particular soil fungus to germinate, and a plant can take many years to flower for the first time. Digging one up almost never works, because the roots and their fungus do not transplant, so leave it in the ground.\n\nHandle it carefully if at all. Glandular hairs on the leaves and stem can irritate skin in some people, much as poison ivy does. Not at risk, but easy to lose from a site once it is disturbed.",
  "purple-pitcher-plant": "A carnivorous plant of open bogs, it feeds by waiting. The leaves form a rosette of hollow, red-veined, pitcher-shaped cups that fill with rainwater and trap insects, which the plant digests to make up for the thin nutrition of bog soil. A single deep red flower nods on a tall leafless stalk above the rosette. Pitchers run 10 to 20 cm; the flower stalk can reach 20 to 60 cm.\n\nLook in summer, and only in open sphagnum bogs and fens. That habitat restriction is the surest clue, since nothing else in an Ontario bog looks like it. It grows in bogs and fens across the province, including the north. It is the floral emblem of Newfoundland and Labrador, but it is native and reasonably common here too.\n\nNative and not at risk. The bogs it depends on are fragile, and walking on sphagnum compresses it, so keep to firm ground or boardwalks where they exist.",
  "round-leaved-sundew": "Small and easy to walk past, this is another of Ontario's carnivorous bog plants. It forms a low rosette of tiny round leaves set flat in wet sphagnum. Each leaf is covered in red hairs tipped with sticky droplets that catch small insects and digest them, which is how the plant feeds where the soil gives little. Small white flowers open along a stalk that uncurls as it rises above the leaves. The rosette is only 3 to 8 cm wide; the flower stalk reaches 5 to 25 cm.\n\nSummer is the time to find it, in open sphagnum bogs, fens, and wet peaty shores across the province. Get down close, because at a glance it reads as a red smudge on the moss. Native and not at risk. As with all bog plants, the ground it grows in is easily damaged underfoot, so tread lightly and keep to firmer footing.",
  "showy-ladys-slipper": "Among the largest native orchids in Ontario, and hard to mistake in bloom. A big white pouch flushed with rose-pink around the opening sits below white upper petals and sepals. The leafy stem is tall, 40 to 80 cm, and carries several ribbed hairy leaves. Plants often grow in clumps of many stems.\n\nIt flowers in summer in fens, swamps, and wet conifer woods, always on calcium-rich soil, mostly through eastern and northern Ontario. That soil requirement keeps it patchy. It is not at risk provincially, but individual colonies are slow-growing and worth leaving undisturbed.\n\nBe careful about contact. The glandular hairs on the leaves and stem can raise a rash much like poison ivy in people who are sensitive, so admire it without handling it. Digging or transplanting fails, as with the other lady's-slippers, because the roots depend on soil fungi.",
  "tall-sunflower": "One of the tallest wildflowers native to Ontario, this one can reach three metres in a good damp meadow. The stem is rough to the touch and topped by yellow sunflower heads with yellowish centres. The narrow leaves are also rough. Heads run 5 to 8 cm across. Height and that sandpapery stem separate it from the big-headed garden sunflowers, which are annuals grown from seed.\n\nLook for it in summer and into fall in damp meadows, thickets, and along marsh edges, mostly across southern and central Ontario. By late season it stands well above most of the plants around it.\n\nNative and not at risk. Several native sunflowers overlap in range and can be hard to tell apart, so if you want a firm identification, note the wet habitat, the rough narrow leaves, and the plain yellowish centre rather than a dark disk. For casual watching, the height alone usually points the way.",
  "wood-lily": "Most of Ontario's native lilies nod, their flowers hanging down. This one does the opposite and holds its bloom straight up, facing the sky, which is the quickest way to name it. The flower is orange to red, 5 to 8 cm across, with dark spots near the base and petals that narrow to a point at the bottom. The plant stands 30 to 90 cm tall.\n\nSummer is its season, in open woods, meadows, and along sandy shorelines across central and southern Ontario. It turns up in bright, open ground more than in deep shade.\n\nNative and not at risk, but worth leaving alone. The flowering stem is the plant's only leafy shoot, so picking the flower usually kills the whole plant rather than just costing it a bloom. A photograph takes nothing from it. Left in place, it will stand in the same ground the next year.",
  "yellow-ladys-slipper": "A bright yellow pouch is the centrepiece of this woodland orchid, with twisted brownish or greenish side petals spiralling out from it. Broad ribbed leaves clasp the leafy stem, which stands 20 to 60 cm tall. The pouch is not just for show. It works as a trap, funnelling a visiting bee down past the flower's pollen and out a narrow back exit, so the insect leaves dusted and carries pollen to the next plant.\n\nYou can find it in spring and summer in moist woods, fens, swamps, and shaded slopes with rich soil. It is the most widespread of Ontario's lady's-slippers, turning up across the province wherever the ground suits it.\n\nNot at risk, though the glandular hairs on the plant can irritate skin in some people, so look without handling. Like its relatives it depends on soil fungi and does not survive transplanting, so leave any you find rooted where they are.",
  "bearberry": "A ground-hugging evergreen that mats over bare sand and rock. It rarely rises above 15 cm. Instead it trails flat, with small paddle-shaped leaves that stay green right through the winter. Pink and white urn-shaped flowers open in spring and give way to dull red berries later in the year.\n\nLook for it on dry, open ground: sand, dunes, rock barrens, and the bare Shield outcrops that run across much of Ontario. It holds these harsh, exposed sites where taller plants struggle, and it turns up on sandy and rocky ground across the province.\n\nNative and common. The red berries are dry and mealy and have a long history of use, but they are easy to confuse with other red berries on low plants, and firm identification matters before anyone eats a wild berry. If you are not certain what you are looking at, treat it as one to admire rather than sample.",
  "common-elderberry": "A shrub of damp, disturbed ground, 1 to 3 m tall, common along ditches, streambanks, roadsides, and field edges. The compound leaves carry 5 to 11 toothed leaflets. In summer it puts up broad flat-topped clusters of tiny white flowers, and by late summer these hang as heavy drooping clusters of purple-black berries.\n\nThe fruit is well known, but it comes with a real caution. Raw berries, along with the leaves, bark, and roots, contain cyanide-forming compounds and can make you sick. The berries are safe only when cooked, which breaks those compounds down, so nothing off this plant should be eaten raw. There are also similar-looking shrubs with red or otherwise coloured fruit that are not the same thing, so certain identification matters before you cook or eat anything.\n\nNative and not at risk. Found through southern and central Ontario. Left alone, a stand feeds birds heavily in late summer.",
  "labrador-tea": "A low evergreen shrub of cold, wet ground, 0.3 to 1 m tall. The surest field mark is underneath the leaves. The edges roll under, and the underside carries a dense coat of rusty hairs. White flower clusters top the stems in early summer, and it keeps its leathery leaves through the winter.\n\nIts home is open bogs, peatlands, and cold coniferous swamps, most common across the northern peatlands but present through much of Ontario. In a bog it often grows among leatherleaf and other low shrubs, and the rusty leaf undersides set it apart.\n\nNative and not at risk. The plant has a long history as a brewed tea, but that history comes with limits worth taking seriously. The leaves contain compounds that can be harmful if the brew is made strong or drunk in quantity. It can also be confused with other bog shrubs, so sure identification matters before anything from it goes in a cup.",
  "meadowsweet": "An upright shrub of wet open ground, 0.6 to 1.2 m tall, with narrow toothed leaves. In summer it carries dense pyramid-shaped clusters of small flowers, white to pale pink, held above the foliage. The blooming runs through mid to late summer.\n\nYou find it in wet meadows, marsh edges, moist fields, and ditches across southern and central Ontario. It often stands in old fields and damp ground left to grow, mixed in with grasses and other low shrubs.\n\nNative and not at risk, with no particular caution attached. The narrow, pyramid-shaped flower clusters and the wet-ground habitat are the features to fix on. A close relative, steeplebush, shares the habit but carries pink flowers in a narrower spike and has woolly leaf undersides, so a look at the flower cluster and the underside of a leaf settles which one you have.",
  "nannyberry": "A shrub that often grows tall enough to pass for a small tree, nannyberry turns up in moist woods, along streambanks, and in the fencerows between fields. The leaves are finely toothed and taper to a long, drawn-out point, and they sit on leaf stalks with a narrow wing running down each side. That winged stalk is a reliable way to separate it from other viburnums. By fall the shrub carries blue-black fruit hanging on reddish stalks.\n\nThe ripe fruit is edible and sweet, closer to a raisin than a fresh berry, though each one holds a single large flat seed that takes up most of the space. It is a southern and central Ontario plant, common enough at forest edges once you learn the leaf. Flowering comes in spring, and the fruit follows in fall.",
  "ninebark": "The name gives away the best field mark. On older stems the bark peels off in thin papery layers, one over another, and the count is supposed to run to nine. Ninebark grows along riverbanks, on rocky shores, and in moist thickets, often right at the water's edge. The leaves are rounded and lobed, a little like a currant leaf, and in summer the shrub carries tight clusters of white flowers that later dry into reddish seed pods.\n\nIt is native across southern and central Ontario. Nothing about it is edible, but the peeling bark and the dry pods make it easy to name once the flowers are gone. Cultivated forms are common in gardens and plantings. In the wild the shrub keeps to rocky shores and riverbanks.",
  "northern-wild-raisin": "Also called witherod, northern wild raisin is a shrub of cool, wet ground: swamps, the edges of bogs, moist woods, and shaded thickets. The leaves are dull rather than glossy and finely toothed around the edge. Watch the fruit through the season. It passes from green through pink and red before it finally ripens blue-black, and a single cluster often shows several colours at once.\n\nThe berries dry and wrinkle right on the shrub, which is where the wild raisin name comes from. This is more a central and northern Ontario plant than a southern one, and it favours the acidic, boggy places where many other shrubs thin out. Flowering is in summer, with fruit following into fall.",
  "red-elderberry": "Red elderberry looks much like common elderberry at a glance, with the same compound leaves divided into toothed leaflets, but the flower cluster tells them apart. Here it is domed rather than flat-topped, and the fruit that follows is bright red, not purple-black. It also runs ahead of common elderberry on the calendar, blooming and setting fruit earlier in the season. Look for it in moist woods, clearings, on rocky slopes, and along forest edges, right across Ontario.\n\nThe red berries can be tempting, but treat this plant with care. Raw berries, along with the seeds, leaves, and stems, are toxic and must not be eaten raw. The fruit is sometimes cooked and strained for use, but that takes knowledge and correct handling. If you are not certain of both the identification and the preparation, leave it alone.",
  "sheep-laurel": "Low and wiry, sheep laurel rarely tops a metre and grows on ground that starves out most shrubs: open bogs, acidic barrens, and bare sandy or rocky flats. The narrow leathery leaves sit in whorls of three, and in early summer deep pink cup-shaped flowers open in a neat ring around the stem, just below the newest leaves. That ring of bloom below fresh growth is the clearest mark.\n\nThe plant is also called lambkill, and the name is a warning. Every part of sheep laurel is poisonous if eaten, a danger to livestock and to people alike. It is native through central and eastern Ontario and up into the north. Look at the flowers and leave the plant untouched. There is nothing here to forage.",
  "skunk-currant": "Crush a leaf or a stem and this one names itself. The smell is plainly skunky, and it lingers. Skunk currant is a low, sprawling shrub, usually under a metre, with no spines anywhere on it. It grows in cool moist woods, on rocky slopes, and around the edges of bogs, mostly through central and northern Ontario. The berries are red and covered in short bristly hairs.\n\nThe lack of spines separates it from the gooseberries, and the odour separates it from nearly everything else. The bristly berries are not worth gathering and are best left for wildlife. It is the smell, more than any single leaf or flower, that fixes the identification in the field.",
  "snowberry": "White berries are the giveaway. Through fall and into winter, snowberry holds clusters of round, waxy white fruit on its bare twigs, well after the leaves have gone. The leaves themselves are oval and blue-green, set on slender stems, and the shrub stands somewhere between knee and shoulder height. It grows in dry open woods, on rocky slopes, and along riverbanks across southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe berries look almost artificial and often draw curiosity, but they are mildly toxic to people and can cause stomach upset if eaten in any quantity. They feed some birds, not us. As a winter marker on an otherwise bare shrub, though, the white fruit is hard to mistake for anything else.",
  "steeplebush": "The flower spike is shaped like a church steeple, narrow and tapering, and packed with fuzzy rose-pink flowers. Steeplebush is a modest shrub of wet ground, found in wet meadows, along bog margins, and in damp old fields, rarely much over a metre. Turn a leaf over and the underside is covered in dense wool, white at first and ageing toward brown. That is a good mark when the plant is out of flower.\n\nThe spike opens from the top down, so by midsummer the tip may already be fading while the base is still coming into bloom. That gives an open flower head a two-toned look. It grows across southern and central Ontario and holds its dried seed heads into winter, standing above the meadow grass long after the colour is gone.",
  "wild-black-currant": "Rub a leaf between your fingers and wild black currant announces itself with a strong, resinous smell. The leaves have three to five lobes and, turned over, show scattered yellow resin dots on the underside, a dependable mark for this species. It grows in moist woods, swamps, and along streambanks across southern and central Ontario. The berries ripen black and hang in loose, dangling clusters.\n\nThe ripe black fruit is edible and has a long history of use in jams and preserves. As with any wild edible, be sure of what you have before eating. The resin-dotted leaves and the smell are the features to confirm. Flowering comes in spring, with fruit ripening through summer.",
  "wild-gooseberry": "Short spines at the leaf nodes mark this as a gooseberry rather than a currant. Wild gooseberry is a low shrub, seldom over waist height, with lobed leaves shaped a little like a small maple leaf. The smooth, round berries hang singly or in pairs and ripen from green to a dull purple. Look for it in moist woods, at swamp edges, on rocky slopes, and in clearings across much of Ontario.\n\nThe smooth berries are edible raw or cooked once fully ripe, and they hold their tartness well for the pot. Green ones are sour and firm, so give them time. Watch the spines when you reach in to pick. Flowering is in spring, with fruit through summer.",
  "winterberry": "This is a holly that drops its leaves, which throws people who expect the evergreen kind. Through the growing season winterberry looks unremarkable, with dull toothed leaves, no spines, and grey twigs, and it hides in swamps, wet thickets, damp woods, and along shoreline edges. Its moment comes after leaf fall. Once the leaves drop, tight rows of bright red berries sit close against the bare grey branches, and they carry on into winter.\n\nThose berries are a late-season food for robins and cedar waxwings after most other fruit is gone, which is much of the point of the plant. For people they are mildly toxic and should not be eaten. It is a wetland shrub of southern and central Ontario, easiest to find in the cold months when the red fruit stands out against snow and bare wood.",
  "bog-rosemary": "Despite the name, this has nothing to do with the herb on the spice rack. Bog rosemary is a low shrub of open peatlands, growing on wet peat and sphagnum in bogs and fens where the ground is soft and acidic. The leaves are narrow and blue-green, with edges rolled strongly under and a white, waxy underside. In spring small pink urn-shaped flowers nod in loose clusters at the branch tips.\n\nThe resemblance to the kitchen herb ends at the leaf shape. Every part of bog rosemary is poisonous if eaten, so the name should not tempt anyone. It grows in bogs and fens across central and northern Ontario, low among the moss and easy to walk past. Step carefully in these peatlands. The ground is fragile and slow to recover.",
  "hobblebush": "The name is a warning. Low branches arch out from this forest shrub and root where they touch the ground, forming loops that catch a boot and pitch a walker forward. It grows in cool, moist forests and on shaded slopes across central and eastern Ontario, usually a couple of metres tall.\n\nThe leaves are large and rounded, and in spring the flower clusters are broad and white, ringed at the edge with a few big showy sterile blooms surrounding smaller fertile ones in the centre. That outer ring is the easiest field mark. Once you have tripped over hobblebush a few times you start reading the ground for its arching stems before you step. It is a native understorey plant and no threat beyond the tripping.",
  "spicebush": "Crush a leaf or a twig and you get a strong smell of citrus and spice. That scent is the surest way to know this shrub, which grows two to three metres tall in rich, moist deciduous woods and shaded stream valleys. In Ontario it is a Carolinian plant, restricted to the forests of the far southwest, so most of the province will never see it.\n\nEarly in spring, before the leaves open, small yellow flowers cluster along the bare branches and put a soft yellow haze through the woods. In fall the leaves turn and the female plants carry red berries. A native shrub of the southern hardwood forest, uncommon here because that forest type barely reaches into Canada.",
  "canada-wild-rye": "A cool-season grass of riverbanks, sandy shores, open woods, and roadsides through southern and central Ontario. The seed head is the giveaway. It is thick and bristly, and as the seeds ripen over summer it nods and arches over, so it looks like a curved head of cultivated rye or wheat.\n\nMost grasses hold their seed heads stiff and upright, which makes this drooping, whiskered one stand out at a glance. It reaches knee to chest height and grows in loose stands rather than dense turf. Native and widespread, it is often used in prairie and shoreline plantings because it establishes quickly and holds sandy soil. Nothing here to worry about handling, though the long bristles can work their way into socks.",
  "christmas-fern": "Green in December, which is where the name comes from. This is one of the few Ontario ferns that keeps its fronds through winter, and old custom put them to use as holiday greenery. The fronds are leathery and once-divided, 30 to 80 centimetres long, and they spread from a central crown in a loose clump.\n\nLook closely at a single leaflet and you will see a small lobe or ear at its base, a shape often compared to the toe of a stocking. It favours rocky shaded slopes, ravines, and moist deciduous woods through southern and central Ontario. In winter the flattened green fronds lie against the leaf litter and are easy to find when almost nothing else is green. A native fern, common and harmless.",
  "field-horsetail": "Two different stems come from the same plant. In spring, pale unbranched stalks push up first, each tipped with a small cone that releases spores. These wither, and the green summer stems follow, ringed at intervals with whorls of thin branches for a bottlebrush look. Horsetails are ancient, part of a lineage that reproduces by spores rather than seeds, and their stems are gritty with silica. That grit made them useful for scouring pots and smoothing wood.\n\nField horsetail turns up province-wide on disturbed ground - roadsides, ditches, fields, gravel, and railway embankments - and it can spread stubbornly once established. The plant is toxic to horses and other livestock if eaten in quantity, so it matters in hay and pasture. For people it is mostly a curiosity of waste ground.",
  "ground-pine": "It looks like a pine seedling a hand's-width tall, but it is not a tree at all. This is a clubmoss, a spore-bearing plant far older in lineage than any conifer. Upright shoots, 10 to 25 centimetres high, branch into flat evergreen sprays that really do resemble a tiny pine or cedar.\n\nWhat you see are the tips of a much larger organism. The shoots are linked by underground stems, and a single colony can be decades old. It grows in moist conifer and mixed woods, often on acidic soil, through southern and central Ontario, and it stays green year round. A native clubmoss, old and slow to recover, so a patch is best left in place rather than picked.",
  "hay-scented-fern": "Bruise a frond and it smells of freshly cut hay. That sweet scent, strongest as the fronds dry, is the source of the name and an easy thing to confirm in the field. The fronds are pale yellow-green, lacy, and finely divided, 40 to 80 centimetres tall, and they rarely grow alone.\n\nSpreading by underground stems, hay-scented fern forms dense colonies that can carpet an opening and crowd out other plants. Look for it in open woods, clearings, along roadsides, and on rocky slopes through southern and central Ontario, from spring into fall. The colour is a good clue on its own, a soft slightly yellow green that stands apart from the darker ferns around it. Native and widespread, it is one of the ferns most likely to form a solid stand you can pick out from a distance.",
  "interrupted-fern": "Named for a gap. The tall green fronds rise and arch to over a metre, and partway up the blade a few pairs of leaflets look different from the rest: shrivelled and brown, carrying the spores. They wither early and fall away, leaving a bare interruption in the middle of an otherwise leafy frond.\n\nThat gap is unmistakable once you have seen it, and it separates this fern from its relatives at a glance. It grows in moist woods, clearings, and on roadside banks through southern and central Ontario, with the fertile leaflets showing in spring and early summer before they drop. The plant forms a rounded clump of arching fronds that can stand waist-high or taller. A native woodland fern, common in the right ground and easy to name by the interruption alone.",
  "lady-fern": "An adaptable woodland fern, found in some form across most of Ontario. The fronds are lacy and twice-divided, 40 to 90 centimetres long, and grow in a loose clump rather than a tight vase. Two details help confirm it: thin papery scales along the stalk, and spore cases on the leaflet undersides that are curved and elongated rather than round.\n\nIt takes to a range of damp settings, from moist woods and swamp edges to ditches and shaded streambanks. Because it is so variable and so common, it makes a good baseline to learn early. Once you know lady fern, the odder ferns are easier to tell apart. Native, harmless, and rarely absent from wet shaded ground in Ontario.",
  "maidenhair-fern": "Few ferns look less fern-like at first glance. The frond spreads in a fan or horseshoe, with several fingerlike segments radiating from the top of a single thin stalk, and the whole thing sits almost flat like a green hand held open. The stalk is the other mark: dark, thin, and polished to a purple-black, wiry enough that the plant seems too delicate for the woods it grows in.\n\nIt sheds water so well that the fronds stay dry to the touch even in damp shade. Rich, moist deciduous woods and shaded slopes suit it, mostly in southern and central Ontario. This is a plant worth crouching for, since the layout of the frond is unlike any other fern here. Native and no worry to touch, though like most woodland ferns it does not transplant well and is best left where it grows.",
  "marginal-wood-fern": "The spores tell you which fern this is. Turn a frond over and the round spore dots sit right on the outer edge of each leaflet, along the margin rather than in from it, and that placement gives the plant its name. The fronds are leathery and blue-green, 30 to 75 centimetres long, standing in a neat vase-shaped clump, and they hold their colour through winter.\n\nLook for it on rocky slopes, ledges, and in shaded deciduous woods across southern and central Ontario. Being evergreen, it is one of the ferns you can still identify in the cold months, when the marginal spore dots and the stiff blue-green blades are easy to check. Native and common on rocky ground. No hazard in handling it.",
  "marsh-fern": "Unlike most ferns, this one does not grow in a clump. Slender pale-green fronds rise singly and scattered from creeping underground stems, so a marsh fern colony spreads thinly across wet ground rather than forming a crown. It reaches 30 to 80 centimetres and favours exactly the places the name suggests: marshes, wet meadows, swamps, and ditches, province-wide.\n\nA useful check is the lowest pair of leaflets on each frond, which point slightly forward toward the tip instead of sitting square. The scattered, single-stalked habit is the first thing to notice, since few other ferns of open wetland grow this way. It is present from spring into fall. Native and widespread, and a fair sign that the ground underfoot is genuinely wet.",
  "rock-polypody": "A small evergreen fern that grows directly on rock, rock polypody forms low mats over shaded boulders, ledges, and cliff faces. Much of its range in Ontario follows the Canadian Shield, where it takes hold in the thin skin of moss and grit that collects on the tops and sides of stone. The fronds run about 10 to 25 cm, leathery and once-divided, and they stay green through winter. Turn one over and you find round spore dots along the underside, orange when ripe, set in neat rows. You can meet it in any season, since it does not die back. Look on shaded rock, in damp spots rather than open sun. It asks for little soil and holds on where most plants cannot. On a walk through Shield country, a green fringe softening a grey boulder is often this fern. It spreads slowly by creeping stems and does no harm.",
  "royal-fern": "Royal fern does not look much like a fern at first glance. Its widely spaced leaflets read more like the leaf of a pea or a locust, and the whole plant stands tall, with fronds reaching about 0.6 to 1.5 m. It is one of the largest ferns in Ontario. Fertile fronds carry brown, tassel-like spore clusters at their tips, which is where the plant does its reproducing. Look for it in wet ground: swamps, wet woods, the edges of bogs, and along shorelines. It often stands with its base in shallow water and rarely strays far from moisture. Spring and summer are the seasons to find it in full, before the fronds fade. It occurs across the province. Nothing about it is hazardous to walk past, and its clump habit and locust-like leaflets make it one of the easier large ferns to name once you have seen it a few times.",
  "running-clubmoss": "Clubmosses are not mosses and not ferns, but something older. Running clubmoss trails a stem along the ground, sometimes right at the surface, and sends up bushy little shoots 7 to 15 cm tall, each densely packed with tiny needle-like leaves. Pale spore cones stand above the shoots on slender stalks. Look for it in dry to moist conifer and mixed woods, clearings, and sandy acidic soils, mostly through central and northern Ontario. The fine yellow spores, called lycopodium powder, are so flammable that they were once used for flash effects in early photography and on the stage. That is history rather than something to try. The plant grows slowly and spreads by those runners, so a patch can be old even when each shoot is small. It is evergreen, easy to overlook until you notice the runner threading between the shoots. It does no harm to touch, and the slow-growing colonies are best left alone.",
  "scouring-rush": "Stiff and dark green, scouring rush stands like a bundle of hollow, unbranched pipes along moist sandy banks, streamsides, ditches, and roadside slopes. Each stem has a dark ring at every joint and a small cone at the tip, and stems can reach 50 to 150 cm. Run a finger along one and it feels rough, almost gritty. That grit is silica built into the stem walls. The abrasiveness gave the plant its use and its name. The stems were once cut and used to scour pots and smooth wood, doing the work of fine sandpaper. It is evergreen and holds its stems through the year, an ancient horsetail relative that reproduces by spores rather than seed. One caution: the plant is toxic to horses if eaten in quantity, so it matters in pasture and hay ground. It spreads by creeping underground stems and can form dense stands in damp, disturbed soil.",
  "softstem-bulrush": "The quickest test for softstem bulrush is in the name. Pinch a stem and it squashes easily between the fingers, spongy rather than firm. That squash separates it from the similar hard-stem bulrush, whose stems stay stiff. The round, soft grey-green stems stand 1 to 3 m in marshes, along pond and lake margins, and in slow shallow water. Near the top, drooping clusters of brown spikelets hang from the stem. Summer is the season to name it, when the spikelets are out. It grows in the shallow-water zone where cattails and other marsh plants gather, often in loose stands. The stems are round in cross-section, not triangular like the true sedges, which is another useful check in the field. It occurs across the province. Muskrats and marsh birds use stands like these for food and cover, and the plant helps hold soft margins together. There is nothing to be wary of here beyond wet feet and soft footing at the water's edge.",
  "big-bluestem": "In late summer big bluestem rises well above the other grasses of open ground, sometimes to 2.5 m. The old name for its seed head was turkeyfoot, from the way the top splits into three finger-like spikes. Watch for that shape, and for stems that flush reddish-bronze as fall comes on. This was the dominant grass of the tallgrass prairie, a system now reduced to scattered remnants in southern Ontario. You are most likely to meet it in those remnants, on sandy open ground, and along railway edges where prairie species hang on. It is a warm-season grass, so it does its growing in the heat of summer and stands tallest into autumn. The deep roots that let it survive fire and drought also make it a soil-builder, which is part of why it is used in prairie restoration. It is native and harmless to be near. Finding a patch is a small sign of the grassland that once covered these sandy plains.",
  "bottlebrush-grass": "Most grasses want sun, but bottlebrush grass makes its living in shade. It grows in moist to dry deciduous woods and along shaded edges, one of the few native grasses at home under Ontario's hardwoods. The seed head gives it away: bristly spikelets spread out at wide angles from the stem, sticking out like the bristles of a bottle brush. Stems reach about 0.6 to 1.5 m. Summer is when the seed head is out and the plant is easiest to name. Before that it is easy to walk past among the other woodland growth. The spreading spikelets are distinctive enough that once you have seen one, later plants are quick to place. It is native to southern and central Ontario and poses no hazard. In a shaded forest where you would not expect to find a grass at all, this is often the one standing among the ferns and wildflowers, its odd seed head catching the light along a trail.",
  "indian-grass": "Come fall, Indian grass shows a narrow plume of golden-bronze at the top of a tall stem, and that plume is the easiest way to know it. The plant reaches 1 to 2 m. For a closer check, look where the leaf clasps the stem and you may find small yellow forked projections, a detail that helps separate it from other tall grasses. It is one of the signature grasses of the tallgrass prairie, and in southern Ontario its plumes stand out in the surviving prairie remnants each autumn. Look for it there, on sandy open ground, and in savanna remnants. Like other prairie grasses it grows in the warm season and comes into its own late in the year. It is native and harmless. A stand in low autumn light shows gold against the sky. Much of this grassland has been lost to farming and development.",
  "little-bluestem": "Little bluestem earns its keep twice a year. In summer the clump-forming stems are blue-green, and in fall they turn a rich coppery-orange that holds well into winter. Fluffy silvery seed tufts run along the upper stems and catch the light. It stays modest in size, roughly 30 to 90 cm, and grows in tight bunches rather than spreading. Dry ground suits it: sandy fields, alvars, prairies, and open rocky places across southern and central Ontario. Alvars, the thin-soiled limestone flats, are a good place to look. It is a warm-season grass, greenest in the heat and reddest after the first cold nights bring out the colour. It is native and harmless, and the colour it holds through winter makes it one of the more noticeable grasses in the cold months, when the copper stems stand against snow. Birds take the seed, and the bunched form gives cover to small animals on otherwise open ground.",
  "switchgrass": "Switchgrass carries its seed in an open, airy head shaped like a loose pyramid, which sets it apart from the tighter plumes of other prairie grasses. It grows in tall clumps, 1 to 2 m, and a small tuft of fine hairs sits where each leaf meets the stem. That tuft is a reliable check in the field. You will find it in prairies, along sandy shorelines, in moist open ground, and on roadsides through southern Ontario. It tolerates a wide range of conditions, wet or dry, which is part of why it has spread beyond its native ground into planted stands. It is grown now for biofuel and for holding soil against erosion, on top of its place in the native prairie. A warm-season grass, it stands tallest in late summer and fall. It is native and harmless. In a stiff wind the loose seed heads move as a mass, which is one way the head reads at a distance before you are close enough to see the detail.",
  "autumn-olive": "A shrub that looks generous and behaves badly, autumn olive was planted for wildlife and erosion control and then spread far beyond where anyone wanted it. It reaches 2 to 5 m, with leaves green above and silvery-scaly below, twigs that are often thorny, and small speckled red berries that ripen in fall. The silvery underside of the leaf is the quickest field mark. The trouble is in the roots. Autumn olive fixes nitrogen and enriches poor soil, which sounds helpful but lets it outcompete native plants adapted to lean, sandy sites. Birds eat the berries and scatter the seed widely, so it reseeds heavily and turns up in old fields, along roadsides, on dunes, and across disturbed open ground. This is an aggressive invasive. Do not plant it and do not help it spread. If you are clearing it, bag the fruit rather than dropping seed. It is well established in southern Ontario, and on sandy sites it can take over ground that once held rarer native species.",
  "bittersweet-nightshade": "Bittersweet nightshade is a sprawling or climbing vine that turns up in thickets, fencerows, streambanks, gardens, and damp waste ground. The flowers give it away: purple stars, each with a yellow beak at the centre, in the shape you see in tomato and potato blooms, which are close relatives. The flowers are followed by egg-shaped berries that ripen from green through to bright red, and those berries often hang on the vine into winter. Take the caution seriously. The berries and foliage are toxic if eaten, and the bright red berries are a real poisoning risk for children, who may be drawn to the colour. Do not taste any part of it, and learn to know it if you have young children where it grows. It is an introduced plant, common through southern and central Ontario, spread in part by birds that eat the fruit and pass the seed. It does no harm to touch in passing. The danger is only if it is eaten, which makes correct identification worth having.",
  "common-tansy": "Fern-like divided leaves and flat-topped clusters of yellow buttons make this one easy once you learn it. The flowerheads have no petals or rays, just tight yellow disks arranged in a level spray above the foliage. Crush a leaf and it gives off a strong smell. You will meet it along roadsides, fields, riverbanks, and waste ground through southern and central Ontario, flowering through summer and into fall.\n\nIt came over as a medicinal and insect-repelling herb, then escaped and settled along roads and rivers. Now it spreads on its own and crowds native plants on disturbed ground. Treat it with care. The plant contains thujone, a toxic compound, and it is poisonous if eaten. Handling the foliage can bring on a skin rash in some people. There is old folk use as a medicine and repellent, but the margin between a traditional dose and a harmful one is small, and poisonings have happened. It is not something to eat or brew.",
  "cow-parsnip": "Ontario's native lookalike of giant hogweed, and far more common of the two. Broad maple-like leaves and white flower umbels sit on ridged green stems. It runs smaller than giant hogweed and lacks the purple blotches that mark the invasive plant, though a tall one in a wet ditch can still stand taller than a person. Look for it in moist meadows, ditches, streambanks, and roadsides, right across the province. It flowers through spring and summer.\n\nThe sap deserves respect. Sap on skin, followed by sunlight, can cause burns and blistering. The reaction is chemical, not allergic, so it can happen to anyone. If you are clearing it or brushing past it on a bright day, cover exposed skin and wash off any sap as soon as you can. Keep the affected skin out of the sun for a day or two afterward. It is a native plant, not an invasive, but one to handle carefully.",
  "glossy-buckthorn": "Smooth oval leaves with untoothed edges set this shrub apart from common buckthorn. Count the veins and you find eight or nine pairs running out to the leaf margin. The twigs carry no thorn, another point of difference from common buckthorn, and the berries ripen from red through to black. It grows as a shrub or small tree in fens, bogs, wet thickets, and damp woodlands across southern and central Ontario, leafing and fruiting through summer and fall.\n\nWhere common buckthorn favours drier ground, this one pushes into wetlands and forms dense thickets that shade out native plants. Birds eat the berries and carry the seeds well beyond the parent plant. The berries are not food. They act as a strong laxative and are mildly toxic if eaten. Removing established shrubs takes persistence, since cut stumps resprout unless treated. If you have wet ground to protect, catching it early is far easier than clearing a thicket later.",
  "multiflora-rose": "Arching thorny canes and clusters of small white to pink flowers mark this rose out. The surest field mark is at the leaf base: a fringed, comb-like stipule where the leaf meets the stem, which most native roses do not have. It grows as a spreading shrub in old fields, pastures, forest edges, and roadsides through southern Ontario, flowering in summer.\n\nOne plant is a seed factory, capable of hundreds of thousands of seeds in a year. It also spreads by layering, where an arching cane roots wherever its tip touches the ground. Between the two, a single plant can become a thicket. Those thickets turn impenetrable, closing off pasture and edge habitat. The stiff curved thorns catch skin and clothing, so wear heavy gloves and long sleeves if you tackle it. Pulling small plants before they fruit is the easiest control. Once canes are thick and rooting at the tips, removal becomes slow work.",
  "spotted-knapweed": "Wiry branched stems topped with pinkish-purple thistle-like flowerheads. Look closely at the bracts below each head and you find dark, fringed spots at their tips, which give the plant its name and separate it from true thistles. It stands on dry fields, roadsides, gravel pits, and sandy open ground across southern and central Ontario, flowering in summer.\n\nIt has a chemical advantage. Its roots release compounds that suppress the growth of nearby plants, which helps it spread across dry sites and take over pasture. On sandy or gravelly ground with little competition, it can dominate quickly. Handling the plant can irritate skin, so wear gloves when pulling it. Small infestations can be pulled or dug, ideally before the seed sets, since the seed carries the population forward year to year. On open disturbed ground it is worth watching for early, because a thin scatter of plants is much easier to deal with than an established stand.",
  "yellow-iris": "The only large wild yellow iris in Ontario, which makes it hard to confuse once it blooms. Bright yellow flowers rise on tall stalks above flat, sword-shaped leaves, always in wet ground. Look for it in marshes, along shorelines, in ditches, and in wet meadows through southern and central Ontario. It flowers in late spring and into summer.\n\nIt spreads two ways, by seed and by thick rhizomes, and builds dense stands that push out native cattails and sedges. Along a shoreline it can replace a varied marsh edge with a solid block of one plant. The plant is toxic if eaten, and the sap and rhizomes can irritate skin. Wear gloves if you dig or cut it. It is sometimes sold and planted as an ornamental for pond edges, which is how many stands got started. Keeping it out of gardens near water is one of the simpler ways to slow its spread.",
  "black-swallow-wort": "A twining vine with glossy paired leaves and small star-shaped flowers, dark purple-brown and five-petalled. The slender pods split to release seeds on silky fluff, much like milkweed, and the resemblance is not accidental. It climbs and sprawls in fields, roadsides, forest edges, and old lots through southern and eastern Ontario, flowering in summer.\n\nThe vine is in the milkweed family, and that connection causes real harm. Like its relative dog-strangling vine, it draws monarch butterflies into laying eggs on it. The caterpillars cannot survive on the plant, so the eggs are wasted. It also smothers other vegetation and is toxic to grazing livestock. There is no easy fix once it is established, since the roots resprout after cutting. The most useful thing most people can do is avoid spreading the seeds and report new infestations so they can be dealt with early. Cleaning seeds off clothing and gear after walking through a patch helps keep it from moving to new ground.",
  "common-pokeweed": "Few native plants this size are as easy to spot. Smooth reddish-purple stems carry big oval leaves and drooping clusters of dark purple berries on pink stalks. The whole plant can top an adult by late summer. It reaches the northern edge of its range in southern Ontario, where it turns up on forest edges, in clearings, along fencerows, and on disturbed ground. Berries ripen through summer and fall.\n\nThe colour and size draw the eye, and the ripe berries look tempting, which is the danger. All parts are toxic if eaten, the root and unripe berries most of all. The purple berries can poison children, so learn it on sight if you have young kids who put things in their mouths. There is a tradition of eating cooked young shoots as poke sallet in parts of its range, but this needs specific preparation and repeated boiling, and mistakes cause serious poisoning. It is not a beginner's wild food. For most people the safest approach is to admire it and leave it be.",
  "european-frog-bit": "Small heart-shaped leaves floating in loose rosettes, each one like a miniature lily pad. The plants drift free rather than rooting in the bottom, and in summer they carry small white flowers with three petals. Look in quiet bays, marshes, ditches, and slow channels through eastern and southern Ontario. Summer is when the mats are thickest.\n\nBecause it floats unrooted, it moves easily and gathers into dense mats on still water. Those mats block light, and when they sink and rot each fall they pull oxygen out of the water. That is hard on the fish and other life below. Boats, trailers, and gear are the main way it travels between waters. It also makes small overwintering buds that survive to start new plants. Cleaning your boat and equipment before moving between lakes and wetlands is the single most useful step in keeping it from spreading. Once it is in a quiet bay, it is very hard to get back out.",
  "japanese-barberry": "A compact spiny shrub with small spoon-shaped leaves and a single sharp spine at each node. Bright red oblong berries hang on through winter, which makes it easy to pick out in a bare woodland. It grows in the forest understory, along edges, and on shaded disturbed ground in southern Ontario. Spring leaf-out and fall colour are good times to notice it.\n\nThis one carries a health risk. Its dense low stands raise the humidity at ground level, and that moist microclimate supports high numbers of blacklegged ticks, the species that can carry Lyme disease. The spines are sharp, so wear cover if you handle it, and check yourself for ticks afterward. It was widely planted as an ornamental hedge, which is how much of it escaped into woodlands. Choosing native shrubs for the garden instead is one way to avoid adding to the problem. Small plants pull out with the roots when the soil is moist.",
  "jimsonweed": "Coarse and foul-smelling, this plant announces itself before you look closely. The leaves are jagged and toothed. The flowers are large white to pale purple trumpets. The seed pods are egg-shaped and covered in spines. It comes up in cultivated fields, barnyards, roadsides, and waste ground in southern Ontario, flowering and fruiting through summer and fall.\n\nA single spiny pod holds hundreds of seeds, and those seeds stay alive in the soil for years, sprouting again long after the parent plant is gone. Disturbed ground keeps it coming back. This one is genuinely dangerous. All parts are highly toxic, holding tropane alkaloids that can be fatal. Poisonings have come from people eating parts of the plant or brewing it, sometimes on purpose, and the dose that harms is close to the dose that kills. There is no safe amount to take. Keep children and livestock away from it, and never ingest any part. If you are pulling it, gloves and washed hands afterward are sensible.",
  "leafy-spurge": "Narrow blue-green leaves on upright stems, topped by showy yellow-green bracts in paired arrangements that people often mistake for flowers. Break a stem and it bleeds milky sap, which is a reliable field mark. It grows on dry fields, roadsides, pastures, and sandy open ground through southern and central Ontario, showing through spring and summer.\n\nThe trouble is underground. Its roots run deep and spread wide, reaching several metres down, which makes an established patch very hard to remove. Pull the top and the roots send up more. The milky sap irritates skin and eyes and is toxic if eaten. Wear gloves and eye protection if you cut or pull it, and keep the sap away from your face. Livestock generally avoid it, which lets it take over grazing land. Because the roots defeat most hand-pulling, catching new plants early, before the root system is deep, is the realistic way to keep it in check.",
  "oriental-bittersweet": "An escaped ornamental that has become one of southern Ontario's harder invasive vines to control. It climbs by twining, wrapping woody stems around whatever it can reach and running up to about 12 metres into the canopy. Round, toothed leaves and a habit of forest edges, thickets, roadsides, and fencerows help place it. The clearest sign comes in fall, when yellow capsules split to show scarlet berries. On this species the berries cluster along the length of the stem, not just at the tips, and that is the main way to separate it from the native American bittersweet, which it can hybridize with. Left alone, it girdles trunks, and its weight can drag down mature trees. It spreads by seed and by root, so cut stems resprout. The berries are toxic if eaten, so keep them from children and do not use the fruiting vines in wreaths or decorations, which is one way it reaches new ground. Removal usually means repeated cutting and careful disposal rather than a single pull.",
  "poison-hemlock": "All parts of this plant contain coniine, a potent toxin, and eating any of it can kill. It grows 1 to 3 metres tall on smooth hollow stems marked with purple blotches. The leaves are lacy and fern-like, and the tiny white flowers sit in umbrella-shaped clusters typical of the carrot family. Crushed foliage gives off a musty, unpleasant smell. You meet it in summer along roadsides, ditches, streambanks, and disturbed damp ground, mostly in southern Ontario. It is an introduced plant and spreads readily on wet, disturbed soil. This is the species traditionally linked to the death of Socrates. The real hazard for a walker is confusion. Hemlock resembles several harmless white-flowered relatives, and mistakes have been fatal, so it is not a plant to forage near or test by taste. Even the sap can irritate skin. If you need to remove it, wear gloves and long sleeves, and do not burn the cut plant. Learn it well enough to keep your distance, and leave carrot-family foraging to people who can tell these plants apart.",
  "black-eyed-susan": "Golden-yellow rays around a dark brown central cone make this one easy to name in a summer field. The plant stands 30 to 90 centimetres, with rough, hairy stems and leaves that feel bristly, and flowers 5 to 8 centimetres across. It is a hardy native that moves quickly into recovering fields, roadsides, and disturbed open ground, which is why it shows up early in restored prairies and meadows. Look for it from summer into fall across southern and central Ontario. It does well in poor, dry soil and full sun, and it draws bees and other insects while in bloom. The dark cones persist after the petals drop and hold seed into the cooler months. As a short-lived perennial or biennial, it tends to reseed rather than form long-lived clumps, so a patch can shift ground from year to year. It is an easy plant to grow from seed if you want native colour in an open, sunny spot.",
  "bloodroot": "Among the earliest woodland flowers, and one of the shortest-lived. Each white bloom, 3 to 5 centimetres across, opens wrapped in a single lobed leaf and drops its petals within a few days, so a patch in flower is easy to miss. It grows 15 to 25 centimetres tall in rich, moist deciduous woods and on shaded slopes across southern and central Ontario, flowering very early in spring. The name comes from the red-orange sap that seeps from the cut root and rhizome. That sap is toxic if swallowed and can irritate skin and eyes, so handle the plant carefully and keep it away from anyone who might chew on it. The seeds carry a fleshy attachment that ants collect and haul off, which is how bloodroot spreads slowly across a forest floor. Because the flowering is so brief and the plant depends on undisturbed woods, it rewards a spring walk timed to catch it, and it does poorly when dug or trampled.",
  "blue-flag-iris": "Ontario's most common native blue iris. Violet-blue flowers with yellow-veined falls rise on stems 50 to 90 centimetres tall from clumps of flat, sword-shaped leaves. It stands in wet ground: marshes, wet meadows, and shorelines. It grows province-wide and flowers through summer. The three drooping outer petals, called falls, carry pale veining that guides bees in toward the nectar. It spreads by thick rhizomes and forms stands along quiet shores and slow water. Before it blooms, the flat leaf fans can be confused with cattail or sweet flag. That matters, because the rhizomes and leaves are toxic if eaten and the sap can irritate skin, so this is not a plant to forage or taste. A close look and a photograph cost the plant nothing, and a marsh in early summer is the place to find one.",
  "canada-goldenrod": "Tall arching plumes of tiny yellow flowers over old fields in late summer. This native stands 60 to 150 centimetres and takes over roadsides, meadows, and forest edges from summer into fall across the province. It spreads by rhizome and forms dense colonies that can crowd out other plants. The most useful thing to know about it corrects a common complaint. Goldenrod is often blamed for hay fever, but it is insect-pollinated, and its pollen is heavy and sticky rather than airborne. The real cause of late-summer allergies is ragweed, which blooms at the same time with drab green flowers and wind-blown pollen. Goldenrod's bright plumes simply make it the visible plant to accuse. In bloom it is one of the busiest nectar sources of the season, drawing bees, wasps, flies, and beetles. Left standing over winter, the dried stems and seed heads feed birds and shelter insects. It is a common native, and easy to recognize once you know it is not the plant making you sneeze.",
  "common-milkweed": "Round clusters of pink-purple flowers, sweetly scented, over broad opposite leaves that bleed milky sap when broken. This is the plant most tied to monarch butterflies: their caterpillars feed only on milkweeds, and common milkweed is the one most people meet, in fields, roadsides, meadows, and disturbed ground across southern and central Ontario. It grows 90 to 180 centimetres and flowers in summer. The heavily scented blooms draw a wide range of insects. After flowering, the plant forms the familiar warty pods that split in fall to release seeds on silky floss. The milky sap gives the plant its name and its chemistry. It is toxic if swallowed and can irritate the eyes and skin, and that same chemistry protects the monarch caterpillars, which store the toxins. Wash your hands after handling broken stems, and keep the sap out of your eyes. If you are planting for monarchs, this species is a reliable choice, though it spreads by root and can travel farther in a garden than you expect.",
  "fragrant-water-lily": "Floating white flowers, many-petalled and sweetly scented, that open on sunny mornings and close by early afternoon. Each bloom runs 8 to 15 centimetres across and sits among round, deeply notched pads on the surface of still ponds, quiet lake bays, and slow rivers. It is native and grows province-wide, flowering through summer. The plant is rooted in the mud below, sending long stalks up to float its leaves and flowers, so what you see is only the top of it. The timing matters: the flowers track the day, so a bay covered in open blooms at mid-morning may show almost none by late afternoon. Come early if you want them at their fullest. The pads give cover to fish, frogs, and insects, and the flowers are visited by beetles and bees. In sheltered water it can form broad floating mats. It is common on quiet Ontario water through the summer.",
  "jack-in-the-pulpit": "The name describes it well. A hooded green-and-purple striped spathe curls over an upright spadix, like a preacher, Jack, standing in a covered pulpit. It grows 30 to 65 centimetres in moist rich woods, swamp edges, and floodplains across southern and central Ontario, flowering in spring under the leafing canopy. By late summer the flower gives way to a tight cluster of bright red berries on the stalk. One odd habit sets it apart: individual plants can switch sex between years, often flowering as male when small and as female when larger and better fed. The whole plant carries needle-like calcium oxalate crystals, and the raw plant causes intense burning of the mouth and throat if chewed. That reaction is immediate and painful, so it is not a plant to taste, and the red berries in particular should be kept from children. Left in place, it is a quiet, long-lived resident of good woodland. Learn the shape and it becomes easy to spot every spring.",
  "marsh-marigold": "One of the first splashes of colour in spring wetlands. Glossy, bright-yellow flowers, buttercup-like and about 4 centimetres across, sit over rounded, heart-shaped leaves in wet ground. The plant stands 20 to 60 centimetres and grows province-wide in wet meadows, swamps, ditches, and along stream edges, blooming in spring. Despite the name it is not a true marigold; it belongs to the buttercup family, which shows in the shine and shape of its flowers. It favours saturated soil and often grows right at the water's edge or in shallow standing water. The bright colour draws early insects when little else is in flower. All parts are toxic if eaten raw, so this is not a foraging plant, whatever old accounts of cooked greens may suggest, and the safest approach is to leave it where it grows. In a cold, brown spring wetland it is often the first strong colour you will see, which makes it easy to find and easy to name.",
  "new-england-aster": "Dense clusters of purple rays around a yellow centre, on plants that reach 60 to 180 centimetres in moist meadows, ditches, and field edges. This is one of the last strong flowers of the season, blooming well into autumn across southern and central Ontario. That late timing is the point of it: when most plants have finished, its flowers feed migrating monarchs and late bees at a time when nectar is scarce. The rays are usually a deep purple, richer than most other asters, which helps pick it out of a group that is otherwise hard to tell apart. It grows in clumps from a woody base and does well in full sun and damp ground, with somewhat hairy stems and leaves. Left standing, the seed heads carry into winter and feed birds. If you keep a native planting for pollinators, this species earns its place by covering the gap at the very end of the year, when almost nothing else is open.",
  "pickerelweed": "Dense spikes of blue-violet flowers standing above glossy, heart-shaped leaves, all of it rising out of shallow water. It reaches 50 to 90 centimetres above the surface in still shallow water, marsh margins, and quiet bays across southern and central Ontario, flowering through summer. It roots in the mud and forms large colonies, and those colonies do real work: the submerged stems shelter fish fry, and the flowers are heavily visited by bees. The name comes from an old belief that pickerel, or pike, spawn among it. Each flower lasts a single day, but the spike keeps opening new ones over weeks, so a stand stays in bloom for a long stretch of summer. It is native and does not run wild the way invasive water plants do, so a bay full of it is a good sign for the health of quiet water. Look for it along the edges of slow lakes and ponds where the water stays calm and shallow.",
  "red-trillium": "A single maroon-red flower nods above a whorl of three leaves, opening in spring woods before the canopy closes over. It grows in moist deciduous and mixed forests with rich soil across southern and central Ontario. Lean in and you may catch a faint smell of spoiled meat. The odour is the point. Rather than courting bees, this trillium draws carrion flies, and they carry its pollen. That habit, together with the flower's dull dark colour, gave it old names like stinking Benjamin and wake-robin.\n\nFlower colour is not fixed. Some plants open dusky pink or nearly white, which can lead to mix-ups with other trilliums. Like its relatives it establishes slowly and recovers slowly, so it is better watched than handled. A picked bloom will not return that season, and repeated picking wears the plant down over years.",
  "round-lobed-hepatica": "Few plants beat it into bloom. While the ground is still cold and largely bare, small flowers open on hairy stalks, ranging from white through pale lilac. Look below them for the three-lobed leaves that give the plant its common name. Those leaves are leathery and liver-shaped, and they hold their green through winter under the snow, so last year's foliage is often still visible when the new flowers appear.\n\nIt grows in dry to moist deciduous woods and on rocky slopes, mostly in southern and central Ontario. The hairy stalks earn a second look. The fuzz helps the plant cope with cold spring air. There is little chance of confusion once the leaves are seen. Standing barely 10 to 15 cm tall, it is easy to walk past, so it rewards slow looking on a woodland floor early in the season.",
  "spotted-joe-pye-weed": "This one stands head-high and then some, reaching two metres in a good spot. The stems are spotted or flushed with purple, a useful field mark, and the leaves circle the stem in whorls. At the top sit broad, flat-topped clusters of dusky-pink flowers that open in late summer and hold into fall. It belongs to wet ground: wet meadows, marshes, ditches, and streambanks through southern and central Ontario.\n\nButterflies find it reliably, and on a warm August day a single plant can be busy with them, along with bees and other insects drawn to the nectar. The height and the wetland setting make it hard to mistake for much else once it is flowering. It reads as coarse and weedy up close, but in a damp meadow it is one of the last big splashes of colour before the season turns.",
  "trout-lily": "Look down in a rich spring wood and you may find whole stretches of the ground covered in paired leaves, mottled green and brown. The markings are said to recall the flanks of a brook trout, hence the name. Most of those plants are not old enough to flower. Only mature ones send up a single nodding yellow bloom on a short stalk, so a large patch may show few flowers.\n\nThese colonies spread slowly, mostly by underground offshoots, and can hold the same ground for decades. It grows in moist rich deciduous woods and on floodplains across southern and central Ontario, coming and going quickly in spring. Like other spring woodland plants it does its whole year's growth before the trees leaf out, then dies back to the ground. A broad carpet of it is usually a very old colony that took a long time to build.",
  "white-trillium": "Ontario's provincial flower, and for many people the signal that spring has arrived in the hardwoods. Three broad leaves sit below a single flower with three white petals, held up on a short stalk. Search shaded forest floors in April and May, before the canopy leafs out and shades everything below. It favours rich, moist deciduous forest, and it is especially at home in sugar maple woods, where it can whiten large stretches of ground. As the flowers age the petals often flush pink, so a late-season patch can look quite different from a fresh one.\n\nThis is a protected plant in Ontario. Picking the flower or digging the plant can kill it, since it may take years to recover the leaf it needs to feed itself, and it is prohibited on public land. Enjoy it where it stands and leave it for the next person on the trail.",
  "wild-bergamot": "Crush a leaf and the identification is half done: the smell is strongly minty, with a note of oregano. The flowerheads are ragged lavender pom-poms sitting atop square stems, a shape shared with other mints. It grows in dry open ground - fields, prairies, roadsides, and sunny slopes - through southern and central Ontario, flowering in summer.\n\nBees work it heavily, and hummingbirds visit the tubular flowers, which is part of why it is a common choice for pollinator plantings. The leaves make a fragrant tea, and the plant is also called wild bee balm. It spreads by runners and can form loose stands. In humid summers the foliage often picks up a dusting of powdery mildew, which looks worse than it is and rarely troubles the plant much. On a dry roadside in July it is one of the easier native wildflowers to name by nose alone.",
  "wild-columbine": "The nodding flower is hard to mistake once seen: red on the outside, yellow within, with five spurs pointing straight back like a small lantern hung upside down. Those spurs hold nectar at their tips, out of reach of most insects. Hummingbirds and long-tongued moths are the main visitors that can drain them.\n\nIt tends to grow where soil is thin - rocky woods, cliff ledges, and gravelly slopes - which sets it apart from many other spring woodland flowers. Look for it from spring into early summer across southern and central Ontario. The plant is airy, with delicate blue-green foliage below the dangling flowers, and it often takes hold in cracks and ledges where little else will. It self-sows readily and can turn up in gardens uninvited. There is no real lookalike among Ontario's native flowers, so the red-and-yellow spurred bloom on a rock face is a safe call.",
  "wild-geranium": "Five pink-purple petals open above deeply cut, palmate leaves in spring and early summer, in moist deciduous woods, thickets, and shaded edges. This is a true native geranium, unrelated to the red potted plants that borrow the name. After the flower fades, watch the seed capsule. It lengthens into a slender beak, splits, and flings its seeds outward under spring tension, a mechanism that earned the old name cranesbill.\n\nIt grows across southern and central Ontario and often forms loose drifts on a wood floor. The flowers face upward and are easy for bees to work, unlike some of its shade-loving neighbours. Colour runs from soft pink to a richer rose-purple. There is not much to confuse it with once the palmate leaves and open flowers are seen together. It is a steady, unfussy plant of the spring woods, neither rare nor loud enough to draw a picker, which suits it.",
  "wild-leek": "In early spring the floor of a sugar maple wood can fill with pairs of broad, smooth green leaves. Bruise one and the answer is immediate: a strong onion-garlic smell. That smell is the single most reliable field mark, and it matters, because the leaves alone resemble those of lily-of-the-valley and false hellebore, both of which are toxic and neither of which smells of onion. If there is no onion-garlic scent, do not eat it.\n\nThe leaves die back before the white flower cluster opens on a bare stalk in summer, so the plant is easy to miss later in the year. Wild leek is a well-known spring edible, but it grows slowly and its colonies are easily wiped out by heavy digging. A patch can take many years to rebuild. If you harvest, take very little, cut rather than dig where you can, and leave most of the stand to recover. It occurs across southern and central Ontario.",
  "cardinal-flower": "Little else along an Ontario shoreline is this red. In late summer the plant sends up spikes of scarlet flowers at the water's edge, and they are hard to overlook. The tubular flowers are shaped for hummingbirds, and ruby-throated hummingbirds do nearly all of the pollinating. It grows on streambanks, wet meadows, and shorelines through southern and central Ontario, flowering into fall.\n\nIt needs consistently damp ground and open light, so it tends to appear right where land meets water. The colour alone makes it one of the easiest wetland plants to name. The plant is toxic if eaten in quantity, so it is one to look at rather than sample. It is short-lived as an individual but reseeds where conditions suit it, sometimes shifting along a bank from one year to the next. Find a wet, sunny margin in August and it may already be there.",
  "wild-lupine": "Upright spikes of blue-purple pea flowers stand over palmate leaves spread like open hands, on dry sandy ground. This is a plant of a narrow habitat: sandy oak savannas, dunes, and open sandy woods, mostly in southwestern Ontario. It flowers in late spring and early summer.\n\nIts best-known connection is a loss. Wild lupine is the only plant Karner blue butterfly caterpillars can eat, and the Karner blue is now gone from Ontario, a plain reminder of how tied some insects are to a single host. The plant fixes nitrogen and helps hold loose sandy soil, which is part of why it matters in savanna restoration. It is not one to taste. Seeds and foliage carry toxic alkaloids. Native lupine differs from the tall garden lupines sometimes seen on roadsides, which are introduced. A patch on open sand in early summer means you are standing in an increasingly uncommon kind of Ontario habitat.",
  "american-ginseng": "This is a plant to know so that you leave it alone. A whorl of three to five compound leaves rises from a single stalk, topped in summer by a small cluster of greenish flowers that later ripen into bright red berries. It grows only in undisturbed, rich, moist deciduous woods on shaded slopes in southern Ontario, and it is now scarce.\n\nWild ginseng was once far more common. Decades of digging for its root, which carries high value in trade, drove it to endangered status in the province. It is legally protected. Harvesting, possessing, or disturbing wild ginseng is illegal in Ontario, and that holds for plants you meet on your own walks. The right response to finding one is to note it, leave it fully in place, and avoid wearing a path or drawing attention to it. It grows slowly and takes years to reach flowering size, so every mature plant counts. Photograph it if you like, and go no further.",
  "beaked-hazel": "A native shrub of woodland edges, clearings, and mixed forest understory, growing one to three metres tall. The name comes from the nut. It sits wrapped in a bristly green husk that tapers into a long tubular beak, and there is nothing else in the Ontario woods that looks quite like it. Late summer is the time to check, on the shaded margins of forest and in openings. The nuts inside are rich and edible, close to cultivated hazelnuts.\n\nGetting to them is the hard part. Squirrels, chipmunks, and blue jays work the shrubs steadily, often stripping the crop before the nuts are fully ripe, so anyone hoping for a share has to watch a patch closely. The husk bristles can irritate skin, so handle the beaks carefully when peeling them out. Found across the province.",
  "chokecherry": "Elongated clusters of small white flowers appear in spring, and by late summer they hang as strings of dark cherries. This is a shrub or small tree of woodland edges, thickets, fencerows, and roadsides, two to six metres tall and common across Ontario. The longer flower and fruit clusters help separate it from pin cherry, which carries its flowers in small flat-topped bunches.\n\nRaw, the fruit is intensely astringent, enough to pucker the mouth, which is where the name comes from. Cooked and strained to take out the seeds, it becomes jelly and syrup. That step matters. The leaves, twigs, and seeds contain cyanide compounds and are toxic if eaten in quantity, and wilted foliage is especially dangerous to livestock. Eat only the cooked, de-seeded fruit, and do not chew or swallow the pits. Birds and bears take the ripe cherries readily.",
  "common-blackberry": "Tall arching canes armed with stout, sharp thorns mark this cane shrub of old fields, thickets, forest edges, and fencerows. It reaches one to two and a half metres and spreads into dense tangles. The berries ripen from green through red to glossy black, and unlike raspberries they keep their white core when picked, coming away as a solid berry. Fruit ripens in summer, mostly across southern and central Ontario.\n\nPick when the berries are fully black and pull free with a light tug. Red ones are still sour and not worth taking. The thorny thickets earn their keep for wildlife as much as for people, giving songbirds well protected cover for nesting and feeding. Wear long sleeves. The thorns catch and tear, and a good patch will mark your hands.",
  "common-juniper": "A low, sprawling evergreen that hugs rocky barrens, open slopes, and dry sandy ground, seldom more than knee to waist height. The needle-like leaves are stiff and sharp, set in whorls, each with a pale band on the upper surface. What look like blue-grey berries are small fleshy cones that take two to three years to ripen. This is the most widespread juniper in Ontario, found province-wide.\n\nThe cones are the traditional flavouring of gin. They are usable in small amounts as a seasoning but strong and resinous, not a fruit to eat by the handful. They persist through fall and winter, which makes the plant easy to find in the cold months. As a rule, juniper cones are best avoided medicinally during pregnancy. Give the foliage room when you crouch to look. The needles prick.",
  "highbush-cranberry": "Despite the name, this is a viburnum, not a cranberry. It grows two to four metres in moist woods, along swamp edges, and on streambanks throughout the province. Spring brings flat, lacy white flower clusters, with larger sterile flowers ringing the outside. By fall the shrub carries dense bunches of tart, translucent red berries, and these hang on well into winter.\n\nBirds tend to leave the sour fruit until other food runs out, so it works as a kind of emergency ration in late winter. For people the berries are edible cooked and quite tart, usually made into jelly, though they carry a musky smell some find off-putting. The plant resembles a couple of introduced viburnums closely enough that certain identification matters before you use the fruit. Check for the three-lobed, maple-like leaves together with the persistent red clusters.",
  "lowbush-blueberry": "Low mats of small, glossy leaves spread over acidic rock and sandy ground, rarely rising above the ankle to the knee. This is the classic wild blueberry of the Canadian Shield, at home on barrens, thin rocky soils, and burned-over ground across central and northern Ontario. The dusty blue berries ripen in summer and are good eaten fresh.\n\nStands often fruit most heavily a year or two after a fire, since the plants resprout from underground stems and take advantage of the opened ground. Wild blueberries are among the safest wild fruits to learn, with no dangerous lookalike sharing that blue berry topped by a small five-pointed crown. Picking is slow given the size of the fruit, but the flavour beats anything cultivated. Bears feed on the same patches, so make noise when you work good berry ground.",
  "red-osier-dogwood": "Winter is the easiest time to know this shrub. The stems turn a vivid red that stands out against snow, brightest on young growth in full sun. It grows one to three metres in wet thickets, along streambanks and shorelines, and in damp ditches across the province. Summer brings flat clusters of small white flowers, followed by white to bluish berries.\n\nThose berries are taken readily by many birds and are not good eating for people. The shrub spreads where branches touch damp ground and root, forming thickets that hold soil along water. It is often planted for that reason and for the winter colour. The paired leaves with curved veins, together with the red twigs, make it hard to confuse with much else once you have the look. Told at a glance in the cold months when little else on a shoreline shows any colour.",
  "serviceberry": "Among the earliest shrubs to flower in Ontario, often blooming before the leaves are fully out. Clusters of white, five-petalled flowers open in early spring, and by summer they give way to sweet purple berries that taste something like a blueberry with a hint of almond. The plant goes by several names, saskatoon and Juneberry among them, and grows one to four metres in open woods, on rocky slopes and shorelines, and in thickets.\n\nThis particular species belongs to northwestern Ontario, but closely related serviceberries grow across the province and are used the same way. The fruit is good fresh, and it dries and cooks well. The various species can be hard to tell apart. That is less of a worry here than with most wild fruit, since all the native serviceberries have edible berries. Birds strip the ripe crop fast, so the picking window is short.",
  "staghorn-sumac": "Upright cones of fuzzy dark-red berries and stout twigs covered in velvety hairs give this shrub its name, the twigs recalling a stag's antlers in velvet. It reaches two to five metres in dry fields, along roadsides, on slopes, and at woodland edges through southern and central Ontario, often in spreading colonies from root suckers. In fall the long compound leaves turn deep scarlet.\n\nThis is a harmless relative of poison sumac, and the two are easy to keep apart. Poison sumac has loose, drooping white berries and grows in swamps. This one holds its red berry cones stiffly upright on dry ground. The tart red berries can be steeped in cold water and strained into a pink, lemonade-like drink. Use only the upright red-coned sumac, never the white-berried swamp species. The red cones stand through winter and feed birds when little else is left.",
  "wild-red-raspberry": "A pioneer of disturbed ground, quick to fill clearings, forest edges, roadsides, and burned areas. The arching canes stand one to two metres and carry fine bristles rather than heavy thorns, and the leaves show a whitish underside that helps confirm the plant. Ripe red berries pull free of their pale core, leaving a hollow cap. That hollow is the simplest way to tell a raspberry from a blackberry, which keeps its core.\n\nFruit comes in summer, across the province. Wild plants bear heavily in old clearings and along trails, and the berries match any cultivated kind for flavour, if not for size. There are no dangerous lookalikes among the red, hollow-capped brambles, which makes this a safe fruit to learn. First-year canes usually grow leaves only, with fruit borne on second-year canes, so an established patch keeps producing year after year.",
  "wild-rose": "Fragrant pink flowers with five petals open through summer in meadows, thickets, on rocky shores, and along roadsides. This is a modest native shrub, half a metre to a metre and a half tall, common across Ontario. It is nearly thornless on its upper stems, which sets it apart from some of the pricklier wild and introduced roses.\n\nAfter the flowers fade, red hips form and hang on the canes into winter. The hips are rich in vitamin C and are an important cold-season food for birds. People use them too, brewed into tea or made into jelly, best after the first frost softens them. Trim away the seedy, hairy centre before use, since the irritating hairs around the seeds are unpleasant to swallow. The petals are edible as well. All the true wild roses bear edible hips, so this is a forgiving group to learn.",
  "witch-hazel": "One of the last plants to flower each year in Ontario. As the leaves fall in October and November, thin, ribbon-like yellow petals unfurl along the bare twigs, an odd sight so late in the season. It grows as a shrub or small tree two to five metres tall in the understory of moist deciduous and mixed woods and on shaded slopes across southern and central Ontario. The oval leaves have a wavy edge and an uneven base.\n\nThe seed capsules ripen a full year after flowering and split open with force, flinging the seeds several metres. Bark and leaves are the source of the astringent witch hazel sold as a skin preparation. It is not a food plant. The yellow ribbon flowers this late in the year make it hard to mistake for anything else, and the wavy-edged leaves confirm it.",
  "bracken-fern": "A coarse fern of dry, open ground, bracken spreads by underground stems into wide colonies that can blanket a hillside. The frond is the mark: large and roughly triangular, held close to horizontal on a single upright stalk, and divided into three main parts. You meet it in sandy woods, clearings, roadsides, and ground that has burned, where it is often the first green to return after fire. It is one of the most widespread ferns anywhere, and in Ontario it turns up across the province through the summer.\n\nBracken is not food. The plant is toxic and carcinogenic to people, and it poisons cattle, horses, and other grazing livestock that eat it. Reports that young fiddleheads are edible are best ignored. Leave it be and take it for what it is, a tough ground cover that does well in poor soil where little else takes hold.",
  "cinnamon-fern": "In spring the wet woods fill with tall green shuttlecocks, and at the centre of each stands a cluster of upright brown stalks that give this fern its name. Those fertile fronds are packed with spores and look like cinnamon sticks set among the leafy green. The green fronds around them form a wide vase, sometimes waist high or more. Look for it in swamps, wet woods, and along the edges of marshes, where it often grows in loose colonies.\n\nCommon across Ontario. The fertile stalks appear early, wither by midsummer, and leave the green vase standing on its own for the rest of the season. It is a plant of consistently damp ground and does not stray far from it. Nothing here is dangerous to touch, though like most ferns it is not a food plant.",
  "common-cattail": "Almost anyone can name the brown seed head, the dense sausage-shaped spike that stands above the water through late summer and fall. Below it rise flat, sword-shaped leaves, and the whole plant can reach head height in a good marsh. Cattail grows across Ontario in shallow standing water, ditches, and along pond and marsh edges, often in stands thick enough to hide a nesting bird. Those dense beds are among the most important cover in a wetland, used by red-winged blackbirds, rails, and many other marsh creatures.\n\nParts of the plant have long been used for food and fibre. If you are tempted to gather it, know that before it flowers the leaves resemble those of iris, which is poisonous, so certain identification matters. The ripe seed head breaks apart into a mass of fluff that carries the seeds on the wind.",
  "hard-stem-bulrush": "Run a stem between your fingers and it feels firm and round, which is where the name comes from. This is a tall sedge of open water, growing in dense stands of grey-green stems that rise a metre or more, some reaching three. Near the tip, small brown flower clusters droop to one side. It roots in marshes, along lake margins, and in slow, shallow water, often forming beds you could lose a canoe in.\n\nThose beds matter. They give waterfowl and muskrats cover and places to nest, and they hold the shoreline against wave and current. You will find it province-wide, most obvious in summer when the stands are full and green. It is easy to pass off as just more marsh growth, but the round, unjointed stems set it apart from the flat-leaved cattails and the grasses nearby.",
  "ostrich-fern": "This is the fern behind the fiddleheads sold at Ontario markets each spring. The mature plant grows in tall, vase-shaped clumps on rich, damp ground - floodplains, riverbanks, and moist bottomland woods where spring water lingers. The fiddlehead is the part to know: tightly coiled, smooth, bright green, with a distinct U-shaped groove running down the inner side of the stalk, and a papery brown covering rather than fuzz.\n\nIf you gather them, identification matters, because other ferns produce fiddleheads that are not safe. Even ostrich fern fiddleheads must be cooked properly. Boil or steam them thoroughly. Raw or lightly cooked ones cause nausea, cramps, and vomiting, and this happens often enough that public health notices repeat the warning every spring. Cooked well, they are a genuine wild vegetable with a short season. The clumps are worth learning to recognize in summer too, when the tall green fronds mark the spot to return to.",
  "sensitive-fern": "The common name comes from a quirk: the leafy fronds are quick to blacken and die at the first hard frost, more sensitive to cold than most ferns. Its sterile fronds are broad, coarse, and nearly triangular, cut into a few wide segments rather than fine lacework. Apart from them stand separate fertile stalks strung with hard, bead-like segments that hold the spores. Those stalks turn brown and persist through winter, still standing when the snow goes.\n\nLook for it in wet meadows, ditches, swamps, and damp thickets across Ontario, usually in summer while the green fronds are up. It spreads by creeping stems and can form loose patches. One caution if you keep animals: the plant is toxic to horses and other livestock that eat it, fresh or dried in hay.",
  "wild-rice": "Manoomin to Anishinaabe peoples, wild rice is a tall aquatic grass and a grain of deep cultural importance, traditionally harvested from a canoe by bending the heads over the gunwale and knocking the ripe seed loose. It roots in the soft mud of shallow lakes, quiet bays, and slow rivers, rising well above the surface on grassy stems. By late summer the seed heads ripen, airy and open, and the grain fills out.\n\nIn Ontario it is mostly a plant of the central and northern parts of the province. Stands can be large where the water is shallow and still and the bottom soft. It is native and not at risk here, but the beds are sensitive to changes in water level and to disturbance. If you paddle through a stand, go carefully. Harvesting is tied to Indigenous rights and local rules, so it is not something to help yourself to without knowing them.",
  "common-buckthorn": "An import gone wrong, common buckthorn is a shrub or small tree that has spread through southern and central Ontario woodlots, fencerows, and thickets. Two things help you spot it. Many twigs end in a short, stout thorn, and the leaves stay green well into fall after native trees have turned, so an understory still green in October is often this. On female plants, clusters of dark, pea-sized berries hang on through autumn.\n\nBirds eat those berries and spread the seeds widely, which is how it gets into woodlots and then takes over. Once established it casts dense shade that stops native seedlings from getting started. The berries are no treat for people either: they act as a strong laxative and are mildly toxic, so do not eat them. If you are clearing it, cutting alone usually brings resprouts, and control takes persistence.",
  "dog-strangling-vine": "Despite the name, this plant strangles habitat, not dogs. It is a twining vine that winds up over other plants and over itself, forming dense mats that smother whatever is underneath. The leaves are oval and set in opposite pairs, and small star-shaped flowers of dark pink to maroon appear in summer. Slender pods follow and split to release seeds carried on fluff, much like the milkweed it is related to.\n\nThat family tie is the real trouble. Monarch butterflies mistake it for milkweed and lay eggs on it, but the caterpillars cannot survive on the plant, so every clutch laid there is lost. It has spread through fields, forest edges, ravines, and disturbed ground across southern and central Ontario. If you find it, avoid moving the seeds on clothing or gear, and report infestations. Digging out the deep root crowns is hard work, and fragments left behind will grow back.",
  "garlic-mustard": "Crush a leaf and it smells of garlic. The plant is in the mustard family. In spring it lifts small white flowers, each with four petals, above toothed, heart-shaped leaves. It is a biennial, spending its first year as a low rosette and its second sending up the flowering stalk before it dies.\n\nLeft alone it carpets the forest floor, and it does more than crowd for space. Its roots release chemicals that suppress native wildflowers and tree seedlings, changing the ground for years. It has taken hold in shaded forests, along trail edges, and in floodplains across southern and central Ontario. The useful thing a walker can do is pull it before it sets seed, then bag and remove the plants rather than composting them, since cut plants can still ripen seed. A single season of seeds keeps the patch going for a long time.",
  "japanese-knotweed": "Few plants are as hard to be rid of as this one. The stems are hollow and bamboo-like, often flushed reddish, and grow in dense clumps to head height or taller in a single season. The leaves are broad and shovel-shaped, and by late summer sprays of small creamy flowers appear. It turns up on roadsides, riverbanks, and disturbed urban ground across southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe danger is underground and in the fragments. It regrows from tiny pieces of root or stem, so a cutting dropped in the wrong place starts a new stand, and the tough roots push into pavement, drains, and foundations. This is why it ranks among the worst invasive plants anywhere. Never compost it and never haul cuttings to a new site. If you have it, getting rid of it usually takes repeated treatment over several years, and small mistakes undo the work. Report it where local programs ask you to.",
  "phragmites": "The towering tan reed filling roadside ditches and marsh edges is usually this, the invasive European strain of common reed. Stands rise well overhead, two to five metres, topped by large soft plumes that fade from purplish-brown to grey as the seed ripens. The stems are packed so tightly that little else grows among them.\n\nIt spreads two ways, by seed and by creeping runners, and the result is vast single-species stands that push out native wetland plants and degrade habitat for fish and wildlife. It is counted among Ontario's worst invasive plants, most conspicuous through summer and fall when the plumes are up. Telling it from the native reed and from native grasses takes some care. If you deal with it, do not cut and move the stems, since pieces can resprout and start it somewhere new. Control is a long job, best coordinated with local programs.",
  "poison-ivy": "Few plants cause more grief on an Ontario trail than this one, and it hides in plain sight. It grows as ground cover, as a low shrub, or as a hairy climbing vine, so shape alone will not give it away. The reliable mark is leaflets in threes, glossy and often reddish in spring and fall. The old line 'leaves of three, let it be' still holds. You will meet it along woodland edges, trails, shorelines, and fencerows through southern and central Ontario.\n\nEvery part carries urushiol, an oil that causes an itchy, blistering rash on skin contact. The reaction can appear hours later and spread from anything the oil touched, including clothing, tools, and pet fur. Never burn it. The smoke carries the oil and can seriously harm the lungs and eyes. It is a native plant. Leave it alone and give it room.",
  "purple-loosestrife": "Across a marsh in midsummer, tall spikes of magenta flowers in dense stands are almost always this plant. It reaches one to two metres, and where it takes hold it can carpet a wetland to the water's edge. It was brought over as a garden ornamental, planted for its colour, and escaped.\n\nA single plant can release millions of seeds in a year. Those seeds and its spreading roots let it crowd out cattails, sedges, and the other native marsh plants that wetland wildlife depends on. What looks like a healthy purple meadow is often a wetland losing its diversity. Do not plant it, and do not move it around. If you pull it, bag the flower heads so seeds do not scatter. Some areas manage established stands with introduced leaf beetles rather than by hand. Report large infestations to your local conservation authority.",
  "stinging-nettle": "Brush bare skin against a patch of this and you will know it within seconds. Square stems and toothed leaves set in opposite pairs are covered in fine hairs that act like tiny needles, injecting irritants that raise a burning welt. It grows province-wide in rich moist soils, floodplains, thickets, and disturbed ground, usually in dense patches you can walk into before you notice.\n\nThe sting is not dangerous for most people, though it can last a few hours. Wear gloves and long sleeves if you are working around it. The plant has a long history as food and fibre. Young leaves, gathered in spring with gloves and then cooked, are nutritious and lose their sting entirely once heat breaks the hairs down. Never eat it raw. Older summer plants turn gritty and are best left alone. If you are not certain of the identification, do not gather it.",
  "wild-parsnip": "The flat-topped clusters of small yellow flowers lining an Ontario roadside in summer usually belong to this plant. It stands half a metre to a metre and a half on a grooved stem, and it is the same species as the parsnip grown for its root. Escaped from cultivation, it now spreads aggressively along roadways, ditches, fields, and other open, disturbed ground through southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe danger is in the sap. On skin, in sunlight, it triggers phytophotodermatitis. The exposed area burns and blisters, sometimes badly, and can leave marks that linger for months. Reactions can show up a day or two after contact. If you are clearing it or walking through a stand, cover your skin. If sap gets on you, wash it off, keep the area out of the sun, and watch it. The yellow flowers are the easy tell, since most other roadside umbels here are white.",
  "giant-hogweed": "There is no mistaking the size of this one when it is mature. It can reach five metres, topped by enormous white flower heads shaped like upturned umbrellas, on thick stems blotched with purple and covered in coarse bristles. It grows along streambanks, roadsides, ravines, and damp waste ground in southern and central Ontario. Do not touch it to confirm the identification.\n\nThe watery sap is the reason for the caution. Like wild parsnip but far worse, it reacts with sunlight to cause severe burns and blistering, and sap in the eyes can cause permanent damage, including blindness. This is a regulated invasive in Ontario. Do not try to remove it yourself. Report sightings to your municipality or a conservation authority and let trained people deal with it. If sap contacts your skin, get out of the sun, wash the area, and seek medical advice. Keep children and pets well back.",
  "poison-sumac": "This is a wetland plant, and that habitat is the first clue to telling it apart from its harmless relatives. It grows as a shrub or small tree, two to six metres, in bogs, swamps, and other boggy ground scattered across southern Ontario. Look for compound leaves with smooth edges, reddish stems, and loose, drooping clusters of whitish berries. The common staghorn and smooth sumacs of dry roadsides carry upright red berry cones and do not grow in swamps, which is the difference that matters.\n\nEvery part contains urushiol, the same oil found in poison ivy, and the rash it causes is often worse. Expect a severe, blistering reaction that can spread from anything the oil touched. Never handle it, and never burn it, since the smoke carries the oil and can harm the lungs and eyes. Because it keeps to wet ground that most people rarely cross, encounters are uncommon, but boardwalks and bog trails can bring you close.",
  "spotted-water-hemlock": "Among wetland plants in Ontario, this is one to learn precisely and then avoid. It stands one to two metres in marshes, wet meadows, ditches, and along streambanks province-wide, carrying umbrella-shaped clusters of tiny white flowers on stems streaked with purple. One useful mark is in the leaves. The veins run to the notches between the teeth rather than to the tips.\n\nThe warning here is serious. This is among the most poisonous plants in North America, and a small amount is enough to kill a person if eaten. The real danger is confusion. It resembles edible wild carrot and parsnip closely enough that foragers have died from the mistake. That is reason enough never to gather white-flowered umbels from wet ground unless an expert has confirmed the identification, and even then most people should not take the risk. Do not handle the plant or forage near it, and keep children and livestock away from stands.",
  "common-ringlet": "Walk through almost any grassy field in Ontario in summer and you will likely put up a small pale butterfly that bobs weakly over the tops of the grass before dropping back down. This is the Common Ringlet, a member of the satyr group, with soft ochre to buff wings. The best marks are on the underside: a grey-green hindwing crossed by a wavy whitish line, and usually a single small eyespot near the tip of the forewing. Its flight is low and unhurried, easy to follow.\n\nThe caterpillars feed on grasses, which helps explain why the adult turns up so widely. Meadows, roadsides, and any open ground with grass will do. It flies from spring through fall across the whole province. Once a more limited insect, it expanded its range across Ontario through the 20th century and is now one of the most abundant grassland butterflies here.",
  "common-wood-nymph": "A large, dark satyr that flops through tall grass on a slow, bobbing flight. The Common Wood-nymph is brown all over, with two big yellow-ringed eyespots on each forewing that stand out even at a distance. In southern Ontario those eyespots often sit inside a broad yellow forewing patch, a feature that fades in butterflies farther north, so a southern individual and a central one can look noticeably different.\n\nLook for it in tall-grass meadows, old fields, prairie remnants, marsh edges, and open woods, wherever the grasses its caterpillars eat are left standing. It flies in summer into fall across southern and central Ontario. The flight is weak and easy to track, and adults often settle low in the grass or nectar at flowers. Nothing else of its size in this habitat carries that pair of bold eyespots, so once you learn the pattern it is a straightforward butterfly to name.",
  "european-skipper": "This small tawny-orange skipper boils up out of hayfields in early summer, often in large numbers. Its wings are clean tawny-orange with narrow black edges and no pale spots, and the antennae are short with orange-tipped clubs. In a good year they rise in clouds over a hayfield as you walk through it.\n\nThe origin is well documented. This is a European species, first found in North America at London, Ontario around 1910, where it arrived in a shipment of timothy seed. It spread across the continent from that start. The caterpillars feed on timothy and other grasses, which ties the adults to hayfields, grassy meadows, roadsides, and weedy pastures. Look for it in summer in southern and central Ontario. It is an introduced species rather than a native one, though it is now a permanent and abundant part of the grassland fauna.",
  "little-wood-satyr": "The name points you into the forest, but this soft brown butterfly is just as often found bouncing along a grassy field edge or a shaded trail. Each wing, front and back, carries two bold yellow-ringed black eyespots with small silvery centres, a distinctive pattern. The flight is low and bouncy, weaving through dappled shade.\n\nLook for it in open woodlands, forest edges, brushy fields, and along shaded trails in southern and central Ontario. It flies in spring and into summer, and the caterpillars feed on grasses. Despite the wood in its name, clearings and field margins suit it as well as the woods themselves, so you need not be deep in forest to meet one. The four eyespots make it easy to separate from the plainer ringlets and wood-nymphs sharing the same edges.",
  "northern-crescent": "Through central and northern Ontario, the common small orange crescent flitting along forest edges and roadsides is usually this one. The Northern Crescent looks much like the Pearl Crescent but tends to be a little larger, with a more open orange upperside and less of the fine black netting. The club at the tip of the antenna is often orange, a detail worth checking. It flies in a single main brood in summer.\n\nThe caterpillars feed on asters, and adults turn up in clearings, meadows, and roadsides. It replaces the Pearl Crescent as the common crescent through central and northern Ontario, and where the two meet they are difficult to tell apart. In that overlap no single mark is reliable on its own; antenna colour, size, and habitat together give the best case. If you are north of the Pearl Crescent's range, the identification is usually simple by default.",
  "pearl-crescent": "Finely veined with black on an orange ground, this is the crescent of open country in southern Ontario. The Pearl Crescent is marked all over with thin black lines, and the underside of the hindwing carries the pale crescent-shaped mark near the margin that gives the group its name. It flies from spring through fall in fields, meadows, roadsides, and pastures, and the caterpillars feed on asters.\n\nIdentification is the catch. This butterfly is nearly identical to the Northern Crescent, and where the two overlap in southern Ontario a single glance is not enough. The colour of the antenna club and the habitat help separate them, but even experienced observers leave some individuals unnamed. If a crescent will not resolve cleanly, that is normal and honest; the two were long treated as one for good reason. In the southern part of the province, most crescents in open fields are this species.",
  "pecks-skipper": "Lawns, gardens, and roadside verges across Ontario turn up this small orange-and-brown grass skipper through much of the season. The field mark to learn is on the underside of the hindwing: a large, ragged patch of pale yellow spots set against a dark ground, unlike anything on the similar skippers around it. Adults often nectar on clover and other low flowers, holding the wings in the half-open pose typical of the grass skippers.\n\nIt is one of the most common lawn and garden skippers in the province and, across the south, produces two broods a year, so it is on the wing from spring into fall. The caterpillars feed on grasses, which suits it to mown and weedy ground as much as to wild meadows. Wet fields and gardens hold it too. Learn the yellow hindwing patch and you can name it without netting it.",
  "summer-azure": "A tiny blue that appears after the spring azures have finished. Males are pale silvery-blue above and females blue with dark borders, but the underside is the more useful view: chalky white with fine grey speckles, usually paler and whiter than the closely related Spring Azure. Both are small enough to be easy to overlook as they drift along a woodland edge or garden bed.\n\nTiming is the main clue. It flies later in the year than the very similar Spring Azure, with fresh adults appearing through the summer once the spring species has stopped flying. Look for it in summer and fall at woodland edges, gardens, meadows, and roadsides in southern and central Ontario. The caterpillars feed on flowers, including dogwood, meadowsweet, and New Jersey tea. Where the two azures could be confused, the season does much of the work: a fresh azure in mid to late summer is far more likely to be this one.",
  "virginia-ctenucha": "At first glance many people take this for a wasp or a beetle rather than a moth. It flies by day, with dark charcoal wings, a metallic blue-green body, and a bright orange-red head, and it often rests on flowers with the wings folded back along the body. The overall effect is dark and glossy, nothing like the drab brown of most moths.\n\nIt turns up in summer in damp meadows, fields, roadsides, and marsh edges in southern and central Ontario. Adults are commonly seen nectaring on milkweed and goldenrod, so a stand of either in flower is a good place to check. The caterpillars feed on grasses, sedges, and irises, tying the moth to open, often moist ground. Because it is active in daylight and boldly coloured, it is one of the easier moths to notice and to identify without a light trap or a net.",
  "baltimore-checkerspot": "Few Ontario butterflies are as cleanly patterned as this one. The wings are black, checkered with rows of white and cream spots and edged with a band of orange-red crescents, and orange spots mark the wing bases. Nothing else in its wet-meadow habitat looks quite like it, so a good look usually settles the identification.\n\nThe catch is where to find it. Its caterpillars depend on white turtlehead, a wetland plant, so colonies are local and tied to healthy wet meadows, fens, and marshy fields where that plant grows. Adults also use English plantain as a host in some places. It flies in summer in southern and central Ontario, and tends to stay close to the wetland that produced it rather than wandering widely. Because the butterfly is only as secure as its wetlands, draining or degrading a fen can wipe out a colony even though the species as a whole is not considered at risk.",
  "compton-tortoiseshell": "A big, ragged-winged butterfly of the northern woods, orange-brown above with dark blotches and a single small white spot near the leading edge of each wing. Turn it to the underside and the wings are bark-grey with a tiny white comma mark, so a resting adult can vanish against a tree trunk. The scalloped wing edges add to the dead-leaf effect.\n\nIt lives in northern deciduous and mixed forests, along clearings and dirt roads, in central and northern Ontario. The caterpillars feed on birch, willow, and aspen. What sets it apart from most butterflies is timing: it overwinters as an adult and can be seen flying on warm days in early spring, sometimes during a winter thaw, before any flowers have opened. A large orange butterfly on a bare, sunny road in early spring is very likely this one. It is on the wing across spring, summer, and fall.",
  "io-moth": "This large silkmoth carries a surprise on its hindwings. At rest the forewings show plainly - yellow in the male, reddish-brown in the female - but each hindwing hides a big black-and-blue eyespot that the moth flashes when startled, a sudden pair of false eyes meant to give a predator pause. It flies at night in summer and comes to lights in and near deciduous woods, forest edges, and gardens in southern Ontario.\n\nThe caution is the caterpillar, not the moth. The green larva feeds on many trees and shrubs and is covered in clusters of branched spines that deliver a painful sting. Do not touch it. The sting is a defence, not aggression, and it is easy to brush against one by accident while handling foliage. The adult moth is harmless and does not feed. The caterpillar is the one to be careful with.",
  "milberts-tortoiseshell": "You will often see this one before you can name it: a small, dark butterfly that bursts off a roadside flower and flies fast and low. Up close the dark brown wings carry a broad band that shades from orange out to yellow near the edge, with a few red flecks toward the forewing tips. Nothing else in Ontario looks quite like it.\n\nThe caterpillars feed on stinging nettle, living together in silk-webbed nests before they spread out to finish growing. Adults live a long time for a butterfly. They pass the winter tucked into woodpiles, bark crevices and sheds, then come out on the first warm days of spring, so a tortoiseshell in April is one that overwintered. You can find them in moist meadows, marsh edges, gardens and forest openings right across the province, and often into the fall on late flowers.",
  "northern-pearly-eye": "Most butterflies want sun. This satyr does not. It flies in the dappled shade of deciduous and mixed woods, often along streams and wooded trails, and stays active toward dusk when others have settled. Look for it perched head-down on a tree trunk, grey-brown wings edged with a row of black eyespots ringed in yellow. The underside is a pearly grey-brown with clean eyespots, which gives the butterfly its name.\n\nIt rarely visits flowers. Instead it feeds at running sap, rotting fruit and dung, so a baited log or a wound on a tree can be a good place to watch one. The caterpillars feed on woodland grasses. You are most likely to meet the adults in summer, in shaded wooded country across much of Ontario. Their habit of flying in low light and vanishing against bark makes them easy to walk past.",
  "promethea-moth": "A large silkmoth of deciduous woods, forest edges and swamps, the promethea breaks the usual moth rule about darkness. The males fly in the late afternoon and early evening, following the scent of a calling female rather than waiting for full night. They are nearly black with narrow tan wing borders, and in flight they can be mistaken for a dark butterfly. Females are larger, reddish-brown and more strongly patterned.\n\nThe caterpillars feed on a range of trees and shrubs, among them spicebush, black cherry, sassafras, ash and lilac. Like other giant silkmoths, the adults do not feed at all and live only long enough to mate and lay eggs. Look for them from late spring into summer in southern and central Ontario. The best chance of seeing a male is on a warm afternoon, when they patrol woodland edges in fast, direct flight.",
  "rosy-maple-moth": "Few Ontario insects are as easy to name. The wings are pink and yellow in soft blocks, the colour of sherbet, and the fuzzy body is a woolly yellow. There is nothing else it can be confused with. This is the smallest of the giant silkmoths in the province, and it turns up wherever maples grow, including parks and suburbs.\n\nIt flies at night in summer and is often found at porch lights, resting with its wings held over its back. The adults do not feed. The caterpillar, called the green-striped mapleworm, feeds mainly on maple leaves and sometimes oak, and in large numbers can strip patches of foliage, though the trees recover. Handling the moth does no harm, but there is little reason to. A resting one is best left to warm up and fly off after dark.",
  "white-lined-sphinx": "At dusk in a flower garden you may see what looks like a small hummingbird hovering at the blooms, wings a blur, uncoiling a long tongue into each flower. Look closely and it is a moth. The white-lined sphinx has brown forewings marked with a broad tan stripe from base to tip and pale lines along the veins, and a pink band that flashes on the hindwing in flight.\n\nIt is a strong flier and a migrant. In most years it is uncommon in Ontario, but in some seasons large numbers move up from the south, and gardens and meadows can hold several feeding at once from dusk into the night. The caterpillars eat a wide range of plants, among them evening primrose, purslane and grape. You are most likely to find the adults in southern Ontario in summer and fall, working flowers in the last of the light.",
  "imperial-moth": "One of the largest moths a person can find in Ontario, though few will. The pale yellow wings are heavily blotched and speckled with pinkish-brown to purplish markings, and a resting moth against bark or fallen leaves reads as a dead leaf itself. The camouflage is good enough that they are usually noticed only at lights.\n\nOntario sits at the northern edge of this moth's range. It is rare here and found mainly in the Carolinian forests of the extreme south, where oak, maple and pine grow. The caterpillars feed on the leaves and needles of those trees, and the adults, like other giant silkmoths, do not feed at all. Watch for them at night in summer near lit windows and porch lights in the far south of the province. Anywhere north of the Carolinian zone a sighting would be unusual and worth reporting.",
  "canada-darner": "Mosaic darners are large dragonflies patterned in blue and black, and telling them apart takes a close look. On this one the mark to check is the first pale side stripe on the thorax, which is deeply notched and bent forward at the top. Males carry blue spots down the abdomen; females may be blue, green or yellow.\n\nIt is among the more common mosaic darners in Ontario's cottage country, breeding at marshy ponds and boggy lake edges. Through late summer and into fall you can watch males patrol the shoreline and the open lanes between trees, hawking small insects on the wing. They keep flying into the evening, later than many dragonflies, and often hunt well back from the water in forest clearings. Caught in the hand or in a good photo, the notched stripe settles the identification. From a distance it is simply a big blue darner working the edge of the lake.",
  "chalk-fronted-corporal": "Anyone who spends early summer at a Shield lake knows this dragonfly, even without a name for it. It emerges in huge numbers and settles flat on sun-warmed rocks, logs, docks and bare trails, sometimes so thickly that you step around them. Mature males are dark, with two short chalky pale-blue stripes on the front of the thorax, the corporal bars that give the insect its name, and a pale patch at the base of the abdomen. The wings show dark bases.\n\nIt breeds at boggy ponds, lakes and slow streams across central and northern Ontario. The peak comes in late spring and early summer, when a single dock can hold dozens basking side by side. They are tame and easy to approach, which makes them a good first dragonfly to look at closely. Later in the season the numbers thin out and the survivors spread away from the water.",
  "common-baskettail": "One of the first dragonflies of the year in Ontario, out over ponds while the water is still cold. It is small, brown and noticeably hairy, with green eyes and an abdomen that thickens slightly toward the tip. A small dark spot at the base of each hindwing is a useful field mark, and the wings are otherwise clear or faintly amber.\n\nIn spring, sometimes as early as May, large numbers can emerge together and swarm low over ponds and lake edges, feeding on the wing. They patrol just above the surface and settle in nearby trees and shrubs to rest. Later in the season they are harder to notice, as the bigger, brighter dragonflies take over. Look for them at weedy ponds and slow water across southern and central Ontario. Baskettails are a tricky group, and separating the species with confidence usually takes a close look at the male appendages.",
  "dot-tailed-whiteface": "The face is the giveaway. Against an otherwise blackish body, the bright chalk-white face stands out even at a distance, a trait shared by the whitefaces as a group. On this species the male carries a single yellow spot on top of the abdomen near the tail, which names it. Females and immatures show more yellow, spotted along the back.\n\nIt is a small dragonfly of ponds, marshes and boggy lake edges, wherever lily pads and floating plants give it a place to perch. You will usually see it settled flat on a pad or a shoreline leaf, tilted to face the sun. Look for it from late spring into summer across southern and central Ontario. It is common within its habitat and often one of the easier small dragonflies to name, since the white face and the tail spot together rule out most lookalikes.",
  "eastern-forktail": "Probably the damselfly you have seen most often without noticing. It is small, thin and easy to lose among the pond plants, but it is almost everywhere there is weedy still water. Males have a green-and-black thorax, a mostly black abdomen and a small patch of blue at the tail tip. Mature females change to a powdery pale blue-grey and look quite different.\n\nIt turns up at nearly any weedy pond, ditch or marsh across the whole province, and it is on the wing from late spring well into fall, a longer season than many. Because it is so common and so widespread, it is a good species to learn first. Get close, since the small size and the way it perches low among vegetation make it easy to overlook. Once you have your eye in, you will see it almost every time you stop by still water.",
  "familiar-bluet": "Bluets are the slender blue-and-black damselflies that hover over pond edges in summer, and there are many similar species. This one leans toward more blue than black along the abdomen, but that alone will not clinch it. The bluets are a genuinely hard group, and the sure way to name one is to look closely, usually in the hand or in a sharp photo, at the shape of the small appendages at the tip of the tail.\n\nWhat sets this species apart in the field is its habit. It is a wide-ranging, adaptable bluet that moves quickly into new and man-made ponds, including recently dug or stocked water where other species have not yet arrived. It is common at wetlands and lake margins across southern Ontario through summer and into fall. If you find a bluet at a brand-new pond, this is a likely candidate, but treat any bluet identification as tentative until you have checked the details.",
  "four-spotted-skimmer": "A medium brownish dragonfly that holds still on a sunny perch, wings held flat, is often this one. The name comes from four dark spots, one at the middle of the leading edge of each wing, with amber-tinged dark patches at the base of the hindwings. Males and females look the same, which is unusual among skimmers.\n\nFew dragonflies are as widely spread. Its range circles the northern hemisphere, and in Ontario it turns up almost everywhere there is standing water, from marshes and bog ponds in the south to the wetlands of the Hudson Bay lowlands. Look for it over slow, well-vegetated water where it perches in the open and darts out after passing insects. It flies through spring and summer. On a breezy day at a marsh edge it is one of the easier dragonflies to name, since the wing spots show clearly even in flight.",
  "sedge-sprite": "Easy to walk past. This is one of the smallest damselflies in the region, a thread of an insect with a metallic green back and a small patch of pale blue at the tip of the tail. It flies weakly and stays low, drifting a few centimetres above the sedges rather than out over open water.\n\nTo find it you have to look down into sedge meadows, boggy fens and grassy wetland edges, then watch for the faint green glint as one shifts perches. Despite the small size and hidden habits it is widespread across Ontario, common in the right wet ground even where few people notice it. It flies in spring and summer. A close look, ideally with a hand lens or a photo, helps, because at this size the field marks are hard to read with the naked eye.",
  "american-rubyspot": "Broad-winged damselflies are built differently from the slim pond species, and this is one of the more colourful. A mature male carries a brilliant metallic red patch at the base of each wing and a red-bronze body, colours that flash as it flutters. Females are duller, with amber rather than red at the wing bases.\n\nIt is tied to moving water. Look along clean rivers and streams with riffles in southern Ontario, where males take up perches on streamside plants and rocks over the current and defend them against rivals in slow, fluttering chases. The broad wings and streamside habit set it apart from the narrow-winged dancers and bluets that share the same shores. Flight runs through summer into fall. Because it needs clean, well-oxygenated water, a good population is a fair sign of a healthy stream.",
  "black-saddlebags": "The dark blotches at the base of each hindwing look like saddlebags slung over a horse, and they name the insect. It is a large dark skimmer, the body black with a row of pale spots down the abdomen, and it spends most of its time on the wing, soaring and gliding on broad wings rather than perching.\n\nYou often see it well away from water, cruising over fields and roadsides. It reaches Ontario mainly as a migrant, moving north each summer, and in fall southbound individuals gather with other dragonflies along the Great Lakes shorelines, where migration can be watched on a good day. It favours open ponds, lakes and wetlands for breeding. The constant gliding flight and the saddlebag marks make it recognizable at a distance, even when it never settles long enough for a close look.",
  "powdered-dancer": "Most of our small damselflies stay at ponds, but the dancers favour moving water, and this is the largest of them. Mature males develop a coat of pale blue-white powder, a bloom that is heaviest toward the tail and gives the insect its name. Both sexes perch on sun-warmed rocks along the water and flick their wings in a quick, restless way as they rest.\n\nLook for it on rivers, streams and rocky lakeshores in southern and central Ontario, out on the stones over moving current rather than back in the vegetation. The powdery finish and the habit of settling on bare rock help separate it from the bluets. It flies through summer and into fall. On a warm afternoon a rocky riffle can hold a scattering of them, each holding its own patch of stone.",
  "racket-tailed-emerald": "Emeralds are named for their eyes, and up close this one shows the brilliant green stare of the group. The body is small and dark, nearly unmarked, with a slender abdomen that widens into a small club, or racket, near the tip. That shape is the field mark the name points to.\n\nIt is a dragonfly of the Canadian Shield, at home on acidic, boggy ponds and small lakes in central and northern Ontario. On sunny mornings males patrol low over the water, running the same short circuit again and again, which makes them easier to watch than to catch. The flight season is short, mainly through summer. Boggy Shield water is not always easy to get to, and that, along with the modest size and dark colour, is why this is a species many people overlook.",
  "brown-belted-bumble-bee": "A common bumble bee of open country in the south. The thorax is yellow with a black spot between the wings, and the belt across the second segment of the abdomen is the mark to check: yellow-brown, narrow, often broken or shaped like a crescent, and fading to reddish at the sides. Queens run a little over two centimetres, workers smaller.\n\nIt nests on or just below the ground, often in a grass tussock, in fields, meadows, gardens and farmland. It tolerates heat better than some of its relatives, and it has held its numbers while several other native bumble bees in Ontario have declined, so it is one you are still likely to meet. It is out from spring through fall, working a wide range of flowers. Away from the nest it is docile, but like any bumble bee it can sting, and repeatedly, if it is handled or the nest is disturbed. Leave a ground nest alone and there is no trouble.",
  "leafcutter-bee": "The tidiest evidence of this bee is not the insect but the neat semicircular notches cut from the leaves of roses and other garden plants. Those pieces are not eaten. A female carries them back to her nest to build with. The bee itself is stout and dark, and it carries pollen under the abdomen rather than on the legs, so a returning female shows a bright yellow or orange belly.\n\nNests go in hollow stems, old beetle borings and other narrow cavities in gardens, meadows, farmland and woodland edges. Inside, the female rolls her cut leaf discs into a thimble-shaped cell, packs it with pollen and lays a single egg. Most are native, though the introduced alfalfa leafcutter bee is kept and managed as a crop pollinator in the province. The flight is a summer affair. These are solitary bees with no nest to defend, non-aggressive, and they sting only if handled. The sting is mild.",
  "mason-bee": "Among the first bees on the wing in spring, often out while the mornings are still cold. These are small, rounded bees, many of them a metallic blue-black or dark green that catches the light. Females carry pollen on dense hairs under the abdomen rather than on the legs.\n\nThe name comes from how they build. A female seals each nest cell with a wall of mud, working in hollow stems, old beetle holes and the paper tubes of a backyard bee house. Because they are out so early and visit blossom readily, native mason bees are valuable pollinators of apples and other orchard fruit in Ontario, and orchardists sometimes encourage them. Most here are native, though a few are introduced. They are active in spring and into summer. Mason bees are gentle and rarely sting, and when they do the sting is very mild, so a bee house near a doorway is no cause for worry.",
  "mining-bee": "A small mound of soil around a hole about the width of a pencil, out in a lawn or a sandy bank, is usually the work of a mining bee. The bees are small to medium and fuzzy, often brownish or grey, with dense pollen hairs on the hind legs and a groove of pale hairs beside each eye. Many species fly only in early spring and are gone within a few weeks.\n\nFemales dig branching tunnels in bare or sparsely vegetated soil, then stock each side cell with pollen for a single larva. You find them right across the province, in lawns, gardens, meadows, sandy banks and woodland edges. Where the soil suits them, dozens may nest close together, which can look alarming. It is not. These are solitary bees, each female tending her own burrow, and they are very docile. They almost never sting, and the sting is weak. A patch of nesting mining bees in the yard can simply be left alone.",
  "tricoloured-bumble-bee": "Few Ontario bumble bees are as easy to name. A bright orange band runs across the middle of the abdomen, set between yellow and black, above a yellow thorax marked with a black spot. That orange belt is unlike anything on our other bumble bees, and it gives the insect its other common name, the orange-belted bumble bee.\n\nThis is a bee of cooler country. It is most common through central and northern Ontario, in boreal clearings, bogs, meadows and gardens, and it grows scarcer toward the far south. Colonies nest underground. It works blueberry, goldenrod and rose among many other flowers, and in the north it is one of the bumble bees you are most likely to see. The season runs from spring, when queens emerge, through to fall. It is generally gentle, but a nest is defended, and the workers can sting more than once if it is disturbed. Give a ground nest room and it will leave you be.",
  "european-hornet": "The only true hornet established in Canada, brought over from Europe and now living in the woodlands and older neighbourhoods of southern Ontario and Quebec. It is a very large wasp, well bigger than a yellowjacket, with a rusty-brown thorax and a yellow abdomen marked with brown. One habit sets it apart from most wasps: it flies at night and will come to lit windows, which is often how people first meet it.\n\nColonies build paper nests in hollow trees, wall voids and outbuildings, and they can go unnoticed until the nest is large. Away from the nest, a foraging hornet is not looking for trouble. Close to one, it is another matter. The sting is painful, it can be delivered repeatedly, and the hornet defends its nest vigorously. Stings can cause severe allergic reactions in some people. If you find a nest near a house, keep clear of it and have it handled by someone equipped for the job rather than knocking it down yourself. It is active mainly in summer and fall.",
  "hoary-squash-bee": "If you grow squash or pumpkins, this is the bee most likely doing the work. About the size of a honey bee, robust and brown, with pale bands of hair across the abdomen. Males carry a yellow spot on the face and often spend the night curled inside a closed squash flower, which is a reliable place to find them. In Ontario the bee depends almost entirely on cultivated squash and pumpkin for pollen, and it is the main pollinator of those crops. It works at dawn, before honey bees are up, so much of its foraging is done by the time most gardeners are out.\n\nFemales dig nest burrows in the ground, often right in or beside the crop they visit. There is little reason to worry about it. Males cannot sting at all, and females sting only if you take hold of one.",
  "potter-wasp": "The best clue is the waist. This slender black wasp has a narrow, stalk-like connection between thorax and abdomen that gives the body a long-necked flask shape, marked here and there with thin ivory or yellow lines. The name comes from the nest. A female gathers mud and builds a small round pot about the size of a marble on a twig, stem or wall, packs it with caterpillars she has paralyzed, lays a single egg and seals the lid. The larva then feeds on the stored caterpillars inside.\n\nYou meet the adults in summer around gardens, meadows and woodland edges where there are flowers to feed on. It lives alone rather than in a colony, and it has no shared nest to defend, so it pays people little attention. A sting is possible only if you handle one.",
  "sand-wasp": "On a hot dune or open beach, watch for a stout wasp flying low and fast just above the surface. The abdomen is black with bold pale green or ivory bands, and the eyes are large. Several often dig their burrows near one another, forming loose colonies in the sand, though each female tends her own nest.\n\nWhat sets this wasp apart is how it feeds its young. Rather than stocking a nest all at once and sealing it, the female returns day after day to bring her growing larva fresh flies, a habit called progressive provisioning. You find it in summer on dunes, sandy beaches, pits and bare ground near water, including along Great Lakes shorelines. It shows no interest in people. The main way to get stung is to grab one or step on it with bare feet.",
  "wool-carder-bee": "At a glance this looks like a wasp, black with a row of bright yellow spots down each side of the abdomen, but it is hairy and stocky, and it is a bee. It is not native. Brought over from Europe, it is now established across southern Ontario, including city gardens. The name refers to the female's habit of scraping, or carding, the fuzzy hairs from plants such as lamb's ear into a ball, which she carries off to line her nest in an existing cavity.\n\nMales are larger than females, unusual among bees, and they patrol patches of flowers, hovering and driving off anything that intrudes. A male has no sting, but he will body-slam rival insects out of his territory. Females can sting if you handle one. Look for it in summer wherever soft-leaved plants and mint are in bloom.",
  "colorado-potato-beetle": "Ten black stripes running the length of yellow wing cases make this one easy to name, and the orange head is dotted with black. The larvae are just as recognizable: plump and orange, with rows of black dots along each side. It is a serious pest of potatoes, and also feeds on tomato, eggplant and their relatives. Both the adults and the larvae eat the leaves, and a heavy infestation can strip a plant bare.\n\nThe beetle is well known for how quickly it evolves resistance to insecticides, which makes it hard to control by chemicals alone. In a garden, picking beetles and larvae off by hand and dropping them in soapy water is a practical option on a small scale. Do not move infested plants around, since that spreads the problem. It does no harm to people. You can find it from spring through fall across southern and central Ontario wherever potatoes grow.",
  "convergent-lady-beetle": "This is one of the native ladybugs, oval and orange-red with as many as thirteen black spots. The name comes from the plate behind the head, which is black with a white border and two short white dashes that angle toward each other. Both the adults and the larvae hunt aphids and eat them in large numbers, which makes the beetle welcome in a garden. Look for it from spring through fall in fields, gardens, crops and meadows, wherever aphids build up on plants.\n\nIn the western mountains it is known for gathering in huge clusters to spend the winter, and collectors sometimes scoop these up and sell them for pest control. Released beetles tend to disperse quickly, so the practice is less useful than it sounds. In Ontario you are more likely to come across it at work on an infested stem.",
  "margined-carrion-beetle": "Turn up a dead animal in the woods and this flattened black beetle may already be there. The plate behind the head is ringed with a broad pinkish to orange margin, and that pale border is the quickest way to tell it from the similar American carrion beetle. Adults and larvae feed on carrion, and also on the fly maggots competing for the same carcass, which speeds a dead animal back into the soil. It is often among the first beetles to arrive at a fresh body, and it turns up as well at dung and rotting fungi.\n\nYou can find it from spring through fall in forests and along woodland edges across much of Ontario. Handling one is unpleasant rather than harmful. It may give off a foul smell when picked up.",
  "red-milkweed-beetle": "Search a patch of milkweed in summer and you may find this red-orange longhorn beetle with black spots and long black antennae. It is tied entirely to milkweed. The larvae bore in the roots and the adults feed on the leaves, so where there is no milkweed there is no beetle. Like the monarch, it takes up the plant's toxins, and its bright red colour is a warning to birds that it tastes bad.\n\nThere is an odd detail behind the name. The base of each antenna sits right where the eye would be and divides it into an upper and a lower half, so the beetle has, in effect, four eyes. Its genus name, Tetraopes, means just that. Look for it through the summer in meadows, fields, roadsides and gardens wherever milkweed grows across southern and central Ontario.",
  "rose-chafer": "In early summer these beetles can appear almost overnight on sandy ground, feeding in groups. The body is slender and tan to greenish-brown, small, with long spiny orange legs that look too big for it and a fuzzy underside. They gather on rose, grape and other blossoms and leaves, and in numbers they can do real damage in gardens, vineyards and orchards. The larvae develop underground in sandy soil, feeding on grass roots, which is why the adults are most common where the soil is sandy.\n\nThis matters if you keep birds. The beetles carry a toxin, and poultry or pet birds that eat them can be poisoned or killed, so it is not a beetle to feed to chickens. You will see the adults in late spring and early summer, then they are gone for the year.",
  "banded-net-winged-beetle": "Soft-bodied and flat, this beetle does not look built for defence, and it relies on chemistry instead of armour. The wing covers are wide and orange, crossed by a broad blue-black band, and they carry a raised, net-like pattern of ridges that gives the group its name. Toward the rear the wing covers flare out wider, so the beetle looks slightly wedge-shaped.\n\nThe bright colours are a warning. It is loaded with bitter chemicals, and birds learn to leave it alone, so much so that several harmless insects copy its pattern to borrow the protection. Look for the adults in summer resting on foliage and flowers at the edges of moist woodland. The larvae live out of sight in rotting wood. There is nothing here to trouble a person.",
  "fiery-searcher": "Few beetles here are as colourful. The wing cases shine metallic green edged with red-gold, and the thorax and legs are a deep blue-violet. It is a large ground beetle, and a hunter. Both the adult and its larva chase down caterpillars, and unlike most ground beetles they will climb well up into trees after them, which is how the second name, caterpillar hunter, was earned. That appetite makes it useful. When a forest caterpillar such as the tent caterpillar breaks out in large numbers, these beetles help bring the population down.\n\nYou may find one in spring or summer on the ground in forests, fields, gardens and along roadsides, or moving through the branches. Handle it and you will regret it a little. It can spray a pungent defensive fluid and deliver a sharp nip with its jaws.",
  "locust-borer": "Come late summer, check flowering goldenrod for a black longhorn beetle crossed by bright yellow bands, including a zigzag or W-shaped band across the middle, with reddish legs. The goldenrod is where the adults gather in autumn to feed on pollen and to mate. Its life is tied to a single tree. After mating, females lay their eggs in the bark of black locust, and the larvae tunnel into the wood, where their feeding weakens the tree and can leave it prone to breaking.\n\nBecause of this the beetle is found only where black locust grows, which in Ontario means scattered sites across the south and centre, since the tree itself is not originally native this far north. There is nothing about it to harm a person.",
  "nine-spotted-lady-beetle": "This ladybug used to be one of the most common in Ontario. That changed in the late 1900s, when its numbers crashed for reasons still not fully understood, and it is now listed as Endangered in the province. Recognizing it takes care. Look for a red-orange beetle carrying nine black spots, four on each wing case with a ninth split across the seam where the two covers meet. The plate behind the head is black with two pale front corners. Introduced ladybugs look much the same, so count the spots and take a photo rather than trust a quick glance.\n\nIt lives in meadows, farmland and gardens where aphids are plentiful, active from spring through fall, though it is now extremely localized. Any find is worth reporting to a conservation authority, since records help track how the species is doing. It is legally protected here. Observe and photograph it where it sits, and leave it alone. Do not collect it.",
  "american-hover-fly": "Hover in place near a flower, then dart off and hold still again, and you may be watching one of these. The black abdomen crossed by yellow bands reads as a small wasp at first, but the resemblance is a bluff. This is a fly. It has one pair of wings rather than two, large fly eyes, and none of the narrow waist a wasp carries. It cannot sting or bite.\n\nYou will meet it province-wide from spring into fall, anywhere flowers bloom. Gardens, meadows, roadsides and the edges of farm fields all suit it. Adults feed at flowers and move pollen as they go. The larvae are the real reason gardeners welcome the species. They look like small pale maggots and crawl over leaves and stems eating aphids, often clearing a colony before it gets started.",
  "backswimmer": "Watch a still pond in spring and you may see a small boat-shaped insect rowing along upside down, back toward the bottom. That upside-down swim is the quickest way to tell it from the water boatman, which travels right-side up. Long, oar-like hind legs push it through the water, and it is often among the first insects to move into a new garden pond.\n\nIt breathes by trapping a silvery film of air against its body, then hangs head-down at the surface now and then to renew the supply. Underwater it hunts hard, taking other insects and even small tadpoles. If you scoop one up and hold it, expect a sharp jab from its beak, which is why some people call it the water wasp. The bite stings but does no real harm. Better to watch it work from the bank than to catch it in the hand.",
  "crane-fly": "At a glance this looks like a mosquito enlarged to an alarming size, with thread-thin legs trailing below a slow, drifting flight. The size prompts a lot of needless worry. It has no biting mouthparts and cannot draw blood. The legs are so fragile they snap off at a touch, which is often how you end up holding a single leg while the fly bumbles away.\n\nNicknames like mosquito hawk and daddy longlegs fly suggest it preys on mosquitoes. It does not, and the adults barely feed at all. Most live just long enough to mate and lay eggs. The larvae, sometimes called leatherjackets, develop out of sight in moist soil, wet leaf litter or shallow water. Adults turn up province-wide from spring through fall in damp meadows, along streams, on lawns and around gardens, usually resting on a wall or drifting close to the ground.",
  "european-earwig": "The pincers at the tail end give this insect away at once. It is slim, flattened and shiny reddish-brown, and the curved forceps look more menacing than they are. On males the pincers curve strongly; on females they are nearly straight. Both are used in defence and in courtship, not for attacking people.\n\nBrought over from Europe, it is now common across settled parts of southern and central Ontario, at home in gardens, compost, mulch and the damp spaces under stones and boards. It comes out mainly at night and tucks into tight, moist crevices by day, which is how it sometimes turns up indoors in a damp corner. The old story that earwigs crawl into sleeping people's ears is just that, a story with no truth to it. At worst the pincers manage a light pinch you would barely feel.",
  "fork-tailed-bush-katydid": "Green and slender, with thread-like antennae longer than its own body, this katydid holds its narrow leaf-shaped wings roof-wise over its back and melts into the foliage. The name comes from a small forked appendage at the tip of the male's abdomen, visible only up close. Unlike the heavier true katydid that calls from high in the trees, this one often sits on flowers and garden plants well within reach.\n\nIts song is easy to overlook. Rather than a loud, repeated phrase, the male gives a short, soft series of lisping tsip notes that fades into the background of a summer evening. Look for it on shrubs, tall weeds and forest edges through summer and fall in southern and central Ontario. It feeds on leaves and flowers and does no meaningful harm to the plants it visits.",
  "green-stink-bug": "Evenly grass-green and shaped like a shield, this bug is one of the easier stink bugs to name on sight. A narrow pale yellow or orange line runs along the outer edge of the body. The antennae are plain, without the white bands of the introduced brown marmorated stink bug. Look for it from summer into fall in gardens, orchards and along field edges, where it feeds on leaves, seeds and developing fruit.\n\nThe name is earned. When handled or threatened it releases a strong, lasting smell from glands on the thorax. The odour is a defence that puts off birds and other predators, and it clings to skin and clothing longer than you would like. There is no need to kill it. In a home garden it does minor feeding damage at most and is easily nudged onto a leaf and moved along.",
  "meadow-spittlebug": "The easiest way to find this insect is to look for its spit. In spring the nymph hides inside a frothy white blob on plant stems, the mass country people call cuckoo spit. The froth is not saliva. The nymph excretes fluid and blows air through it to build a cluster of bubbles that hides it from predators and keeps it from drying out in the sun.\n\nThe adult is a small, dull brown to grey hopper with a hunched, frog-faced look. Disturb one and it is gone, launched by a jump that, for its size, is among the most powerful of any insect. It turns up in meadows, gardens, old fields and along roadsides on many soft-stemmed plants across southern and central Ontario, mostly in spring and early summer. Naturalized rather than native, it is common and does little harm at ordinary numbers.",
  "red-banded-leafhopper": "Few insects this small are so easy to pick out. The wings are boldly striped in bright red and blue-green, which has earned it the second name candy-striped leafhopper. The body is slender and wedge-shaped, tapering to the rear like most of its kind. Even a quick look is usually enough to be sure of it.\n\nIt feeds on plant sap across a wide range of hosts, roses, brambles and rhododendron among them, and shows up in gardens, hedgerows and forest edges through southern and central Ontario from spring into fall. Approach one and it does what leafhoppers do, scuttling sideways around the stem or flicking off in a short hop before you get close. It does no serious damage to garden plants and is worth a second look for the colour alone.",
  "red-legged-grasshopper": "If you flush a grasshopper from a weedy Ontario field, odds are good it is this one. It is the most common and widespread grasshopper in the province, medium brown with a reddish cast to the back and bright red hind shins lined with dark spines. Those red shins are the mark to check. The underwings are clear rather than coloured, so it does not flash any colour in flight the way some grasshoppers do.\n\nLook for it in meadows, roadsides, gardens and old pastures through summer and fall. Numbers build as the season goes on, and by late summer it can become common enough to nibble crops and garden plants, though the damage is usually minor. It is harmless to handle. Like other grasshoppers it may spit a drop of brown fluid, the so-called tobacco juice, when picked up, and that washes off easily.",
  "spined-soldier-bug": "Sharp, pointed shoulders on the thorax set this brown stink bug apart from the pest species it otherwise resembles. Compared with the plant-feeding brown stink bugs, its shoulder spines are longer and more sharply drawn, and it carries a slim, spear-like beak rather than a broad one. That beak is a clue to how it lives.\n\nThis is a hunter, not a plant feeder. It stalks caterpillars and beetle larvae over leaves and stems, spearing them with the beak and feeding on the contents. Its prey includes garden pests such as the Colorado potato beetle, which makes it worth knowing and worth leaving alone when you find it. Look for it from spring through fall in gardens, crops, meadows and along woodland edges in southern and central Ontario. Where it is common, it does quiet work keeping pest numbers down.",
  "water-boatman": "Oval and flattened, marked with fine dark cross-lines on the back, this bug rows through still water with fringed, oar-like hind legs. It swims right-side up, which is the simple way to separate it from the backswimmer that travels belly-up. Unlike that relative, it does not bite.\n\nMost water boatmen are gentle feeders. They sweep algae and fine particles from soft, mucky bottoms, which makes them one of the few plant-eating true bugs in fresh water. Males have an odd habit worth listening for: they draw a faint, fluttering underwater song by rubbing body parts together, part of courtship in ponds and marshes. The group turns up in ponds, lakes and slow water across Ontario from spring through fall, often among algae in the shallows. Scoop a netful of pond water and one or two usually come up with it.",
  "ambush-bug": "On a goldenrod head in late summer, one of the flower's own petals can turn out to be a predator. The ambush bug is small and angular, yellow and brown, with a broadened, jagged abdomen and front legs thickened like a tiny mantis. It sits motionless where the colours of the bloom hide it, then waits for the flower to bring food to it. Bees, flies and butterflies that land to feed are seized in those grasping front legs, often prey much larger than the bug itself.\n\nLook for it on late-summer flowers in meadows, gardens and old fields across southern and central Ontario, active by day through summer and fall. It is native and does no harm. Handled, it may give a small jab with its beak that stings briefly, so it is better watched than picked up. Once you have found one, you tend to start noticing them on flower after flower.",
  "brown-marmorated-stink-bug": "A mottled brown, shield-shaped bug that turns up on sunny walls and, come autumn, inside houses. Several native stink bugs look similar, so the marks matter: alternating white and dark bands on the last two antenna segments, white notches along the edge of the abdomen, and shoulders that are smooth and rounded rather than spined.\n\nThis is an import from Asia, first confirmed in Ontario around 2010 and now established across the south, including the Niagara, Hamilton and Toronto areas. It feeds on fruit and vegetable crops and can do real damage in orchards and gardens. In fall it gathers on warm outer walls and works its way indoors to overwinter, sometimes in large clusters. It does not bite or sting, but it releases a pungent smell when disturbed or crushed, so vacuum indoor clusters rather than squashing them. Sightings are still worth reporting, since tracking its spread helps growers know where it has reached.",
  "common-true-katydid": "More often a voice than a sighting. High in the oaks on warm late-summer nights, males trade a loud, raspy call back and forth, the sound written out as katy-did, katy-didn't, that gives the insect its name. Finding the singer is another matter. It is robust and leaf-green, with a strongly humped back and broad, ribbed wings that match a leaf so well it all but disappears among them.\n\nIn Ontario the common true katydid lives only in the Carolinian south, in the forests and leafy neighbourhoods around Windsor, Niagara and Toronto. It keeps to the deciduous canopy, especially oaks, and rarely comes down to eye level. Listen for the chorus from mid-summer into fall, after dark. There is nothing to be wary of. If you want to see one, the reliable method is patience and a light, and even then the trees usually win.",
  "eastern-dobsonfly": "Few Ontario insects are this big. An adult dobsonfly is a soft, grey-brown creature with long wings folded flat over the body, and in the males a pair of curved, tusk-like mandibles far longer than the head. The effect is alarming and mostly bluff. Those great jaws are used to grip females during mating and cannot close hard enough to hurt. Females are the ones to respect: their jaws are short but strong, and can deliver a firm, memorable pinch.\n\nThe species depends on clean, fast-flowing water. Its larva, the hellgrammite, spends two or three years under stream stones as a fierce aquatic predator, and anglers prize it as bait. Adults emerge in summer, rest on streamside plants by day and come to lights at night. Look for them along rivers and streams through southern and central Ontario. Handle with a little care, mind the females, and there is nothing here that can do you real harm.",
  "wheel-bug": "The gear on its back settles the identification. No other insect in Ontario carries a raised, cog-like crest on the thorax, shaped like half a wheel, and once you have seen it the wheel bug is unmistakable. It is a large grey assassin bug with long legs and a stout, curved beak folded back under the head.\n\nThis is a southern insect at the very edge of its range here, reaching only the Carolinian zone of extreme southern Ontario around Windsor and Niagara. It hunts by day on shrubs and flower heads at woodland edges, in old fields, orchards and gardens, ambushing caterpillars and beetles, spearing them with its beak and draining the body. That same beak is the reason to keep your hands off. A wheel bug can deliver a sharp, painful stab if handled, and the wound is slow to heal, though not dangerous. Watch it work, but do not pick it up.",
  "clouded-sulphur": "A lemon-yellow butterfly of clover and alfalfa, common enough to be one of the first most people learn. The upper wings carry a solid black border, broken by yellow spots in females, and a small dark spot on the forewing cell. Groups often gather to puddle at damp soil, drawing up moisture and minerals.\n\nIt turns up almost anywhere open and weedy across the whole province, from roadsides and lawns to farm fields, from spring through fall, wherever legumes grow for the caterpillars to eat. Telling it from the orange sulphur can be tricky, and part of the answer is hidden from us. Males carry an ultraviolet pattern on the wings that we cannot see but that the butterflies use to recognize their own kind. Some females skip the yellow altogether and emerge in a pale whitish form known as alba. None of this is a problem to be near; it is simply one of the steadier sights of an Ontario summer.",
  "eastern-comma": "Wings folded, it is a dead leaf. Opened, they flash ragged-edged orange marked with black spots. The name comes from the underside, where a small silver-white hook with swollen ends sits at the centre of each hindwing like a comma. That mark and the tattered wing outline are the things to check.\n\nThe eastern comma is a butterfly of deciduous woods, riverbanks, swamp edges and gardens through southern and central Ontario, wherever its caterpillars can find nettles, hops or elm. It is not much of a flower visitor. Adults prefer tree sap, rotting fruit and dung, so a fermenting windfall apple will draw one in more reliably than a flower bed. Because the adults overwinter rather than dying off, they are among the first butterflies out, and on a mild late-winter day you may find one basking on bark or bare ground long before the season proper has begun.",
  "eastern-tailed-blue": "Easy to walk past. This is one of the smallest butterflies you will meet, a scrap of colour low over short grass and trail edges. Males are powder-blue above, females greyer; both show a pale grey underside sprinkled with black dots, an orange spot or two near the margin, and a very short, thread-like tail on each hindwing. That tail sets it apart, since most of our small blues have none.\n\nLook for it in short-grass fields, lawns, trail edges and disturbed weedy ground across southern and central Ontario, from spring into fall, wherever clovers and other small legumes grow for the caterpillars. Those caterpillars keep interesting company. Ants tend them for the sugary droplets they secrete and, in return, guard them from predators, an exchange that plays out unseen at ground level. It asks nothing of a passer-by beyond a little attention to notice it is there at all.",
  "great-spangled-fritillary": "A big, strong-flying orange butterfly of summer meadows. From above the wings are orange-brown, patterned with black spots and chevrons; the surer mark is underneath, where the hindwing is warm brown scattered with bright silvery-white spots and crossed by a wide pale band. That silver spangling gives the butterfly its name.\n\nIt favours moist meadows, forest clearings and roadsides with plenty of nectar, and visits milkweed, thistle and other tall summer flowers in southern and central Ontario through summer and into fall. Its life is bound to violets. Females lay their eggs in late summer near the plants rather than on them, and the tiny caterpillars hatch, then settle in for winter without feeding. They wait out the cold and begin to eat only in spring, when fresh violet leaves push up. No violets, no fritillary, which ties it to the health of the ground layer in its meadows and woods.",
  "isabella-tiger-moth": "Most people know the caterpillar and never notice the moth. The woolly bear is the familiar bristly caterpillar, black at both ends with a rusty-orange band across the middle, found crossing roads and paths in fall. The adult it becomes is a fuzzy moth in yellow-orange to tan, faintly specked with dark, out at night and easy to overlook.\n\nOld weather lore says a wider orange band means a milder winter to come. It does not. The band simply reflects the caterpillar's age, widening as it grows. The real trick is harder to see: the woolly bear can survive being frozen solid, producing a natural antifreeze that carries it through an Ontario winter so it can finish growing in spring. You will find it province-wide in fields, meadows, forest edges and gardens, feeding on plantain, dandelion, grasses and many other low plants. The bristles are not venomous, though handling one may leave a mild, brief itch on sensitive skin.",
  "question-mark-butterfly": "Close relative of the eastern comma and a step larger. The two share the ragged wings and dead-leaf undersides, but the question mark carries a small violet-tipped tail on each hindwing, and its silver underside mark is a curved line with a separate dot below it, the pair reading like a question mark. That extra dot is the thing to look for.\n\nIt lives along woodland edges and in parks, city gardens and wooded suburbs of southern and central Ontario, where its caterpillars feed on elm, hackberry, nettle and hops. The butterfly comes in two looks through the year. Summer adults have mostly dark hindwings, while the form that emerges to overwinter shows more orange and stronger violet along the wing edges. Like the comma, it prefers sap and rotting fruit to flowers, and the overwintering adults are out early and late in the season, on the wing on mild days at the margins of the cold.",
  "silver-spotted-skipper": "A large skipper that reads as brown in flight until it settles. On the forewing sits a golden-orange band, and under each hindwing a bright silvery-white patch that names the insect and confirms it at a glance. It flies fast and low with quick, darting wingbeats, and often pauses on a flower with wings half-open.\n\nMeadows, gardens, forest edges and roadsides of southern and central Ontario hold it through the summer, wherever its caterpillars can reach legumes such as black locust, hog-peanut or wisteria. The caterpillar has an unusual habit. It lives inside a shelter of leaves sewn shut with silk, and it fires its droppings clear of that shelter, up to forty body-lengths away. The likely reason is defence: predators that hunt by scent have a harder time finding a caterpillar that keeps its waste at a distance. Nothing about the adult calls for caution.",
  "spring-azure": "One of the first butterflies most people notice once the snow starts to go. Small and silvery-blue above with narrow black wing margins, it is chalky pale grey below with fine dark specks. Look for it along woodland edges, in clearings, at swamp borders and in gardens, sometimes flying while patches of snow still linger. The caterpillars feed on the flower buds of dogwoods, viburnums and other shrubs.\n\nAzures have long given naturalists trouble. What was once treated as a single species is now understood to be a group of closely related forms, and telling them apart in the field is not always possible. For most observers the safe call is simply a spring azure. It flies in spring and into summer, active by day, and favours southern and central Ontario.",
  "white-admiral": "A large woodland butterfly of the north, blackish above with a broad white band crossing both wings and rows of blue and orange spots near the margins. It glides between sunny patches along forest trails and dirt roads, in mixed and deciduous forest, and the caterpillars feed on birch, poplar, willow and cherry.\n\nThe white form belongs to central and northern Ontario. Further south the same species shows up as the 'red-spotted purple,' a tailless form that drops the white band entirely and takes on darker, bluer colours. That form is thought to mimic the distasteful pipevine swallowtail, gaining some protection by the resemblance. Where the two ranges overlap they interbreed, and intergrades with partial white bands turn up. A butterfly that looks part admiral and part red-spotted purple is one of these intergrades, one species wearing two patterns that meet across the province.",
  "hummingbird-clearwing": "Plenty of gardeners meet this one before they know what it is. A plump sphinx moth that flies by day, it hovers at flowers on whirring wings and is easily taken for a very small hummingbird. The body is olive above and burgundy below, and the wings carry clear panels that give the group its name.\n\nThose windows are not there at emergence. A newly emerged adult has fully scaled wings, and the scales over the clear patches shake loose during its first flight. Find it at gardens, meadows, forest edges and roadsides wherever flowers are thick, from spring through fall in southern and central Ontario. The caterpillars feed on honeysuckle, hawthorn and viburnum. It does not sting and does no harm at a flowerbed.",
  "polyphemus-moth": "Among the largest moths in the province, with a wingspan that can pass 10 cm. The wings are tan to reddish, and each hindwing carries a big transparent eyespot ringed in blue and yellow, with a smaller spot on each forewing. When something threatens it, the moth flashes those hindwing eyes, which can startle a predator long enough to matter. The name comes from Polyphemus, the one-eyed giant of Greek myth.\n\nAdults do not feed. They emerge to mate and live only about a week, so a resting one on a wall or screen is spending part of a short life. The caterpillars are the long-lived stage, feeding on oak, maple, birch and many other hardwoods in deciduous and mixed woods, orchards and shaded suburbs. Adults fly at night and come to lights, which is where most people run into them, in spring and summer across southern and central Ontario.",
  "autumn-meadowhawk": "A small red dragonfly that outlasts almost everything else. Mature males have a bright red abdomen, and the mark that sets them apart from other meadowhawks is the leg colour: yellowish rather than black. Look on warm surfaces near ponds, marshes, ditches and slow water, where it likes to bask late in the day and late in the year.\n\nThis is often the last dragonfly still flying in Ontario, sometimes into late October or November after the first frosts have taken the others. It turns up through summer and fall in the southern and central parts of the province. If you find a red meadowhawk perched on a sunny boardwalk in November, check the legs. Pale legs make the call.",
  "blue-dasher": "Males are hard to mistake: a powdery pale-blue abdomen with a dark tip, a striped thorax and bright blue-green eyes. Females and immatures look different, with a dark abdomen marked by bold yellow dashes. It lives at weedy, well-vegetated ponds, marshes and slow water with plenty of emergent plants, and flies in summer in southern Ontario.\n\nOn hot days watch for the obelisk pose. The dragonfly tips its abdomen straight up toward the sun so that less of its body is exposed to the heat, a simple way to keep from overheating on an open perch. Like most skimmers it hunts from a perch, darting out after small flying insects and returning to the same twig or leaf. It is a common sight around suitable ponds and does not trouble people.",
  "common-whitetail": "The male is the source of the name. His abdomen is stout and chalky white, and a single broad dark band crosses each wing, a combination that reads at a distance. Females look nothing like that, being brown with a row of pale zigzag marks down the sides and three dark spots on each wing. Both haunt ponds, slow streams, ditches and muddy wetlands, often in sunny, disturbed places, and they fly from spring into summer across southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe white abdomen is not just decoration. A territorial male flashes it at rivals along the shoreline as a visual keep-out sign, holding a stretch of bank against other males. Look for them perched flat on the ground or on a dock, a habit that helps separate them from many other dragonflies.",
  "eastern-pondhawk": "Green is the giveaway. Females and young males are bright grass-green with black bands down the abdomen, and even after mature males turn powdery blue they keep a green face, which clinches the identification. It perches flat, often right on the ground, a dock or a lily pad, at ponds, marshes and slow streams in southern Ontario, flying through summer.\n\nThis is a bold ambush hunter. It takes large prey for its size and will catch and eat other dragonflies, including damselflies and sometimes other pondhawks. A green-faced dragonfly sitting low near still water, launching after anything that flies past, is almost certainly this one. It is common and widespread wherever there is warm, still water with open perches nearby.",
  "widow-skimmer": "The wings tell you what it is. Each one carries a broad dark brown-black patch at the base, and in mature males a chalky white band follows just beyond the dark, so the wing looks banded in flight. Females and young males show the brown bases without the white, and wear yellow side-stripes along the body. The dark patches gave the insect its name, likened to a mourning shawl draped over the wings.\n\nFind it at still and slow water - ponds, marshes, lake edges and ditches - wherever there are open, sunny perches, flying in summer across southern and central Ontario. It perches in the open and is easy to watch, which makes the wing pattern simple to check against similar skimmers.",
  "calico-pennant": "A small pennant with a tidy field mark. Down the top of the abdomen runs a row of pale heart-shaped spots, red in males and yellow in females, and the wings are patterned with amber and dark spots. That neat line of hearts makes it one of the easier small dragonflies to name on sight.\n\nLook at vegetated pond and lake edges, marshes and boggy wetlands with grassy perches, in summer across southern and central Ontario. Like other pennants it perches at the tips of grass and sedge stems and rides the breeze there. The combination of spotted wings and a back marked with a string of hearts is not shared by much else at the water's edge.",
  "halloween-pennant": "Named for its colours, orange and dark brown. The wings are broadly washed with orange-amber and crossed by dark bands, which gives a stained-glass look and a slow, fluttering flight closer to a butterfly than to most dragonflies. It perches at the very tips of grass and sedge stems and sways there in the wind like a small flag, which is where the name pennant comes from.\n\nIts haunts are marshes, ponds and lake margins with tall grasses and open meadow nearby, and it flies in summer across southern and central Ontario. The floppy flight and the boldly patterned wings make it easy to pick out over a grassy shoreline, even at a distance. On breezy days a row of them can dot the tops of the tallest stems.",
  "bicoloured-striped-sweat-bee": "A native bee with a metallic green head and thorax that catches the light. Females carry a boldly black-and-white striped abdomen; males swap that for yellow-and-black bands. It nests communally, with many females sharing a single burrow entrance in bare or thinly vegetated ground, and forages widely at gardens, meadows, prairies, roadsides and city parks. It does well in cities, and in 2018 it was named the official bee of Toronto in recognition of its work as an urban pollinator.\n\nSweat bees earn the name honestly. This one may land on skin to drink perspiration on a hot day, but it is not aggressive and will give only a mild sting if it is pressed or trapped against the body. Brush it off gently rather than swatting and it will move on. Look for it from spring through fall across southern and central Ontario, cities included.",
  "two-spotted-bumble-bee": "Among the first bumble bees out each year, this species has queens on the wing in early spring, working spring beauty and Dutchman's breeches while most other bees are still underground. Look for a yellow thorax and a yellow first abdominal segment, then a black second segment marked with a two-lobed yellow spot that reads like a rough 'W'. That mark, along with the early flight, helps separate it from the many other yellow-and-black bumble bees. Queens run close to two centimetres; workers are smaller.\n\nYou will find them in woodland, along forest edges, and in gardens and meadows across southern and central Ontario. Nests go underground or into cavities such as old logs and abandoned rodent burrows. Away from the nest it is docile and rarely stings. Like all bumble bees, a female can sting more than once when she is defending the colony, so leave nests alone.",
  "eastern-cicada-killer": "Few Ontario insects look as intimidating as this wasp, which can reach four centimetres and patrols low over bare ground on amber-tinted wings. Its rusty-red thorax, black abdomen banded with pale yellow, and large size make it hard to confuse with anything else. It turns up in summer on sunny, well-drained sandy sites: garden beds, lawns, banks, trail edges and ballfields, usually near trees where annual cicadas are singing.\n\nThe hunting is done by the female. She stings and paralyzes a cicada, then hauls the heavy prey back to a burrow she has dug, where it becomes living food for a single larva. Despite the reputation, this is a solitary wasp with little interest in people. The males that buzz and chase near nest sites are staking out territory and have no stinger at all. Females can sting but do so only if grabbed or stepped on. It is most common in the sandy soils of the Carolinian zone.",
  "great-golden-digger-wasp": "A slim, sharply marked wasp of open sunny ground, recognized by the golden hairs covering its black head and thorax, its orange-red legs, and an abdomen that is orange at the base and black toward the tip. It works flowers for nectar through late summer and into fall, so you often meet it in gardens, prairies, roadsides and sandy banks in southern Ontario.\n\nNesting is a solitary affair. The female digs her own burrow in light soil and stocks each cell with katydids or grasshoppers she has paralyzed with her sting. Her routine is rigidly instinctive. She sets the prey down, inspects the burrow, then returns to drag it in, and if the prey is moved while she checks, she begins the inspection again. The larvae feed on this stored provision. This is a non-aggressive hunter with no interest in defending against people. It can sting, but almost never does unless it is roughly handled.",
  "yellow-banded-bumble-bee": "This is a fuzzy, mostly black bumble bee with a patch of yellow at the front of the thorax and yellow bands across the second and third abdominal segments. The head is black. It once ranged widely across Ontario and was among the most common bumble bees here, but it declined sharply in the 1990s and is now listed as Special Concern.\n\nYou may still find it from the southern farmland north into the boreal, in meadows, forest edges, wetlands and clearings, though it turns up far less often than it once did. Colonies nest underground, frequently in abandoned rodent burrows, and workers fly from spring through fall. Because it is a protected species in decline, the right response to a sighting is to observe and photograph it rather than collect it, and to submit records where local monitoring projects ask for them. It is generally docile, though like any bumble bee a female can sting repeatedly if she is handled.",
  "american-carrion-beetle": "The pale yellow shield behind the head, marked with a single dark central spot, gives this flattened black beetle away at a glance. It makes a living at carcasses, and that is usually where you find it, crawling over a dead animal or flying in to one, though it also visits dung and other decaying matter. It works through the warm months in forests, fields and along roadsides across Ontario.\n\nBoth the adults and the pale larvae feed on carrion, and they also eat the fly maggots competing for the same resource, which makes the beetle part predator as well as scavenger. Adults commonly carry tiny mites as hitchhikers and ferry them from one carcass to the next. None of this harms people. A handled beetle may release a foul smell in defence, so it is better watched than picked up. Like other carrion beetles, it helps recycle nutrients back into the soil.",
  "goldenrod-soldier-beetle": "On goldenrod in late summer you often see clusters of these soft-bodied beetles feeding and mating in the open. They are elongate and orange-yellow, with a black spot on each wing case and another on the shield behind the head. The wing covers are leathery and flexible rather than hard, which is where the alternate name Pennsylvania leatherwing comes from. Meadows, gardens and roadsides across southern and central Ontario all suit them, and they concentrate on late-blooming flowers through summer and fall.\n\nBoth stages earn their keep. The adults feed on pollen and nectar and move pollen between flowers as they go, so they act as pollinators. The larvae live in soil and leaf litter and hunt the eggs and larvae of other insects, including some garden pests. There is nothing to be wary of here. The beetle does not bite or sting, and a good stand of goldenrod is the easiest place to watch it.",
  "six-spotted-tiger-beetle": "Catch a flash of metallic green on a sunlit woodland path and it is likely this beetle. The colour is iridescent green, sometimes closer to blue-green, set off by long legs, large bulging eyes and curved sickle-shaped jaws. The name comes from the small white spots along the edges of the wing cases, though the count varies and some individuals have none. It favours open sunny ground: footpaths, trail edges and clearings in deciduous and mixed woodland, where it runs and flies ahead of you in short bursts.\n\nIt is a fast, visual hunter of small insects, and for its size it is one of the fastest runners known. It sprints so fast that its eyes cannot keep up, so it dashes, stops to relocate the prey, then dashes again. Adults are out in spring and summer through southern and central Ontario, and it is a common find. It is harmless to people, though the jaws can give a slight pinch if you hold one.",
  "whirligig-beetle": "Those small dark beetles spinning in tight circles on the surface of a pond are whirligigs. They are shiny blue-black, oval, and usually gather in loose groups that scatter and gyrate when disturbed. The most interesting feature is hard to see without a close look. Each compound eye is divided into an upper and a lower half, so the beetle watches the air above the waterline and the water below it at the same time. That double view helps it spot both prey and predators while it patrols the surface film.\n\nLook for them on ponds, lakes and slow stretches of stream across Ontario through the warmer months. They feed on small insects that fall onto the water and on other prey they find at the surface. Handled, a whirligig gives off a fruity, apple-like fluid that some people can smell on their fingers. It is a defence, and it is harmless. These are common and easy to watch from a dock or shoreline on a calm day.",
  "eastern-eyed-click-beetle": "Two large black eyespots, each ringed in white, sit on the shield behind this beetle's head, and they are the quickest way to know it. The eyespots are false, a bluff aimed at predators, since the real eyes are much smaller. The body is long and black, finely speckled with white and grey, and at three to four centimetres this is one of the larger beetles you will meet in southern Ontario's deciduous forests. Adults appear in spring and summer on and around decaying logs and dead wood, which is where the larvae develop.\n\nThe trick that gives the family its name is worth seeing. Placed on its back, the beetle arches, then snaps a spine into a groove on its underside, flinging itself into the air with a sharp audible click. If the first jump does not land it upright, it tries again. It does not bite or sting, and it is harmless to handle.",
  "reddish-brown-stag-beetle": "Shiny mahogany brown, the males armed with long antler-like jaws, this is a large beetle of southern Ontario woodlands. The 'antlers' are enlarged mandibles that males use against rivals, not weapons meant for people. Size varies, and the biggest males owe much of their length to those jaws. A useful field mark is the legs: a distinct yellowish-orange patch on the thigh segments sets this species apart from the darker giant stag beetle.\n\nAdults fly on summer nights and come to lights, so a porch or a lit window near woodland is a common place to find one. The life cycle runs mostly out of sight. Females lay in or near rotting logs, stumps and buried decaying wood, and the grub-like larvae feed inside that wood for one to two years before they pupate. A handled male can give a firm pinch with its jaws, which is startling but does no real harm. It does not sting.",
  "american-dog-tick": "Grouped here with the insects, this is actually an arachnid, with eight legs and no wings. It is larger and more rounded than the blacklegged (deer) tick, brown with silvery-white mottled markings on the shield behind the head. It waits on grasses and low vegetation along trail edges, in old fields and scrubby ground, often on paths used by people and dogs, and is active in spring and summer through southern and eastern Ontario.\n\nIt does not transmit Lyme disease, which the blacklegged tick spreads. It can, rarely, carry the agents of Rocky Mountain spotted fever and tularemia, both uncommon in Ontario, and it does bite people and pets. Sensible precautions matter. Check yourself, children and dogs after time in tick habitat, and remove any attached tick promptly by gripping it close to the skin with fine-tipped tweezers and pulling straight out. Save the tick if you can, and watch for fever or a rash in the days after a bite. See a doctor if either appears.",
  "boxelder-bug": "Come autumn, these flattened dark bugs gather in numbers on warm, sun-facing walls, fences and foundations, and that is when most people notice them. Adults are brownish-black with three thin red-orange lines on the thorax and red veins outlining the folded wings. The nymphs are bright red and often mixed in with the adults. Through the growing season they live on and around boxelder, maple and ash, feeding on seeds and foliage without doing meaningful harm to the trees.\n\nTheir habit of clustering on sunny surfaces in fall, and of slipping into homes through gaps to overwinter, is what earns them nuisance status. Indoors they do no damage. They do not bite or breed inside, and they do not feed on the house or its contents. The one drawback is that a crushed bug can leave a stain, so the tidiest way to deal with strays indoors is to sweep or vacuum them up rather than squash them. They are common in southern Ontario.",
  "carolina-grasshopper": "On bare gravel or a sandy trail edge, this large grasshopper is nearly invisible until you almost step on it. It flushes with a low, fluttering flight and flashes broad black hindwings edged in creamy yellow. Then it drops, snaps the wings shut, and disappears against the ground again. That flight fools plenty of people into calling out a mourning cloak butterfly. The grey-brown body, around 3 to 5 cm, suits open, dry, disturbed ground: gravel lots, roadsides, railway cuttings, weedy fields and sandy lakeshores.\n\nYou meet it through summer and into fall across southern and central Ontario, active by day. The dark hindwings stay hidden at rest and appear only in flight, where the sudden flash may make a predator hesitate. Males sometimes hover in place, wings working, as a courtship display. It is native and common. Give whatever flushes a second look before you name it.",
  "fall-field-cricket": "A robust, shiny black cricket with long antennae and heavy jumping legs, common in fields, lawns, gardens and roadsides, and under logs and stones. Females carry a long, needle-like ovipositor at the rear, used to place eggs in soil. It is not a stinger. Males sing a mellow, repeated chirp by rubbing their wings together, and females are silent.\n\nThis is a sound of autumn in Ontario. The species overwinters as eggs, matures in late summer, and chirps on through cool fall nights until frost. Listen for it in the south and centre of the province, mostly after dark. As nights turn cold it often works its way indoors, and a single male behind the baseboards can chirp for hours. Native and harmless, it does no damage beyond the noise. If you want it back outside, it is easy enough to catch under a cup and carry to the door.",
  "large-milkweed-bug": "Wherever milkweed sets seed pods, you may find these elongate true bugs clustered on the pods in orange and black. The key mark is a broad black band straight across the middle of the back, separating two orange zones. That tells it from the small milkweed bug, which shows a black-bordered red X instead. Size runs about 10 to 18 mm.\n\nLook in milkweed patches in meadows, old fields, ditches and gardens through summer and fall, in southern Ontario. The bugs feed by piercing developing seeds and drinking the contents, and they often gather in groups of mixed ages. The bright colouring is a warning. Feeding on milkweed loads them with cardenolides, the same class of toxins that protects monarchs, and birds learn to leave the orange-and-black pattern alone. It is native and does no real harm to gardens beyond the milkweed it feeds on. There is no reason to handle it.",
  "snowy-tree-cricket": "More often heard than seen, this is a delicate, pale whitish-green cricket with long antennae and flat, translucent wings held over the back. It sits in shrubs, hedgerows, tall weeds and the edges of gardens and deciduous woods, where its colour and stillness hide it well. The call is a soft, steady, rhythmic chirping, cleaner and more even than the harsher trill of the ground crickets.\n\nIts nickname is the thermometer cricket. Chirp rate rises and falls with temperature, and there is a rough rule: count the chirps in 13 seconds and add 40 for the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit. That relationship is known as Dolbear's law, and on a warm evening it works well enough to surprise people. You hear it at night through summer and fall across southern Ontario. Native and harmless. The easiest way to find one is to walk slowly toward the sound and watch a single stem for the faint motion of the wings.",
  "giant-water-bug": "This is one of the largest true bugs in Ontario, a flat brown oval 4 to 6 cm long with grasping front legs folded like a mantis and two short breathing appendages at the tail. People often take it for a beetle. It lives in weedy ponds, marshes, ditches and quiet lake margins, and on summer nights the adults fly to lights, which is where most people meet one.\n\nIt hunts by ambush, seizing tadpoles, insects and even small fish with those front legs and injecting digestive saliva before it feeds. Two nicknames sum it up: toe-biter, for the sharp defensive jab it delivers when grabbed, and electric light bug, for the flights to lamps. The bite hurts. It is not venomous and not dangerous, but do not pick one up bare-handed. If you find one stranded under a porch light, nudge it into a container rather than lifting it with your fingers. Native, and active from spring through fall province-wide.",
  "northern-walkingstick": "Ontario's longest insect is also one of its hardest to spot. Extremely slender and wingless, with thread-thin legs and antennae, it looks like a length of twig, brown or greenish, and moves slowly, swaying a little as if a branch were stirring in the breeze. Females reach roughly 7.5 to 9.5 cm, males a bit shorter. It lives in deciduous and mixed woodland and along the edges, on the foliage of oak, hazel, cherry and other hardwoods.\n\nIt feeds on leaves at night and spends the day motionless, relying entirely on shape and stillness. Young nymphs can regrow a lost leg at the next moult, though the replacement is often smaller. Look for it in southern Ontario, in Carolinian and deciduous forest, through summer and fall. Native and harmless. You are most likely to notice one when it moves, or when it turns up on a screen door or a pale wall where the twig disguise no longer works.",
  "black-swallowtail": "Black wings marked with two rows of yellow spots and a wash of blue on the hindwing set this swallowtail apart. Males carry more yellow, females more blue. Wingspan runs about 7 to 9 cm. It flies over open fields, gardens and roadsides across southern and central Ontario, from spring into fall.\n\nGardeners meet the caterpillar as often as the adult. It feeds on plants in the carrot family, including dill, parsley and Queen Anne's lace, and turns up green with black bands and yellow dots on garden herbs. When threatened, the caterpillar everts a forked orange organ behind its head, called an osmeterium, which gives off a foul smell that deters some predators. The damage to a few parsley plants is minor, and leaving the caterpillars in place gives you the butterfly later in the season. Native and common. Watch flowering borders and open fields on warm days for the adults.",
  "cabbage-white": "The small white butterfly you see over almost any open ground in Ontario is most likely this one. It shows black wingtips and, on the forewing, black spots: two on females, one on males. Wingspan is about 4 to 6 cm. It turns up in gardens, farm fields, vacant lots and any disturbed patch, from spring through fall, province-wide.\n\nIt is not native. Introduced from Europe in the 1860s, it spread quickly and is now among the most abundant butterflies in the province. The green caterpillar, the cabbage worm, feeds on cabbage, broccoli and other members of the mustard family, and it is a familiar pest in vegetable gardens. Rows of holed leaves and dark droppings usually mean it is present. For a butterfly this common, it repays a close look. The plain white adult is easy to overlook, but it is often the first and last butterfly on the wing in a given year.",
  "eastern-tiger-swallowtail": "Few Ontario butterflies are as large or as easy to name. The wings are yellow with four black tiger stripes across each forewing and a black border, and the hindwings carry short tails. Wingspan can reach 8 to 14 cm. In the south of the province some females occur in an all-black form that mimics the toxic pipevine swallowtail. It patrols deciduous woodland edges, riverbanks, gardens and roadsides in spring and summer.\n\nMales often gather at damp ground, puddles and streamsides to drink, taking up mineral salts they cannot get from flowers. Several may cluster together at a wet spot, a behaviour called puddling, and a group of yellow swallowtails on a muddy track is a common sight on a warm day. Native. Look for it wherever there is moist ground near woods, and check any large all-black swallowtail in the south, since it may be this species rather than a black swallowtail.",
  "monarch": "Few insects are as well known as the monarch, and few make a journey as improbable. The orange-and-black butterflies that drift through Ontario gardens in late summer are the last of several generations born that year, and they will not breed here. Instead they turn south and fly, some of them more than four thousand kilometres, to a handful of mountain forests in central Mexico, where the whole eastern population gathers on the same fir trees their great-grandparents left the previous spring.\n\nTheir caterpillars eat only milkweed, storing the plant's bitter compounds so that birds learn to leave the adults alone. That single dependence is also the monarch's weakness. As milkweed has vanished from farm fields and roadsides, monarch numbers have fallen, and the species is now assessed as at risk. Planting native milkweed, and leaving it standing, is the simplest thing anyone can do to help.",
  "mourning-cloak": "Often the first butterfly of the Ontario year, this one flies on warm days in early spring while snow still lies in the shade. The wings are deep maroon-brown edged with a ragged cream-yellow border and a row of blue spots inside it. Folded shut, the underside looks like dead bark, which makes a resting adult very hard to see. Wingspan is about 6 to 10 cm.\n\nThe reason it appears so early is that the adults overwinter, tucked under loose bark or in woodpiles, and emerge before most flowers open. It favours woodlands, forest edges and clearings, and feeds at sap flows and rotting fruit more than at blossoms. You can find it province-wide from spring through fall. Native and long-lived by butterfly standards. On a mild afternoon in March or April, a dark butterfly gliding along a woodland trail before anything is green is almost certainly this one.",
  "painted-lady": "Orange, black and white above, with a marbled underside carrying four small eyespots, this is one of the most widely distributed butterflies anywhere. In Ontario it is a visitor rather than a resident. It cannot survive the winter here and recolonizes each year from the south, so numbers swing from scarce to abundant. In big flight years it arrives in irruptions, and gardens and fields fill with them.\n\nLook for it in open, flowery places: gardens, fields and roadsides, through summer and fall, province-wide. It nectars readily and is easy to approach at flowers. Because the population depends on migration and southern breeding conditions, a good year is impossible to predict far ahead, and a season with almost none may be followed by one with thousands. Native to the continent, harmless, and worth watching for from midsummer on, when the first arrivals move north.",
  "red-admiral": "Black wings crossed by bright orange-red bands, with white spots near the forewing tips, mark this fast flier. Wingspan is about 4.5 to 6 cm. It often lands on tree trunks, fenceposts and even on people, wings flat, and it turns up at woodland edges, gardens, parks and moist clearings. The caterpillars feed on nettles.\n\nLike the painted lady, it migrates north into Ontario each spring, and you can see it province-wide from spring through fall. Males are territorial. They pick a sunlit spot in the late afternoon, perch, and dart out at passing butterflies, birds and anything else that crosses the patch before returning to the same perch. That habit makes them easy to watch once you find an occupied corner. Native and harmless. A basking red admiral will often let you come quite close before it flicks away.",
  "spongy-moth": "This is the caterpillar most likely to defoliate an oak woodlot in southern and central Ontario. Once called the gypsy moth, it was brought over from Europe and now runs in boom-and-bust cycles. In an outbreak spring the larvae can strip whole oak stands bare, and the trees then have to releaf at a cost to their reserves. You can name the caterpillar by the two rows of raised spots down its back, five pairs of blue followed by six pairs of red. The adults divide the work by sex. Females are buff-coloured and flightless, so they stay near where they hatched and lay a tan, spongy egg mass. Brown males fly a searching zigzag by day.\n\nThe hairs on the caterpillars cause an itchy rash in some people, so handle them carefully or not at all. The larger problem is spread. Egg masses ride on firewood, trailers, vehicles and camping gear. Scrape them off and do not carry them to new ground.",
  "viceroy": "For years the textbook example held that this orange butterfly was a harmless copy of the poisonous monarch. That story turned out to be incomplete. The viceroy is distasteful in its own right, so the two species share the cost of teaching predators to leave orange-and-black insects alone. Biologists call that arrangement Mullerian mimicry, a mutual warning rather than a bluff.\n\nTell it from a monarch by size and by one field mark. This one is noticeably smaller, and a thin black line arcs across the veins of the hindwing. The flight helps too, a few quick shallow flaps followed by a flat glide. Look for it near water, along wet meadows, marsh edges, lakeshores and ditches where willows and poplars grow, since those trees feed the caterpillars. Adults are on the wing from spring through fall across southern and central Ontario.",
  "cecropia-moth": "Nothing else in Ontario matches the size of this silkmoth. Reddish-brown wings span close to the width of a hand, marked with crescent bands of white and red. You are most likely to find one at rest by day on a wall or trunk near woodland edges, orchards and yards with maples and cherries. At night the adults come to lights.\n\nThe adult stage is short and it does not feed. It emerges without working mouthparts and lives on fat stored during the caterpillar months. It has a week or so to find a mate. The males do the searching. Their broad feathery antennae pick up a female's pheromone from more than a kilometre off, and they track the scent upwind to her. Once she has laid her eggs the next generation waits out the winter in a tough cocoon.",
  "giant-swallowtail": "Canada's largest butterfly is a Carolinian specialty that has been pushing north as the climate warms. The wings are dark above and crossed by a broad diagonal band of yellow spots, with a mostly yellow underside. In flight the size alone tends to give it away. Look along woodland edges and in gardens in southern Ontario, especially where its host plants grow, prickly-ash and hop-tree, both members of the citrus family.\n\nThe caterpillar earns a second look. It is coloured and shaped like a fresh bird dropping, brown and glossy with a saddle of white, which lets it feed out in the open without drawing a bird's eye. If alarmed it can also evert a forked orange gland that smells sharp. Adults fly in summer.",
  "luna-moth": "Few insects here are as easy to name as this one. A pale lime-green silkmoth with long twisted tails on the hindwings and a small eyespot on each wing does not resemble anything else. It flies at night and turns up at porch lights, then rests by day among the deciduous trees its caterpillars feed on, birch, walnut and hickory among them.\n\nLike its giant silkmoth relatives, the adult carries no working mouthparts and never eats. It lives about a week on stored reserves, and breeding is the whole point of that week. The trailing tails do more than decorate. As the moth flies they spin and flutter, throwing back a confusing echo that leads hunting bats to strike the tail instead of the body. A moth can fly on with a torn tail. It cannot fly with a bite taken out of its middle.",
  "common-green-darner": "One of the first large dragonflies out in spring, and one of the last seen in fall, because this species migrates. Generations move north over the province in spring and a later generation heads south in autumn, a pattern closer to birds than to most insects. You can meet it well away from water, hawking for small insects over fields and clearings, as well as at ponds, marshes and slow streams.\n\nSize and colour make it easy. The thorax is bright green, the abdomen blue on males and purplish on females, and a dark bull's-eye mark sits on top of the forehead. It turns up province-wide from spring through fall. Like all dragonflies it is harmless to people, and both the adults and the aquatic young eat large numbers of biting insects.",
  "ebony-jewelwing": "Along shaded forest creeks in summer you may notice what looks like a small black butterfly drifting low over the water. It is a broad-winged damselfly. The wings are entirely black, and the body glints metallic emerald-green on males, browner green on females, which also carry a small white spot near each wingtip. The flight is weak and fluttery, unlike the darting of most damselflies, and it often perches with its wings folded up over the back.\n\nIt keeps to slow, shaded water with plenty of overhanging plants, so a walk along a wooded stream is the place to look. Southern and central Ontario, through the summer. The larvae develop down in the streambed and the adults stay close to the same stretches of bank.",
  "twelve-spotted-skimmer": "Count the wing spots and the name explains itself. Each of the four wings carries three dark brown patches, twelve in all. On a mature male, white patches fill the gaps between them, so the wings read as a bold brown-and-white checkerboard. Females and young males show the brown spots without the white.\n\nThis is a dragonfly of open, sunny wetlands, marshes, ponds and slow water with bare perches to sit on. Males hold territory. One will patrol a length of shoreline from a favourite perch, coming back to it between flights and driving off any rival that crosses the line. Watch a good perch for a few minutes in summer and the same individual often returns to it. Found across southern and central Ontario.",
  "dragonhunter": "The largest clubtail dragonfly in the region hunts along clean rivers and forest-lined streams. It is black and yellow and heavily built, and in flight it often carries its long abdomen curled downward, which gives it an odd hunched look at a distance. Clear, well-oxygenated water is where the young develop, so finding one is a fair sign of a healthy stream.\n\nThe name is earned. This is a predator of other large insects, taking butterflies, big beetles and even other dragonflies, darners included. It will grab prey close to its own size and carry it off to a perch to eat. For all that it is no threat to people. Summer is the season to look, along shorelines and sunny openings in southern and central Ontario.",
  "bald-faced-hornet": "Despite the name this is a large yellowjacket rather than a true hornet. It is stout and black with an ivory-white face and white markings toward the tail. The clearest sign is the nest, a grey papery ball the shape of a football, hung in a tree or shrub or under an eave and often unnoticed until it is large. By late summer a single nest can hold several hundred workers.\n\nGive it room. The colony defends its nest hard, and a disturbed worker can sting again and again. The venom carries an alarm scent that draws nestmates to the same target, so one sting near the nest can quickly become many. Stings are painful, and for people with insect-sting allergies they can be dangerous. Away from the nest the wasps pay you little attention and do useful work catching flies and caterpillars. If a nest sits where people pass close by, leave removal for the cool of evening or to someone equipped for it.",
  "common-eastern-bumble-bee": "The bumble bee you are most likely to see in the province, in gardens, meadows, farmland and right into city yards. It is robust and densely furred, black with a yellow thorax and a single band of yellow across the front of the abdomen. Nests go underground or in cavities, started in spring by a lone overwintered queen who raises the first workers herself.\n\nIts trick at flowers is worth knowing. On plants like tomatoes and blueberries it grips the bloom and vibrates its flight muscles, shaking loose pollen that honey bees cannot reach. That buzz pollination makes it valuable in gardens and on farms. It can sting, and unlike a honey bee it can do so more than once, but it is even-tempered and stings only to defend the nest or when handled. Active from spring through fall.",
  "eastern-carpenter-bee": "At a glance this looks like a large bumble bee, until you notice the abdomen. It is shiny and bare and black, where a bumble bee is fuzzy all over. Around gardens, farmland and forest edges the females excavate nest tunnels in bare, weathered wood, with fence rails, deck boards and eaves among the favourites. The entrance is a clean hole almost perfectly round, bored by chewing the wood away and discarding it rather than eating it.\n\nMales spend the spring hovering and darting at anything that passes the nest wood, people included, which looks alarming. They have no stinger and cannot hurt you. The females can sting but are docile and rarely do unless they are handled. Damage from a single bee is minor, though repeated use of the same wood over years can add up. Look for them in spring and summer in southern Ontario.",
  "eastern-yellowjacket": "The wasp that shows up uninvited at a late-summer barbecue is usually this one. It is short and stocky, with a shiny black-and-yellow banded abdomen that has none of the fuzz you see on a bee. Colonies start small in spring and build through the summer, nesting underground or inside wall voids where the entrance is easy to miss. By fall the workers turn to scavenging, crowding around meat and sweet drinks. That is when most stings happen.\n\nUnlike a honey bee, a yellowjacket can sting more than once, and it defends a hidden nest fiercely. Step on a ground nest and the whole colony may pour out. For people sensitive to the venom a sting can trigger anaphylaxis, so treat sudden swelling or breathing trouble as an emergency. You meet them across southern and central Ontario, in fields, lawns and parks, and anywhere food is left sitting out.",
  "honey-bee": "Brought from Europe for honey and crop pollination, this bee is now part of the Ontario landscape, living in managed hives and occasionally in feral colonies in hollow trees. A worker is slim and golden-brown, with a fuzzy thorax and dark bands across the abdomen. On a good foraging day you can watch one pack pollen into bright baskets on its hind legs before heading home. A single colony may hold tens of thousands of workers, all daughters of one queen. You see them from spring through fall wherever flowers bloom, across gardens, orchards, meadows and farmland.\n\nThey are not aggressive away from the hive, but they will sting when handled or when the colony is threatened. The barbed stinger tears loose and stays in the skin, so scrape it out rather than pinching, which squeezes in more venom. Most stings are only painful, but the venom can cause a severe allergic reaction in sensitive people. If breathing or swelling follows a sting, get help right away.",
  "paper-wasp": "Look under an eave or a shed rafter and you may find an open, umbrella-shaped comb of grey paper with a wasp standing guard over it. The insect is slender and brownish with thin yellow markings, longer-legged than a yellowjacket, and its hind legs dangle as it flies. Northern paper wasps chew wood fibre into pulp to build with, which gives the nest its papery look.\n\nThese wasps have an unusual talent. Individuals recognize one another by the fine markings on their faces, and that recognition helps hold the pecking order in a shared nest. They are slower to attack than yellowjackets, but a nest beside a doorway is a problem, because they will sting if you crowd it. The sting is painful and can cause an allergic reaction in some people. You find them around gardens and buildings through the warmer months in southern and central Ontario.",
  "rusty-patched-bumble-bee": "This is a bee you are very unlikely to see, and that is the point. Workers and males carry a rusty-brown patch centred on an otherwise yellow second segment of the abdomen, the mark that gives the species its name. It once lived in gardens, savannahs and open woodlands that offered flowers across the whole season. Populations have fallen by an estimated 90 percent, and it is now endangered and possibly gone from Ontario altogether.\n\nIt is legally protected here. Do not try to capture or disturb one, and note that it can sting if handled. If you think you have found one, the useful thing is to photograph it and report the sighting to a provincial or conservation authority rather than to collect it. Because several bumble bees look broadly similar, a clear photo of the abdomen matters for confirmation. Any credible record from Ontario would be significant, so it is worth reporting even when you are not certain.",
  "emerald-ash-borer": "Small and narrow, this beetle would be easy to overlook if it were not so destructive. The wing cases are a bright metallic emerald green, but the clearest signs are usually the damage rather than the insect: neat D-shaped exit holes in the bark, and S-shaped tunnels winding underneath it where the larvae fed. An invasive species from Asia, it was first found in Ontario near Windsor in 2002 and has since killed tens of millions of ash trees across the province.\n\nThe larvae do the killing. They feed in the thin layer just under the bark that carries water and nutrients, and a girdled tree dies within a few years. Adults are out in summer. There is no easy fix once a woodlot is infested, but people can slow the spread by not moving firewood, which carries larvae to new areas. If you heat with wood, buy or cut it locally and burn it where you got it.",
  "common-eastern-firefly": "On warm summer nights in southern Ontario, the flashes drifting low over a damp meadow are these beetles at work. Fireflies are not flies at all but soft-bodied beetles, dark, with a pink-and-black shield over the head and a pale edge along each wing case. By day they are unremarkable and easy to miss. The glow is the reliable way to tell them apart.\n\nThe big dipper gets its name from the male's flash, a rising J-shaped streak of light he repeats as he patrols. A female watching from the grass answers with her own flash, and each species uses its own timing and pattern so signals reach the right partner. Look for them at the edges of marshes, forests and lawns near water, where the larvae live in damp ground. They do not bite or sting. A dark night with little artificial light gives the best show, since bright yards wash the flashes out.",
  "japanese-beetle": "Few garden pests are as easy to name. The body is a shiny metallic green, the wing cases a coppery bronze, and along each side of the abdomen sits a row of small white hair tufts that separate it from lookalikes. It is invasive and now well established across southern Ontario. Adults feed in summer, often in groups, chewing the soft tissue between leaf veins until leaves are skeletonized. Roses, grapes, lindens and hundreds of other plants are all on the menu.\n\nThe larvae cause a second kind of damage. They are white grubs that live in the soil and feed on grass roots, which can leave brown patches in a lawn. Because the grubs travel in soil and root balls, moving turf, potting soil or infested plants is a reliable way to carry the beetle to new ground, so it is best avoided. Handpicking adults into soapy water works on a small scale. They do not bite or sting.",
  "june-beetle": "Anyone who has sat on a porch on a warm evening in early summer knows the sound: a heavy beetle blundering into the light, then into the wall. June beetles, also called June bugs, are glossy reddish-brown and fly clumsily, with a loud buzz. Several similar species share the name and the habit. They emerge in late spring and are strongly drawn to porch lights and lit windows.\n\nThe adults are mostly a nuisance rather than a real problem. Their larvae are white grubs that live in the soil of lawns, pastures and woodland edges, feeding on roots, and in numbers they can thin out turf. You find the beetles province-wide. They do not bite or sting, and the buzzing and bumbling are the worst of it. Turning off outdoor lights, or switching to a warmer bulb, keeps them from gathering at the door on the nights they are flying.",
  "asian-lady-beetle": "This is the ladybug most likely to turn up inside a house. It is highly variable, ranging from pale orange to deep red with anywhere from none to nineteen spots, so colour and spot count are unreliable on their own. The mark to check is on the whitish plate behind the head, where a black pattern often reads as an 'M' or a 'W'. It was introduced for pest control and now outcompetes several native ladybugs.\n\nIn autumn the beetles gather on warm, south-facing walls and work their way indoors to overwinter, sometimes in large numbers. Handled or disturbed, one may deliver a small nip and will ooze a foul-smelling yellow reflex fluid that can stain. Big indoor aggregations can also trigger allergies in some people. They are not dangerous, but they are unwelcome guests. Sealing gaps around windows and vents before fall does more good than swatting them once they are in.",
  "seven-spotted-lady-beetle": "Count the spots and this one names itself: a domed, red-orange beetle with exactly seven black marks and a pair of small white patches beside the head. It was brought over from Europe as a control for aphids and is now found province-wide in gardens, crops, meadows and along roadsides. Wherever aphids gather, it tends to follow.\n\nBoth the adult and the larva are hungry aphid predators, which makes the species useful in a vegetable patch even though it is not native. The larva looks nothing like the adult - it is elongated and dark with orange markings, more like a tiny alligator than a ladybug - and it is worth learning to recognize so you do not remove it by mistake. The beetles do not bite or sting. Their spread has come partly at the expense of some native ladybugs, a reminder that even a helpful introduced insect shifts the balance it lands in.",
  "dogbane-beetle": "Small, oval and hard to ignore in sunlight, this leaf beetle shifts through green, gold, copper and blue as it turns. The colour is not pigment. It comes from microscopic layers in the shell that bend light, so the shade you see depends on the angle you catch it from. Look for it in summer on dogbane and milkweed along meadows, ditches and field edges.\n\nIt feeds almost entirely on dogbane, a plant with toxic, milky sap that deters most animals. The beetle handles that chemistry and stays close to its host, which is why finding the plant is the surest way to find the beetle. It does no harm to gardens or crops and does not bite or sting. This is one to watch and photograph rather than worry about. You meet it across southern and central Ontario wherever its food plants grow.",
  "black-fly": "Spring and early summer in Ontario come with these tiny biting flies, worst in the north and anywhere near running water. A black fly is small and stout, humpbacked, with broad clear wings, and a fresh bite often bleeds and swells more than a mosquito's. The larvae need clean, fast, oxygen-rich water and anchor to rocks in streams, so a heavy black fly season actually signals healthy flowing water nearby.\n\nOnly the females bite, and they do it by day, often working around the head and ankles. The bites are painful and itchy, can raise welts, and cause allergic reactions in some people. Nothing stops them completely. Long sleeves, light colours, a hat and a head net help on bad days, and they ease off in the heat of high summer. Swarms drift well away from the water where they hatched, into forests, trails and yards, so you do not have to be at a stream to be bitten.",
  "green-lacewing": "Slender and pale green, with golden eyes and large clear wings folded roof-like over the back, this is one of the more delicate insects you will meet in an Ontario garden. Adults turn up in gardens, fields and along woodland edges from spring through fall, and they often come to porch lights after dark. In flight they look weak and fluttery, and they are easy to overlook against foliage.\n\nThe real work is done by the young. The larva is a spiny, flattened grub nicknamed the aphid lion, and it earns the name, hunting down aphids, mites and other soft-bodied pests and eating them in large numbers. That makes the lacewing a friend to anyone growing plants. It is native and harmless to handle, though the larva can give a faint nip if pressed. If you find small pale eggs set on tall thread-like stalks under a leaf, those are lacewing eggs.",
  "water-strider": "You have probably watched these without knowing the name. A slim dark bug that skates across still water on long splayed legs, the water strider lives on the surface film of ponds, slow streams, pools and quiet lake edges. On a bright day its feet dimple the surface and cast small round shadows on the bottom, which is often the easiest way to spot it.\n\nIt stays up on dense, water-repellent hairs that keep its legs from breaking through the surface tension. The middle legs row and the back legs steer. The short front legs grab prey. It finds food by feeling ripples, homing in on an insect struggling on the surface. Active from spring into fall across the province. It does not bite people. In fall the adults leave the water and overwinter on land under leaf litter and debris.",
  "deer-fly": "You meet this fly on warm, sunny days on open trails, at marsh and forest edges and along lakeshores, usually as it circles your head before it lands. It is broad and flat, with patterned banded wings and bright green or golden eyes.\n\nFemales bite. The mouthparts work like small scissors, cutting the skin to lap up blood, which is why the bite is sharp and hard to ignore rather than a simple prick. They are persistent and will follow a moving person for some distance. Bites can swell, and like any wound they can become infected or, in some people, set off an allergic reaction. Covering skin, light-coloured clothing and repellent all help, and keeping moving on breezy days keeps them off you. This is a native summer insect and part of the season near water in Ontario. Only a nuisance, but a real one.",
  "dog-day-cicada": "The sound comes first. A loud, whining buzz high in the canopy is the classic voice of the hot dog days of late summer in Ontario, and it belongs to a stout insect patterned in green, black and white with clear, green-veined wings held tent-like over the body. You will hear it far more often than you see it, since it sings from mature trees in forests, woodlots, parks and leafy neighbourhoods.\n\nMales make the sound with drum-like organs called tymbals on the abdomen, buckling them rapidly to produce the buzz. The nymphs live underground for years, feeding on sap from tree roots, then climb out, split their skins and emerge as adults. The cast nymph shells often stay clinging to bark and fence posts. Found through southern and central Ontario, active in summer and into fall. It does not bite or sting, and it is harmless to handle if one lands on you.",
  "northern-house-mosquito": "Slender and long-legged, with a piercing proboscis, this is the mosquito behind a good share of Ontario's summer bites. It rests with the body angled and gives itself away with a thin whine in flight. Look for it near standing water of any kind - the larvae develop in ditches, containers, rain barrels and stagnant pools, so a neglected bucket becomes a nursery.\n\nOnly females bite; they need a blood meal to develop their eggs. This species matters beyond the itch because it is the main carrier of West Nile virus in the province, and rarely it can be involved in eastern equine encephalitis. Most bites lead to nothing worse than a welt, but the disease risk is real, mostly late in summer. It is most active around dawn and dusk. Use repellent, cover up at those hours, and drain or empty standing water around the home to cut the numbers close to you.",
  "blacklegged-tick": "Despite sitting in a list of insects, this is an arachnid: eight legs, no wings. The body is reddish-orange with a dark shield, and the legs are dark, with no white markings. That last point matters, because the common dog tick carries white patterning and does not spread Lyme. Look for it in leaf litter, tall grass and the brushy edges of woods, mostly in southern and eastern Ontario, though its range is expanding.\n\nIt does not jump or fly. It waits at the tips of vegetation with its front legs out and latches onto a host that brushes past. Nymphs are the size of a poppy seed and cause most human Lyme cases, precisely because they are so easy to miss. This is the primary carrier of Lyme disease in Ontario, and it can pass on anaplasmosis and other pathogens too. Check yourself after being outdoors, remove any attached tick promptly with fine-tipped tweezers by pulling straight out, and see a doctor if a spreading bull's-eye rash or flu-like illness follows a bite.",
  "european-mantis": "Long-bodied and either green or brown, with a triangular head that swivels to follow movement and spined forelegs folded as if in prayer, this is the mantis most people run into in Ontario. A black-ringed spot on the inner face of each front leg is a good confirming mark. It lives in meadows, gardens, old fields and hedgerows where tall grasses and flowers give it cover.\n\nIt is not native. Introduced to North America around 1900, it has been part of southern Ontario's insect life since. It hunts by ambush, sitting still until something passes, then shooting out the forelegs to seize it. Prey includes other insects, and larger individuals will take fairly big catches. Active in summer and fall, the adults are most obvious late in the season. It can grip hard and the spined legs feel prickly, but it poses no real harm to people. Females lay eggs in a frothy case that hardens on stems, and it overwinters that way.",
  "bay-bolete": "A bolete with a bay-brown to chestnut cap, slightly sticky when wet, found in conifer and mixed forests across central and northern Ontario. Look underneath for pale yellow pores that bruise blue-green when pressed - the colour change is harmless and fades quickly. The brownish stem is streaky, with no raised net pattern, and the spore print is olive-brown. It fruits in summer and fall, usually on or near rotting conifer wood and mossy ground.\n\nBeginners are often steered toward this one because it has no deadly look-alike. That is a fair reason, but not a reason to relax the usual checks. Avoid any bolete with orange or red pores and a raised net on the stem, since some red-pored boletes cause severe stomach upset. The bitter bolete looks similar but has pink-tinged pores and a taste so bitter it ruins a meal. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "gem-studded-puffball": "Pear- to club-shaped and white, this puffball is covered in cone-shaped spines that rub off to leave a net-like pattern of scars. It grows on soil and needle duff in woods and along woodland edges, often in troops, through summer and fall. As it matures it browns and puffs a smoke of spores from a single pore at the top, set off by a squeeze or a raindrop.\n\nEdibility depends entirely on the inside. Slice every small round fungus from top to bottom before eating, and require pure, uniform white flesh throughout. Any outline of a cap, gills, or stem inside means it is the egg stage of a deadly Amanita, the destroying angel. A firm white-to-purple-black interior means a toxic earthball. Both mistakes are dangerous, so the cut is not optional. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "hedgehog-mushroom": "Turn one over and the underside gives it away: no gills, but soft, brittle white to pale spines that hang down and run partway onto the stem. The cap above is cream to pale orange, dry, and often lobed and irregular, 3-10 cm across. Flesh is white and bruises yellowish to orange. It grows on the ground in scattered troops in mossy coniferous and mixed forests, and it is common through central, northern, and cottage-country Ontario in summer and fall.\n\nNo deadly mushroom carries soft spines under a chanterelle-like cap, which makes this one of the safer wild edibles to learn. The rule still holds. Confirm the underside has soft spines, not gills, folds, or pores, before anything else. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "velvet-foot": "Cold weather brings this one out. Velvet foot fruits in clusters on dead hardwood - elm, willow, and aspen especially - in late fall and during winter thaws, when almost nothing else is up. The cap is sticky and orange to orange-brown, the gills whitish to pale, and the tough stem darkens from the base upward to a velvety blackish-brown. Spore print is white.\n\nThe pale enoki sold in stores is the same species, grown crowded in the dark so it stretches long, thin, and white. In the wild it needs care. The deadly Galerina marginata also clusters on wood in cool weather, but it has a rusty-brown spore print, brown gills, and a ring on the stem. Velvet foot has a white print, no ring, and that dark velvety stem base. A spore print is essential here. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "wood-ear": "Rubbery and gelatinous, shaped like an ear or a shallow cup, this fungus grows on dead wood, mostly conifer branches and logs, and appears in wet weather. It is reddish-brown to tan, smooth to veined on the inner surface and finely downy outside, with no gills and no pores. In dry spells it shrivels to a hard flake, then revives when rain returns, and it can repeat that through a season.\n\nThis is the North American relative of the wood ear used in hot-and-sour soup. Jelly fungi have few dangerous look-alikes, but confirm the rubbery ear shape with no gills, and do not mistake it for the unrelated brown cup fungi that also grow on wood. It turns up in moist forests across central and northern Ontario from spring through fall. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "bears-head-tooth": "A white branching mass hung with long icicle-like spines, this fungus looks like little else in the woods. The teeth hang in clusters from the branch tips and grow longer than a centimetre. There is no cap and no gills, and the whole thing ages yellowish. It grows on dead and dying hardwood, especially maple and beech, on logs and at trunk wounds, in summer and fall.\n\nAll Hericium in Ontario are edible, and the group has no toxic look-alike, which is unusual. The related lion's mane and coral tooth are also edible. The check is simple: confirm a white, spine-covered structure growing on wood. You will find it in hardwood forests of southern, central, and eastern Ontario. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "black-morel": "Spring is the only season for this one. The conical cap is a network of dark ridges over pale sunken pits, and the ridges darken further with age. The base of the cap attaches fully to the stem, and a lengthwise slice shows one continuous hollow chamber from tip to base. Look in hardwood and mixed forests, old orchards, and ground that burned the year before, often near ash, aspen, and conifers. In Ontario black morels tend to be among the earliest morels, arriving ahead of the yellow ones rather than after.\n\nThe danger in the same spring woods is the false morels, Gyromitra esculenta and its relatives. They have a reddish-brown brain-like cap that is folded rather than pitted, and they are stuffed with cottony chambers instead of hollow. Slice lengthwise every time to confirm a single hollow interior. Morels are toxic raw and must be cooked thoroughly. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "blewit": "Colour is the first thing you notice. Young caps, gills, and stem are lilac to violet-blue, fading to tan with age, and the stout stem stays pale purple. The flesh smells sweet, sometimes like frozen orange juice, and the spore print is pale pinkish-buff. Blewits come up in leaf litter, compost, and forest duff in late fall, often in arcs, and they tolerate frost, so they often fruit after the first light freezes when little else remains.\n\nThe purple colour has toxic imitators. Cortinarius webcaps look similar but leave a rusty-brown spore print and show a cobweb-like veil when young, and some are deadly kidney poisons. Purple Entoloma species have pink spore prints and are toxic. Take a spore print every time. A blewit prints pale pinkish-buff. Cook them thoroughly as well, since raw ones cause stomach upset. Found in woodlots, gardens, and compost across southern and central Ontario. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "cauliflower-mushroom": "You find it at the base of a conifer or oak, a rounded rosette of cream to pale-yellow ribbons packed tightly together, looking like a head of cauliflower or a nest of egg noodles. It can be large, 10-35 cm across and up to a couple of kilograms, and it attaches to a rooting base at the tree. Because it is a root and butt-rot fungus, it tends to return to the same tree base for several years running.\n\nThe noodle-like rosette at a tree base has no dangerous look-alike, which is rare among wild edibles. The real work is cleaning it, since soil and debris lodge deep between the folds and take patience to rinse out. Look in pine and oak stands of central and southern Ontario in summer and fall. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "half-free-morel": "The name describes the cap. Its pitted surface hangs like a short skirt and joins the stem only about halfway down, leaving the lower half of the cap free. The stem is long relative to the small cap, and a lengthwise slice shows the whole mushroom hollow. It grows in rich, moist hardwood forests and floodplains, often under ash and elm, and fruits early in the morel season, alongside the black morels and before the yellow morels peak.\n\nTwo things to rule out. The wrinkled thimble-cap, Verpa bohemica, has a thimble-shaped cap that hangs completely free and attaches only at the very top, and its stem is stuffed with cottony pith rather than hollow. Verpa poisons some people. The deadly false morels, Gyromitra, have brain-like unpitted caps. Slice lengthwise and cook thoroughly. It occurs in hardwood floodplain forests of southern and central Ontario. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "shrimp-russula": "Smell tells this Russula apart. The whole mushroom gives off a strong odour of cooked shrimp or shellfish, more so as it ages, and the stem and flesh slowly stain brownish. The cap is dark wine-red to purplish, the flesh firm and white, the gills cream to ochre, and the spore print pale ochre. It grows on the ground under conifers, especially pine and spruce, in summer and fall, through central and northern Ontario.\n\nRussula is a hard genus. Many species are acrid, including the sickener, Russula emetica, which causes vomiting, and a hot, peppery taste marks any inedible one. This species is confirmed only by the seafood odour and the brown staining together, and both are easy to misjudge. Take that difficulty seriously, even though this is one of the few Russula rated a good edible. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "depressed-hedgehog": "Smaller and slimmer than the common hedgehog, this one carries a navel-like dimple in the centre of its orange-tan cap. The underside has the same soft white spines, running down onto a thin stem, and the flesh is white. It prefers wetter, mossier ground than its larger relative, turning up in sphagnum under hemlock and spruce in wet conifer forests and bogs. The dimpled cap is a reliable field mark. Season is summer and fall, across central and northern Ontario.\n\nThe toothed edibles are friendly to learn because no deadly mushroom shares the soft-spined underside beneath a chanterelle-like cap. As with all of them, the check is the underside. Confirm soft spines rather than gills or pores before anything else. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert confirms it.",
  "two-coloured-bolete": "This is a bolete, so look under the cap for a spongy layer of pores rather than gills. The dry cap runs rosy-red to pinkish-red when fresh and fades as it ages. The pores are yellow. The stem is red over its lower part and yellow near the top, with no netted pattern. You meet it in deciduous forests through summer and early fall, most often under oak in southern and eastern Ontario. It lives in partnership with the tree roots, which is why it returns to the same stands year after year.\n\nColour alone will not identify it safely. Both the flesh and the pores bruise blue, but slowly and only weakly. That faint reaction is the useful clue. Several toxic red-pored boletes stain blue instantly and intensely and can cause severe gastrointestinal illness. Boletus sensibilis and its relatives are the ones to worry about. Certain identification matters here, and a mistake is dangerous. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "white-pored-chicken-of-the-woods": "Soft, overlapping shelves in salmon-orange to pinkish tones, arranged as a rounded rosette at the base of a tree. That growth habit is the first thing to notice. Flip a shelf and the underside is white to pale cream, covered in fine pores, never gills. Unlike the sulphur shelf that stacks in brackets on standing trunks, this species rises from oak roots and forms a flower-like cluster at ground level. Summer and fall are the seasons, in the oak forests of southern and eastern Ontario.\n\nYoung flesh is pale and soft. That is the part worth eating, and only the tender margins, and only from oak or other hardwoods. Older growth and conifer-grown chicken mushrooms cause stomach upset, and some people react even to well-cooked specimens. Cook it thoroughly. Certain identification matters, and as with any wild mushroom, do not eat it unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "common-earthball": "A firm, rounded ball sits on the ground or on rotten wood, dull yellow to ochre, with no real stem. The rind is thick, tough, and scaly. It grows in acidic soil, mossy woods, and sandy banks through summer and fall, across southern, central, and northern Ontario. From the outside it can pass for a puffball, which is exactly the problem.\n\nSlice it in half. An earthball is firm inside and marbled purple-black to grey-black even when young. An edible puffball is soft and pure white throughout when it is good to eat. That difference is instant and reliable. Eating an earthball brings nausea, vomiting, and cramps. People mistake it for a puffball every year, so always cut round fungi open and reject any with a firm dark marbled interior or a thick rind. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "poison-pie": "The name is a fair warning. This is a small to medium mushroom with a sticky cap in tan to pinkish-buff, palest at the margin. The gills are crowded and clay-brown, and they often weep tiny water droplets that dry to rusty spots along the edges. Near the top the stem is whitish and mealy. Crush a piece and it smells strongly of radish. The spore print is a dull brown. It grows on the ground under both hardwoods and conifers, sometimes in rings, from late summer into fall across southern and central Ontario.\n\nTaken together, the radish odour and the weeping, rusty-spotted gills are good warning signs. It is toxic. Eating it causes severe vomiting, cramps, and diarrhea. It is rarely deadly, but it is genuinely dangerous to children and frail adults, who dehydrate quickly. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "the-sickener": "Bright scarlet to cherry-red, with a shiny sticky skin that peels away easily from the cap. Underneath sit pure white gills, brittle enough to snap, over a white stem. The spore print is white too. You find it in wet mossy conifer woods, bogs, and sphagnum, often near pine and spruce, through summer and fall in central and northern Ontario.\n\nThe name is earned. It causes violent vomiting, along with nausea and diarrhea. A tiny taste, spat straight out and never swallowed, is intensely hot and peppery, and that burning, acrid bite is the classic mark of a toxic Russula. Even so, a red cap on its own tells you almost nothing, since the Russula group is large and hard to separate. Do not lean on colour. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "deadly-conocybe": "Small and easy to overlook, which is the danger. The cap is conical to bell-shaped and rusty-brown, sitting on a slender fruitbody with brown gills. The detail that matters is a distinct movable ring partway up the stem. The spore print is rusty-brown. It comes up on lawns, mulch, gardens, and grassy ground, often right beside harmless lawn mushrooms, through summer and fall in southern and central Ontario.\n\nA little brown mushroom with a ring on a thin stem growing in grass is a serious warning. This one carries amatoxins, the same liver-destroying poisons as the death cap, and a small amount can kill. Symptoms are delayed by hours, so by the time a person feels ill the damage is already under way, and there is no home antidote. The safe rule is simple. Do not gather little brown mushrooms from lawns or mulch, and never eat them.",
  "deadly-dapperling": "A killer hides in this small, plain mushroom of lawns and wood-chip beds. The pinkish-brown cap breaks into fine concentric scales toward the margin over a smoother centre. The gills are white and crowded, the slender stem carries a flimsy ring zone, and the spore print is white. It turns up on lawns, gardens, disturbed ground, and sometimes greenhouses through summer and fall in southern and central Ontario.\n\nDo not let the size fool you. Small Lepiota like this are among the most dangerous mushrooms in a yard, because even a tiny body holds a lethal dose of amatoxins, the same poisons found in the death cap. A few caps can kill. Symptoms are delayed by hours, and there is no home antidote. Never gather small brown-scaled mushrooms from lawns or wood chips.",
  "livid-pinkgill": "Large and stout, with an ivory to pale grey-brown cap whose margin rolls inward when young. The gills begin pale yellow and shift to salmon-pink as the spores ripen, the trait that names the pinkgills. The stout white stem has no ring, and the spore print is pink. Crush the flesh and it smells strongly mealy, like rancid flour. It favours rich soil under hardwoods, especially oak and beech, in summer and early fall in southern Ontario.\n\nWhere it grows it is one of the leading causes of serious mushroom poisoning, largely because it gets mistaken for edible species. It brings on violent, prolonged vomiting and diarrhea, and the fluid loss can become dangerous on its own. The large size, mealy smell, and pink spore print set it apart from the grey-capped and pink-gilled mushrooms people confuse it with. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "birch-polypore": "Found on birch and nothing else, which makes it one of the easier brackets to place. The fruitbody is a smooth, rounded, kidney-shaped shelf with a thin tan to grey-brown skin that peels back from a white pore surface underneath. Young ones are soft and rubbery. With age they turn corky and hard. Look for them on dead and dying birch trunks and fallen logs, from summer through winter, widespread across Ontario and common in the birch forests of the north.\n\nThe tough flesh has a long human history. Strips of it were once used to put a final edge on a razor, which earned the old name razor strop fungus. The Iceman found in the Alps carried pieces of this species, probably as medicine. It is not a food. The flesh is bitter and corky, though it is harmless to handle.",
  "chicken-fat-suillus": "Tied to eastern white pine, so it never strays far from that tree. The cap is slimy and yellow with reddish-brown streaks or scales, and under it lies a spongy layer of yellow pores rather than gills. The thin yellow stem is speckled with tiny brown dots. It grows on the ground under white pine through summer and fall in southern and central Ontario, trading soil nutrients for sugars from the tree's roots.\n\nThis is a bolete, not a gilled mushroom. It is reported as edible but slimy and poor eating, and it is not recommended. Some people who handle it get an itchy skin rash, and some who eat it get stomach upset. There is little reason to bother with it. As with any wild mushroom, do not eat it unless an expert has confirmed it is safe.",
  "common-greenshield-lichen": "Flat, leafy patches press against tree bark in pale yellow-green, one of the most common leaf lichens on trees in the province. The lobes are broad and wrinkled, and the surface turns coarse and powdery toward the centre. Underneath it is grey to blackish. Look on bark, fence rails, and sometimes rock in open woods and yards, in southern and central Ontario, in every season.\n\nA lichen is a partnership between a fungus and an alga, not a plant. This one puts up with moderate air pollution better than the beard lichens do, which is why it still grows on city and roadside trees where fussier species have dropped out. That tolerance makes it a rough, informal gauge of local air quality. It is not a food, and it is harmless.",
  "dog-vomit-slime-mould": "Bright yellow to orange froth that looks spilled onto the ground is the usual first sighting. It spreads as a spongy mass across bark mulch, wood chips, a lawn, or a rotting log, usually in warm wet weather. Within a day or two the colour dulls to tan or grey and the whole thing breaks down into a crust of dark powdery spores. It shows up in summer and fall, widespread across Ontario in gardens and mulched beds.\n\nDespite the look, it is not a fungus. It is a slime mould, and for part of its life it creeps slowly over the ground as a single giant cell, feeding on bacteria and decaying matter, before drying into that spore crust. It does no harm to people, pets, or plants, and it does not damage whatever it climbs over. It is not a food. If the look bothers you, a rake breaks it up.",
  "old-mans-beard-lichen": "Pale grey-green tufts, shrubby or hair-like, drape from the bark and branches of trees in clean forests. To separate this lichen from other dangling look-alikes, pull a single strand gently apart. Inside runs a tough white elastic cord that stretches before it breaks. That cord is the giveaway. Tufts range from a couple of centimetres to a good hand's length.\n\nIt is one of the better natural gauges of air quality. Usnea takes up what is in the air directly, and it fades out where pollution is heavy. A forest well hung with beard lichen is telling you the air is clean, which is why it thrives across northern Ontario and cottage country and thins out near cities and industry. It can be found in any season. It is not a wild food. It carries usnic acid, which can damage the liver in quantity, so it should not be swallowed as an edible or a supplement.",
  "orange-mycena": "Clusters of small orange caps on a dead hardwood log usually turn out to be this species. The cap is bell-shaped and sticky, bright orange when young and fading paler as it ages, set on a slender orange stem that grows in tight bundles from the wood. Look at the gills. Their edges are a stronger orange to reddish colour than the faces, and that detail helps confirm the find.\n\nCaps stay small, a few centimetres across at most, so it is a mushroom to notice rather than to gather. The orange pigment in the gill edges has drawn some scientific interest for antibacterial properties. You will meet it through summer and into fall on rotting hardwood in southern and central Ontario. It has no food value and is not eaten. As with any wild mushroom, do not put it in your mouth unless an expert has confirmed what it is.",
  "pinwheel-marasmius": "On fallen twigs and sticks in damp hardwood woods, look for troops of tiny white parachutes on black wire. Each cap is white and pleated like a folded umbrella, only a few millimetres across. The gills are widely spaced and, oddly, do not reach the stem. Instead they attach to a small collar that rings the top of the stem, which is thin, shiny, and blackish. That collar is the feature to check.\n\nIt is built to weather dry spells. When the woods dry out the little caps shrivel, and when rain returns they take up water, revive, and shed spores again, repeating the cycle through the season. You will find it in summer and fall in southern and central Ontario. There is nothing to eat here. The caps are too small and the stems too tough and wiry to be food, but they are harmless, and a hand lens makes the collar and pleats worth the look.",
  "reindeer-lichen": "Springy pale grey carpets on open ground, on rock barrens, in bogs, and under thin conifer cover, are usually this lichen. Up close each cushion is a mass of branches that fork again and again into ever finer tips, with no leaves and no real stem. It crunches underfoot when dry and turns soft and pliable after rain. Mats stand up to about 10 cm tall and can spread across a lot of ground.\n\nThis is caribou food. Through winter caribou paw down through snow to reach it, and it makes up a large part of what carries them through the cold months. It grows slowly and can take decades to recover where it has been heavily grazed or burned, so it is worth treading around rather than through. It is common across northern Ontario and the Shield and thins out to the south. It is not a food for people. It holds bitter acids and would need special preparation, so it is best left for the animals that depend on it.",
  "split-gill": "Rows of small grey fans on a dead branch or a piece of firewood are a good bet for split gill. The caps are tough and fan-shaped, a centimetre or few across, coated in fine white or grey hairs, and fixed straight to the wood with no stem. The name comes from the underside. What look like gills are split lengthwise down their edges, a feature a hand lens shows clearly and nothing else quite copies.\n\nIt is one of the most widespread fungi on the planet, recorded on every continent but Antarctica. In dry weather the fans curl and dry, then take up water and revive to release spores again, even after years. You can find it in any season across Ontario. It is not a food here, too small and leathery to bother with. It is eaten in parts of the tropics, but it is also one of the very few fungi known to cause infections in people with weakened immune systems. Leave it be.",
  "tinder-polypore": "Shaped like a horse's hoof and just as hard, this grey to greyish-brown bracket sits on birch trunks year-round, living and dead alike. The top is a smooth crust marked with concentric bands that record its growth, and the underside is a flat sheet of tiny pores. It is perennial, adding a fresh pore layer each season, and it will not budge when you push on it.\n\nThe name points to its old use. The soft felty layer inside the crust can be cut out, pounded thin, and dried into a material called amadou that takes a spark and holds a coal, which made it a fire-carrying tool for thousands of years. The 5,000-year-old man found frozen in the Alps was carrying pieces of it. You will see it across Ontario, most often on birch. It is not edible, being solid wood in all but name, but it is harmless to handle.",
  "yellow-fairy-cups": "A barkless log flecked all over with bright lemon-yellow dots is often the work of this little fungus. Each fruitbody is a smooth yellow disc, nearly stalkless and rarely more than two or three millimetres across, but they come in numbers. Hundreds can scatter across a single damp log until the wood looks as though someone flicked a loaded paintbrush at it. The colour and the very small size together are enough to name it.\n\nIt favours bare, rotting hardwood where the bark has fallen away, and it fruits through summer and fall in damp woods across Ontario. There is nothing to eat, the cups being far too small to matter, but they are harmless, and a hand lens brings out the disc shape and the colour.",
  "chocolate-tube-slime": "What looks like a patch of fine brown bristles on a rotting log is not a fungus at all but a slime mould. The tufts are made of many thin stalked tubes standing upright and packed side by side, each only a few millimetres tall. They come up whitish and wet, then ripen to a chocolate or rust-brown as the spores mature.\n\nThe upright tubes are only the last act. For most of its life the organism creeps over and through the wood as a shapeless mass, feeding on bacteria and the like, and only when it is ready to reproduce does it gather itself into these little towers to release spores into the air. It shows up on damp logs, stumps, and woody debris in summer, mostly in southern and central Ontario. It is not a food and is harmless to touch, an easy thing to overlook until you crouch down and see the tubes.",
  "elegant-stinkhorn": "You often smell this one before you find it. It begins as a white gelatinous egg half-buried in mulch, then pushes up into a tapering spongy spike, pink to orange, with no cap and no gills. The top end is smeared with a slimy olive-brown coat, and that coat is the source of the stench, a smell of rotting meat.\n\nThe smell is the whole strategy. Flies are drawn in, walk across the slime, and fly off with spores stuck to their feet, doing the work that wind does for other fungi. It turns up in gardens, wood chips, and rich hardwood debris, and mulched beds in southern Ontario suit it well. Summer and fall are the times to expect it. It is not a food. The mature spike reeks of carrion and is not eaten, though it is harmless to touch if curiosity gets the better of you.",
  "eyelash-cup": "Bright orange discs no bigger than a shirt button, each rimmed with a fringe of stiff dark hairs, are hard to mistake once you have seen them. The top of the cup is orange to red and flat, and around its edge stands a ring of brown to black bristles that look for all the world like eyelashes. A hand lens shows them plainly, though on the larger cups you can pick them out by eye.\n\nIt grows in groups on wet ground and soaked rotting wood, often on mossy logs where the damp never quite leaves. The cup does more than sit there. It flexes and puffs its spores toward the light, a trick many cup fungi share. Spring through fall it turns up in damp woods across Ontario. It is not a food, being small and of no substance, and it is best simply looked at and left where it grows.",
  "green-stain-fungus": "Usually you find the colour before you find the fungus. A rotting hardwood log stained an unexpected blue-green is the sign, and the stain runs through the wood even where no fruitbodies show. When it does fruit, the fruitbodies are small blue-green saucers on short stalks, the same colour as the wood they sit on, but they are seen far less often than the stain itself.\n\nIt works on decaying, barkless hardwood lying on the forest floor. The blue-green wood has a long history in craft. For centuries it was cut into decorative inlay work, the English tradition of Tunbridge ware among the best known uses. Look for it in the hardwood forests of southern and central Ontario through summer and fall. It has no food value and is not eaten. The wood it colours, though, is worth turning over for a look.",
  "fairy-ring-mushroom": "A ring or arc of tan mushrooms in a lawn is the classic sign of this species. The cap is buff to tan and bell-shaped with a broad central bump, and it dries to a paler shade. The gills are pale, widely spaced, and stop at the stem rather than running down it. The stem is thin, tough, and pliable, hard to snap. A spore print comes out white, and dried caps revive and shed spores again after rain.\n\nThe rings are the famous part. The underground mycelium grows outward evenly, year after year, so the mushrooms come up in a widening circle that can persist in a lawn for decades.\n\nIt is edible and sought by some, but the same lawns grow mushrooms that can kill. Fool's funnel and other small white Clitocybe hold muscarine and are deadly; they differ in having crowded gills that run down the stem and no central bump. Poisonous little brown mushrooms grow in grass too. Certain identification is everything, and mistakes here can be fatal, so eat none of these unless an expert has confirmed the mushroom.",
  "meadow-mushroom": "Turn up in a mown field or pasture after late-summer rain and you may find this mushroom scattered across the grass in numbers. The silky white cap sits above gills that start a clear pink and darken through to chocolate-brown, matching a dark brown spore print. A thin ring circles the stem, the flesh stays white or bruises only faintly pink, and there is no cup at the base. It is the wild cousin of the white button mushroom sold in grocery stores.\n\nCommon as it is, this mushroom keeps dangerous company. Young destroying angels (Amanita bisporigera and A. virosa) can look similar from above, but they hold white gills, leave a white spore print, and sit in a cup at the stem base. Always dig up the whole base and check the gill colour. The yellow-stainer (Agaricus xanthodermus) flashes bright chrome-yellow when cut and smells of phenol or ink. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "pear-shaped-puffball": "Most puffballs come up from soil. This one clusters on rotting wood instead, and that habit alone sets it apart. Pear- to top-shaped bodies grow on decaying logs, stumps, and buried wood, often in dense troops of dozens, anchored to the wood by white cords called rhizomorphs. The surface is smooth to finely warted. When young and edible the interior is pure white and uniform. As it matures the inside turns olive-brown and puffs a cloud of spores from a pore at the top.\n\nSlice every puffball from top to bottom before considering it for the pan. The flesh must be solid, even, and white throughout. Any outline of a developing cap, gills, or stem inside means you are holding the 'egg' stage of a deadly Amanita, not a puffball. Earthballs (Scleroderma) are another trap, with firm purple-black interiors, and they are toxic. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "winter-chanterelle": "One of the last edibles of the season, this small funnel keeps fruiting into late fall, often after the first frosts. The cap runs brown to greyish and sits 1 to 6 cm across over a hollow yellow to yellow-brown stem. Look underneath for shallow, blunt, forking ridges that run down the stem. These are false ridges, not the sharp knife-edged gills of an ordinary mushroom. It favours damp, mossy conifer and mixed forest, growing in troops on rotting wood, needle duff, and moss beds through the cottage country and boreal north.\n\nTwo look-alikes are the ones to know. The false chanterelle (Hygrophoropsis aurantiaca) has crowded, forked true gills and is treated as toxic. The jack-o'-lantern (Omphalotus illudens) grows in clusters on wood, has true blade-like gills, and is poisonous. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "aborted-entoloma": "This fungus is usually collected in its strange 'aborted' form: a lumpy, off-white to greyish puffy mass with pinkish, marbled flesh inside. The normal form is a grey-capped mushroom with salmon-pink gills that run down the stem and a distinctly pink spore print. Both turn up on soil and rotting wood in hardwood and mixed forest, often near honey mushrooms. The lumps form when this fungus parasitizes the honey mushroom, which is the reverse of what mycologists long assumed.\n\nCaution matters here. The normal grey form resembles other pink-spored Entoloma species, and several of those, including Entoloma sinuatum (E. lividum), cause severe poisoning. The pale lumps can also be muddled with other whitish fungi. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "wine-cap-stropharia": "Wood-chip mulch and garden beds are the place to meet this one. The cap opens wine-red to reddish-brown and fades to buff with age, sometimes reaching 30 cm across on a stout white stem. That stem carries a thick, wrinkled ring, grooved and star-shaped like a cog-wheel on its underside. Gills shift from pale grey to dark purple-brown, matching the spore print. It takes well to cultivation in wood chips, which has earned it the nickname 'garden giant,' and it is often grown among vegetables.\n\nThe dark spores and brown gills invite confusion. The deadly Galerina marginata is smaller, grows on wood, and drops rusty-brown spores. Toxic Cortinarius and some Agaricus species share the same mulch and lawns. A ringed stem by itself proves nothing. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it.",
  "angel-wings": "For generations these pure white, fan-shaped caps were gathered and eaten. That is no longer advised. Thin, petal-like caps grow in overlapping clusters on dead conifer wood, hemlock especially, with crowded white gills and almost no stalk. The real hazard is how closely they resemble edible oyster mushrooms, which grow mainly on hardwood and are thicker and greyer.\n\nIn 2004 an outbreak in Japan sickened 59 people and killed 17, all linked to this mushroom, with an acute brain disease striking hardest in people who had kidney disease. The toxin behind it is still not fully understood. On that record the species is now treated as unsafe. Do not eat it. If you are after oyster mushrooms, note the wood they grow on and the thickness of the flesh, and leave a thin white cluster on conifer wood alone.",
  "brown-roll-rim": "Often found near birch, this ground-dwelling mushroom has a yellow-brown to olive-brown funnel-shaped cap with a margin that rolls strongly under itself. The crowded gills run down the stem and bruise rusty-brown when handled. It was eaten in parts of Europe for a long time, and only in the 20th century was it recognized as dangerous.\n\nThe threat is unusual and severe. Repeated meals can set off a delayed autoimmune reaction, known as Paxillus syndrome, in which the body destroys its own red blood cells and the kidneys can fail. The risk builds unpredictably, so a person may eat it for years and then react badly to a meal. Cooking does not make it safe. It is easily mistaken for harmless brown mushrooms. Do not eat it.",
  "eastern-american-panthercap": "This is the true panther cap of eastern North America; the European Amanita pantherina does not grow here. The cap ranges white to pale grey and is dotted with white pyramidal warts. Below are white gills, a ring on the stalk, and a swollen base finished with a distinct funnel-like collar or roll of tissue. It grows on the ground in mixed and oak-pine woods, in partnership with tree roots.\n\nIts neurotoxins, ibotenic acid and muscimol, are often more concentrated than in the related fly agaric, so reactions tend to be worse. Delirium, agitation, nausea, and seizures can set in within about one to three hours. Deaths are rare, but this is a seriously poisonous mushroom. Do not eat it.",
  "white-fibrecap": "Barely bigger than a thumbnail, this small white mushroom is one to learn and then avoid. The silky cap, sometimes tinged lilac, is conical to humped and splits radially as it ages. It smells earthy or spermatic and leaves a dull brown spore print. It comes up on soil in mixed and conifer woods, along paths and trail edges. It belongs to Inocybe, an enormous genus best left alone entirely.\n\nAlong with the fool's funnel, it is a leading cause of muscarine poisoning. The toxin brings on rapid sweating, drooling, tearing, and a dangerously slow heartbeat within 15 to 30 minutes, and it can be fatal. Small brown or white mushrooms of this sort, the ones often waved off as 'little brown mushrooms,' should never be eaten. Do not eat it.",
  "yellow-staining-mushroom": "Cut or bruise the base of a white lawn mushroom and it flashes bright chrome-yellow at once. It carries an unpleasant smell of phenol, ink, or iodine that gets stronger with cooking. Otherwise it looks the part of an edible Agaricus, with pink gills that brown as they age, which is exactly why it fools people. It grows on the ground in lawns, gardens, parks, meadows, and along hedgerows and woodland edges.\n\nIt is the commonest cause of poisoning among the button-mushroom relatives. The result is a gastrointestinal upset with cramps, vomiting, and diarrhea, unpleasant though not usually dangerous. The yellow base and the chemical smell are the two things that give it away. If a white mushroom stains chrome-yellow at the cut base and smells of ink, discard it. Do not eat it.",
  "fools-funnel": "A small chalky white to buff funnel in the lawn, sometimes growing in rings, this is a mushroom to recognize and walk past. The crowded whitish gills run down the stem. It also goes by ivory funnel. It shows up in grass and along path edges through summer and fall.\n\nThe problem is muscarine. Within 15 to 30 minutes it can bring on drenching sweats, streaming eyes, drooling, blurred vision, vomiting, and a slowed heartbeat, the pattern sometimes called SLUDGE. The reaction can be serious, though it responds to atropine under medical care. The real trap is company. It grows in the same lawns as the edible fairy-ring mushroom (Marasmius oreades), and mixing the two has poisoned people. Do not eat it.",
  "deadly-webcap": "Tawny orange-brown, with a conical cap drawn up into a pointed central bump, this mushroom grows with spruce on damp, mossy, acidic ground in boreal forest. The gills are rusty-brown and the spore print rusty as well. A cobweb-like veil, the cortina that gives webcaps their name, drapes the stalk when young. In Ontario it is rare and local, confined to the northern conifer forests.\n\nThe danger is that nothing warns you. Its toxin, orellanine, has no taste and produces no early symptoms. Then, after a delay of two days to three weeks, it attacks the kidneys, and the damage can be permanent, sometimes leading to dialysis or a transplant. By the time a person feels ill the harm is already done. No webcap in the genus Cortinarius should ever be eaten. Do not eat it.",
  "birds-nest-fungus": "At a glance you might walk past these as spilled seed or flecks of bark. The bird's nest fungi are tan cups five to ten millimetres across, cottony on the outside, each holding a few flat discs when mature. Those discs, called peridioles, are packets of spores. Look on wood chips, mulch, fallen twigs, and rotting wood - mulched gardens and trail edges are reliable spots. They appear in summer and fall and turn up across Ontario.\n\nThe cup works as a splash device. When a raindrop lands at the right angle, the water flings the peridioles out and up, sometimes a full metre. Each packet trails a sticky thread that can snag on a stem or leaf where it lands, giving the spores a start above ground. It is one of the neater dispersal tricks among fungi, run entirely on falling rain. Too small and too tough to eat, and harmless.",
  "dead-mans-fingers": "Stiff dark clubs pushing up from the base of a stump have an obvious nickname, and Dead Man's Fingers earns it. The fruitbodies stand three to eight centimetres tall, often in small clusters, finger- or club-shaped. Young ones are pale blue-grey; with age they blacken and turn warty on the outside while the flesh inside stays tough and white. You find them on hardwood stumps and buried roots, beech and maple especially, through spring, summer, and fall across southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe name comes from the way the blackened clubs rise out of buried wood like fingers reaching from a grave. The fungus is quietly at work below, breaking down the roots and stump it grows from. It is a slow, long-lasting species, and the same clump can persist for months. Not a food mushroom. The flesh is woody and inedible, though there is no harm in handling it. A good one to learn early because it is distinctive and hard to confuse with anything you would want to eat.",
  "witchs-butter": "Bright golden folds on a bare winter branch are a good sign you have found Witch's Butter. The gelatinous masses run two to seven centimetres across, golden-yellow to orange, folded and brain-like. When wet they swell and shine. In dry spells they shrink to a hard, dark crust and are easy to overlook until the next rain plumps them up again. Look on dead hardwood branches and twigs, often after rain or during a mild winter thaw. It is widespread across Ontario and shows through fall, winter, and spring.\n\nIt sits on wood but does not eat it. Instead it parasitizes the crust fungi (Peniophora) living inside the branch, which is why you often find it where those fungi are at work. It is reported as edible but flavourless and gelatinous, and rarely worth the trouble. Its yellow and orange jelly look-alikes (Dacrymyces, Tremella aurantia) are also non-toxic, and there are no deadly yellow-jelly look-alikes. Even so, never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it is safe.",
  "wolfs-milk-slime": "Squeeze a young one and it bleeds pink, which is how Wolf's Milk Slime got its name. The little spheres run three to fifteen millimetres across and grow in groups on damp, well-rotted logs and stumps in shaded forest. Fresh ones are salmon-pink to greyish and filled with a pink paste. Older ones dry out and turn to a dusty grey spore mass, so the colour tells you roughly how far along it is. Summer and fall are the seasons, and it turns up across Ontario.\n\nDespite the cushions it forms on wood, it is not a true fungus but a slime mould. Earlier in its life it exists as a creeping mass that can move slowly across the wood surface, feeding, before it gathers itself into these pink balls to release spores. That habit of crawling sets slime moulds apart from fungi, which stay put. Not a food species. It is worth a look and a gentle squeeze, nothing more.",
  "cinnabar-red-polypore": "The colour is what stops you. The Cinnabar-red Polypore is a vivid orange-red bracket, two to thirteen centimetres wide, and the same bright shade on top as on the pored underside. That even colour, top and bottom, is a good field mark. It fades somewhat with age but holds its reddish tone. Look on dead hardwood branches and logs, cherry and birch in particular, through summer, fall, and into winter. It is native and found across southern and central Ontario.\n\nThe pigment responsible, cinnabarin, has antibacterial properties and readily stains whatever it touches, so handle it knowing it may mark your fingers or bag. The bracket is tough and corky rather than fleshy, and it is not a food species. It is a wood-rotter, doing slow work on the dead branch it sits on. Because the colour is so distinctive and holds through the seasons, it is one of the easier brackets to recognize on a winter walk when little else is fruiting.",
  "scarlet-elf-cup": "A spot of brilliant red on the forest floor in early spring, while snow is still melting, is often the Scarlet Elf Cup. The cups are shallow, one to five centimetres across, on a short pale stalk, scarlet inside with a whitish, minutely hairy outer surface. They grow on damp, mossy fallen hardwood sticks in rich, moist woods. Winter and spring are the seasons. It occurs across southern and central Ontario, one of the first fruitings of the year.\n\nWarm a ripe cup with your breath or let the sun hit it and it may puff out a visible cloud of spores, sometimes with a faint hiss. That is the fungus releasing its spores into the air. It is considered non-toxic but thin, tough, and of little food value. Its close relatives, the other scarlet cups, are also non-toxic but can only be told apart under a microscope, so a confident species name in the field is not really possible. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed it is safe.",
  "chicken-of-the-woods": "Bright sulphur-yellow shelves stacked on an oak trunk are hard to miss, and Chicken of the Woods is one of the more recognizable brackets. The overlapping shelves run five to thirty centimetres wide, with clusters reaching half a metre, coloured yellow to orange with a soft, suede texture. There are no gills; the underside is a layer of fine pores. It grows on living and dead hardwoods, oak especially, through summer and fall in southern and central Ontario, and can regrow on the same log for years.\n\nThe name comes from the meaty texture and bright flesh, which some cooks treat as a chicken substitute. It carries real cautions. It can cause stomach upset in some people even when correctly identified, and specimens growing on conifers or eucalyptus should be avoided. It also has look-alikes. Cook it thoroughly and try only a small amount the first time. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "dryads-saddle": "Among the first brackets of spring, Dryad's Saddle appears about the same time as the morels. The fan- or saddle-shaped cap spans eight to thirty centimetres, tan and patterned with concentric brown feather-like scales, which is where the other name, pheasant back, comes from. Underneath are large angular white pores, and the short stalk blackens at the base. Crush a piece and it smells of watermelon rind. Look on living and dead hardwoods, dead elm, maple, and box elder in particular. It grows across Ontario, mainly in spring into summer.\n\nIt is a wound and wood-rot fungus, working into trees through damaged bark. Only the tender growing edge is worth eating; the rest quickly turns corky and tough. It has other polypore look-alikes, so the smell and the feather-scaled cap matter for a confident call. Take only the soft margin, and cook it. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "giant-puffball": "Few fungi are as unmistakable at a distance as a Giant Puffball sitting white in a meadow. It is a smooth white ball with no stalk, cap, or gills, commonly fifteen to fifty centimetres across and sometimes larger than a basketball, occasionally exceeding twenty kilograms. The inside is firm and pure white when it is good to eat. Yellow or brown flesh means it is past its prime and spent. Look in meadows, pastures, roadsides, and open woods on rich soil, in summer and fall, across Ontario.\n\nA single mature puffball can hold several trillion spores. If every one grew, the offspring would outweigh the Earth, which is a useful way to picture how much a fungus invests in spread. There is one safety point that matters. Deadly young Amanita buttons can resemble small puffballs, so always slice any puffball vertically and confirm uniform solid white flesh with no outline of gills inside. If you see a developing cap or stem shape, discard it. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "golden-chanterelle": "Egg-yellow from cap to stalk, the Golden Chanterelle is one of the more sought-after wild mushrooms and one worth learning carefully. The funnel-shaped cap runs three to ten centimetres across on a body four to twelve centimetres tall. The key feature is underneath: blunt, forking false gills, really ridges, that run down the stalk rather than sharp blades that snap off. It often smells faintly of apricot. Look on mossy ground under hardwoods and conifers, oak, birch, and pine among them. Summer and fall are the seasons, across Ontario.\n\nChanterelles form a partnership with living tree roots, which is why they resist cultivation and nearly all are wild-foraged. That same tie means you find them in the same patches year after year. Learn the toxic look-alikes before eating any. The jack-o'-lantern (Omphalotus illudens) has true sharp gills and grows in clusters on wood, and the false chanterelle also mimics the colour. The false gills and growth habit are what separate the real thing. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "king-bolete": "The King Bolete, or porcini, is the same mushroom prized in European kitchens, and Ontario's woods produce closely related king boletes valued for drying. Look for a brown, bun-shaped cap eight to twenty-five centimetres across on a fat pale stalk marked with a fine raised net. Instead of gills the underside is a sponge of pores, white ageing to olive, never red. Cut it and the flesh stays pale; it does not stain blue. It grows in partnership with conifers and hardwoods, along mossy woodland edges and trails, in summer and fall, across Ontario.\n\nBoletes as a group are mostly safe, but some are toxic, and a few simple checks weed those out. Avoid any bolete with red pores, any that stains blue when cut or bruised, and any with bitter flesh. The king bolete shows none of these. Its firm texture and rich flavour make it a favourite for drying, which concentrates the taste. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "oyster-mushroom": "Shelving clusters of pale fan-shaped caps on a dead poplar are a common sight, and the Oyster Mushroom is among the easier edible fungi to recognize. Caps run five to twenty-five centimetres across, white to tan, oyster- or fan-shaped, with gills running down a short off-centre stalk or no stalk at all. They grow on dead and dying hardwoods, poplar, beech, and maple especially, and fruit in cool weather through spring, fall, and even winter across Ontario.\n\nThere is an odd detail to the biology: the mycelium is carnivorous, paralyzing and digesting tiny roundworms to draw nitrogen from the wood it lives on. The cautions are real. Toxic look-alikes include the orange jack-o'-lantern and the mock oyster (Phyllotopsis), and most dangerous is the pure-white Angel Wings (Pleurocybella porrigens), which grows on conifers and has caused fatalities. The wood matters here: true oysters favour hardwoods. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "shaggy-mane": "You find these on disturbed ground - lawns, gravel shoulders, roadsides, and fresh wood-chip beds - often in loose rows that push up overnight. The cap is tall and cylindrical, white, and covered in shaggy upturned scales that give the mushroom its name. It stands 4 to 15 cm and looks like a closed umbrella wrapped in torn paper. Within a day the cap dissolves from the bottom up into a black, inky liquid, a process called deliquescence that once supplied writing ink. That habit makes it a poor keeper, so picked shaggy manes have to be cooked within hours or they turn to sludge in the basket.\n\nLook for it through summer and fall across Ontario. It is common and easy to spot, but the group it belongs to needs care. Other inky caps look similar, and the related Coprinopsis atramentaria causes severe illness if eaten with alcohol. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "yellow-morel": "Spring is the only season for this one. The cap is a pitted, honeycombed sponge, tan to yellow-brown, standing 4 to 12 cm on a pale stalk. The single most useful test is to slice it top to bottom. A true morel is completely hollow inside, and the lower edge of the cap is fully attached to the stalk. Search under dead or dying elm and ash, in old apple orchards, and on ground that burned the year before. Morels often flush in large numbers the spring after a fire or when elms die, so a stand of grey elm snags is worth a slow walk.\n\nThey turn up across southern and central Ontario. The catch is the false morel, Gyromitra, which is toxic and shares the season. Its cap is wrinkled and brain-like rather than pitted, and its inside is chambered and cottony, not cleanly hollow. True morels must also be thoroughly cooked. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "black-trumpet": "Easy to walk past, this one hides in plain sight. The fruiting body is a thin, hollow, grey-black funnel, a wavy vase 3 to 8 cm tall and 1 to 6 cm wide, with no real gills, just a smooth to faintly ridged outer surface. Against dark leaf litter it disappears, which is why people who find one often find dozens once their eye adjusts. Look in moss and leaf litter under hardwoods, especially beech and oak, through summer and fall.\n\nThe old name horn of plenty suits the shape. It is native across southern and central Ontario, edible, and it holds and even deepens its flavour when dried. The catch is that the dark funnel form is shared by other fungi, so certain identification still matters. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "hen-of-the-woods": "At the base of old oaks in fall, look for a grey-brown rosette the size of a dinner plate or larger, built from many small spoon-shaped caps rising off one branched base. Each cap is grey above with a white pore surface beneath rather than gills. The whole cluster can span 15 to 60 cm and weigh several kilograms, and exceptional ones top 20 kg. It fruits from the roots, so the same tree often produces in the same spot year after year.\n\nKnown in Japan as maitake, the dancing mushroom, it is valued both as food and in traditional medicine. In Ontario it appears in fall across the south and centre, favouring oaks. It has bracket look-alikes, including Berkeley's polypore, so identification needs care. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "lions-mane": "Few mushrooms are as easy to know on sight. It grows as a single white cushion, unbranched, hung all over with soft downward-pointing spines 1 to 4 cm long. There is no cap and there are no gills, just the teeth, which carry the spores. Young ones are clean white. With age the mass yellows and browns. Find it on wounds and dead spots of living hardwoods, especially beech, maple, and oak, from summer into fall.\n\nIt has a following as both a gourmet and a medicinal mushroom, studied for nerve-growth compounds, and its texture when cooked has earned it the name poor man's crab. This is one of the safer wild mushrooms to identify, and its toothed relatives Hericium americanum and H. coralloides are also edible. Even so, never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "lobster-mushroom": "The name is a double surprise. The lobster mushroom is not one species, and not really a mushroom you would recognize. It is a parasitic fungus, Hypomyces lactifluorum, that coats and consumes a host mushroom, turning it hard, bright orange, and faintly seafood-scented. The host's normal shape distorts and its gills vanish under the orange crust, leaving a dense, lumpy mass 5 to 15 cm across. You find them on the ground in mixed woods where the host Russula and Lactarius species grow, through summer and fall in central and northern Ontario.\n\nThe colour and smell make a found one memorable, but they also hide what lies beneath. The mould is safe to eat only when the host under the crust is a benign Russula or Lactarius. An unknown or already-toxic host stays a risk, and that uncertainty is exactly why certain identification matters here. Never eat any wild mushroom unless an expert has confirmed the identification with certainty.",
  "deadly-galerina": "A plain little brown mushroom on wood that deserves real fear. Galerina marginata has a small brown cap 1.5 to 4 cm across, rusty-brown spores, a fragile ring on the stalk, and a habit of growing in groups on rotting conifer and hardwood logs and wood chips. Nothing about it looks dangerous, which is the problem.\n\nThis drab mushroom carries the same amatoxins as the death cap and can kill. It grows on wood, exactly where foragers look for edible wood mushrooms and honey mushrooms, and it is easily mistaken for them. There is no home remedy for amatoxin poisoning, and symptoms are delayed, so the harm is done before a person feels sick. It is common and widespread across Ontario. Do not eat it, and be doubly cautious of any small brown mushroom fruiting on wood.",
  "destroying-angel": "Pure white from cap to gills to stalk, this is one of the deadliest mushrooms in Ontario. The cap runs 5 to 12 cm, the stalk to about 15 cm, with a skirt-like ring partway up and a sac-like cup, the volva, around a swollen base. Digging to expose that cup is part of recognizing an Amanita. It grows on the ground in mixed and hardwood forests through summer and fall, joined to tree roots as a mycorrhizal partner, and it is common across the province.\n\nIt accounts for a large share of fatal mushroom poisonings worldwide. The amatoxins cause liver and kidney failure, and a single cap can kill an adult. The cruel part is the timing. Symptoms are delayed 6 to 24 hours and often ease before organ damage becomes clear, which gives false reassurance. There is no reliable home antidote. Never eat it, and never taste-test a white mushroom to check.",
  "false-morel": "Spring, sandy soil under conifers, often pine. That is where you meet Gyromitra esculenta, and where it can be mistaken for the true morels people hunt at the same time of year. The cap is reddish-brown and wrinkled into a brain or saddle shape, 4 to 12 cm, not pitted like a honeycomb. Slice it and the inside is chambered and cottony rather than cleanly hollow, which separates it from a real morel.\n\nIt is eaten in some cultures after special preparation, but that history is misleading. Its toxin, gyromitrin, converts in the body to monomethylhydrazine, a compound in the same chemical family as rocket fuel. It can cause liver failure and death, and poisoning has followed even from breathing the cooking vapours. It is common in central and northern Ontario. Do not eat it.",
  "fly-agaric": "This is the red-and-white mushroom of fairy tales, and it is real and common in Ontario. The cap is scarlet to orange, 8 to 20 cm, dotted with white wart-like patches that are remnants of a veil and can wash off in rain. Below are white gills, a ring on the stalk, and a bulbous, ringed base. It partners with birch, pine, and spruce and turns up in mixed forest through summer and fall. The old name comes from a folk practice of steeping caps in milk to stupefy flies.\n\nIt is poisonous. Ibotenic acid and muscimol act on the nervous system and cause delirium, nausea, and sometimes seizures. Deaths are rare, but the poisoning is serious and unpleasant, and potency varies from one mushroom to the next. Do not eat it.",
  "sulphur-tuft": "Crowded yellow tufts on a rotting stump are a common sight in Ontario forests. This mushroom fruits on dead hardwood and conifer stumps, logs, and buried roots, sometimes in large clusters, from spring through fall. The caps are 2 to 6 cm, sulphur-yellow to tawny with a greenish tinge, over crowded gills that age from olive-green to purple-brown. The flesh is intensely bitter, a useful warning to anyone who tastes a scrap and spits it out.\n\nThe trouble is its habit. Growing in clusters on wood, it looks much like the edible honey mushrooms people gather, and mistakes happen. It contains fasciculol and steroid toxins that bring on vomiting and diarrhea and can damage the liver. It is common and widespread across the province. Do not eat it.",
  "green-spored-parasol": "Lawns after warm summer rain are its ground. Chlorophyllum molybdites is a large white parasol, cap 7 to 30 cm on a tall stalk, patterned with brownish scales and carrying a ring. Young caps look much like the edible parasols and puffballs that share the same grass. The giveaway comes with age. The mature gills and the spore print turn a dull green, so a quick spore print onto white paper settles it.\n\nThis is the single most frequent cause of mushroom-poisoning calls in North America, and the lawn habit is why. It grows where people live and eat. Swallowing it brings violent vomiting and diarrhea. It is native and appears in southern Ontario through summer and fall. Do not eat it, and take a spore print before trusting any lawn parasol.",
  "jack-o-lantern": "Bright orange clumps at the base of a hardwood stump usually mean this species rather than the chanterelle people hope for. The whole mushroom is orange, cap and flesh alike, and it grows in dense tufts fused at the base on stumps, roots, and buried wood. Look under the cap. It has true gills, thin and sharp-edged, running a short way down the stalk. A golden chanterelle instead has blunt, forking ridges and grows singly from soil, not clustered on wood. In a fully dark room the gills give off a faint green glow, the old foxfire of rotting logs.\n\nYou will find it in southern and central Ontario through summer and fall. It is poisonous. People who mistake it for a chanterelle get severe stomach cramps, vomiting, and diarrhea within a few hours. The reaction is rarely life-threatening but it is miserable. Certain identification matters here, and the clustered growth on wood is the tell. Do not eat it.",
  "death-cap": "Responsible for more fatal mushroom poisonings than any other species, this one arrived in Ontario with the roots of imported ornamental trees. Look for it in southern Ontario in urban and planted settings, under oak, chestnut, and hornbeam rather than in wild forest. The cap runs greenish to yellowish, 5 to 15 cm, over white gills. Two features matter most: a ring on the upper stalk and a prominent cup-like volva sheathing the base, often below the soil line, so lift the whole thing to check.\n\nIt fruits in summer and fall. The danger is the delay. Amatoxins cause no symptoms for six hours or more, long enough to seem harmless, then bring on violent illness and, without treatment, fatal liver and kidney failure. The toxins are not destroyed by cooking, drying, or freezing. There is no home test and no safe amount. Learn this mushroom so you can leave it alone, and keep children and dogs away from it.",
  "artists-conk": "The name comes from the white underside. Press a fingernail or twig to the fresh pore surface and it bruises brown at once, holding the mark like a pencil line, so the shelf can be drawn on and the picture keeps for years once dried. This is a hard, flat, grey-brown woody bracket, anywhere from a hand's width to well over half a metre across, growing on living and dead hardwoods.\n\nIt is perennial. The same shelf thickens and adds a new pore layer each year, so you can find it in any season, including winter when little else is up. The top is often dusted rusty brown with its own spores, which the fungus releases in enormous numbers. As a decomposer it breaks down heartwood and can also rot the living trees it sits on. It is too tough and woody to eat, though it is sometimes used dried in teas. Mostly you notice it for the drawing surface and for how long a single individual persists.",
  "bracket-shelf-fungi": "This is a catch-all for a large group rather than a single species. Bracket or shelf fungi are the stalkless, shelf-forming polypores that jut straight out from trunks, logs, and stumps. Turn one over and the underside shows pores, a surface of tiny tube mouths, instead of the gills you would see on a typical cap-and-stem mushroom. Textures run from leathery to hard as wood, and sizes from a few centimetres to over half a metre.\n\nTheir real work is decay. Many can break down lignin, the tough compound that stiffens wood and that few other organisms can digest, which makes them central to how a forest recycles its fallen trees. Because so many are tough and perennial, they show up in every season, winter included. The group is large enough that naming one to species takes attention to the host tree, the pore size, and often a spore print. Some are edible and some are not, so the shelf on the log is a starting point, not an answer.",
  "british-soldiers-lichen": "Low on an old stump you might catch a scatter of tiny grey-green stalks, each capped with a dot of bright red. The red coats give the lichen its name. The stalks, called podetia, stand only a centimetre or two tall and grow on rotting wood, mossy logs, and bare sandy soil.\n\nA lichen is not one organism but two living as one: a fungus that provides the structure and an alga that photosynthesizes food. The red tips belong to the fungal partner and are its spore-producing cups. Because the alga makes its own food, the whole thing needs no soil and grows slowly on surfaces little else will use. You can find it in any season across Ontario, and it holds its colour through winter. It asks for nothing and is easy to miss. Once you have seen one patch you tend to start spotting the red caps on stumps everywhere.",
  "coral-fungi": "Upright and branching like a piece of ocean coral, these fungi push up from the forest floor and from rotting wood in shaded woods. The fruitbody divides again and again into slender tips, with no cap and no gills anywhere on it. Colours run through white, tan, yellow, and pink, and a cluster stands anywhere from a few centimetres to about 20 cm tall.\n\nDespite the marine look, these are land fungi, decaying wood and leaf litter or partnering with tree roots. They appear in summer and fall and are widespread across Ontario. Some people eat certain kinds, but this is not a group to trust on sight. Several coral fungi, including some Ramaria, are mildly toxic and act as laxatives, and telling the edible from the unpleasant needs expert identification down to species. Treat a coral in the woods as something to admire rather than collect, unless you truly know which one you have.",
  "honey-mushroom": "In fall, dense tufts of honey-coloured caps appear at the base of trees, on stumps, and over buried roots. A ring sits on the stalk, the spore print is white, and if you peel back the host's bark you may find black, shoelace-like strands called rhizomorphs. Those cords let the fungus spread from tree to tree, and it is an aggressive one. Armillaria causes a root rot that kills living trees, and related species form some of the largest organisms on Earth, including a single individual in Oregon that spans more than 9 square kilometres.\n\nSome people eat this mushroom cooked, but the caution here is serious. The deadly Galerina marginata grows in the same clustered way on wood, at the same time of year, and a careless picker can mix them. Galerina carries the same liver-destroying amatoxins as the death cap. If you cannot tell the two apart with certainty, including a spore print and close attention, do not eat either.",
  "red-belted-polypore": "A reddish band near the growing edge names this bracket. The rest of the crusted top shades from that red belt through grey to near black with age, and the underside is a cream to pale yellow layer of fine pores. The shape starts hoof-like and broadens into a shelf, 5 to 30 cm across, hard and firmly fixed to dead conifers and, less often, hardwoods.\n\nIt is perennial, so you meet it in any season through central and northern Ontario, often on fallen logs. As a decomposer it produces a brown cubical rot, breaking the wood into small crumbling blocks and returning fallen conifers to the soil. The flesh is corky and not eaten. It is common in northern woods, a steady recycler that keeps a forest floor from burying itself in dead timber.",
  "turkey-tail": "Few fungi are easier to find on a dead hardwood log. Thin, flexible brackets grow in overlapping tiers, each one banded in concentric rings of brown, grey, rust, and sometimes blue or green, with a faintly velvety top. The give-away is underneath. Turn a bracket over and the surface is whitish and finely porous, not gilled. That pore surface separates true turkey tail from several lookalikes that share the banded top but have a smooth or toothed underside.\n\nIt fruits from summer into winter and persists on wood across Ontario. Beyond being common, it is one of the most studied medicinal fungi. A compound derived from it, PSK, is used in Japan as an approved adjuvant alongside cancer treatment. It is too thin and tough to eat as food, and is instead dried and simmered for tea. If you are collecting for that, the porous white underside is the feature to confirm every time.",
  "chaga": "What looks like a burnt, cracked lump of charcoal bursting from a birch trunk is chaga. The black exterior is not the whole fungus but a sterile mass of mycelium and wood. Break into it and the inside is a rusty orange-brown, corky and marbled. It grows almost always on living birch, where it lives for years as a slow parasite, and it turns up through central and northern Ontario in every season, dark against the pale bark even in winter.\n\nPeople harvest chaga to dry and brew as tea, and interest in it has grown enough to matter for the trees. A conk takes many years to reach a usable size and regrows slowly, so take modest amounts and leave some behind. Never strip a tree. Overharvesting has thinned it in accessible areas. If you gather it, be sure of the birch host, since the look is distinctive but a charred burl or old bracket can fool a quick glance.",
  "earthstar": "It begins as a small buried ball, then the outer skin splits into pointed arms that curl back like the points of a star. The peeling rays lift and settle a round spore sac in the centre. That sac has a small pore at the top, and when a raindrop strikes it the fungus puffs out a cloud of brown spores. In some kinds the star's arms even flex open and shut with changing humidity, closing over the sac when dry.\n\nLook for them on the ground in leaf litter and sandy soil under trees, mainly in southern and central Ontario, from fall into winter. Because the whole structure is tough and papery, an opened earthstar can sit intact on the forest floor for a long time after it has finished. They are curiosities rather than food. Once you know the star shape you will find last season's dried ones as readily as fresh.",
  "hemlock-varnish-shelf": "The surface looks painted. A young shelf of this fungus is red-brown to deep mahogany with a shine like poured varnish, edged in a pale yellow-white margin, and the underside is a white layer of pores. It grows kidney-shaped, 5 to 30 cm wide, often on a lacquered stalk, and it favours dead conifers, especially eastern hemlock stumps and logs. Look in southern and central Ontario through summer and fall.\n\nThis is the local relative of the Asian reishi, or lingzhi, long dried and brewed as a medicinal tea for its triterpene compounds. The flesh is corky and never eaten as food; any use is as a simmered tea. The varnished surface and the conifer host separate it from the hardwood-loving artist's conk. Take only what you need if you gather it, and leave the shelf if you simply want to look."
};
